# origin-allow-secret-patterns: assigned-credential, google-api-key
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32223, "title": "Add support to transpile modules inside node_modules", "body": "Now some of us ships NPM packages (specially components) written in ES2015 without transpiling them.\r\n\r\nThat's a pretty good thing specially if they are gonna used in a project like Next.js or CRA (which does transpiling). They offer benefits like:\r\n\r\n* No need to transpile before shipping to NPM\r\n* Get the benefit of Webpack 2's tree-shaking\r\n\r\nBut we can't do this now we [exclude](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/master/server/build/webpack.js#L166) everything inside node_modules from babel transpiling.\r\n\r\nSo, here's the proposed solution. \r\n\r\nWe have an entry in `next.config.js` to include modules which needs to go through babel. See:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n   transpileModules: [\r\n     \"my-component\",\r\n     \"redux/src\"\r\n   ]\r\n}\r\n```", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "closed", "state_reason": "resolved", "comments": 138, "reactions_total": 548, "reactions_plus": 383, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 14, "reactions_hooray": 48, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 67, "reactions_rocket": 19, "reactions_eyes": 17, "author": "arunoda", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2017-01-09T03:39:05Z", "updated_at": "2026-07-21T23:04:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32223", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 861, "title": "Offline First Support", "body": "Offline support is very useful, and is crucial for creating a modern PWA. Along with HTML manifests it helps to bridge the gap between a website and a native app. \r\n\r\nThis feature has two different flavours:\r\n\r\n- Offline support: This is the ability to navigate the site even when offline, if the site has been loaded while online. This should be the easiest feature to implement.\r\n\r\n- Offline first support: This is the ability to open the site even when offline, even if the site has not been loaded in the browser.\r\n\r\nBoth should be doable thanks to `webpack-offline` plugin. Anyhow since I'm learning both React and Next.js at once, I haven't been able to make it work yet.\r\n\r\nSteps to make it work:\r\n\r\n- Install `webpack-offline`\r\n\r\n`npm install offline-plugin --save-dev`\r\n\r\n- Create a custom `next.config.js` in your root folder\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst OfflinePlugin = require('offline-plugin');\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  webpack: (config, { dev }) => {\r\n        config.plugins = [\r\n            new OfflinePlugin()\r\n        ];\r\n    return config\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Initialize webpack-offline\r\n\r\nThis is the step I'm having problem with. I think you should do it in a component, inside `componentDidMount`, inside the top level.\r\n\r\n\r\nThis issue is both a reminder for me to keep working on this, and a request for help from someone more knowledgeable.\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 51, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 6, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "espoal", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2017-01-23T10:23:38Z", "updated_at": "2025-06-10T02:59:22Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/861", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23923, "title": "Component-based server-side caching strategy", "body": "Internal, opt-in component SSR caching/memoization instead of [page caching](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/master/examples/ssr-caching).\r\n\r\n[`react-dom-stream/lru-render-cache`](https://github.com/aickin/react-dom-stream#experimental-feature-component-caching) by @aickin\r\n> First, you must instantiate a cache object and pass it to either `renderToString` or `renderToStaticMarkup` as the `cache` attribute on the optional options argument. Currently, there is only one implementation of a cache, `LRURenderCache`, contained in the module `react-dom-stream/lru-render-cache`. It takes in an options object that has one attribute, `max`, which specifies the maximum number of characters in the cache. It looks like this:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nimport ReactDOMStream from \"react-dom-stream/server\";\r\nimport LRURenderCache from \"react-dom-stream/lru-render-cache\";\r\n\r\n// create a cache that stores 64MB of text.\r\nconst myCache = LRURenderCache({max: 64 * 1024 * 1024});\r\n\r\napp.get('/', (req, res) => {\r\n    ReactDOMStream.renderToStaticMarkup(<Foo prop={value}/>, {cache: myCache}).pipe(res);\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n> Second, you need to have your components opt in to caching by implementing a method called `componentCacheKey`, which returns a single String representing a useable cache key for that component's current state. The String returned by `componentCacheKey` **must include all inputs to rendering** so that it can be used as a cache key for the render. If `componentCacheKey` leaves out some of the inputs to rendering, it's possible that you will get cache collisions, and you will serve up the wrong content to your users. Here's an example of a correct implementation:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nimport React from \"react\"\r\n\r\nexport default class CacheableComponent extends React.Component {\r\n    render() {\r\n        return <span>Hello, ${this.props.name}!</span>;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    componentCacheKey() {\r\n        // the name prop completely specifies what the rendering will look like.\r\n        return this.props.name;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo [`next.js/examples/data-fetch/index.js`](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/master/examples/data-fetch/pages/index.js) becomes:\r\n\r\n```JavaScript\r\nimport React from 'react'\r\nimport Link from 'next/prefetch'\r\nimport 'isomorphic-fetch'\r\n\r\nexport default class MyPage extends React.Component {\r\n  static async getInitialProps () {\r\n    // eslint-disable-next-line no-undef\r\n    const res = await fetch('https://api.github.com/repos/zeit/next.js')\r\n    const json = await res.json()\r\n    return { stars: json.stargazers_count }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  render () {\r\n    return (\r\n      <div>\r\n        <p>Next.js has {this.props.stars} \u2b50\ufe0f</p>\r\n        <Link href='/preact'><a>How about preact?</a></Link>\r\n      </div>\r\n    )\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  componentCacheKey () {\r\n    return this.props.stars;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 10, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 6, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2017-02-19T20:11:03Z", "updated_at": "2026-04-29T08:03:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23923", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32230, "title": "How to catch and handle errors to report logs on server side", "body": "Hi, \r\nI'm in the situation where we want to sent errors, both on server and client side, to Sentry tool.\r\n\r\nOur app uses Express as a custom server. Basically we create an express app, apply some middlewares but delegate all the real job to the next.js handle:\r\n\r\n```\r\n  const app = nextJs({ dev: process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production' });\r\n  const handler = routes.getRequestHandler(app);\r\n  const expressApp = express();\r\n\r\n  ...\r\n  ...\r\n\r\n  expressApp.use(morgan('combined', { stream: logger.stream }));\r\n  expressApp.use(statsdMiddleware);\r\n\r\n  // Add security\r\n  expressApp.use(helmet());\r\n\r\n  // Sentry handler\r\n  expressApp.use(sentry.requestHandler());\r\n\r\n  // Load locale and translation messages\r\n  expressApp.use(i18n);\r\n\r\n  // Next.js handler\r\n  expressApp.use(handler);\r\n\r\n  // Sentry error handler.\r\n  // MUST be placed before any other express error handler !!!\r\n  expressApp.use(sentry.errorHandler());\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith this approach next.js takes control over the rendering process and any error is catch by next.js and the only way I have to process it is overriding the `_error.js` page file.\r\n\r\nWithin that `_error.js` file I need a universal way to report errors to Sentry. Currently there are two libraries (`raven` for node and `raven-js` por javascript). The proble is I can't import both of them because `raven` works for SSR but fails when webpack builds the bundle, and also `raven-js` fails due XMLHTTPRequest dependency too.\r\n\r\nIs there a way I can be notified for next.js error on server side?", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 113, "reactions_total": 81, "reactions_plus": 81, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "acanimal", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2017-05-02T14:58:33Z", "updated_at": "2024-02-14T20:27:28Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32230", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32231, "title": "Route cancellation", "body": "<!--- Provide a general summary of the issue in the Title above -->\r\n\r\n<!--\r\n    Thank you very much for contributing to Next.js by creating an issue! \u2764\ufe0f\r\n    To avoid duplicate issues we ask you to check off the following list\r\n-->\r\n\r\n<!-- Checked checkbox should look like this: [x] -->\r\n- [x] I have searched the [issues](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues) of this repository and believe that this is not a duplicate.\r\n\r\n## Expected Behavior\r\n<!--- If you're describing a bug, tell us what should happen -->\r\n<!--- If you're suggesting a change/improvement, tell us how it should work -->\r\nA method in router API that allows aborting upcoming route change.\r\n\r\n## Current Behavior\r\n<!--- If describing a bug, tell us what happens instead of the expected behavior -->\r\n<!--- If suggesting a change/improvement, explain the difference from current behavior -->\r\nRight now router exposes `abortComponentLoad` method but it seems to be designed only for internal use and it doesn't work if it's called in `routeChangeStart` handler. It works only after this piece of code was executed:\r\n```js\r\nasync fetchComponent (route, as) {\r\n  let cancelled = false\r\n  const cancel = this.componentLoadCancel = function () {\r\n\u00a0   cancelled = true\r\n  }\r\n```\r\nWorkaround:\r\n```js\r\nRouter.ready(() => {\r\n  Router.router.on('routeChangeStart', () => {\r\n    setTimeout(() => {\r\n      Router.router.abortComponentLoad();\r\n    });\r\n  });\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Context\r\n<!--- How has this issue affected you? What are you trying to accomplish? -->\r\n<!--- Providing context helps us come up with a solution that is most useful in the real world -->\r\nThis is useful for example when building complex forms and we want to warn a user before he leaves the page without submitting.\r\n\r\n## Your Environment\r\n<!--- Include as many relevant details about the environment you experienced the bug in -->\r\nANY\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 152, "reactions_total": 178, "reactions_plus": 178, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "cmwd", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2017-07-06T05:53:10Z", "updated_at": "2026-04-13T06:30:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32231", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32216, "title": "Use relative URLs instead of absolute", "body": "There are many tags in the HTML which NextJS generates which contain absolute URLs. For example:\r\n\r\n```\r\nhref=\"/_next/blah\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nI've added selenium + chrome headless testing to my Next project, and it works fine when I test http://localhost:3000, but when I do a `next export`, and then try to to test `file:///home/user/project_dir/out/index.html`, all of those absolute links are broken.I.e, it tries to fetch `file:///_next/blah` instead of: `file:///home/user/project_dir/out/_next/blah`.\r\n\r\nIf I post-edit the generated HTML to make those links relative, then it all works fine.\r\n\r\nI need to be able to run my tests against the static files which are generated by export to ensure that they have been generated correctly before publishing.\r\n\r\nI suspect this is also an issue when people simply wish to run their nextjs application from a location other than the root of their website.\r\n\r\n<!-- Checked checkbox should look like this: [x] -->\r\n- [x] I have searched the [issues](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues) of this repository and believe that this is not a duplicate.\r\n\r\n## Your Environment\r\n<!--- Include as many relevant details about the environment you experienced the bug in -->\r\n| Tech    | Version |\r\n|---------|---------|\r\n| next    | v3.0.1-beta.15 |\r\n| node    | v8.1.3 |\r\n| OS      | Debian 9 |\r\n| browser | Google Chrome 59.0.3071.115 |\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 72, "reactions_total": 91, "reactions_plus": 90, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "mikecardwell", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2017-07-17T06:35:04Z", "updated_at": "2025-05-10T07:16:49Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32216", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 62352, "title": "Provide a mechanizm to disable SSR", "body": "**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**\r\nThe problem is that sometimes server load is high because of SSR. Right now it can be optimized by turning off SSR for some components, but it doesn't solve the problem fully.\r\n\r\n**Describe the solution you'd like**\r\nIdeally - to provide something like Electrode has: [Server Side Rendering Modes](https://docs.electrode.io/chapter1/intermediate/server-side-data-hydration.html#server-side-rendering-modes).\r\n> Electrode constantly monitors the performance of your app and turns off server-side rendering if app performance is degrading.\r\n\r\n**Describe alternatives you've considered**\r\nAn alternative (and probably more elastic) solution would be to provide SSR Modes without any performance monitoring.\r\n\r\n**Additional context**\r\nI know that right now next.js is tightly coupled with SSR, but SSR modes should help a lot at large scale.\r\n\r\nP.S. Thanks you guys for working on such a great project ;)", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 39, "reactions_total": 228, "reactions_plus": 196, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 21, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 11, "author": "cherniavskii", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2018-05-15T08:59:31Z", "updated_at": "2025-10-12T09:45:48Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/62352", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32215, "title": "Feature request: make logging possible on ejected custom server", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nUsing custom server interferes with other logging in my app.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nA way to silence `next` and an interface to tap into `ready`, `recompile`, `error` events.\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst app = next({ dev: true, quiet: true })\r\napp.on('ready', myLogger.info);\r\napp.on('recompile', myLogger.trace);\r\napp.on('error', err => myLogger.error('something went wrong', { err }));\r\n\r\nconst handle = app.getRequestHandler()\r\n// ...\r\n```\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 54, "reactions_total": 117, "reactions_plus": 117, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Janpot", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2018-07-20T09:33:49Z", "updated_at": "2024-08-09T11:33:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32215", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32217, "title": "Truly static pages without react app", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\nWe want some static pages to be as lightweight as possible and we're fine with full page reloads navigating out of them if we can chop off extra 200Kb.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\nAdd an option to path map in next.config.js to indicate this specific page doesn't need to include react app and can operate as plain HTML page.\r\nReact app is only removed in case the flag is set to `true` explicitly.\r\n\r\nLooks like it should be enough to just make `files` content  empty there if option is set. https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/server/render.js#L100\r\n\r\nFor the simple example from tutorial setting up\r\n```javascript\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  exportPathMap: function(defaultPathMap) {\r\n    return {\r\n      '/': { page: '/', withoutReact: true },\r\n      '/about': { page: '/about' }\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAs a result `/out` would be populated with\r\n```html\r\n<!--/out/index/index.html-->\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html>\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charSet=\"utf-8\" class=\"next-head\"/>\r\n  </head>\r\n<body>\r\n   <a href=\"/about\">About</a>\r\n</body>\r\n</html>\r\n\r\n\r\n<!--/out/about/index.html-->\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html>\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charSet=\"utf-8\" class=\"next-head\"/>\r\n    <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"/_next/9d298d49-6fb5-4c6f-a992-31fdb0d3e01a/page/about.js\" as=\"script\"/>\r\n    <!--other scripts-->\r\n  </head>\r\n<body>\r\n   <!--page content -->\r\n   <script>\r\n     NEXT_DATA = {\"props\": \"etc..\"}\r\n   </script>\r\n  <script id=\"__NEXT_PAGE__/about\" src=\"...\"></script>\r\n</body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis way index page is going to be very lightweight, with a tradeoff of full page reload navigating to /about. While /about would load the app, so navigation back to / would be handled on the client-side.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\nStatic site generators :) But we need way more than just static pages and next.js fits us best except for this tiny quirk.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\nHappy to implement that, just wanted to make sure this is not something going against the roadmap for next.js. Or if it's something already available, but hidden. I'm new to the library, only playing around for the second day.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 46, "reactions_total": 194, "reactions_plus": 136, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 44, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 14, "author": "EugeneKostrikov", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2018-08-29T13:16:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-30T21:16:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32217", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 5205, "title": "Mock `withRouter` and other Contexts or Globals in tests and UI previews", "body": "## Mock `withRouter`\r\n\r\n### What we need\r\nAn easy way to mock `withRouter` and other context related stuff. \r\n\r\n### What it currently breaks\r\n\r\nIf you create a component utilizing `withRouter`, you're not able to render it on its own any longer, as it's dependent on a global context.\r\n\r\nThis mainly breaks: \r\n1. Tests \r\n2. Component based UI libs like `storyboard` or `react cosmos`. \r\n\r\n### Trying to work around it\r\n\r\nA simple, manual mock for example via:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nimport {withRouter} from \"next/router\";\r\n\r\nwithRouter =  Component => props => <Component {...props} router={mockedRouter} />\r\n```\r\nwill throw an error (`TypeError: Cannot set property`),  like this one https://github.com/babel/babel/issues/8363.\r\n\r\nThis is a related issue, where this approach was suggested: https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/1827\r\n\r\nThis suggestion (https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/1827#issuecomment-420735395) is a monstrosity for a simple thing. And as the author, @ssylvia, correctly remarked - relies on the legacy context API. But i will use it in the meantime.\r\n \r\n## What i'd like\r\nSome way to replace the whole Next context or pieces of it, without having to know **how** Next is creating and providing context internally.\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 8, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "nickluger", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2018-09-19T14:42:55Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T16:59:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/5205", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32232, "title": "Add sourcemap support for Node.js in development", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nI get errors like:\r\n\r\nTypeError: Cannot read property 'i18n' of undefined\r\n    at new MyApp (/Users/sheerun/Source/Project/search/.next/server/static/development/pages/_app.js:85530:22)\r\n\r\nWhen error happens on server side, with newest next.js 7\r\n\r\nHonestly it's hard to debug such errors when I need to dig into compiled file.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 20, "reactions_total": 19, "reactions_plus": 19, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "sheerun", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2018-09-27T10:23:03Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T19:02:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32232", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32233, "title": "Link API breaks a11y intuition", "body": "# Feature request\r\nI want a more intuitive `Link` API which respects expectations we have around native `a` tags.\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\nThe current link API looks like the following:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n<Link href=\"/about\">\r\n  <a>here</a>\r\n</Link>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is problematic for a few reasons:\r\n1. It breaks intuition around a11y. When I see an `a` tag without an `href`, alarm bells go off, because that is a UX anti-pattern. In fact, this API makes it impossible to appease the `anchor-is-valid` eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y rule (see https://github.com/evcohen/eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y/issues/402).\r\n1. It requires a call to `cloneElement` which should be avoided as its behavior is confusing. (How would I know my `a` tag will be given an `href`? Nothing about this API suggests that.)\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\nI would like a solution which doesn't require you to type an `a` tag without an `href` and also doesn't require you to duplicate the `href` information.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\nThere are a few good alternatives:\r\n\r\n1. Default to `a` tag if no children are given:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n<Link href=\"/about\" />\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is the approach [react-router](https://reacttraining.com/react-router/web/api/Link) uses and it works quite well.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n2. Use a render prop:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n<Link href=\"/about\">\r\n  {href => <a href={href}>here</a>}\r\n</Link>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis method appeases the `anchor-is-valid` rule and doesn't require the user to duplicate href information since the build href is passed down to the component they are rendering.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n3. Component injection:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n<Link href=\"/about\" component={a} />\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nA combination of 1 and 2 would be powerful enough to cover all reasonable use cases.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\nhttps://github.com/zeit/next.js/commit/<HASH_REDACTED>\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 24, "reactions_total": 261, "reactions_plus": 240, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 14, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 7, "author": "danny-andrews-snap", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2018-10-26T17:04:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T08:12:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32233", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32294, "title": "Add subdomain routing example", "body": "Nextjs is awesome framework, but i have website with subdomain\r\n\r\n* blog.domain.com\r\n* forum.domain.com\r\n\r\nI want to handle them on single app with nextjs, like laravel routing system allow use multiple subdomain on single app", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 27, "reactions_total": 36, "reactions_plus": 36, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ardyfeb", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2018-11-15T18:36:31Z", "updated_at": "2024-09-08T08:08:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32294", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 6009, "title": "[RFC] cleaning distDir on next build", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nCurrently Next.js will keep the `distDir` (by default `.next`) when running `next build` because some deployment strategies involve running `next build` while the production application is running. However in most cases you actually want to start with a clean directory. For example when running `next export` after the build and Next.js having been in development mode before the build, this would cause HMR files to be written to the export out directory as outlined in #5554.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nOne solution is cleaning distDir by default and providing an option to opt-out of this behavior. I'd like to get some feedback on this though.\r\n", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 44, "reactions_total": 64, "reactions_plus": 61, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 3, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "timneutkens", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-01-08T14:22:19Z", "updated_at": "2025-03-20T14:02:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/6009", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 6014, "title": "Set aria-current attributes on next/link", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\nRight now you have to do this yourself and I'd bet many forget this. It would be really nice to have this included in `next/link` by default.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\nSet a `aria-current` attribute on the active link(s). I could work on this, but I might need some guidance on how this should be implemented since we probably have to wrap the link component in a `withRoute` hoc.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\nN/A\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\nhttps://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria-1.1/#aria-current\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 27, "reactions_plus": 26, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "alexandernanberg", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-01-09T09:38:59Z", "updated_at": "2022-12-31T00:19:00Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/6014", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 24822, "title": "Support for Sucrase", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\nNot a problem, but in large applications, compile time can take time, even with all the improvements made in Webpack and Next. Time is precious!\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n[Sucrase](https://github.com/alangpierce/sucrase) is a super fast alternative to Babel (20x faster, according to the maintainer) that transpiles only the codes that are not natively supported by Javascript, such as JSX, Typescript Typing, anyway.\r\nIt can drastically decrease Next build time in the development environment. \r\n\r\nLook at the benchmark provided in the package documentation:\r\n\r\n```\r\n             Time      Speed\r\nSucrase      2.928s    225752 lines per second\r\nswc          13.782s   47966 lines per second\r\nTypeScript   39.603s   16693 lines per second\r\nBabel        52.598s   12569 lines per second\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\nI do not know if it is possible to implement this by default in the Next development mode (because the codes have to work in both the browser and nodejs) or if you are interested, but it would be cool to add an example to the /examples folder of how to do the implement it.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 16, "reactions_plus": 15, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "iagobruno", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-03-21T20:15:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-09T16:16:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/24822", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 34051, "title": "RFC: API Routes", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nCurrently a lot of users create a custom `server.js` to handle creating their API.\r\n\r\nHowever this causes some issues:\r\n\r\n- React rendering is sync, meaning that while rendering performance for requests to the API can differ\r\n- The API and routing solely depend on the custom server, meaning that it can only be deployed as one server\r\n- Because it's one server the bundle size of this server is quite heavy (slow bootup)\r\n- Because it's one server your complete API goes down if one handler has a uncaught exception\r\n\r\nThere's also some other ergonomic issues:\r\n\r\n- Because the server runs outside of Next.js it is not compiled, meaning you can't use `import` / `export`\r\n- Because `import` / `export` is not supported we've seen many users start compiling their custom `server.js` which introduces another build step, that is different from Next.js. Furthermore they add `nodemon`, to sort of get hot reloading of the server file, which means Next.js is rebooted every time you make changes to the API, losing all compilation state.\r\n- We've seen this extra build step being implemented wrong many times, for example in doing so tree-shaking was disabled because of Babel options being different for client/server compilation.\r\n\r\nThe solution to most of these problems revolve around 3 issues:\r\n\r\n- Dynamic routing\r\n- Being able to create APIs\r\n- Middleware (see #7208)\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nThis proposal goes into the API creation part of the issue.\r\n\r\nThe solution that I have in mind involves a few small changes to Next.js. It comes down to:\r\n\r\n- The user will be able to create a `api` directory inside of `pages`. This is because `pages/api` has to do with routing and we might change `pages` to `routes` in the future.\r\n- This `api` directory is automatically mapped to `/api` (as expected)\r\n- So for example you can create `api/posts.js` and it will be mapped to `/api/posts`\r\n- The handle will look like: `export default (req, res) =>` this means that you can use any of the http server frameworks like `express` `micro` etc.\r\n- In case of `micro` I think we should change the API of the programmatic usage to this:\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport micro from 'micro'\r\n\r\nexport default micro((req, res) \u21d2 {\r\n   return 'Hello World'\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nCurrent invoking `micro()` will return a Node.js `http.Server` which doesn't work well with just `req, res` as a handler. This also ties into using micro standalone with `@now/node` . Doing this will allow us to deprecate `micro-dev` too in favor of using Next.js /  `now dev` + `@now/node`\r\n\r\n\r\nUsage for express would look like:\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport express from 'express'\r\n\r\nconst app = express()\r\n\r\nexport default app\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nNote that express would not be in charge of routing, just the request handling, meaning that in most cases you actually want to use `micro` as it's much smaller and has better performance.\r\n\r\n", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "locked", "state_reason": null, "comments": 34, "reactions_total": 129, "reactions_plus": 95, "reactions_minus": 1, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 5, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 28, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "timneutkens", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-05-10T13:37:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-11T02:15:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/34051", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23034, "title": "How to mock useRouter?", "body": "# Question about Next.js\r\n\r\nI'm want to make sure my component renders correctly with useRouter hook (actually I'm trying to understand how new dynamic routing works), so I have code:\r\n```js\r\nimport React from 'react';\r\nimport { NextPage } from 'next';\r\nimport { useRouter } from 'next/router';\r\n\r\nconst UserInfo : NextPage = () => {\r\n  const router = useRouter();\r\n  const { query } = router;\r\n\r\n  return <div>Hello {query.user}</div>;\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport default UserInfo;\r\n```\r\nAnd what I'm trying is:\r\n```js\r\n// test\r\nimport { render, cleanup, waitForElement } from '@testing-library/react';\r\n\r\nimport UserInfo from './$user';\r\n\r\n// somehow mock useRouter for $user component!!!\r\n\r\nafterEach(cleanup);\r\n\r\nit('Should render correctly on route: /users/nikita', async () => {\r\n  const { getByText } = render(<UserInfo />);\r\n\r\n  await waitForElement(() => getByText(/Hello nikita!/i));\r\n});\r\n```\r\nBut I get an error `TypeError: Cannot read property 'query' of null` which points on `const router = useRouter();` line.\r\n\r\nP. S. I know dynamic routing is available on canary verions just for now and might change, but I get a problem with router, not with WIP feature (am I?).", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 100, "reactions_total": 18, "reactions_plus": 18, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "nikitowsky", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-05-31T22:12:24Z", "updated_at": "2025-07-14T14:46:18Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23034", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 7563, "title": "[RFC] - Module/nomodule for NextJs", "body": "#### WIP \r\n\r\nI have these changes working on my branch. I am actively testing the changes with different sites and also trying to improve the build size. I will raise a PR soon, but would love to get any feedback about the approach.\r\n\r\n## Goal\r\n\r\nShip modern JS to modern browsers.\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\n`next build` compiles all client side JS with a default babel-preset-env options, which transpiles all ECMAScript 2015+ code. While this ensures support for older browsers, it ends up shipping unnecessary Babel helpers and polyfills to modern browsers.\r\n\r\nTranspiling JS to target modern browsers will have a significant impact on bundle size and performance.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nGenerate modern JS files as part of the current build, by compiling them with babel-preset-env options:\r\n\r\n```\r\n[\r\n  [\r\n    \"@babel/preset-env\",\r\n    {\r\n      \"targets\": {\r\n        \"esmodules\": true\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n]\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith this option, Babel transpiles files to target a [modern browser set](https://github.com/babel/babel/blob/master/packages/babel-preset-env/data/built-in-modules.json) and generates relatively modern code.\r\n\r\nWe can then conditionally ship modern/legacy bundle with the module/nomodule pattern. (Read [this blog for more info](https://philipwalton.com/articles/deploying-es2015-code-in-production-today/)). The module/nomodule pattern provides a convenient and reliable mechanism for serving modern JavaScript to modern browsers while still allowing older browsers to fall back to polyfilled ES5.\r\n\r\n`<script type=\"module\" src=\"modern-build.js\">` - to load the modern files\r\n\r\n`<script nomodule src=\"current-build.js\">` - fallback for browsers that don\u2019t support the modern features.\r\n\r\n## Integrating with NextJS\r\n\r\nCreate a plugin similar to [BabelESMPlugin](https://github.com/prateekbh/babel-esm-plugin) that integrates into Nextjs and outputs two versions of bundles on build, a default bundle and a modern bundle. This plugin spawns an additional child compiler inside webpack and re-runs the entire build with the modern babel config options.\r\n\r\n## Optional feature\r\n\r\nThis feature will be enabled behind a flag in `next.config.js`. The flag would be `modern: true` to enable and `modern: false` to disable.\r\n\r\n## Things to note\r\n\r\n- I built and tested this plugin locally with a couple of NextJs sites (built using NextJS). Compiling two versions of bundles increase the overall build time slightly. I noticed an average increase of `15-20%` in build time. _But in general, the build time increase varies a lot based on the project's build config and its size/structure._\r\n\r\n- Some stats on the before/after bundle sizes\r\n\r\n| Site                                       | Size Before | Size after |\r\n| ------------------------------------------ | ----------- | ---------- |\r\n| Hello World app                            | 72.64 kb    | 72.61 kb   |\r\n| [geo.data.gouv.fr/en](https://geo.data.gouv.fr/en) | 463.03 kb   | 456.37 kb  |\r\n| Zeit.co                                    | 7.74 MB     | 7.41 MB    |\r\n| dp.la                                      | 653.15 kb   | 639.09 kb  |\r\n\r\n- While the size wins from modern compilation might seem small from the table, there are a couple of factors that we need to consider\r\n\r\n  - Pre-transpiled node_modules - A large part of the above sites are node_module files. I ran my changes only on user files, since most of the published node_module libs are already transpiled.\r\n  - Default polyfills - Having a modern chunk provides an opportunity to do further/modern optimization. For eg: We can remove some of the default polyfills included in the current NextJs build like fetch/regenerator-runtime.\r\n  - Parse time wins - I don't have quantifying numbers for this, but In the longer term, modern code will be parsed/executed faster than the legacy code.\r\n\r\n- Parallel work - Some additional optimizations that we are looking into\r\n\r\n  - Removing/move polyfills from the modern build. NextJs by default ships polyfills like fetch and regenerator-runtime, these can be safely removed from the modern build.\r\n  - node_modules source - We are working to have node_modules (atleast the ones NextJs directly depends on for now) to have them publish non-transpiled code as ESmodules. This gives us a lot of flexibility to choose the level of tranpilation needed for our modern bundle. Further/better optimization can be performed on these modern code such as better dedup, and tree-shaking, etc.\r\n\r\n  - Investigation into using [useBuiltIns: usage](https://babeljs.io/docs/en/babel-preset-env#usebuiltins-usage-experimental) for modern build\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n**Overall, having a modern bundle unlocks a lot of new optimization opportunities that can be further explored.**\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 27, "reactions_total": 189, "reactions_plus": 75, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 56, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 27, "reactions_rocket": 15, "reactions_eyes": 16, "author": "janicklas-ralph", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-06-12T17:33:46Z", "updated_at": "2024-09-10T07:20:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/7563", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 43294, "title": "Entire app remounts on error", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nI'm not sure if this is intentional or not, but it seems undesirable at least. This is in production mode.\r\n\r\nWhen an error occurs during rendering, triggering Next.js\u2019 error boundary, the entire `<App>` component remounts. This is counter to what I think the developer\u2019s expectation is, which is that `<App>` is only mounted once and remains mounted for the entire duration.\r\n\r\nI can't think of a good reason this would be intentional. It seems more likely that the tree above `<App>` changes in some incidental way, such that React can no longer update the existing element instances but remounts them instead. For example:\r\n\r\n```\r\n<SomeContainer>                 <OtherContainer>\r\n  <App>                           <App>\r\n    <Page />   -- on error -->      <Page />\r\n  </App>                          </App>\r\n</SomeContainer>                </OtherContainer>\r\n```\r\n\r\nor:\r\n\r\n```\r\n                              <ErrorContainer>\r\n<App>                           <App>  \r\n  <Page />   -- on error -->      <Page />\r\n</App>                          </App>\r\n                              </ErrorContainer>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe downside is that code people expected to mount only once (potentially expensive initialization, recreating things that don't need to be recreated, etc.) can happen multiple times.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nThis repo demonstrates the issue: https://github.com/exogen/next-error-remount\r\n\r\nClicking the button will logs what is being unmounted.\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nTransition to the error state should be just like transitioning pages: the `<Page>` should unmount and the `<Error>` should mount, but the `<App>` should remain mounted.\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- Version of Next.js: 8.0.4, 8.1.0, canary", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 6, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "exogen", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-06-14T16:33:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-23T14:22:59Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/43294", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 7579, "title": "Turn on source maps by default (for dev + production)", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\ntl;dr: Can we please turn source map generation on for dev and production? :)\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nAs of https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/943, Next.js does not output source maps by default. This is wee bit inconsistent with other popular React tooling such as CRA, which [do output maps without developer opt-in](https://github.com/facebook/create-react-app/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/packages/react-scripts/config/webpack.config.js#L42) when you `npm run build` or `npm run start`. I would like Next to consider changing this behavior to align with developer expectations of React scaffolding tooling.\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/110953/59524911-e3802280-8e89-11e9-8405-4b474b88b750.png)\r\n\r\nOverall, despite some discussion of shortcomings (e.g https://github.com/facebook/create-react-app/issues/139), the CRA community appears to have found more value keeping source maps enabled than not. Preact CLI also outputs source maps by default in dev and prod builds.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI would like Next.js to switch on source map generation for dev and production by default. \r\n\r\nReasons to turn them on:\r\n\r\n* Bundle analysis tooling (like webpack-bundle-analyzer or source-map-explorer) require source maps in order for developers to dive into why their bundles may be bloated. Without this turned on, developers need to do additional work to take advantage of these tools.\r\n* Browsers like Chrome are starting to explore adding audits for opportunities to optimize what is in your bundle via Lighthouse (requires source maps) and improved debugging via DevTools if we know the structure of your application (also requires source maps)\r\n* It should be trivial for developers to disable source maps via DevTools during their developer iteration workflow if they want them unmapped for debugging.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nAn alternative to turning on source maps by default is a docs change where we just encourage more developers to opt into using source maps for dev and prod. I don't believe this will lead to as many developers getting the full advantages of having source maps turned on however... :) \r\n\r\ncc @developit in case he has further input\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 37, "reactions_total": 100, "reactions_plus": 80, "reactions_minus": 3, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 17, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "addyosmani", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-06-14T17:35:29Z", "updated_at": "2024-12-12T08:48:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/7579", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 26427, "title": "Dev mode hot reload loses database connection", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nLoving v9! Just ran into an issue where I can\u2019t use an existing database connection with the new API routes.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\n`mongooseConnect.js`:\r\n```js\r\nconst mongoose = require(\"mongoose\").set(\"debug\", true);\r\nconst connection = {};\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = async () => {\r\n  if (connection.isConnected) {\r\n    // Using existing database connection\r\n    return;\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // Using new database connection\r\n  const db = await mongoose.connect(\"mongodb://localhost:27017/my-app\",\r\n    { useCreateIndex: true, useFindAndModify: false, useNewUrlParser: true }\r\n  );\r\n\r\n  connection.isConnected = db.connections[0].readyState;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. Require the above function into an API endpoint that uses the new `pages/api/` feature and requires\r\n2. Run `next dev`\r\n3. Ping the API endpoint and see the correct response\r\n4. Edit `mongooseConnect.js` and save\r\n5. Ping the API endpoint again\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nThe response will be the same. Instead, I get the following error:\r\n\r\n```\r\nMongooseError: You can not `mongoose.connect()` multiple times while connected.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is because `db` returns to `{}` with the HMR.\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: macOS\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.0.0\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nIs there a different way we should be handling serverless database connections or is this a bug?\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 39, "reactions_total": 16, "reactions_plus": 16, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "seejamescode", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-07-09T00:11:31Z", "updated_at": "2024-06-26T07:57:01Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/26427", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32234, "title": "Allow skipping getInitialProps on history navigation", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\nMake it possible to somehow skip getInitialProps when a page is loaded as the result of the user navigating back/forward in history, reusing the initial props that were provided when that page was first loaded instead.\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\n**Demo app: [Preview](https://headbangstagram.adamwathan.now.sh/) - [Source](https://github.com/adamwathan/next-nested-routing)**\r\n\r\nRight now whenever you press back or forward, getInitialProps is run which often means making a network request to fetch some data.\r\n\r\nThis is a little bit annoying when combined with same page route transitions, because it can often lead to a slower than expected user experience for something that seems like it should be instant.\r\n\r\nFor example, say you have an Instagram style page that lists a bunch of photos, and clicking one photo focuses that photo in a modal dialog, and also changes the URL.\r\n\r\nWhen the user hits back (or closes the modal manually, given you've implemented closing the modal as a `Router.back()` call), `getInitialProps` runs again on the page, causing the page to wait for the network request before closing the modal.\r\n\r\nThis seems like a waste because the data that was provided on the *original* getInitialProps call is *still visible behind the modal*, fetching it again actually seems counter to what the user would expect.\r\n\r\nThis points to a bigger point of discussion which is:\r\n\r\n*Do end users generally expect history navigation to trigger a refresh of the data on the page they are navigating to?*\r\n\r\nI would argue that no, this is not really the normal behavior of the web. Hitting \"back\" in your browser loads the previous page from disk cache, it doesn't make a fresh network request, and I think users are actually trained to expect and rely on this behavior (ever been in a situation where your network connectivity all of a sudden went to crap and you needed to see a page you already visited and instinctively knew to navigate to it using back/forward and *not* by clicking any links on the page?)\r\n\r\nThere are probably a lot of Next.js apps relying on the current behavior so it's likely not something that would make sense to completely overhaul, but I think it does make sense to make it possible for users to opt-out of the \"fresh props on back/forward\" behavior and instead have their apps behave more like how the browser behaves by default with traditional server-rendered web pages.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI'm not sure what the best API for this would look like, perhaps another option you can export in the `config` object for a page or something configured globally at the app level?\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nI tried to sort of hack this by forcing history navigation to act like a shallow link using `beforePopState`:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nclass MyApp extends App {\r\n  componentDidMount() {\r\n    Router.beforePopState(({ url, as, options }) => {\r\n      options.shallow = true\r\n      return true\r\n    })\r\n  }\r\n  // ...\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n...and this actually *does* work for same page route transitions, but not when hitting back/forward to go from one page to another.\r\n\r\nI also tried caching the results of getInitialProps in my page components, but it still runs the first time you hit back, since the call that happens on the server can't populate the client side cache. I'd also like to avoid worrying about cache invalidation if possible, so I'd really like to find a solution other than caching as it has implications on regular `<Link>`-based page navigation as well which is not ideal.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 11, "reactions_total": 55, "reactions_plus": 42, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 10, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 3, "author": "adamwathan", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-07-09T01:05:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-30T21:37:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32234", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 8000, "title": "TypeError: Cannot read property 'INTERNAL' of undefined at FirebaseNamespace.initializeApp", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nI've filed the issue with a reproduction [here](https://github.com/firebase/firebase-admin-node/issues/593) on the firebase-admin-node repo, but it seems to be a problem that specifically occurs when running this code in a Next 9 app, so I also want to post it here.\r\n\r\nThe firebase admin SDK initialization throws an error when I attempt to initialize it inside of a lamdba defined through the `pages/api` routing method.\r\n\r\nIs there any special setup required for firebase-admin to be used inside of a Next app?\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nVisit the issue link to see the reproduction repo: https://github.com/firebase/firebase-admin-node/issues/593", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "liamdanielduffy", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-07-16T15:27:07Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T09:27:21Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/8000", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23972, "title": "Ability to pass a custom tsconfig for `next dev`", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nI'm bored of seeing `someVar is declared but its value is never read`:\r\n\r\n<p align=\"center\"><img src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13215662/64862750-bee44400-d64c-11e9-9a90-859ada1d3b00.png\" width=\"80%\"></p>\r\n\r\nI'm actively write and delete code, exploring some libraries' features - I don't want my dev server to stuck me into an error. Of course it's bad to leave them. Of course my IDE should highlight that - and for that reason, I want to have `noUnusedLocals` or `noUnusedParameters`, `strict` in overall. Of course there should be some pre-commit hook that will prevent me from committing this clutter into the repository. But I don\u2019t want my dev server to throw `noUnusedLocals` and `noUnusedParameters` - I don't want it to prevent me playing with the code.\r\n\r\nUPD. That\u2019s okay for the default behavior, but there should be an option that these keys should be enabled by VSCode, and by linter, but not used by dev server so it won\u2019t throw error that prevents you to do anything.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI'd like an ability of passing a tsconfig path for `next dev` command.\r\n\r\nSomething like:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nnext dev --tsconfig=tsconfig.devserver.json\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhile I can use the original tsconfig.json for my IDE.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nWell, I could make an issue for IDE (`vscode` doesn't support anything other than `tsconfig.json` too), but anyway - both sides should support that.\r\n\r\nUPD. There's one: https://github.com/Microsoft/vscode/issues/12463. Still, both sides should support that.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nIdk maybe it's possible. But I don't see it from `next dev --help`:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13215662/61650497-4b1d5f00-accd-11e9-89c6-ab5dca8a6d21.png)\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 15, "reactions_total": 17, "reactions_plus": 17, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jerrygreen", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-07-22T17:09:39Z", "updated_at": "2023-07-20T21:38:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23972", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32235, "title": "Next.js should have an unobtrusive linting toast", "body": "Next.js should emit linting and non-critical build errors in an unobtrusive toast instead of a full-screen error overlay.\r\n\r\nNotably, this is desirable for TypeScript since some users rely on TypeScript as a **linter instead of type checker**.\r\n\r\nCloses https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/8060", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 17, "reactions_plus": 13, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 2, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 2, "author": "Timer", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-07-22T20:31:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T14:43:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32235", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 8207, "title": "RFC: Next.js <Link to=\"\"> to handle dynamic routes", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nNext.js should be able to deduce what route should be rendered for a given URL.\r\n\r\nRight now, Next.js requires you specify both the original page and route:\r\n```jsx\r\n<Link href=\"/news/[id]\" as=\"/news/1234\">\r\n  <a> ... </a>\r\n</Link>\r\n\r\n// or\r\n\r\n<Link href=\"/news/[id]?sort=desc\" as=\"/news/1234?sort=desc\">\r\n  <a> ... </a>\r\n</Link>\r\n```\r\n\r\nHowever, Next.js should know that `/news/1234` matches `/news/[id]` as it does with the production web server.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nNext.js should accept a Link prop that automatically handles this behavior, potentially like so:\r\n```jsx\r\n<Link to=\"/news/1234\"> {/* knows to render `./pages/news/[id].js` */}\r\n  <a>News Article 1234</a>\r\n</Link>\r\n\r\n// or\r\n\r\n<Link to=\"/news/1234?sort=desc\"> {/* knows to render `./pages/news/[id].js` */}\r\n  <a>News Article 1234</a>\r\n</Link>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis proposal uses `to` instead of making `href` have different behavior so we could potentially make it opt-into not using the child component, instead, rendering `<a>` automatically like React Router or Reach Router do.\r\n\r\nMeaning:\r\n```jsx\r\n<Link to=\"/news/1234\"> {/* knows to render `./pages/news/[id].js` */}\r\n  News Article 1234\r\n</Link>\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nNext.js could fall back to matching a dynamic route if not found:\r\n```jsx\r\n<Link href=\"/news/1234\"> {/* knows to render `./pages/news/[id].js` */}\r\n  <a> ... </a>\r\n</Link>\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nNext.js currently does not ship a routing manifest, and that'd be a requirement for this feature to work.\r\nThis routing manifest would make the app size heavier than if the user always instructed Next.js with `href` and `as`, but some could argue the DX is worth it.\r\n\r\n## TBD\r\n\r\n1. We should ban trailing slashes in `to` so we can make this a framework feature, i.e. `trailingSlash: true`.", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 46, "reactions_total": 202, "reactions_plus": 147, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 46, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 9, "author": "Timer", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-08-01T15:06:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T00:25:16Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/8207", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32236, "title": "Next.js API routes (and pages) should support reading files", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nIt's currently not possible to read files from API routes or pages.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI want to be able to call `fs.readFile` with a `__dirname` path and have it \"just work\".\r\n\r\nThis should work in Development and Production mode.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nThis may need to integrate with `@zeit/webpack-asset-relocator-loader` in some capacity. This plugin handles these types of requires.\r\n\r\nHowever, it's not a necessity. I'd be OK with something that _only_ works with `__dirname` and `__filename` (no relative or cwd-based paths).\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n```jsx\r\n// pages/api/test.js\r\nimport fs from 'fs'\r\nimport path from 'path'\r\n\r\nexport default (req, res) => {\r\n  const fileContent = fs.readFileSync(\r\n    path.join(__dirname, '..', '..', 'package.json'), \r\n    'utf8'\r\n  )\r\n  // ...\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n> Note: I know you can cheat the above example \u261d\ufe0f with `require`, but that's not the point. \ud83d\ude04 ", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 226, "reactions_total": 280, "reactions_plus": 228, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 1, "reactions_rocket": 44, "reactions_eyes": 7, "author": "Timer", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-08-05T18:13:45Z", "updated_at": "2024-04-13T07:55:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32236", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 26431, "title": "Async setup work in API route", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem?\r\n\r\nCurrently it's not possible to make async operations before exporting the handler from a API route.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI'd like to be able to use the same mecanism from [micro](https://zeit.co/blog/micro-9-1) to be able to do async work before returning a handler.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nI opened a discussion about it on spectrum : https://spectrum.chat/next-js/general/async-api-route~b1d7dddd-50fc-4f6f-a565-63fa68a725be\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 17, "reactions_total": 4, "reactions_plus": 4, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jgoux", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-09-05T08:49:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-04T17:43:18Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/26431", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 8969, "title": "Clearer handling of application initialization", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nThere are numerous examples of how to \"bootstrap\" an app with state: Redux, Apollo, authentication, and so on.\r\n\r\nThese typically rely on \"augmenting\" the app with various HOC wrappers. For example, `next-redux-wrapper` provides a wrapper that injects itself into `App`, whereas `examples/with-apollo-auth` provides a wrapper that must be wrapped around every single page.\r\n\r\nThey all have various solutions for the same thing: Initializing global state, storing it in the context, handling hydration/dehyration, plus using heuristics like `typeof window !== 'undefined'` to conditionally store the state in locally or in a global variable (e.g. for the Apollo client or Redux store) for when it is used client-side.\r\n\r\nIt's messy. \r\n\r\nMy use case: I am using Apollo, and I want Apollo to be available both as context (so I can use components like `Query`, and `useApolloClient()`), and I need it in every page's `getInitialProps` because that's where the translation of query parameters to actual data should be done:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nArticlePage.getInitialProps = async ({ctx, query}): Promise<IArticlePage> => {\r\n  return {\r\n    user: ctx.apolloClient.query({query: gql`{ user { id, name } }`}),\r\n    article: ctx.apolloClient.query({\r\n      query: gql`query Article($id: String!) {\r\n        article { id, name }\r\n      }`,\r\n      variables: {id: query.id}\r\n    }),\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI _also_ need to load some global data such as the user's account, which belongs in `App`. I also have some global data such as the current URL, extracted from `ctx.req`, that I need to share as context. (The current URL also needs to be used to initialize Apollo, coincidentally.)\r\n\r\nThe question is _where_ to initialize it.\r\n\r\nI don't like explicitly wrapping every page component (as in `examples/with-apollo-auth`) for various reasons: It's a violation of DRY; it adds an unnecessary implementation detail to every page; it adds initialization logic at the page level instead of at the application level; and it's something that needs to be done identically in every single page file (makes it easy to forget).\r\n\r\nInitially, I followed the `next-redux-wrapper` method and tried replacing `App` itself with one that loads the Apollo client (`export withApollo(App)`). However, the page's `getInitialProps` runs _before_ `App`, so it does not get access to the Apollo client!\r\n\r\nSo I tried the `with-apollo-auth` method and wrapped each page instead (`export withApollo(MyPage)`). But this has the issue that Apollo only becomes available inside the page. `App` does not ever get access to the Apollo context. That means `App` can no longer be used as a place to insert global components that require Apollo. They all have to be pushed down to the page level.\r\n\r\nFrom my perspective, Next.js has a big gap in functionality here. There's no single point where this sort of initialization can be handled for _both_ `App` and individual pages. There's also this implicit contract that each page needs to handle the transition from server-side to client-side, when Next.js itself should be the one driving this.\r\n\r\nIn other words, \"app-level\" concerns (as opposed to page/component-level) have no explicit flow. `App` seems a bit misnamed; it's initialized after the page, but at the same time rendered around the page; it doesn't seem to represent the _app_ as much as the outer container. (This is exemplified by the fact that `App` in fact has `getInitialProps` called on every router navigation, meaning that that method has to be lightweight and must be careful to not, say, load the user's account from the database on every call.) It's where one should _apparently_ place global concerns, but not all global concerns can be placed there.\r\n\r\nI don't see what a clean solution might be for my use case, with today's Next.js, other than wrapping _both_ individual pages and `App`. Maybe I'm missing something. But this stuff shouldn't be so hard. I shouldn't have to write complicated HOCs just to initialize some data on startup. It shouldn't be necessary to wrap pages.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nThere should be a single place to initialize the application as a whole. It should execute before the app is mounted as a React app, allowing one to write logic that can inspect `req` and the application context in a single location before a page is mounted, both server- and client-side.\r\n\r\nIf initialization was made more explicit, it would also be a way to allow a \"middleware\" system. Look at all the permutations of `with-apollo-*` or `with-redux-*` examples. Look at `with-apollo-and-redux-saga/lib/withApollo.js`. Why isn't this just a middleware that inserts itself into the initialization stack and gives you an Apollo client and a Redux store?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 17, "reactions_plus": 17, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "atombender", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-10-05T00:46:07Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T02:09:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/8969", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 8981, "title": "RFC: Deprecate `next/head`", "body": "# Goals\r\n\r\n* Support [Concurrent Mode in React](https://reactjs.org/blog/2018/11/27/react-16-roadmap.html#react-16x-q2-2019-the-one-with-concurrent-mode) and Streaming Server-Side Rendering\r\n* Improve load times by decoupling `<head>` and `<body>`\r\n\r\n# Background\r\n\r\nThe `next/head` pattern makes it very easy for your components to configure the rendered `<head>`, but it has some major drawbacks:\r\n\r\n##### HTML is blocked until after the entire React tree is rendered\r\n\r\nSince `<Head>` can appear anywhere in your component tree and in turn modify the `<head>` tag, Next.js must render your *entire* tree before it can start sending *any* HTML to the client.\r\n\r\nThis leads to a chunk of dead time where the browser does nothing other than wait for the server, when it could have otherwise been loading and processing resources like CSS or JavaScript.\r\n\r\n##### Non-deterministic in Concurrent Mode\r\n\r\n`<Head>` depends on the side-effects of rendering happening in a specific order:\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\nfunction ComponentA() {\r\n  return <Head><title>Light is a: wave</title></Head>\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunction ComponentB() {\r\n  return <Head><title>Light is a: particle</title></Head>\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default function MyPage() {\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <ComponentA />\r\n      <ComponentB />\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn Concurrent Mode, `ComponentA` and `ComponentB` can be mounted in any order, leading to a non-deterministic value for `<title>`. \r\n\r\n# Proposal\r\n\r\nNext.js should allow you to define a `<head>` once per page by exporting a `head` component:\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\n// pages/index.js\r\nexport default function Index() {\r\n  return <span>Hello World</span>\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport function head() {\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <title>Index</index>\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`<Head>` should continue to work, but Next.js should warn in development mode that you are missing out on some features and that it may be removed in a future version.\r\n\r\nAn exported `head` component should receive the same props as the page component, so you can support e.g. dynamic titles with `getInitialProps`:\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\n// pages/post.js\r\nexport default function Post({ post }) {\r\n  return <Post post={post} />\r\n}\r\n\r\nPost.getInitialProps = async ({ req }) => {\r\n  const post = await getPost(req.query.id)\r\n  return { post }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport function head({ post }) {\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <title>{post.title}</title>\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou can use components to continue sharing and composing pieces of your `<head>`:\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\n// pages/index.js\r\nimport CommonHead from '../components/common-head'\r\n\r\n// ...\r\n\r\nexport function head() {\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <CommonHead />\r\n      <title>Index</title>\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\n// components/common-head.js\r\nexport default function CommonHead() {\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <meta name=\"author\" content=\"My Co, Inc.\">\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith the exception of `<title>`, most pages already tend to render the same `<head>`. By making this explicit (via the `head` component), rather than a side-effect, your websites can take advantage of new Next.js and React features to improve your user's experience.", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "locked", "state_reason": null, "comments": 20, "reactions_total": 162, "reactions_plus": 84, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 21, "reactions_confused": 14, "reactions_heart": 20, "reactions_rocket": 14, "reactions_eyes": 9, "author": "devknoll", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-10-07T16:45:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-07T18:03:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/8981", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9081, "title": "RFC: Custom Routes", "body": "## Goals\r\n\r\n- Support Redirects\r\n- Support Rewrites\r\n- Support Headers\r\n- Provide an API for [`next-routes`](https://github.com/fridays/next-routes) and related libraries (and eventual migration path)\r\n- Reduce the necessity of a custom server\r\n\r\n## Background\r\n\r\nNext.js has a file system-based router, which supports both static and dynamic routes. This router is normally powerful enough to cover most needs, but sometimes isn't the best fit.\r\n\r\nPresently, it is not possible to extend Next.js' router within the framework \u2014 this means any custom routing or [user-land routing libraries](https://github.com/fridays/next-routes) require you to use a custom server, and there is currently no way to incrementally migrate away from libraries like `next-routes` as they still require a custom server.\r\n\r\nCustom servers were always meant to be reserved for the most advanced or specialized use cases. When you use them, you're partially removed from the [framework's pit of success](https://blog.codinghorror.com/falling-into-the-pit-of-success/).\r\n\r\nWhen talking with companies and users who use Next.js in production, we found custom servers were most commonly used for **redirects** and **rewrites**. While dynamic routes introduced in Next.js 9 cover the majority of use cases, there are still a few cases that have to be covered.\r\n\r\nTherefore, it makes sense for Next.js to support these two features \u2014 allowing users to remove their usage of a custom server in many cases.\r\n\r\nLet's dive into more about what we mean by a **redirect**, a **rewrite**, and a **header**.\r\n\r\n### Redirects\r\n\r\nBy redirects, we mean [HTTP Redirects (aka URL forwarding)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Redirections). Redirects are most commonly used when a website is reorganized \u2014 ensuring search engines and bookmarks are forwarded to their new locations.\r\n\r\nNext.js currently does not support redirects as a primitive. As a result, users rely on a custom server or [\"redirect pages\"](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/wiki/Redirecting-in-%60getInitialProps%60) to implement them. Neither of these solutions are sufficiently ergonomic.\r\n\r\n### Rewrites\r\n\r\nRewrites are the most commonly used form of custom routing \u2014 they're used for [dynamic routes (pretty URLs)](https://github.com/zeit/next.js#dynamic-routing), user-land routing libraries (e.g. [next-routes](https://github.com/fridays/next-routes)), internationalization, and other advanced use cases.\r\n\r\nFor example, the route `/user/:id` rendering a specific user's profile page is a rewrite. \r\n\r\nRendering your company's about page for both `/about` and `/es/acerca` is also a rewrite.\r\n\r\nWe introduced [file system-based dynamic routing](https://github.com/zeit/next.js#dynamic-routing) in Next.js 9 to provide a convention-based way to cover the majority of rewrite use cases. However, some use cases sometimes need more flexibility, especially related to i18n use-cases.\r\n\r\n### Headers\r\n\r\nHeaders allow you to set route specific headers like CORS headers, `content-types`, and any other headers that may be needed. They are applied at the very top of the routes \r\n\r\n## Proposal\r\n\r\n### Redirects\r\n\r\nNext.js should allow users to create an `async redirects() { ... }` function in their `next.config.js`. This function would allow you to return an ordered list of redirects.\r\n\r\nNext.js allows you to specify whether the redirect is permanent or not with the `permanent` field. This is required unless you need to specify the `statusCode` manually\r\n\r\nWhen `permanent` is set to `true` we use a status code of `308` and also set a `Refresh` header for backwards compatibility with IE11.\r\n\r\nWhen `permanent` is set to `false` we use a status code of `307` which is not cached by browsers and signifies the redirect is temporary. \r\n\r\nNext.js only permits the following status codes:\r\n\r\n- **301 Moved Permanently**\r\n- **302 Found**\r\n- **303 See Other**\r\n- **307 Temporary Redirect**\r\n- **308 Permanent Redirect**\r\n\r\nPlease let us know if we missed any redirection status codes.\r\n\r\nShape of a Redirect:\r\n\r\n- `source: string`: the request URL to match, parsed with `path-to-regexp`\r\n- `destination: string`: the destination URL (internal or external)\r\n- `statusCode?: number`: the redirect status code\r\n- `permanent: boolean`: whether the redirect is permanent (required unless statusCode is provided)\r\n\r\n`<Link>` components should validate the specified `as` does not match a redirect. If it does, we should render an error client-side.\r\n\r\n`next export` does not have any special behavior for redirects. Meaning, we **do not emit** redirect pages with `<meta http-equiv=refresh content=... />`.\r\n\r\nRedirects for `next export`-powered websites should be configured manually and varies by hosting provider. These are automatically handled on ZEIT Now as applications.\r\n\r\n**Example Usage**\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  async redirects() {\r\n    return [\r\n      // 307 temporary redirect\r\n      {\r\n        source: \"/\",\r\n        destination: \"/under-construction\",\r\n        permanent: false,\r\n      },\r\n      // 308 permanent redirect\r\n      {\r\n        source: \"/posts\",\r\n        destination: \"/blog\",\r\n        permanent: true // permanent redirect\r\n      },\r\n      // With parameter and custom status code\r\n      {\r\n        source: \"/photos/:id\",\r\n        destination: \"/photo/:id\",\r\n        statusCode: 303 // see other\r\n      }\r\n    ];\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Rewrites\r\n\r\nNext.js should allow users to create an `async rewrites() { ... }` function in their `next.config.js`. This function would allow you to return an ordered list of rewrites.\r\n\r\nRewrites always continue down the matching chain, and merge in the last-matched query objects (overwrite previous matches).\r\n\r\nShape of a Rewrite:\r\n\r\n- `source: string`: the request URL to match, parsed with `path-to-regexp`\r\n- `destination: string`: the destination path (internal or external); these rewrites chain down the list in-order unless external\r\n\r\n`next export` does not have any special behavior for rewrites. Rewrites for `next export`-powered websites should be configured manually and varies by hosting provider. These are automatically handled on ZEIT Now.\r\n\r\n**Example Usage**\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  async rewrites() {\r\n    return [\r\n      // Basic `path-to-regexp` usage\r\n      // Query object shape: { id: string }\r\n      { source: \"/user/:id\", destination: \"/user_profile\" },\r\n\r\n      // Optional Language\r\n      // Query object shape: { lang?: string }\r\n      { source: \"/:lang(en|es)?/about\", destination: \"/about\" },\r\n\r\n      // Advanced rewrite\r\n      // Query object shape: { id: string } (in addition to dynamic route param)\r\n      { source: \"/u/:id\", destination: \"/user/:id\" }\r\n    ];\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Headers\r\n\r\nNext.js should allow users to create an `async headers() { ... }` function in their `next.config.js`. This function would allow you to return an ordered list of headers.\r\n\r\nHeaders always continue down the matching chain, and overwrite any previous matches' headers.\r\n\r\nShape of a Header:\r\n\r\n- `source: string`: the request URL to match, parsed with `path-to-regexp`\r\n- `headers: Array<{ key: string, value: string }>`: an array of header key/value pairs\r\n\r\n**Example Usage**\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  async headers() {\r\n    return [\r\n      { source: \"/user/:id\", headers: [{ key: \"x-path\", value: \"hi\" }] },\r\n\r\n      {\r\n        source: \"/:lang(en|es)?/about\",\r\n        headers: [\r\n          { key: \"x-path\", value: \"hi\" },\r\n          { key: \"x-another\", value: \"hi-again\" }\r\n        ]\r\n      }\r\n    ];\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\n```", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "locked", "state_reason": null, "comments": 226, "reactions_total": 607, "reactions_plus": 317, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 17, "reactions_hooray": 72, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 98, "reactions_rocket": 59, "reactions_eyes": 44, "author": "Timer", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-10-15T12:28:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-11T02:15:24Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9081", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9133, "title": "[RFC] Plugins", "body": "Authored together with @ijjk.\r\n\r\n## Goals\r\n\r\n- Provide an official plugin system for Next.js\r\n- Allow hooking into different parts of the Next.js lifecycle\r\n- Provide the right limits for plugins to prevent them from de-optimizing applications\r\n- Warn users when a plugin is being inefficient\r\n- Provide a zero-config approach to plugins\r\n- Provide code splitting for plugins\r\n\r\n## Background\r\n\r\nOver time the Next.js examples directory has grown to 210+ examples. These examples show how to integrate external libraries for styling, error reporting, data fetching, and many other applications.\r\n\r\nMost of these examples require a bit of configuration in various parts of Next.js, for example, in `_document`, `_app`, or pages. And sometimes the implementation details have to match exactly the example otherwise the library behaves in a different way. We've seen this for the example with material-ui that has a flash of unstyled content if `_document` is not implemented.\r\n\r\nThere are also examples that are using `getInitialProps` where it's not always needed, one of the examples of this is the redux example. It currently requires having `getInitialProps` which causes all pages to opt-out of automatic static rendering.\r\n\r\nTo solve these issues and provide an overall better developer experience we intend to introduce a plugin system.\r\n\r\nPlugins will be able to hook into specific lifecycle parts of Next.js.\r\n\r\nFor example, to execute a specific bit of code after hydration happens or to inject a script like Google Analytics into the body of every page.\r\n\r\nThe main goal for the plugin system will be to make you more productive while preserving the Next.js production performance characteristics.\r\n\r\nOne way we're trying to achieve this is that attached lifecycle methods are automatically code-split in a similar fashion as pages.\r\n\r\nThis new plugins system will provide you with the best developer experience when integrating libraries and we're excited to see the new ways of integrating and extending Next.js' functionality this system will enable.\r\n\r\n## Proposal\r\n\r\nTo provide a seamless plugin experience, Next.js will add built-in support for auto-detecting packages with the naming convention `@next/plugin-`, `next-plugin-`, or `@<scope>/next-plugin-`. We will also investigate allowing local plugins in a `plugins` or `next-plugins` directory that don't require being installed.\r\n\r\nEach of these plugins will have a meta field named `nextjs` in their `package.json` that gives us some extra information about the plugin like the required `env` values it relies on.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n// @next/plugin-google-analytics#package.json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"@next/plugin-google-analytics\",\r\n  \"version\": \"9.1.2-canary.1\",\r\n  \"nextjs\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"Google Analytics\",\r\n    \"required-env\": [\"GA_TRACKING_ID\"]\r\n  },\r\n  \"peerDependencies\": {\r\n    \"next\": \"*\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe only other required step for a plugin is to have a `src` directory with the lifecycle methods it wants to hook into.\r\n\r\nTo start, we explored what it would take to create a Google Analytics plugin and a Sentry plugin. Creating these example plugins gave us insight into the lifecycle methods that were needed.\r\n\r\n## Currently exposed lifecycle methods\r\n\r\nThe naming scheme for lifecycle methods will be based on:\r\n\r\n```\r\nname + '-' + context\r\n```\r\n\r\n`name` will be based on the lifecycle, potentially prefixed by `on` if it's an event.\r\n\r\nThe `context` will be `client`, `server` or `build`. It's appended at the end of the file name so that if a lifecycle is available on both client and server it will be sorted together in editors.\r\n\r\nSome of the initial methods that come to mind:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// on-init-client.js\r\nexport default async function onInitClient({ router }): void {\r\n  // called on client init, before hydration\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// on-error-client.js\r\nexport default async function onErrorClient({ err }): void {\r\n  // called when an error occurs during render on the client\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// on-error-server.js\r\nexport default async function onErrorServer({ err }): void {\r\n  // called when an error occurs during render on the server\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// document-head-tags-server.js\r\nexport default function headTags(DocumentCtx): Promise<ReactElement[]> {\r\n  // called when rendering the initial document, allows adding tags to the head\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <script src=\"googleanalytics.com/code.js\" async />\r\n    </>\r\n  );\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// document-body-tags-server.js\r\nexport default async function bodyTags(DocumentCtx): Promise<ReactElement[]> {\r\n  // called when rendering the initial document, allows adding tags to the body\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <script src=\"example.com/body.js\" async />\r\n    </>\r\n  );\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// babel-preset-build.js\r\nmodule.exports = function babelPreset({ plugins, presets }, config): {\r\n  // Babel config that will be merged inside next/babel\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAn example folder structure for one of these plugins would look like this\r\n\r\n```\r\npackage.json\r\nsrc/\r\n  on-error-client.js\r\n  on-error-server.js\r\n  on-init-client.js\r\n  on-init-server.js\r\n```\r\n\r\nFollowing this structure allows Next.js to provide a zero-config experience for plugins. Not only that, but it also allows us to code split these methods efficiently, only loading the parts of a plugin that are necessary at a specific point. We can also detect which parts of a plugin are being less efficient since they are all their own file and warn users of this.\r\n\r\n## Providing information to plugins\r\n\r\nAlthough we aim to make adding plugins to a project as simple as possible, some plugins still require some information like a `token` or an `id`.\r\n\r\nTo handle this, we allow you to provide these values using the current `env` config in `next.config.js` and the values will be injected into the plugin where needed during build time.\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  env: {\r\n    GA_TRACKING_ID: \"myTrackingId\"\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf a value listed in a plugin's `required-env` is missing in your `next.config.js`'s `env` we will error and require you provide these values so that the plugin is able to function correctly. We may need to investigate name spacing these values to prevent collision between plugins.\r\n", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 82, "reactions_total": 449, "reactions_plus": 210, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 80, "reactions_confused": 6, "reactions_heart": 76, "reactions_rocket": 51, "reactions_eyes": 26, "author": "timneutkens", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-10-18T20:31:44Z", "updated_at": "2024-02-17T15:27:57Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9133", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9310, "title": "[RFC]: A Conformance system for NextJS", "body": "**tldr;** A webpack based conformance system which can look into all source files and generated assets for forbidden anti patterns(in 1P code as well as node modules) and fail the build(or warn) if it does not conform to a preset array of tests.\r\n\r\n# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\nFor a website to have a good performance, a developer or a team of developers generally need to have an in-depth understanding of many pieces in the web ecosystem. These include understanding how a framework works, how numerous web APIs works and how to configure the build system.\r\nHowever, meta frameworks like NextJS make configuring the build system extremely easy. It is still not impossible to mess up the configuration and degrade the website\u2019s performance.\r\nAlso, there are way too many gotchas and traps that one can fall into while focussing on the product features and chasing deadlines.\r\nA conformance system will offload the necessity of having this deep understanding of every performance foot gun from every developer to a set of tests that can reside in the build system and fail the build or throw warnings at the end of the build process.\r\n\r\n### Common performance foot guns\r\nAfter tracing a bunch of NextJS and other framework websites we have gathered a small set of conformance tests, to begin with. These tests are based on some frequent things that we see delaying FCP/TTI for websites.\r\n\r\n- Synchronous scripts\r\nA synchronous script tag blocks the HTML parsers from parsing the rest of the document till the given resource is downloaded and executed. NextJS take specific steps to avoid this for the scripts it generates, but users can easily inject one for third party synchronous scripts. This test will scan such sync script tags and report them.\r\n\r\n_Violation of this would result in a total failure of the build._\r\n\r\n- Image quality\r\nVery often the images served from the `static/public` folder are used by developers as is in their web apps. This system would generate warnings if it finds any resource which is not optimized for web usage and would let the developer know about the same\r\n\r\n_Violation of this would result in warning around each of those resources not optimized for the web._\r\n\r\n- Web Fonts\r\nAdding a custom font to your web app typically requires a developer to add a simple stylesheet however, browsers don\u2019t go ahead and download these fonts unless used somewhere. Which means a waterfall of other fonts request after some stylesheet uses it, defined in a previous stylesheet. \r\nMaking sure that a font is preloaded along with inclusion would be another conformance check to help keep the first meaningful paint healthy.\r\n\r\n- Disabled minification\r\nAlthough NextJS recently started throwing an error if the minification is disabled but this proposal shifts the same to the conformance layer.\r\n\r\n_Violation of this would result in a total failure of the build._\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nA webpack based conformance system that can inspect the code being bundled would be able to throw these errors and warning while webpack parses the code itself. This conformance system could also look at generated assets and the webpack configuration to make sure that the changes made by the user would not regress the web app\u2019s performance.\r\n\r\nNextJS can create and add a Webpack Conformance plugin to its configuration \"just\" before starting the actual compilation. This is essential because such a system would need insight into all the assets generated by all the plugins/child compilers and hence should be the last plugin in the plugins list. Adding this plugin in the static config would make it ignore assets generated by plugins added in `next.config.js`\r\n\r\ne.g.\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.export = {\r\n  webpack: config => {\r\n    config.plugins.push(new FancyWebPackPlugin())\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\nThis would make `WebpackConformancePlugin` ignore assets from `FancyWebPackPlugin` as it is after the conformance plugin.\r\n\r\n#### Sample output\r\nThe plugin will look into all the assets/source code and collect all the errors/warnings from all the tests throughout all the lifecycles and post them all together after webpack\u2019s build is done.\r\n\r\nThe errors and warnings can be in the following format\r\n```shell\r\n> yarn next build\r\n> yarn run v1.15.2\r\nCreating an optimized production build ...\r\n\r\nFound experimental config:\r\nExperimental features can change at anytime and aren't officially supported (use at your own risk).\r\nFailed to compile.\r\n\r\n[BUILD CONFORMANCE]: Minification for this build is disabled. This can have a very serious impact on load times of the web app.\r\n[BUILD CONFORMANCE]: An inline script was found in a react module. in /pages/home.js\r\n\r\n``` \r\n\r\n**Note:** The solution could be extended to similar checks in the ESLint space but would not suffice as it would have no insight on the third party code. Keeping these checks primarily in the build system makes sure that just installing and consuming a rogue node_module would not blow up the performance of a carefully written web app.\r\n\r\n### Exempting from a specific rule\r\nSimilar to all userland configuration, the disabling of a rule should be controlled from `next.config.js`. Next could read a new key `Conformance` and get the desired settings of every rule from this config.\r\n\r\ne.g.\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  //\u2026\r\n  conformance: {\r\n    synchronous-scripts-test: {\r\n      disabled: true,\r\n      reason: \u2018We rely on synchronous scripts for our analytics to work correctly\u2019\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n#### Disabling a rule in a directory\r\nThere can be cases like an ongoing migration where the whole app is not ready to conform to a rule. Users can add directory level exceptions to a particular rule.\r\nUsers can add a `.conformance` file and add an exception in the same as follows\r\n\r\n```\r\n//.conformance\r\n{\r\n  synchronous-scripts-test: {\r\n      disabled: true,\r\n      reason: \u2018We rely on synchronous scripts for our analytics to work correctly\u2019\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 48, "reactions_plus": 28, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 8, "reactions_rocket": 7, "reactions_eyes": 5, "author": "prateekbh", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-11-04T23:07:31Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T11:25:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9310", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32237, "title": "TypeScript imports with .js file extensions don't work", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nWhen importing a TypeScript module from another, TypeScript allows the use of the `.js` file extension:\r\n\r\nfoo.ts:\r\n```ts\r\nexport const foo = 42;\r\n```\r\n\r\nbar.ts\r\n```ts\r\nimport {foo} from './foo.js'\r\n```\r\nThis is important in plain TypeScript projects because browsers don't do any module resolution and require the correct extensions. The compiled output works as it.\r\n\r\nIf you copy the same files into a Next.js project you will get a compile error that `foo.js` cannot be found.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nSee above\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nNo compile errors\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: macOS\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.1.2\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 42, "reactions_total": 29, "reactions_plus": 29, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "justinfagnani", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-11-09T02:49:54Z", "updated_at": "2026-05-31T19:57:00Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32237", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9542, "title": "withApollo Typescript", "body": "Would you have a Typescript version of the `with-apollo` example?\r\n\r\nThanks!\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 27, "reactions_total": 19, "reactions_plus": 19, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "samuelcastro", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-11-26T22:18:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T11:18:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9542", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9557, "title": "history change fired twice on initial page load which leads to wrong analytics result", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nnextjs calls the history api twice with the same values on initial page load. This gives wrong analytics results if you define a \"history change event\" trigger in google tag manager:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"1094\" alt=\"Bildschirmfoto 2019-11-27 um 17 56 06\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1972353/69743878-78788e80-113f-11ea-86e1-34c59aea183f.png\">\r\n<img width=\"897\" alt=\"Bildschirmfoto 2019-11-27 um 17 57 51\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1972353/69743893-7d3d4280-113f-11ea-8647-9989465d63ec.png\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\n1. add google tag manager to nextjs. in _document.jsx add something like:\r\n\r\n```\r\n <Head>\r\n          {GOOGLE_TAG_MANAGER_ID ? (\r\n            <script\r\n              dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{\r\n                __html: `(function(w,d,s,l,i){w[l]=w[l]||[];w[l].push({'gtm.start':\r\n          new Date().getTime(),event:'gtm.js'});var f=d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0],\r\n          j=d.createElement(s),dl=l!='dataLayer'?'&l='+l:'';j.async=true;j.src=\r\n          'https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtm.js?id='+i+dl;f.parentNode.insertBefore(j,f);\r\n        })(window,document,'script','dataLayer','${GOOGLE_TAG_MANAGER_ID}');`,\r\n              }}\r\n            />\r\n          ) : null}\r\n        </Head>\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. configure google tag manager to track history change events. refer to the google tag manager documentation\r\n3. set your google tag manager into preview mode\r\n4. this should add a debug bar to your application where you can see the events that are sent. You will notice that on initial page load the event is sent twice.\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nnextjs should not call the history api with the same arguments twice.\r\n\r\n## Screenshots\r\n\r\nsee above\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: [e.g. macOS, Windows]\r\n- Browser (if applies) [e.g. chrome, safari]\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.1.1\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nits probably also possible to debug that which just attaching events yourself on the browser history api\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "macrozone", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-11-27T17:02:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T06:25:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9557", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 32239, "title": "Support ES module format (ESM) in next.config.js", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\nWith Node 12.17.x landing [native support for ES modules](https://nodejs.org/api/esm.html), I would expect `next.config.js` to support ES module format in addition to CJS.\r\n\r\nWhen using `type: module` in `package.json` on Node v13.1.0 and an ESM `next.config.js` format, Next throws an error due to the config resolution logic relying on `require`.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nNext should follow Node\u2019s module semantics and allow either CJS or ESM file, depending on user configuration.\r\n\r\nCurrently, `next.config.js` uses CJS with `require` and `module.exports`. ESM format would allow users to `import` dependencies and `export default` the config object.\r\n\r\nThis theoretically also opens the door to mirror Node\u2019s explicit file extension pattern, which would suggest support for `next.config.cjs` and `next.config.mjs` files. This is likely controversial, but it is part of the language. \r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nAs statically analyzing the native module format of `next.config.js` likely introduces a fair amount of complexity, this could be used as an opportunity to introduce \"superset\" config files (ESM, TypeScript [see #5318, #8044]) which are transpiled to `CJS`, emitted to the `distDir` (`.next`), and read from that location. This would also allow users pinned versions of Node below 13 to benefit from ESM format or strongly typed config files (see [@stencil/core](https://github.com/ionic-team/stencil/blob/master/src/sys/node/node-config.ts#L89) for prior art.)\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nThe following error is thrown, minimum reproducible files below.\r\n\r\n```\r\nnode:97960) Warning: require() of ES modules is not supported.\r\nrequire() of /PROJECT_NAME/next.config.js from /PROJECT_NAME/node_modules/next/dist/next-server/server/config.js is an ES module file as it is a .js file whose nearest parent package.json contains \"type\": \"module\" which defines all .js files in that package scope as ES modules.\r\nInstead rename next.config.js to end in .cjs, change the requiring code to use import(), or remove \"type\": \"module\" from /PROJECT_NAME/package.json.\r\n/PROJECT_NAME/next.config.js:1\r\nimport path from \"path\";\r\n^^^^^^\r\n\r\nSyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module\r\n    at wrapSafe (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:983:16)\r\n    at Module._compile (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:1033:27)\r\n    at Object.Module._extensions..js (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:1103:10)\r\n    at Module.load (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:914:32)\r\n    at Function.Module._load (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:822:14)\r\n    at Module.require (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:956:19)\r\n    at require (internal/modules/cjs/helpers.js:74:18)\r\n    at Object.loadConfig [as default] (/PROJECT_NAME/node_modules/next/dist/next-server/server/config.js:105:34)\r\n    at new Server (/PROJECT_NAME/node_modules/next/dist/next-server/server/next-server.js:42:43)\r\n    at new DevServer (/PROJECT_NAME/node_modules/next/dist/server/next-dev-server.js:1:2508)\r\n```\r\n\r\n`package.json`\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"type\": \"module\"\r\n  ...\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`next.config.js`\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nimport withCSS from '@zeit/next-css';\r\nexport default withCSS();\r\n```", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "closed", "state_reason": "resolved", "comments": 94, "reactions_total": 371, "reactions_plus": 269, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 60, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 42, "author": "natemoo-re", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-12-03T13:51:22Z", "updated_at": "2025-04-23T09:26:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/32239", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9662, "title": "Detect when user leaves the page", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nI would like to detect when the user leaves the page. I count 3 ways of leaving a page:\r\n- by clicking on a link\r\n- by doing an action that triggers `router.back`, `router.push`, etc...\r\n- by closing the tab (i.e. when `beforeunload` event is fired)\r\n\r\nI know this kind of issue has already been opened [here](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/2236) or [here](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/2694), but they had been magically closed (using some `duplicate` trick) without providing any solution.\r\n\r\nBeing able to detect when a page is leaved is very helpful for, for example, alerting the user some changes have not been saved yet.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI would like something like:\r\n\r\n```\r\nrouter.beforeLeavingPage(() => {\r\n    // my callback\r\n})\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 92, "reactions_total": 221, "reactions_plus": 164, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 23, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 22, "reactions_rocket": 12, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "lcswillems", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-12-08T15:26:24Z", "updated_at": "2026-01-20T11:59:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9662", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 26424, "title": "Bind to Unix socket instead of port", "body": "# Feature request\r\nJust like Express, I would like to be able to bind to a [Unix socket](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_domain_socket) like `/sockets/mysocket` instead of a host:port setup. \r\n\r\nLooking at the code I see there is an assumption that server will always be bound to a port on a host:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/packages/next/cli/next-dev.ts#L56\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nYes, in our internal system we must bind the server to a socket. This is how our server containers connect to Nginx Kubernetes sidecars.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nProbably a `--socket` argument to the `next` CLI command that overrides `--host` and `--port`\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 51, "reactions_plus": 51, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mohsen1", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2019-12-10T17:31:49Z", "updated_at": "2024-09-24T02:49:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/26424", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9801, "title": "browser back not working when using router.push", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nUsing router.push() replaces the url instead of a push and therefore browser back is broken.\r\n\r\nWe use this as part of a larger app where we use a custom express server and getInitialProps and it worked sometimes but not realiable. We explicit want that getInitialProps is called and that the url is pushed into browser history.\r\n\r\nSo i created a very small example (see below) and it fails every time.\r\n\r\nIf we use different urls as 2. paremeter to router.push it works fine and as expected with the major drawback that when a user hard refreshes the page it will fail because for obvious reasons the page does not really exist.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\nSample on codesandbox:\r\n\r\n[![Edit admiring-dew-v4p8q](https://codesandbox.io/static/img/play-codesandbox.svg)](https://codesandbox.io/s/admiring-dew-v4p8q?fontsize=14&hidenavigation=1&initialpath=%2F&module=%2Fpages%2Fabout.js&theme=dark)\r\n\r\n1.) click about\r\n2.) click first button\r\n3.) click second button\r\n4.) click browser back\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nAs i use **router.push()** instead of **router.replace()** i would expect that when i execute step 4 (browser back) it will show me the message _im the FirstComponent_ and that im not going back to the index page.\r\n\r\nI think this codeblock is the issue (or maybe even intented - but then i dont really get why)\r\n\r\n// If asked to change the current URL we should reload the current page\r\n // (not location.reload() but reload getInitialProps and other Next.js stuffs)\r\n **// We also need to set the method = replaceState always** \r\n // as this should not go into the history (That's how browsers work)\r\n // We should compare the new asPath to the current asPath, not the url\r\n if (!this.urlIsNew(as)) {\r\n    method = 'replaceState'\r\n }\r\n\r\nI think i cant follow why this comment states that replaceState should be always set and why it should not go into history. Isnt that what we want when we use **router.push()** instead of **router.replace()**?\r\n\r\nIf this is working as intented, could you give me some hints how i could get following steps done:\r\n\r\n1.) execute some action\r\n2.) let page reload on client side that and execute get intial props\r\n3.) show same url, working browser back to prev view and without query parameter in URL\r\n\r\nthanks!\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: macOS, Windows\r\n- Browser chrome\r\n- Version of Next.js: [latest for the small example, 9.12 for our production app which behaves a little bit different as statet above]\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "pinkeyAT", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-12-20T23:40:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T18:02:55Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9801", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 26434, "title": "Customizing the CSS identifiers (scope, hash etc)", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Problem\r\nOur Selenium tests rely on a specific naming of class names in the HTML. E.g. `class=\"Header__link\"`, where `Header` is the name of a component, and `link` is a class in the component's CSS module).\r\n\r\nWith the current experimental CSS support in Next.js, there's no apparent way to customize the outputted CSS identifiers.\r\n\r\nWith `next-css`, we used to simply override the Webpack config with our own css-loader options `localIdentName` and `getLocalIdent`).\r\n\r\n## Proposed solution\r\n\r\nPlease allow to override the Webpack config like it was the case with `next-css`.\r\nThanks!\r\n\r\nRelated to #8626.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 13, "reactions_plus": 13, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "katspaugh", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2019-12-30T20:00:01Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T13:29:48Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/26434", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9935, "title": "Allow generic `AppContext`", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nThe suggested method is to use `declare` to modify `AppContext`, but it would be better to have a generic `AppContext<T>` to allow us to attach additional properties, such as Apollo Client etc.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\n<img width=\"779\" alt=\"codelab-ui-nestjs____Code_UIB_codelab-ui-nestjs__-_____node_modules_next_dist_pages__app_d_ts\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/26264925/71758533-adba7f80-2e55-11ea-9cad-753ab5440826.png\">\r\n\r\nAllow `AppContext<T>`\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nUse `declare` to override interface\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nDiscussed in this thread https://spectrum.chat/next-js/general/how-would-you-type-this~662bfdb5-b174-4503-94a6-e6ad83bca204\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "webberwang", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-04T02:20:47Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-28T23:05:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9935", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 9957, "title": "Ability to customise API middleware error responses", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nNext.js has API routes with built-in middlewares, such as `bodyParser`. When an error is thrown by those middlewares, Next.js sends an error response, handled by [`sendError`](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/packages/next/next-server/server/api-utils.ts#L251).\r\n\r\nThe current implementation sends a JSON response with the error message as the body. As an example: if we send request, with `Content-Type: application/json` with an invalid JSON request body, the server responds with `Invalid JSON`.\r\n\r\nHowever, usually APIs that I develop have a standard envelope for errors, like:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"statusCode\": 400,\r\n  \"message\": \"Invalid request body\",\r\n  \"other\": \"optional stuff here\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI would like to be able to keep the same response envelope for built-in middleware errors.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nPerhaps, we could somehow be able to tweak the `sendError` by including it in the `config` exported in API routes?\r\n\r\n```js\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    sendError: (res, statusCode, message) => {},\r\n  },\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nI've looked into the code to see if I could somehow monkey-patch to get around the issue temporarily, but I couldn't find a way. Another solution is to turn off all the middleware and use something like micro to have full-control, but seems overkill.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\n<img width=\"358\" alt=\"Screenshot 2020-01-05 at 22 06 09\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1017236/71786781-96bb8f00-3007-11ea-8e16-b7fbe98867b1.png\">\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "satazor", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-05T22:08:19Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-24T09:57:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/9957", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 48427, "title": "Server-Sent Events don't work in Next API routes", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nWhen using Next's API routes, chunks that are written with `res.write` aren't sent until after `res.end()` is called.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nSteps to reproduce the behavior, please provide code snippets or a repository:\r\n\r\n1. Create the following API route in a Next app:\r\n```\r\nexport default async (req, res) => {\r\n  let intervalID = null\r\n\r\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream')\r\n  res.write('data: CONNECTION ESTABLISHED\\n')\r\n\r\n  const end = () => {\r\n    if (intervalID) {\r\n      clearTimeout(intervalID)\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  req.on('aborted', end)\r\n  req.on('close', end)\r\n\r\n  const sendData = () => {\r\n    const timestamp = (new Date).toISOString()\r\n    res.write(`data: ${timestamp}\\n`)\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  intervalID = setInterval(sendData, 1000)\r\n}\r\n```\r\n2. Connect to the route with a tool that supports Server-Sent Events (i.e. [Postwoman](https://postwoman.io/realtime)).\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nThe route sends a new event to the connection every second.\r\n\r\n## Actual behavior\r\n\r\nThe route doesn't send any data to the connection unless a call to `res.end()` is added to the route.\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: macOS\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.1.5\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nWhen using other HTTP frameworks (Express, Koa, `http`, etc) this method works as expected. It's explicitly supported by Node's `http.incomingMessage` and `http.ServerResponse` classes which, from what I understand, Next uses as a base for the `req` and `res` that are passed into Next API routes.\r\n\r\nI'd hazard a guess that #5855 was caused by the same issue, but considered unrelated because the issue was obscured by the `express-sse` library.\r\n\r\nThere are also two Spectrum topics about this ([here](https://spectrum.chat/next-js/general/api-route-and-server-sent-events~89aea810-a9d9-4858-a8fb-a74a5a6ea337) and [here](https://spectrum.chat/next-js/general/next-js-api-cannot-stream-sse-with-res-write~02a846f2-fb79-4021-bbba-dd5af8f8cb16)) that haven't garnered much attention yet.\r\n\r\nSupporting Websockets and SSE in Next API routes may be related, but fixing support for SSE should be a lower barrier than adding support Websockets. All of the inner workings are there, we just need to get the plumbing repaired.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 128, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 6, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "trezy", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-06T17:57:33Z", "updated_at": "2026-03-17T08:00:24Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/48427", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10053, "title": "SSG Improvements, while using the dev server", "body": "After experimenting with the new static gen api discussed in #9524, I'd like to suggest a better approach to developing using the dev server.\r\n\r\nThe problem is, at present, navigating between `getStaticProps` routes causes the dev server to request the results json over and over. This is contrary to production behavior, and it also slows down the development process with extra delays.\r\n\r\n@Timer [argued](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/9524#issuecomment-573376506) that during development, it's in the developer's interest to see the most up-to-date data, and thus, the server request is useful. But I don't see a use-case where this would come into play.\r\n\r\n1. If you're adjusting code, the dev server is going to hot reload anyway, negating the need to navigate around the app to re-request the json response. If you're clicking around your app, it's likely because your testing behavior, which is slowed by re-requests.\r\n2. And if you're adjusting content that isn't tracked by the dev server (e.g. markdown or some headless cms content), then you're going to have to refresh the browser anyway to see the results of the updates. In this case too, re-requesting the json response doesn't help.\r\n\r\nAre there use-cases for re-requests I'm not considering?\r\n\r\nEither way, I think we should have the discussion on two fronts: \r\n\r\n1. What should be the default behavior?\r\n2. Should there be a config option to choose between modes?\r\n\r\nThanks. :)", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mikestopcontinues", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-12T17:23:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T15:36:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10053", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 60143, "title": "Next.js dev server gets stuck after a while, requests just spin", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nWhen I run my Next.js app with `now dev`, after a while (on the order of minutes, definitely less than an hour) my requests to it will stop working.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nI'm not sure how to isolate this one. It happens all the time with this project https://github.com/mjm/courier-js but it's not an easy thing to get running for reproduction.\r\n\r\n1. Run `now dev`\r\n2. Work on the app, making changes to files, saving, and watching them hot reload in the browser\r\n3. Eventually, one of these changes will try to reload and will get stuck. The on-page indicator for a page compiling will be there and keep spinning continuously. Trying to refresh the page just causes the browser to try to load the page until it times out.\r\n\r\nWhen I give up, I can hit Ctrl-C to try to kill the server, but if it's in this state, it can take a minute or two to actually get the message and quit.\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nContinuing to make changes should keep working and trigger refreshes in the browser.\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: macOS Catalina, though I think this happened before upgrading too\r\n- Browser (if applies): I've seen in both Chrome and Safari\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.1.7, has happened in earlier versions of Next 9 as well\r\n- Version of Now CLI: 16.7.3\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nI feel like this could be TypeScript related, but I don't have any evidence. I'm on the latest release for that as well. I'm happy to try things that might help understand what's going on. The only thing I've seen in the log from `now dev` (or `next dev` I guess) is that it seems like when it gets in this state, it will stack up two or three \"compiling ...\" messages when there would otherwise be just one.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 155, "reactions_total": 131, "reactions_plus": 126, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 5, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mjm", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-13T00:24:55Z", "updated_at": "2025-11-20T01:01:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/60143", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 22057, "title": "Asset download during nextjs export", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\nA related conversation to this feature happened here https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/9524#issuecomment-574568879\r\n\r\n\r\nif i use a headless CMS (even wordpress or graphcms or whatever), the asset url is used in the static html.\r\n\r\nThere are two preferences here - that asset links be used as such.\r\nBut more likely - download the asset, build the html (link to it locally) and then layer a CDN in front. This is the far more acceptable practice.\r\n\r\nThis also ties in very well with using deployment systems like Netlify - which have a far, far more suitable globally available infrastructure than something like DatoCMS or Graphcms. So if im using Netlify as deployment, i would want everything to be served from Netlify domain and let it work its magic.\r\n\r\nWe understand that this increases build times. This is ok for our usecase where we use a CMS just for the sake of usability, but deployment has to follow guidelines of compliance (geographic restriction of assets, etc) so we would like the entire build to be inside the export folder. This would mean that CMS that have CDN in front of it (e.g. datocms and www.datocms-assets.com which is Imgix) may not be ideal.\r\n\r\nThere are tons of similar requests. \r\n\r\nhttps://spectrum.chat/next-js/general/how-would-you-handle-importing-remote-images-on-nextjs-static-export~30b2ba84-bc27-4da7-9ec8-21e4d5d287a3\r\n\r\non gatsby side as well - gatsbyjs/gatsby#14076\r\n\r\nhttps://spectrum.chat/gatsby-js/general/adding-remote-images-during-node-creation~e704e6fb-24b2-46c6-b1fc-93189d2e28a4\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/njosefbeck/gatsby-source-stripe/#downloading-files\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 17, "reactions_total": 28, "reactions_plus": 26, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 2, "author": "sandys", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-18T02:34:43Z", "updated_at": "2023-04-26T07:29:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/22057", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23955, "title": "[request] better typescript type for `next.config.js` NextConfig", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\ni only see this, but `any` not a good type\r\n\r\n```ts\r\ndeclare type NextConfig = any;\r\n```\r\n\r\nif have a type/interface for this\r\nwe can more easy write a next.config or a config plugin\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nA clear and concise description of what you want to happen.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nA clear and concise description of any alternative solutions or features you've considered.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nAdd any other context or screenshots about the feature request here.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 11, "reactions_total": 17, "reactions_plus": 17, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "bluelovers", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-24T12:32:02Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-26T07:03:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23955", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10325, "title": "[Feature Request] middleware for all API middleware routes (i.e `pages/api/_middleware.js`)", "body": "# Feature request\r\n## problem statement\r\n\r\nI\u2019d like the ability to have certain functionality apply for all Api middleware routes. Similar to the concept of _document.js or _app.js.\r\n\r\nI could use this for:\r\n* logging all requests and responses in and out.\r\n* Some transformation.\r\n* Whitelisting or black listing requests.\r\n* error tracking/handling\r\n\r\nReally anything without having to import a function from a utility for each route.\r\n\r\n## The solution you'd like\r\n\r\nSomething like a `pages/api/_middleware.js` that would default to current working directly and work up if I want to only opt-in or opt-out in some scenarios (so I could have `pages/api/foo/_middleware.js` only be middleware for `api/foo/*` endpoints if I wanted)\r\n\r\nThis would handle:\r\n* Preflight request middleware support\r\n* post response handling middleware\r\n* error handling middleware\r\n\r\nMaybe something like:\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\n\r\nimport Cors from 'micro-cors'\r\n\r\n// none of these ideally would allow sending response back before hitting a route. \r\nexport async function preRequest(req, res) {\r\n  const cors = Cors({\r\n    allowMethods: ['GET', 'HEAD'],\r\n  })\r\n   return cors\r\n// could wrap functions around each routes exported function, or do something with req or res like logging\r\n\r\n}\r\n// not sure if I like the naming\r\nexport async function postResponse(req, res) {\r\n  // same as above\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport async function onError(req, res) {\r\n  // maybe not needed and could just capture in postResponse\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\nMany other alternatives I\u2019m sure but conceptually this feature would be nice.\r\n\r\nSeen another issue where they just wanted in \u201conerror\u201d I think this capture more use cases", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 47, "reactions_plus": 47, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "garrettmac", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-29T15:58:49Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T13:16:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10325", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23978, "title": "support $PORT environment var in `next start` directly without passing it as argument", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nWe start to spawn more and more nextjs projects and we have ci/cd which allows us to deploy a new project with absolut minimal effort by using some conventions. E.g. we want the apps to run on production using the `start` script in package.json. So every node app should just invoke `npm run start`. \r\n\r\nMany frameworks use some conventions which makes this approach easy, e.g. using $PORT as the default port to listen to if its defined. Unfortunatly, nextjs does not respect this environment variable. So we have to modify newly created nextjs projects and adjust the package.json.start script with: `next start --port $PORT`\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\n`next start` without arguments start the server listening to env var $PORT if its defined, otherways falls back to 3000. --port argument would still have precedence  \r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nwe could do more adjustments on our side, but supporting $PORT in nextjs would be a tiny change:\r\n\r\nin https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/packages/next/cli/next-start.ts#L48\r\n```\r\nconst port = args['--port'] || process.env.PORT ||  3000;\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nWhile this adjustment is extremly easy to do, it _could_ have some impact on environments where PORT is already unexpectedly defined and no `--port` argument is used. i think this is a rare edge case. Most people who self host nextjs will have --port set, so it would not accidentially break for them.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 15, "reactions_total": 108, "reactions_plus": 108, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "macrozone", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-30T14:26:10Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-23T04:05:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23978", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10343, "title": "Error: Unable to find native implementation, or alternative implementation for WebSocket!", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nWebSocket connection continually fails. What is the issue here?\r\n\r\nStack\r\n```\r\ngetClient\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-with-apollo/lib/apollo.js:25:18\r\ninitApollo [as default]\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-with-apollo/lib/apollo.js:12:16\r\n(anonymous function)\r\nC:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:109:54\r\nstep\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-with-apollo/lib/withApollo.js:56:23\r\nnext\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-with-apollo/lib/withApollo.js:37:53\r\n(anonymous function)\r\nC:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:31:71\r\n__awaiter\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-with-apollo/lib/withApollo.js:27:12\r\nFunction._a.getInitialProps\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-with-apollo/lib/withApollo.js:102:61\r\nloadGetInitialProps\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-server/dist/lib/utils.js:50:35\r\nrenderToHTML\r\nC:/Users/d0475/Documents/Projects/flamingo-ecom2/sick-fits/frontend/node_modules/next-server/dist/server/render.js:178:33\r\nprocess._tickCallback\r\ninternal/process/next_tick.js:68:7\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nError: Unable to find native implementation, or alternative implementation for WebSocket!\r\n    at new SubscriptionClient (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\subscriptions-transport-ws\\dist\\client.js:63:19)\r\n    at new WebSocketLink (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\@apollo\\link-ws\\lib\\webSocketLink.js:14:40)\r\n    at createClient (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\.next\\server\\static\\development\\pages\\_app.js:4306:18)\r\n    at getClient (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\apollo.js:25:18)\r\n    at Object.initApollo [as default] (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\apollo.js:12:16)\r\n    at C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:109:54\r\n    at step (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:56:23)\r\n    at Object.next (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:37:53)\r\n    at C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:31:71\r\n    at new Promise (<anonymous>)\r\n    at __awaiter (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:27:12)\r\n    at Function._a.getInitialProps (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-with-apollo\\lib\\withApollo.js:102:61)\r\n    at Object.loadGetInitialProps (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-server\\dist\\lib\\utils.js:50:35)\r\n    at Object.renderToHTML (C:\\Users\\d0475\\Documents\\Projects\\flamingo-ecom2\\sick-fits\\frontend\\node_modules\\next-server\\dist\\server\\render.js:178:33)\r\n```\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\n```\r\nfunction createClient({ headers, initialState }) {\r\n  console.log('initialState');\r\n  console.log(initialState);\r\n\r\n  // Create an http link:\r\n  const httpLink = new createHttpLink({\r\n    uri: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' ? endpoint : prodEndpoint,\r\n    fetchOptions: {\r\n      credentials: 'include',\r\n      //mode: 'cors',\r\n    },\r\n    // pass the headers along from this request. This enables SSR with logged in state\r\n    headers,\r\n  });\r\n\r\n  // Create a WebSocket link:\r\n  const wsLink = new WebSocketLink({\r\n    uri: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' ? endpointWS : prodEndpointWS,\r\n    options: {\r\n      reconnect: true\r\n    },\r\n  });\r\n\r\n  const link = process.window ? split(\r\n    // split based on operation type\r\n    ({ query }) => {\r\n      const definition = getMainDefinition(query);\r\n      return (\r\n        definition.kind === 'OperationDefinition' &&\r\n        definition.operation === 'subscription'\r\n      );\r\n    },\r\n    wsLink,\r\n    httpLink,\r\n  ) : httpLink;\r\n\r\n  const cache = new InMemoryCache({\r\n    dataIdFromObject: o => o.id,\r\n  }).restore(initialState || {});\r\n\r\n  const client_ = new ApolloClient({\r\n    link: ApolloLink.from([\r\n      onError(({ graphQLErrors, networkError }) => {\r\n        if (graphQLErrors)\r\n          graphQLErrors.forEach(({ message, locations, path }) =>\r\n            console.log(\r\n              `[GraphQL error]: Message: ${message}, Location: ${locations}, Path: ${path}`\r\n            )\r\n          );\r\n        if (networkError)\r\n          console.log(\r\n            `[Network error]: ${networkError}. Backend is unreachable. Is it running?`\r\n          );\r\n      }),\r\n      link,\r\n    ]),\r\n    cache,\r\n    assumeImmutableResults: true,\r\n    ...\r\n  })\r\n\r\n  const data = {\r\n    cartOpen: false,\r\n    items: [\r\n      {\r\n        ...\r\n      }]\r\n  };\r\n\r\n  cache.writeData({ data });\r\n\r\n  client_.onResetStore(() => cache.writeData({ data }));\r\n\r\n  return client_;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nA successful connection  to the Apollo server was expected\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: Windows 10 Pro\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.0.0\r\n- @apollo/client: 3.0.0-beta.30,\r\n- @apollo/link-error: 2.0.0-beta.3,\r\n -@apollo/link-ws: 2.0.0-beta.3,\r\n -@apollo/react-ssr: 3.1.3\",\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "TheoMer", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-30T16:29:10Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-21T20:59:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10343", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10366, "title": "Next/head cannot render tags into <head> of src-less iframe", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nOccasionally developers need to render part of their app into a src-less iframe for various reasons (as a sandbox, or isolated styling environment), often using a component like [react-frame-component](https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-frame-component?activeTab=readme). \r\n\r\nHowever, next/head refers to the global document (and thus, `head` element of parent frame) when updating head elements. This means it cannot, for example, add a custom font inside a src-less iframe. Here's a minimum re-producible codesandbox that illustrates the problem: https://codesandbox.io/s/nexthead-iframe-example-yymx0\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/875591/73560394-b7f77b80-440b-11ea-9ba9-f7757dbd1cca.png)\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nI've considered forking all the next/head-related code into my project and creating my own HeadContextProvider that provides a [HeadManager](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/packages/next/client/head-manager.js) which is bound to the currently active `document`. Unfortunately this is a lot of code to fork and it would be great if there was a workaround within the next public API.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI can think of two possible APIs\r\n1. Allow `<Head>` to be passed a `document={document}` prop, which it passes along to HeadManager.updateHead.\r\n2. Expose HeadManager and HeadManagerContext as part of the next public API, allow document to be passed to HeadManager and allow user to wrap their iframe context in a new `<HeadContextProvider>` with the new HeadManager instance. This is likely less preferable to option 1 since it requires exposing more next innards.\r\n\r\nOne challenge is that HeadManager is looking for `next-head-count` which won't exist in the document since the iframe document was not rendered by next.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nThis is admittedly a very obscure request, so feel free to close it or recommend alternatives if you don't feel its worth addressing within next directly. Just wanted to open up an issue to get your thoughts and document the issue for any future users running into it.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 6, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "schnerd", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-01-31T17:44:52Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T10:36:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10366", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23951, "title": "[Feature request] Support for SRI", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\n[Subresource Integrity](https://www.w3.org/TR/SRI/) (SRI) is a security feature that enables browsers to verify that files they fetch (for example, from a CDN) are delivered without unexpected manipulation.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nImplement webpack plugin (https://www.npmjs.com/package/webpack-subresource-integrity) as default, or under a flag just for production environment.\r\n\r\nThanks to it every external resource should have generated `integrity` hash and specified `crossorigin` policy. Example:\r\n```html\r\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/css/bootstrap.min.css\" integrity=\"sha384-Gn5384xqQ1aoWXA+058RXPxPg6fy4IWvTNh0E263XmFcJlSAwiGgFAW/dAiS6JXm\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\">\r\n<script src=\"https://maxcdn.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/4.0.0/js/bootstrap.min.js\" integrity=\"sha384-JZR6Spejh4U02d8jOt6vLEHfe/JQGiRRSQQxSfFWpi1MquVdAyjUar5+76PVCmYl\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nThere are other alternative plugins as well. We can also develop an in-house solution.\r\nThis feature can also be handled \"per app\", however, in my opinion, things related to the security of resources loading should be handled by the framework.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 26, "reactions_total": 74, "reactions_plus": 74, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Exelord", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-01T16:35:24Z", "updated_at": "2025-03-10T13:19:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23951", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23977, "title": "Option to write \"First Load Size\" data to file", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nWe'd previously used the [bundlesize](https://github.com/siddharthkp/bundlesize) package in CI in order to monitor the growth of our application and prevent accidental imports of test libs, etc. With the switch to the new chunking strategy, the hash-based names of the new chunks breaks this approach (we previously just watched `chunks/commons.*.js`). But the new \"First Load Size\" data could easily replace this functionality, if it were easily accessible (I'm not super keen on redirecting and parsing stdout).\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nWould it be possible to add a configuration option to next.config that enabled also writing the size data to a json file such that it could be then analyzed by other tools?\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nCapturing and parsing the stdout of a yarn build command.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 7, "reactions_plus": 7, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "merrywhether", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-02T15:10:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-24T12:59:18Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23977", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23973, "title": "Support phase_development_build", "body": "# Feature request\r\nIt would be nice to have a phase for a ```PHASE_DEVELOPMENT_BUILD``` where a static build can be produced where ```process.env.NODE_ENV``` is set to ```development```\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nThe reason you'd want this is so that you could do react profiling with static builds for a development environment. See [1](https://reactjs.org/docs/profiler.html), [2](https://gist.github.com/bvaughn/<HASH_REDACTED>)\r\n\r\nIn order to enable react profiling in a static build the webpack config must be modified in the next config to alias the profiling versions of the react-dom and scheduler/tracing modules, as documented by the react team. (See ref above.)\r\n\r\nEdit: In a more general sense if there are any third party libraries or your own code that relies on process.env.NODE_ENV being set correctly in a static build used for development this phase is needed. Otherwise it's always set to production and can't be changed because this standard environment variable has been hijacked. See: [3](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/master/errors/env-key-not-allowed.md)\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nIdeally I'd be able to use phase information or ```options.dev``` in my next config to determine whether i want to load the profiling versions of these modules. \r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nThe alternative I've considered for the moment is passing in a non-standard(not NODE_ENV) environment variable to check against. However, it's possible other libraries within my project are relying on the incorrect NODE_ENV value that is set to ```production``` for all static builds.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 16, "reactions_plus": 16, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ethanwillis", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-05T14:57:31Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-07T16:25:00Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23973", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10436, "title": "Is this the Next.js Spectrum replacement?", "body": "I'm curious, is the plan to replace the Spectrum community with this new Github tool?\r\n\r\nI like that it would keep everything centralized, but at the same time feel like the feature is still kind of raw, which is expected since it's an experiment.\r\n\r\nAlso, is the plan for Discussions to be like a forum board with categories and everything?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 17, "reactions_total": 9, "reactions_plus": 8, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "martinbavio", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-05T21:44:12Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-04T11:32:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10436", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10437, "title": "What is the point of SSR these days?", "body": "Lately, I've been reading more and more people suggesting to statically render something and then on client make subsequent requests to complete the experience with proper data, when necessary. Basically the Jamstack approach, which makes sense from a conceptual point of view. \r\n\r\nThing is, with that approach, I'm failing to see a scenario where doing server side requests for stuff like fetching current user would make sense.\r\n\r\nHow does the Next.js team feels about this?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 44, "reactions_total": 7, "reactions_plus": 7, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "martinbavio", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-05T23:51:19Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-06T07:09:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10437", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10438, "title": "Is the basePath feature closer to becoming a reality?", "body": "I know it's been asked a million times before, so if the answer is the same I understand, but looking at the issue #4998 (basePath support) I noticed a recent PR land with experimental support for basePaths (#9872) and then a follow up PR (#9988) which fixed a couple bugs. I know in the original PR it was mentioned that this feature very well could never see the light of day outside of behind the experimental flag. So my question is, is that still the case? If so, any particular reason it might not stick (i.e. support nightmare? complicates routing? something else?)? I'm always interested in hearing some of the behind the scenes discussions of the roadmap and also would love to see this feature land \ud83d\ude04 \r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jamesmosier", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-06T01:29:40Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-13T15:25:48Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10438", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10447, "title": "In development with a custom server, cache page loads if page contents didn't change", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nI'm using a custom express server inside an electron app. When I load the electron app I see \r\n```\r\n[elec] > Using external babel configuration\r\n[elec] > Location: \"/app/babel.config.js\"\r\n[elec] [ ready ] compiled successfully\r\n[elec] [ event ] build page: /\r\n[elec] [ wait ]  compiling ...\r\n[elec] [ ready ] compiled successfully\r\n```\r\nWhen I make a change to any file in the electron app (for example in the main process, which kicks off the server), the app restarts (with electron-reload), and it boots up the custom server again. I then see the exact same output. \r\n\r\nIf the server is restarted, Next.js is recompiling pages unnecessarily.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nOnly compile pages if something relating to the server has changed, not on every restart of the server.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nNone are apparent to me currently, but I'm open to ideas.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nReproduction steps:\r\n1. Start a custom server\r\n2. Let it build some page.\r\n3. Shut the server down with something like Ctrl^C\r\n4. Restart it\r\n5. Wait for it to build again, despite no code being changed. \r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kanjieater", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-07T07:58:30Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-29T09:44:57Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10447", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10456, "title": "Common chunks in serverless page bundles", "body": "As I understand it there is quite a bit of overlap / duplicated code between each serverless page bundle, where each base file at least includes `react` and `react-dom`. Adding external libraries also leads to an increase in each page bundle size.\r\n\r\nThis does not pose a problem when you deploy one Lambda Fn per serverless page bundle (like Now and [serverless-nextjs-plugin](https://github.com/danielcondemarin/serverless-next.js/tree/master/packages/serverless-nextjs-plugin) do. \r\n\r\nHowever for [this project](https://github.com/danielcondemarin/serverless-next.js) I've been maintaining for a while now is not possible to deploy one Lambda Fn. per page as it uses Lambda@Edge behind the scenes where doing this isn't possible. \r\nAn example of the issue can be found in https://github.com/danielcondemarin/serverless-next.js/issues/236 where a user hit the limit of how big the Lambda artifact can be. \r\n\r\nIs it possible to output common chunks between sls page bundles? This would considerably reduce duplication. Considering the next framework is pushing so much for serverless this change would give library maintainers like myself the flexibility to take even further what's possible in this area.\r\n\r\nI'm more than happy to look into it myself, all I'd need would be a bit of initial guidance \ud83d\ude4c", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "danielcondemarin", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-07T22:47:52Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-27T12:33:35Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10456", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10522, "title": "Next JS Export to static HTML always redirect to home page \u201c/\u201d if page refresh", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\nI am creating a blogging application using Next JS and React. I already understand the basics because of this tutorial https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/getting-started.\r\n\r\nSo I tried to implement the blogging application. When I try to test it in development mode `yarn dev` the routing are fine. But if I build it `yarn build` and export into static HTML `yarn export`. The routing misses up when I click on the link it goes on the right page but when I refresh the browser it always takes me back to the `/` page.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nSteps to reproduce the behavior, please provide code snippets or a repository:\r\n\r\n1. Follow this tutorial https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/deploying-a-nextjs-app. But use the \"Deploying to Your Own Environment\" steps\r\n\r\n2. Test it on development mode `yarn dev` and visit the links and refresh each page. It will not redirect to `/` page. \r\n\r\n3. Build it `next build`\r\n\r\n4. Export it `next export`\r\n\r\n5. Upload it into your own server in my case I use nginx local server or use this package https://www.npmjs.com/package/serve\r\n\r\n6. Visit the links and refresh each page. It will always redirect to `/` page.\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nIt should not go back to the `/` page when user refresh the page eg. pressing F5 on the browser rather stays on the current page and load the current data of the page.\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: Debian 9 Linux\r\n- Browser: Goog Chrome\r\n- Version of Next.js: \"next\": \"^9.2.1\", ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 109, "reactions_total": 52, "reactions_plus": 50, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 2, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "yvescoinpass", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-13T16:51:23Z", "updated_at": "2024-09-05T02:16:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10522", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10583, "title": "Allow API routes to tie into specific methods", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nHandling various Methods in API routes is tiresome. If I only want to handle POST, I have to do something like \r\n\r\n```ts\r\nexport default async (req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse) => {\r\n  if (req.method !== 'POST') {\r\n    res.status(405).json({\r\n      error: 'Invalid method',\r\n    })\r\n    return\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // Handle POST\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf I want to handle multiple methods I either have to inline them all or break them into separate functions.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI like having the `default` export as a generic request handler. Expanding on that, it would be nice to have named exports for the various Methods supported. So my above example would become,\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nexport  const post = async (req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse) => {\r\n  // Handle POST\r\n}\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "stramel", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-19T00:51:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-08T02:48:08Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10583", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10639, "title": "Is possible to prefill the React Context API in the getInitialProps step?", "body": "For SSR I need a way to initialize the React Context API.\r\n\r\nThe Redux Example contains a getInitalProps function: https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-redux/pages/index.js\r\n\r\nThe Context Api example doesn't: https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-context-api/pages/index.js\r\n\r\nIs it possible at all or should I migrate to Redux for state management?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "thomaschaaf", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-22T08:54:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T08:07:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10639", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10641, "title": "Best Links and Resources about Next.js", "body": "# Next.js list of links and resources.\r\n\r\n## Repo\r\n\r\n[NextJS Repository](https://github.com/zeit/next.js)\r\n(You're Here on its discussion tab, inside a discussion.)\r\n\r\n## Docs\r\n\r\n[NextJS Docs](https://nextjs.org/docs)\r\n\r\n## Showcase\r\n\r\n[Showcase Examples using NextJS](https://nextjs.org/showcase)\r\n[Companies / Sites using NextJS disccussion](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/discussions/10640)\r\n\r\n## Blog\r\n\r\n[Official Blog](https://nextjs.org/blog/)\r\n\r\n### blogposts\r\n\r\n[Next.js 9.2](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-9-2)\r\n[Next.js 9.1.7](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-9-1-7)\r\n\r\n## Learn NextJS\r\n\r\n### BASICS\r\n\r\n[Getting Started](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/getting-started)\r\n[Navigate Between Pages](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/navigate-between-pages)\r\n[Using Shared Components](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/using-shared-components)\r\n[Create Dynamic Pages](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/create-dynamic-pages)\r\n[Clean URLs with Dynamic Routing](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/clean-urls-with-dynamic-routing)\r\n[Fetching Data for Pages](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/fetching-data-for-pages)\r\n[Styling Components](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/styling-components)\r\n[API Routes](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/api-routes)\r\n[Deploying a Next.js App](https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/deploying-a-nextjs-app)\r\n\r\n### EXCEL\r\n\r\n[Export into a Static HTML App](https://nextjs.org/learn/excel/static-html-export)\r\n[TypeScript](https://nextjs.org/learn/excel/typescript)\r\n[Lazy Loading Modules](https://nextjs.org/learn/excel/lazy-loading-modules)\r\n[Lazy Loading Components](https://nextjs.org/learn/excel/lazy-loading-components)\r\n[Create AMP Pages](https://nextjs.org/learn/excel/amp)\r\n[Automatic Static Optimization](https://nextjs.org/learn/excel/automatic-static-optimization)\r\n\r\n---\r\nP.S: This might somewhat be redundant to awesome-nextjs I guess. but I'm gonna go ahead anyway, It'll be useful for me myself at least, hope it is also useful for the community.\r\n\r\nPlease do share your links and I'll do my best to keep the index/first post updateed\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "agustif", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-22T16:09:01Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-23T15:03:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10641", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10644, "title": "Firebase Functions + express server infinite loop", "body": "when I run express in local it is working fine but when I use firebase function it does not load the page it went into an infinite loading.\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst path = require(\"path\");\r\nconst functions = require(\"firebase-functions\");\r\nconst express = require(\"express\");\r\nconst next = require(\"next\");\r\n\r\n// const port = parseInt(process.env.PORT, 10) || 3000;\r\n// const dev = process.env.NODE_ENV !== \"production\";\r\nconst dev = false;\r\nconst app = next({\r\n  dev,\r\n  conf: { distDir: `${path.relative(process.cwd(), __dirname)}/next` }\r\n});\r\nconst handle = app.getRequestHandler();\r\n\r\nconst server = express();\r\nserver.use(express.json());\r\n\r\nserver.get(\"/\", (req, res, next) => {\r\n  res.send(\"Hello\");\r\n});\r\n\r\nserver.get(\"/berlin/:type\", (req, res) => {\r\n  return app.render(req, res, \"/\", req.query);\r\n});\r\n\r\nserver.get(\"/berlin/:type/:area/\", (req, res) => {\r\n  return app.render(req, res, \"/\", req.query);\r\n});\r\n\r\nserver.get(\"*\", (req, res) => {\r\n  return handle(req, res);\r\n});\r\n\r\nnextApp = functions.https.onRequest(async (req, res) => {\r\n  await app.prepare();\r\n  return server(req, res);\r\n});\r\n\r\nmodule.exports.nextApp = nextApp;\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nnext js config\r\n```\r\nconst withCSS = require(\"@zeit/next-css\");\r\nconst withPlugins = require(\"next-compose-plugins\");\r\nconst isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === \"production\";\r\n\r\nfunction nextConfig(isProd) {\r\n    let nextJsConfig;\r\n    if(isProd) {\r\n        nextJsConfig = {\r\n            distDir: \"../../dist/functions/next\"\r\n        };\r\n    }\r\n    else {\r\n        nextJsConfig = {\r\n            \r\n        };\r\n    }\r\n    return nextJsConfig\r\n}\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = withPlugins([withCSS], nextConfig(isProd));\r\n```\r\n\r\npackage.json script only\r\n```\r\n\"scripts\": {\r\n    \"dev\": \"next \\\"src/app\\\"\",\r\n    \"build-app\": \"next build \\\"src/app/\\\"\",\r\n    \"preserve\": \"npm run build-public && npm run build-funcs && npm run build-app && npm run copy-deps && npm run install-deps\",\r\n    \"serve\": \"cross-env NODE_ENV=production firebase serve\",\r\n    \"build-funcs\": \"babel \\\"src/functions\\\" --out-dir \\\"dist/functions\\\"\",\r\n    \"build-public\": \"cpx \\\"src/public/**/*.*\\\" \\\"dist/public\\\" -C\",\r\n    \"predeploy\": \"npm run build-public && npm run build-funcs && npm run build-app && npm run copy-deps\",\r\n    \"deploy\": \"firebase deploy\",\r\n    \"clean\": \"rimraf \\\"dist/functions/**\\\" && rimraf \\\"dist/public\\\"\",\r\n    \"copy-deps\": \"cpx \\\"*{package.json,package-lock.json,yarn.lock}\\\" \\\"dist/functions\\\" -C\",\r\n    \"install-deps\": \"cd \\\"dist/functions\\\" && npm i\"\r\n  }\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhen I run firebase in local and i fire the URL \"http://localhost:5000/\" ---> Hello\r\nthen the output will be \"hello\", but when I fire the URL  \"http://localhost:5000/berlin/clinic\" then the page does not respond it went into the infinite loop I don't know what to do \r\n\r\nif anyone can help me solve this.\r\n\r\nif you need more information just let me know", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dudhatdhavalm", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-23T08:39:07Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-07T08:30:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10644", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10645, "title": "Enabling Concurrent Mode in NextJS", "body": "Via: https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/10643\r\n\r\nI'd like to test out concurrent mode in my current nextjs project, now that its been released in experimental mode. The way to enable it in a normal react app is quite straght forward by switching `ReactDom.render(<App />, rootEl)` to `ReactDom.createRoot(rootEl).render(<App />)`\r\n\r\nThe problem is nextjs does that part for each page under the hood. It would be nice to have a option in next.config.js to turn concurrent mode on, or even better a way to opt-in per page (e.g. if there are a few old legacy pages that will not work in react strict mode, those dont get opted in, but we can opt in the ones that are ready for this). ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 19, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "cyrus-za", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-23T10:43:23Z", "updated_at": "2026-07-21T21:10:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10645", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10648, "title": "Serving Static Files without a file extension, prevent download?", "body": "I'm trying to serve my apple-app-site-association using the next server. I place the file in the public folder but rather than it being served, it gets downloaded. Is there a way to serve it without using a custom server?\r\n\r\nI'm assuming this is down to the fact that there is no file extension so the next-server doesn't know what content type to add to the header, so it defaults to document. Is there a way to change the headers, or another way to serve this.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 20, "reactions_total": 9, "reactions_plus": 9, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "maitham", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-23T18:58:04Z", "updated_at": "2025-03-20T12:21:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10648", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10654, "title": "Bundling part of the application as a service worker", "body": "Hello there,\r\n\r\nWe have a fairly complex application and we've seen drastic performance gains from including the entire Redux workflow inside a web worker, but that created some maintenance concerns. Is there a way to tell Next.js to build a portion of the app as a web worker? In other words, we need a \"web worker bundle\" and an \"app bundle\". \r\n\r\nWe will most likely use: https://github.com/dai-shi/redux-in-worker", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "m-a-r-c-e-l-i-n-o", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-24T01:45:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-10T11:36:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10654", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10655, "title": "How to eliminate dead code with Next.js 9?", "body": "Routes are auto generated from the file structure in the /pages folder, and they are not imported anywhere in the codebase. Plugins like 'unused-files-webpack-plugin' will list all these pages and their imports as dead code.\r\nIs there any workaround or best practice to find actual dead code, ie. files that are not imported anywhere, and unused exports?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "orszaczky", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-24T07:36:55Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T18:28:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10655", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10656, "title": "Non-english urls not working", "body": "Any ideas how to make non-english urls work with next.js?\r\n\r\nI reported this some time ago and found a workaround way to make it work, but now it's broken again. #9804 \r\n\r\n**Problem**\r\n\r\nI have non-english urls like this:\r\n\r\n> `http://localhost/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442`\r\n\r\nUrl encoded with encodeURI('http://localhost/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442') looks like this:\r\n\r\n> `http://localhost/%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82`\r\n\r\nPages filename is:\r\n\r\n> `pages/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442`\r\n\r\n**Previous attempts to fix this**\r\n\r\nIn some older next.js versions i was able to make it work like this:\r\n\r\n```\r\n experimental: {\r\n    async rewrites() {\r\n      return [{ source: encodeURI('/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442'), destination: '/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442' }]\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n```\r\n\r\nOr even this for all the routes\r\n\r\n```\r\n{ source: '/:rest*', destination: '/:rest*' }\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut after some updates this solutions stopped to work.\r\n\r\n**Current behavior and attempts to make it work**\r\nPure next.js:\r\n1) Client navigation works fine\r\n2) SSR page doesn't load with error: \r\n`GET http://localhost/%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82 404 (Not Found)`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWith rewrites and file rename:\r\n```\r\n  async rewrites() {\r\n      return [{ source: '/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442', destination: '/test' }]\r\n    }\r\n```\r\n\r\n> `pages/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442 renamed to pages/test`\r\n\r\n1) Client navigation doesn't work with errors:\r\n```\r\nhttp://localhost/_next/static/development/pages/%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82.js net::ERR_ABORTED 404 (Not Found)\r\n\r\nGET http://localhost/%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82 404 (Not Found)\r\n```\r\n\r\n2) SSR doesn't work with error: \r\n\r\n`http://localhost/%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82 404 (Not Found)`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAdding encodeURI:\r\n\r\n```\r\n  async rewrites() {\r\n      return [{ source: encodeURI('/\u0442\u0435\u0441\u0442'), destination: '/test' }]\r\n    }\r\n```\r\n\r\n1) Client navigation works, but with error:\r\n`GET http://localhost/_next/static/development/pages/%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82.js net::ERR_ABORTED 404 (Not Found)`\r\n\r\n2) SSR works fine.\r\n\r\nAny ideas to try is welcomed.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ziimakc", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-24T10:49:11Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-18T11:08:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10656", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10658, "title": "Separate Next.js from Express.js in my full-stack app", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nI have a question. Is it possible to separate (isolate) Next.js (let's say in `/src/client`) from Express.js (let's say in `/src/server`, so that there will be 2 `package.json` and 2 `node_modules`, and the client (Next.js) can be started independently in a different port than the server (Express.js)? I know Next.js is Server-Side Rendering, but it is still client stuff (understanding \"client\" here as front-end development). I have already done all the isolating stuff, I got it working, but the problem I have now is how to allow Express.js to manage the routes (not defined before in the Express.js `server.js` file) by Next.js.\r\n\r\nSo when writing a single `server.js` file which initiates Express.js with Next.js we include this line:\r\n`app.all('*', (req, res) => requestHandler(req, res))`;\r\nHere `requestHandler` is a handler defined by Next.js, but as I am not including or initiating Next.js in this `server.js` file I would need something just like it is done with create-react-app and Express.js (which can be easily isolated):\r\n`app.get('*', (req, res) => res.sendFile(path.resolve(__dirname, 'client', 'build', 'index.html')));`\r\n\r\nRegards,\r\nN\u00e9stor Llamas", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-24T13:27:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-15T17:49:00Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10658", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10661, "title": "Any SSG examples using Next.js + Apollo?", "body": "I'm interested in trying out the new `unstable_getStaticProps` api using a headless graphql CMS such as Prismic or Contentful. Before I go down this rabbit hole, does anyone have an example of this type of integration they're willing to share?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 5, "reactions_plus": 5, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "adamsoffer", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-24T15:54:02Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T14:16:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10661", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10668, "title": "production builds and `prerender-manifest.json`", "body": "I'm having an issue with my Next.js site where I'm building the site with `next build`, and when attempting to run the site using my production environment vars and custom server with `NODE_ENV=production node server.js` I'm experiencing two different things:\r\n\r\n1. locally, I can build the site and run it just fine. No issues.\r\n2. after deploying to target host, the site fails to run with NODE_ENV set to `production`, as it is looking for the `prerender-manifest.json` in the build directory `/.next`, `packages/app/.next/prerender-manifest.json`\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Stack Trace</summary>\r\n\r\n```\r\nfs.js:114\r\n    throw err;\r\n    ^\r\n\r\nError: ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '/home/some-user/packages/app/.next/prerender-manifest.json'\r\nat Object.openSync (fs.js:443:3)\r\n    at Object.readFileSync (fs.js:343:35)\r\n    at Object.initializeSprCache (/home/some-user/packages/app/node_modules/next/dist/next-server/server/spr-cache.js:53:53)\r\n    at new Server (/home/some-user/packages/app/node_modules/next/dist/next-server/server/next-server.js:92:21)\r\n    at createServer (/home/some-user/packages/app/node_modules/next/dist/server/next.js:2:133)\r\n    at Object.<anonymous> (/home/some-user/packages/app/server.js:19:13)\r\n    at Module._compile (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:778:30)\r\n    at Object.Module._extensions..js (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:789:10)\r\n    at Module.load (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:653:32)\r\n    at tryModuleLoad (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:593:12)\r\nerror Command failed with exit code 1.\r\n```\r\n</details>\r\n\r\nThis doesn't make any sense to me because the file doesn't exist locally either, but I have no issues running the app.\r\n\r\n### Deploy procedure:\r\n\r\n1. build the app `next build`\r\n2. copy app dir (minus `node_modules`) to target host\r\n3. install deps with `npm install`/`yarn install`\r\n4. run the app with `NODE_ENV=production node server.js`\r\n  1. **NOTE** running the app with `NODE_ENV=staging node server.js` produces no issues, as `prerender-manifest.json` is not used in dev\r\n\r\nFollowing this build procedure locally produces no errors.\r\n\r\n## Question\r\n\r\nIs there an error in my deploy or build procedure? I don't have any issues outside this one error. The reason I build locally and copy is the `next` builder is attempting to use Git to check modified time of MDX files and Git is not/will not be installed on the target host", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 26, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "josefaidt", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-24T19:40:14Z", "updated_at": "2026-04-13T09:09:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10668", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10672, "title": "Next.JS + Firebase Functions", "body": "Hi!\r\n\r\nDoes anyone know of a solid / simple example of how to deploy functions to Firebase (that actually works as is, without a lot of whack-a-mole to figure out paths and other junk)?  It appears the examples that are currently posted in various channels are out-dated and broken.  \r\n\r\nThanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "douglasjkruse-work", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-24T20:59:33Z", "updated_at": "2023-10-24T17:55:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10672", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10675, "title": "Does Next.js support pm2 cluster mode?", "body": "Hi guys,\r\nI'm trying to use next.js with pm2 cluster mode to deploy, but it always returns error.\r\nSo I made a simple express app, but it works well with pm2 cluster mode!\r\nI wonder what's happening.. Is it possible to use pm2 cluster mode with next.js? Or there is something I missed?\r\n\r\nMy pm2 version is 4.2.3, and express version is 4.17.1. next and related version is below.\r\nBelow is my code and some screenshots of my error.\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// pages/index.js\r\nexport default function Index() {\r\n    return (<div><p>Hello Next.js</p></div>)\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```json\r\n// package.json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"test\",\r\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\r\n  \"description\": \"\",\r\n  \"dependencies\": {\r\n    \"next\": \"^9.2.2\",\r\n    \"react\": \"^16.12.0\",\r\n    \"react-dom\": \"^16.12.0\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"devDependencies\": {},\r\n  \"scripts\": {\r\n    \"dev\": \"next\",\r\n    \"build\": \"next build\",\r\n    \"start\": \"next start\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"author\": \"\",\r\n  \"license\": \"ISC\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhen I run the next server\r\n![next](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/35210772/75206242-5089dd00-57b9-11ea-9269-0c9a2de2f190.png)\r\nI found there is no error in id 0. All error occured in id 1.\r\nAlso it works well when I make only one instance..\r\nI thought the problem is related to the port, but isn't pm2 handle this?\r\n\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// test.js (express server)\r\nconst express = require('express')\r\nconst app = express()\r\nconst uuid = require('uuid')\r\nvar instance_id = uuid.v4()\r\nconst port = 3000\r\napp.get('/', function (req, res) { \r\n  res.send(`Hello World! ${instance_id}`)\r\n})\r\napp.listen(port, function () {\r\n  console.log(`application is listening on port ${port}...`)\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhen I run the express server\r\n![express](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/35210772/75206247-541d6400-57b9-11ea-998d-d5e74fdc4611.png)\r\n\r\nI posted this on [spectrum](https://spectrum.chat/next-js/general/next-js-9-pm2-cluster-mode~4dad85e3-1089-4171-b021-1cbb439dd0f6), too.\r\nAlso let me know if this should be in Issues. I'm not sure where it should be..\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 42, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 6, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "choisium", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-25T01:30:06Z", "updated_at": "2025-08-17T15:01:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10675", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10676, "title": "SSG: Download external assets", "body": "With the addition of getStaticProps and Static Optimisation is it possible or are there any plans to support downloading external assets during SSG like traditional static site generator?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "enricoschaaf", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-25T02:09:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-12T08:21:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10676", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10677, "title": "A route page \"dies\" upon reloading, showing a 404 on the Server side.", "body": "Hi, I have this test site:\r\nhttps://next.trejocode.me/\r\nAnd I have a route /comentarios\r\nIf you try to enter directly from the URL to:\r\nhttps://next.trejocode.me/comentarios It will display an Error 404 page.\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/16879799/75213085-1d166680-5757-11ea-91cc-00db692152a9.png)\r\n\r\nBut if you access: https://next.trejocode.me/\r\nAnd since you access the Link \"comentarios\" works.\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/16879799/75213069-0a039680-5757-11ea-84c1-d82a5c7f5327.png)\r\n\r\nMy server is Nginx with PM2 and Reverse Proxy\r\n\r\nConfig:\r\n``` nginx\r\n    # --- Next.js (next.trejocode.me) ---\r\n\r\n    # Aplicaci\u00f3n\r\n    location /_next/ {\r\n        alias /projects/Next-VS-CRA/users-next/.next/;\r\n        expires 15d;\r\n        access_log on;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    # Favicon\r\n    location /favicon.ico {\r\n        alias /projects/Next-VS-CRA/users-next/public/favicon.ico;\r\n        access_log on;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    #Manifest.json\r\n    location /manifest.json {\r\n        alias /projects/Next-VS-CRA/users-next/public/manifest.json;\r\n        access_log on;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    # Proxy inverso\r\n    location / {\r\n        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;\r\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:3001/;\r\n        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;\r\n        proxy_set_header Connection 'upgrade';\r\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\r\n        proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;\r\n    }\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n**nextjs:** 9.2.2\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "TrejoCode", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-25T03:49:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-30T20:19:57Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10677", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10679, "title": "How to implement adobe analytics?", "body": "I have raised an issue here https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/10678 and also posted a question in stackoverflow, https://stackoverflow.com/questions/60376041/how-to-implement-adobe-analytics-in-reactjs but I couldn't get any relevant help..\r\n\r\nCan anyone help me to implement adobe analytics in nextjs project with appropriate examples?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Maniraj-Murugan", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-25T05:52:29Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-12T10:23:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10679", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10682, "title": "Question about removing unused css", "body": "Hello,\r\nI wanted to ask how can one remove all the unused css in his code using Next.js.\r\n\r\nMy project uses scss and has 1 starting point (app.scss) which imports all other partial .scss files.\r\nIn next.config.js i'm using withSass and i've tried using withPurgeCss but it removes all css instead of the unused one.\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tankpower1", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-25T09:09:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T06:36:19Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10682", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10683, "title": "Document for Next.js v7.0.3", "body": "I'm really new to Next.js, and my working project using Next.js v7.0.3.\r\nI couldn't find specific versions document, but I figure out the [old document](https://nextjs.org/docs/old) istead.\r\n\r\nIs the old document available for Next.js v7.0.3?\r\n\r\nThank you \ud83d\ude00\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ryota-murakami", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-25T09:27:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T18:16:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10683", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10684, "title": "SSG and fallback render", "body": "Hey zeiters :hand:\r\n\r\nI'm using the `unstable_getStaticProps` and the `unstable_getStaticPaths` functions in my new project. Theses new APi are really effective and powerful. \r\n\r\nThere is one point in the design that still feel uncertain for me: the current implementation of the fallback mechanism. When using the `unstable_getStaticPaths` method, next.js is trying to generate once the page without any query parameters.\r\n\r\nAs  `unstable_getStaticPaths` is used to pre-generate dynamic paths `/foo/[bar_id]`, rendering a page (executing the page component) without any of the data identifier that qualify that page (`bar_id`) seems counterproductive in the end user point of view. And force us to make or code extremely tolerant or to simply ignore this render: `if (router.isFallback) { return null }` to avoid any risk.\r\n\r\nAfter reading a lot of the past issues and PRs, I discover some of the rationales behind this choice. The main one is that you want to allow a partial static generations of the possible paths. That a really good ideal. And will make this new feature really powerful.  \r\n\r\nAnother way to allow it could be:\r\n- to serve the page as a lambda that will use the `unstable_getStaticProps` API and render like usual the component\r\n- to return automatically a configurable placeholder component and render the page on client side\r\n- add a way to optout/optin this fallback parameter (when I'm rending all the page of the site I did not need it)\r\n\r\nCan you give us more details on where you want to go with the fallback feature? \r\n \r\n\r\nThanks for your amazing work on Next.js!\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "armandabric", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-25T09:29:38Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-19T09:02:01Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10684", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10691, "title": "Building a Blog with Next.js", "body": "Hey folks!\r\nI'm looking to use Next to build a blog.\r\n\r\nHere's what I have in mind:\r\n\r\n- Some folder that I can add md/mdx files to, with front matter to define author, timestamp etc.\r\n- A homepage that automatically picks up the 3 to 5 most recent posts and shows an abstract\r\n- A posts page that shows, well, all posts\r\n- A post page that shows a full post with header etc.\r\n- I should not have to update the homepage manually when I publish a post. That should happen dynamically.\r\n\r\nNow, there's several starter kits out there that do just that, but most of them seem either pretty over-engineered, not particularly nice to use, or using an archaic version of Next.\r\n\r\nI was wondering if there's some a recent, authoritative example out there for a use case like this?\r\n\r\nThe Next docs start building a blog, but unfortunately don't go far enough to get to the point where it's fully dynamic, unless I missed some part. Is that by design, and Next is completely unopinionated in that regard? Are there any \"best practices\"?\r\n\r\nThanks a ton!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "TimoStaudinger", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-25T16:47:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-05T14:31:04Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10691", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10698, "title": "Is it possible to use custom routes and retain the query string?", "body": "I'm testing the experimental redirects feature with a few routes to replace an older redirect component that we use. During my initial test, I noticed that the query string isn't carried through to the destination. This causes my redirects to not work as intended if the user came from an older link which had specific filters as well as stripping query parameters consumed by our analytics like campaign, source, etc. Is there a way to create a source and destination pattern which will carry all query params through without explicitly defining each query param in the pattern?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 8, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "soupaJ", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-26T00:42:40Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T14:06:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10698", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23987, "title": "Implement HTTPS for Development (localhost)", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nI would like to be able to easily use HTTPS on my localhost\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nBe able to change the `dev` script like so:\r\n\r\n```diff\r\n-\"dev\": \"next\",\r\n+\"dev\": \"HTTPS=true next\",\r\n``` \r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nCRA and Gatsby both handle this. Gatsby uses `devcert`.\r\n\r\nhttps://www.gatsbyjs.org/docs/local-https/\r\nhttps://create-react-app.dev/docs/using-https-in-development/\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 18, "reactions_total": 31, "reactions_plus": 31, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "stramel", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-26T05:03:30Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:10:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23987", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10707, "title": "Next.js 9.2, Improved Code-Splitting Strategy, Chunk Naming", "body": "How are the chunk names generated? \r\nWe get names like `chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.8dae1d.js`, where the last part (after the . ) seems to be a content hash and the rest look random. So we get new file names for each build even if there were no changes and wondered if this is the default behavior and if we can change this to hash only to improve cachability?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 4, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 4, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "roman-may", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-26T15:49:05Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-08T09:28:14Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10707", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10708, "title": "For SSA of an e-commerce application, what is the need of Nextjs?", "body": "I want to make a Single Site Application which is an e-commerce application. For this I want use **Django, Django Rest** for API endpoints as backend. And for frontend I want to use **React, Redux, Axios, React-Router, Antd**. \r\nSo for professionally deploy this app, what is need of **Nextjs or Gatsby**? I want to scale my application for million users.\r\nPlease help & Thanks.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "biplobmahadi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-26T17:43:01Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-17T13:25:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10708", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10709, "title": "NextJS GET request for the _error page denied when using basic auth in Internet Explorer", "body": "**All browsers (including Microsoft Edge) work, except for Internet Explorer** when navigating an `_error` page. If a user tries to navigate to a route that is not supported `localhost:3000/banana` then nextJS will call `_error` to render the page. However, in IE, this fails. Console will error the following:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/31263423/75376487-21996700-589e-11ea-9203-101edfd9e55c.png)\r\n\r\n**The problem**\r\n\r\nThis only happens when using basic auth. So looking into the Network logs I find that the GET request to `_error` is denied and a 401 response is sent.\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/31263423/75377743-6f16d380-58a0-11ea-8846-7567c09198e7.png)\r\n\r\nHowever, this only happens for the error page, not for any other page in nextJS. Looking at what happens when loading any other page, this is the result - one GET request that is a 401, and then another immediately after that is successful.\r\n\r\nLike the request for `_error` page, the first request contained no Authorization in the header, but the successful request did have it.\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/31263423/75377808-8f469280-58a0-11ea-9b35-f004a8ffca1c.png)\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jfarris587", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-26T19:04:18Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-21T13:55:48Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10709", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10711, "title": "meta base not working on client side", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nI'm using express with nextjs, the express app has these routes: \r\n`/:lang/yyy`\r\n`/:lang/xxx`\r\n`/:lang/xxx/xxx`.\r\n`/:lang/xxx/xxx/xxx`.\r\n\r\nAll my pages has the meta base with this url: `http://localhost:3000/${lang}/`  while all the links in the pages are `<Link href=\"my-page\" />`.\r\n\r\nIf I'm in this page:\r\n`http://localhost:3000/en/xxx`\r\n\r\nand I click a link like `<a href=\"yyy\"></a>` I navigate to the right page `http://localhost:3000/en/yyy`, but if I'm in this page:\r\n`http://localhost:3000/en/xxx/xxx`\r\n\r\nand I click a link like `<a href=\"yyy\"></a>` instead of navigate to the right page `http://localhost:3000/en/yyy` I'll land in this page `http://localhost:3000/en/xxx/yyy`\r\n\r\nWhen I hover the link I can see the right url, indeed if I open the link in a new page is working fine.\r\n\r\nI'm doing something wrong?\r\n\r\nmany thanks", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "francescovenica", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-26T22:41:40Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-16T01:09:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10711", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10714, "title": "Dynamic route for index.js", "body": "I want my landing page (pages/index.js) to have 0 or 1 param. (kind of user id)\r\nIt is for support personal landing page for those have param (user id) for index.js\r\n(For those not have param, I'll just show general form.)\r\n\r\nHow can I make dynamic route for index.js without making pages/[id].js ?\r\nIf I make pages/[id].js, I should manage 2 files (pages/index.js, pages/[id].js) which has almost same code.\r\n\r\n(I tried next/router but I could get param by /?id=123 which is not semantic url at all.)\r\n\r\nThanks.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "marine1079", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-27T02:13:31Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-31T19:06:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10714", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10715, "title": "Is it worth publishing a fork of react-loadable?", "body": "I noticed react-loadable has been effectively [copied](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/master/packages/next/next-server/lib/loadable.js) and updated with new features. The last time an official version of react-loadable was published was over 2 years ago and it will eventually be unusable as it contains deprecated component lifecycle methods. Since the current maintainer of react-loadable doesn't seem interested in making any improvements to the library and does not have any open channels for discussing fixes for these issues does anyone else think that a fork should be created and namespaced as `@next/react-loadable`?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "closed", "state_reason": "outdated", "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "iMobs", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-27T02:36:07Z", "updated_at": "2023-04-27T02:07:24Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10715", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10717, "title": "Advantages / Disadvantages of adding google closure compiler", "body": "I've been reading up about [Google's closure compiler](https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler) and to my understanding it can produce more performant and smaller javascript bundles.\r\n\r\nIt looks like it is relatively easy to add alongside next js via https://github.com/webpack-contrib/closure-webpack-plugin\r\n\r\nHowever there will probably be multiple places where babel config needs to be adjusted, hence I wanted to ask if it makes sense to try this out, would it improve performance / bundle size at all or is next js already doing better job here?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 1, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "xzilja", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-27T10:55:59Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T19:16:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10717", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10719, "title": "Lighthouse Performance issue", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nI ran the 'npm run build' command, and then put my site live through 'now' command. When I run a Lighthouse audit on my website, I'm currently getting a performance hit, because of the following: \r\n\"Consider using `<link rel=preload>` to prioritize fetching resources that are currently requested later in page load\".\r\nI can see the CSS is being loaded in the <head> tag as follows: <link rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" href=\"/_next/static/css/styles.282bad2c.chunk.css\">. \r\nIs there a way to \"resolve\" this performance issue?\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nSteps to reproduce the behavior, please provide code snippets or a repository:\r\n\r\n1. Go to https://website-thomas.thomasledoux1.now.sh/\r\n2. Run a Lighthouse audit\r\n3. Check the suggestions for Performance\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nWith the default 'build' command and setup, I would expect the CSS to be loaded as efficiently as possible.\r\n\r\n## Screenshots\r\n\r\nhttps://snipboard.io/HCwVSX.jpg\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: Windows\r\n- Browser (if applies) Chrome\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.2.2\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "thomasledoux1", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-27T15:03:39Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T13:32:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10719", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10721, "title": "Use Nextjs handler in API routes to be able to use renderToHTML", "body": "There are some usecases where pages need to display up to date data, which doesn't exist during the build step and can't be fetched on clientside, e.g. product pages in a Catalog which needs a good SEO.\r\n\r\nAs I have not found a way yet to render static pages on demand or something smilar to cover this usecase, it seems like using Initial props / getServerProps together with some page cache would still be the most feasible solution.\r\n\r\nUsing a Cache for SSR pages currently requires using a custom Server.\r\nThe example implementation uses the `cacheable-response` package and then calls `renderToHTML` of the nextjs app handler.\r\n\r\nIn my API route I only have access to `res` and `req`. Would it be possible to make `renderToHTML`, or maybe even other next  handler functionality available over these API routes?\r\n\r\nBTW: I would also appreciate other suggestions on how to solve the underlying issue described above :)", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "janus-reith", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-27T16:24:26Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-04T06:16:16Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10721", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10724, "title": "Best way to secure /admin routes", "body": "Hi, so I'm slowly getting my head around sessions and auth with Next.js (deployed on Now). What I'm running into now is that I have a HOC `ensureAdmin` that I can wrap a page with to check that a user is authenticated and actually an admin. (the API routes are secured ofc so it's mostly a UX/visibility thing). The downside is that you need to repeat this for every page.\r\n\r\nIs there a way to do this globally for everything under this path /admin? It would be nice to for instance have a custom `_app.js` that applies to this folder+children or something.\r\n\r\nHow do you solve this?\r\nShould I just not try to limit access to the page and use a fetch hook to check client side after rendering the skeleton?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 39, "reactions_total": 18, "reactions_plus": 17, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 1, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "GriffinSauce", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-27T20:58:08Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-13T00:46:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10724", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10725, "title": "next dev watch all files?", "body": "I have some apis in `/pages/api`. Those APIs pull in entity models from typeorm. \r\n\r\nIf I change the route itself, things restart without any issue.\r\n\r\nIf I change the typeorm model, I have to manually kill next server and restart.\r\n\r\nIs there anyway to get next dev to restart when any imported file is changed?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 8, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "nickyhajal", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-27T23:00:18Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-01T16:22:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10725", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10729, "title": "[Now] Best way to set the Cache-Control header for an API Route?", "body": "Hey people!\r\n\r\nI've been trying to set the `cache-control` to `s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate` for the API Routes on a NextJS app deployed on Now.\r\n\r\nI tried 2 ways:\r\n- setting the header before sending the data, directly in the API Route function.\r\n\r\n```.js\r\n...\r\nres.setHeader('Cache-Control', 's-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate');\r\nres.status(status).json(data);\r\n```\r\n\r\n- setting rules in `now.json`.\r\n\r\n```.json\r\n...\r\n\"routes\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"src\": \"/api/(.*)\",\r\n      \"headers\": {\r\n        \"cache-control\": \"s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate\"\r\n      },\r\n      \"dest\": \"/api/$1\",\r\n      \"continue\": true\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n```\r\n\r\n...None of them work once deployed :/ What would be the best way?\r\n\r\nThanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Danetag", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-28T03:08:31Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-30T19:25:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10729", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10741, "title": "\"Serverless\" as the only runtime target", "body": "Can anyone clarify what \"serverless as the only runtime target\" means for the roadmap of next.js or organizations that run outside of NOW?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "garnerp", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-28T19:02:33Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T01:12:47Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10741", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10743, "title": "Next.js + Markdown + GraphQL", "body": "Gatsby has a really great plugin called [`gatsby-transformer-remark`](https://www.gatsbyjs.org/packages/gatsby-transformer-remark) that allows you to query markdown files using graphql. How can something similar be achieved with Next.js? I'm keen on using next.js + open authored markdown for my next jamstack site, but I want that graphql goodness!\r\n\r\nPerhaps something similar can be unlocked once [support for plugins](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/9133) lands.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "adamsoffer", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-02-28T19:32:54Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T06:14:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10743", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10745, "title": "IPFS hosting with static `assetPrefix`", "body": "Hi! I'm trying to host a fully statically exported Next.js site on both DNS as well as IPFS. So far, I've achieved this by using a build-time flag, and optionally prefixing a tag `href`s, img tag `src`s, etc., with `'.'` with a custom base tag so that my site can be served on e.g. `https://ipfs.io/ipns/<...>/`. I also have to make assetPrefix `'.'` when building for IPFS. The problem is that once I'm >1 route deep, assetPrefix breaks, as it begins looking for shared `_next` resources relative to the current path, whereas it should actually be looking relative to my custom base tag.\r\n\r\nOne solution to this may be a dynamic `assetPrefix`, but as far as I can tell this is impossible in statically exported sites. Is there another possible solution that I'm missing?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "NoahZinsmeister", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-28T22:57:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-16T03:55:19Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10745", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10747, "title": "Please, how can I change the url background in the Sass files in production?", "body": "Hello in development mode, I have the relative paths as in the following example:\r\n\r\n`background-image: url(/images/bg-header.png) !important;`\r\n\r\nBut in production mode I'm going to upload the website to a subdirectory of the domain, \r\nexample: \r\n\r\n`https://www.domain.com/subdirectory/`\r\n\r\nI need it to build like this:\r\n\r\n`background-image: url(/subdirectory/images/bg-header.png) !important;`\r\n\r\nHow can I get it?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "chiqui3d", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-29T00:11:44Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T05:12:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10747", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10749, "title": "How to rerun script in the head tag?", "body": "I am trying to rerun an external script in the head tag.\r\nBut it doesn't work.\r\nDoes someone know how to do it?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "RayLuxembourg", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-29T13:36:31Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-25T15:36:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10749", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10750, "title": "Architecture in 2020 ", "body": "Hi to everyone,\r\n\r\nI am trying to build an app with a couple of static marketing/information pages and a closed membership part like a dynamic dashboard page.\r\n\r\nMy first try was to build it with next.js and firebase for authentication and apollo client for fetching data from the hasura graphql backend.\r\n\r\nProblems I encountered so far:\r\n\r\n**GraphQL:** I am using apollo/react-hooks and I basically copied the [next.js with apollo example](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-apollo/) in order to flexibly decide which page is static (no external data) and can be delivered via CDN and which page is dynamic and ServerSideRendered. Was working fine till I needed authentication.\r\n\r\n**Authentication:** If i use Firebase for authentication I need to use a custom server with next.js in order to get the firebase.admin.auth running on the next.js server. The problem with that solution is automatically all my sites will become ServerSideRendered and I miss completely out on the CDN serving option. Which brings me to the serverless solution. I guess I need to use the serverless approach and copy the example as [nextjs firebase serverless](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-firebase-authentication-serverless) here in order to still be able to use all the benefits of nextjs (static via CDN and dynamic)? \r\n\r\nI am basically looking for the \"best\" approach to build a site which uses marketing (static) sites and a dashboard (dynamic part). I want to have a solution that's able to scale and is fast and can be later also used with mobile apps. All of the users will be in central Europe.\r\n\r\n_How would you build it with a javascript stack?_\r\n\r\nI've seen from another discussion that the zeit.co dashboard is working as: \r\n[https://github.com/zeit/next.js/discussions/10724#discussioncomment-720](url)\r\n```\r\nUses https://github.com/zeit/swr to ensure data is up-to-date while staying fast\r\nAll pages are auto-exported to static HTML, no getInitialProps on any pages\r\nAll pages implement a loading shell, an example of this is: https://zeit.co/dashboard/abcdefgh\r\nWhen a user opens the page a fetch is done against the API, if you're logged in they have a cookie set already, if not you're redirected to the login screen\r\n```\r\nIs there an example for that approach as it sounds really interesting to me?\r\n\r\nI am also open to the new /api routes in next.js and ditch hasura. But the routes only work with rest and sooner or later there might be the request to add mobile apps. That's the reason why I think it might be better/faster to do it with graphql/hasura? Or I just could go with the next.js routes api and add different endpoints for mobile like /api/mobile and write the resolvers again? Also authentication with firebase is not a must. What are you using and how happy are you? Many questions.... \r\n\r\nThanks for your help!!! ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Manubi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-29T15:27:03Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T15:37:49Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10750", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10754, "title": "[getInitialProps with Redux Saga]: How can I await for data being fetched in getInitialProps when dispatching an async action while using redux-saga?", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nI'm not sure if there's a similar question here, but I've searched a lot but couldn't find an answer.\r\n\r\nI need to know how can I wait for data fetching inside getInitialProps when dispatching an action with redux-saga in use.\r\n\r\nAll the answers I find make sense when using redux-thunk or do the actual API, but not for redux-saga", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "AntwanSherif", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-02-29T18:31:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:25:35Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10754", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10761, "title": "How to detect environment getInitialProps is running in", "body": "I'm trying to understand how `getInitialProps` runs in different environments: On the client and server, as well as during static export. It seems you can only infer these states from other things about the `getInitialProps` context argument. I realize that #9524 will change some of this, but want to understand this piece of the api as well as possible. Is this pretty close? \r\n```jsx\r\n// pages/foo/[foo].js\r\n\r\nFoo.getInitialProps = context => {\r\n  const { query, req } = context;\r\n  console.warn(\"GIP starting\", {\r\n    query,\r\n    reqPresence: req && Object.keys(req).length\r\n  });\r\n  console.log(Object.keys(context));\r\n  if (req) console.log(Object.keys(req));\r\n\r\n  if (process.browser) {\r\n    console.warn(\"running in browser\")\r\n    if (req && Object.keys(req).length === 0) {\r\n      /* empty req object => statically exported */\r\n      console.warn(\"statically exported\")\r\n    } else {\r\n      /* runs after clicking <Link /> or router.push() */\r\n      console.warn(\"running on Link/router.push()\")\r\n    }\r\n  } else {\r\n    console.warn(\"running on server\")\r\n    /* absence of process.browser => node process on server */\r\n\r\n    if (Object.keys(context.req).length === 7) {\r\n      /* Static Export\r\n          7 static export req object has keys: \r\n         'url', 'headers', 'getHeader', 'setHeader',\r\n         'hasHeader', 'removeHeader', 'getHeaderNames'\r\n         _is this the best way to know this?_\r\n      */\r\n\r\n      console.warn(\"export\");\r\n    } else  if (Object.keys(context.req).length > 7) {\r\n      /* req object has > 7 keys => server (SSR/SSG) */\r\n\r\n      console.warn(\"SSR\");\r\n    } else {\r\n    throw new Error(\"this can never happen\");\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  return { foo: query.foo };\r\n};\r\n\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "erikdstock", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-01T00:05:41Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-14T22:27:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10761", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10762, "title": "Passing large csv files into statically-exported pages: pass in as `query`?", "body": "I'd like to pass all csvs in a `data/` directory into individual statically-exported pages, but i'm not clear on the best way to do it. In pseudocode terms i've been doing something like this:\r\n```\r\nexportPathMap() {\r\n  const paths = {}\r\n  dataFiles.forEach(file => {\r\n    const csv = fs.readFileSync(file + \".csv\", 'utf-8')\r\n    paths[\"/results/\" + file] = {page: \"/results/[result]\", query: { csv }}\r\n  }\r\n  \r\n  return paths\r\n}\r\n```\r\nThen in my `getInitialProps` i do more complicated logic on the csv string, parsing it into an object and so on (this is not easy to do from next.config.js due to the stripped-down node environment).\r\n\r\nThat said, this all feels weird. I'm coming from a gatsby background in part but wanted to try next since these csvs felt like they didn't call for a full graphql type system, but it feels like i'm doing something wrong to be assigning `csv` to a 'query' object. Am I missing something? Is there a much easier way to do things? Should i be waiting for the `getStaticProps` method?\r\n\r\n(also sorry for asking back-to-back questions)", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "erikdstock", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-01T00:12:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T02:00:04Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10762", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10769, "title": "Built-In CSS insert order, Tailwind always at the end", "body": "Regarding Next.js 9.2 Built-In CSS, is there any way to make **component styles** not inserted after Tailwind css (global css)?\r\n\r\nI would like Tailwind classes to be more important than any other class since they are used explicitly.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mxfs", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-01T09:15:58Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-21T10:56:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10769", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10778, "title": "using nodemailer in nextjs can't resolve child_process", "body": "Using nodemailer in one of the `pages/api` routes produces the following error message:\r\n`[ error ] ./node_modules/nodemailer/lib/sendmail-transport/index.js\r\nModule not found: Can't resolve 'child_process' in 'C:\\ua-demo\\node_modules\\nodemailer\\lib\\sendmail-transport'`\r\n\r\nWith my understanding being that the next.js `pages/api` routes run in the server environment alone, it is a wonder that this error is occurring. How could this be addressed so that I can send email updates to my users?\r\n\r\nAn example has been added here at [codesandbox.io](https://codesandbox.io/s/nextjs-nodemailer-api-testshared-tmhen). I think that we need to build a copy of the program directly on a local machine to reproduce.\r\n\r\n[Also discussed on SO](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/60496911/cant-resolve-child-process-when-using-nodemailer-in-next-js)", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "steveswork", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-02T06:09:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-11T02:41:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10778", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10779, "title": "Freezing issue when clicking a link, cannot be reproduce consistently", "body": "Hey,\r\n\r\nI'm pretty new to React & Nextjs and just started my second project with it.\r\nI have sometimes the issue that the page is freezing for some seconds when i'm clicking a link to another page.\r\nAs I cannot reproduce it consistently and happens rarely, I have not paid attention on this topic yet. Maybe someone can relate the issue and knows what is causing it, i was going through the issue list, but could not find any connection.\r\nI am using the `next-redux-wrapper` which is might not directly visible as it is used in `_app.js`.\r\n\r\nThe current website can be found here: https://shokz.grief.dev/\r\nAnd the source code is located here: https://gitlab.com/shokz.tv/web-v2/app\r\n\r\nAny help is really apperciated.\r\n\r\nThanks", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "michaelketzer", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-02T09:50:57Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T03:49:21Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10779", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10780, "title": "How to configure `tsconfig.json` to use Next.js together with Nexus?", "body": "Cross-posting this issue from the [`nexus`](https://github.com/prisma-labs/nexus/issues/391) repository here: https://github.com/prisma-labs/nexus/issues/391\r\n\r\nI have an example that combines GraphQL Nexus with Next.js, you can find it here: https://github.com/prisma/prisma-examples/tree/prisma2/typescript/graphql-nextjs\r\n\r\nNow, I'm trying to invoke the Nexus generation via:\r\n\r\n```\r\nts-node --transpile-only pages/api\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere is my `makeSchema`:\r\n\r\n```\r\nexport const schema = makeSchema({\r\n  types: [Query, Mutation, Post, User, GQLDate],\r\n  outputs: {\r\n    typegen: path.join(__dirname, 'nexus-typegen.ts'),\r\n    schema: path.join(__dirname, 'schema.graphql')\r\n  },\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo, I'd expect that the Nexus generation outputs the two files `nexus-typegen.ts` and `schema.graphql`.\r\n\r\nHowever, when I run the command I see this error:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ npm run generate:nexus\r\n> hello-next@1.0.0 generate:nexus /Users/nikolasburk/prisma/github/prisma-examples/typescript/graphql-nextjs\r\n> ts-node --transpile-only pages/api\r\n/Users/nikolasburk/prisma/github/prisma-examples/typescript/graphql-nextjs/pages/api/index.ts:1\r\nimport { idArg, makeSchema, objectType, stringArg, asNexusMethod } from 'nexus';\r\n       ^\r\nSyntaxError: Unexpected token {\r\n    at Module._compile (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:703:23)\r\n    at Module.m._compile (/Users/nikolasburk/.config/yarn/global/node_modules/ts-node/src/index.ts:493:23)\r\n    at Module._extensions..js (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:770:10)\r\n    at Object.require.extensions.<computed> [as .ts] (/Users/nikolasburk/.config/yarn/global/node_modules/ts-node/src/index.ts:496:12)\r\n    at Module.load (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:628:32)\r\n    at Function.Module._load (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:555:12)\r\n    at Function.Module.runMain (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:822:10)\r\n    at Object.<anonymous> (/Users/nikolasburk/.config/yarn/global/node_modules/ts-node/src/bin.ts:158:12)\r\n    at Module._compile (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:759:30)\r\n    at Object.Module._extensions..js (internal/modules/cjs/loader.js:770:10)\r\nnpm ERR! code ELIFECYCLE\r\nnpm ERR! errno 1\r\nnpm ERR! hello-next@1.0.0 generate:nexus: `ts-node --transpile-only pages/api`\r\nnpm ERR! Exit status 1\r\nnpm ERR! \r\nnpm ERR! Failed at the hello-next@1.0.0 generate:nexus script.\r\nnpm ERR! This is probably not a problem with npm. There is likely additional logging output above.\r\nnpm ERR! A complete log of this run can be found in:\r\nnpm ERR!     /Users/nikolasburk/.npm/_logs/2020-02-28T16_13_47_524Z-debug.log\r\n```\r\n\r\nI was able to fix the error with the help of [this SO question](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/56546541/typescript-syntaxerror-unexpected-token), I needed to adjust the `module`  in the `compilerOptions` of my `tsconfig.json` from `esnext` to `commonjs` to make this work.\r\n\r\nHowever, when starting my Next.js app again, it autommatically overrides that setting to `esnext` again:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ npm run dev\r\n\r\n> hello-next@1.0.0 dev /Users/nikolasburk/prisma/github/prisma-examples/typescript/graphql-nextjs\r\n> next\r\n\r\n[ wait ]  starting the development server ...\r\n[ info ]  waiting on http://localhost:3000 ...\r\nThe following changes are being made to your tsconfig.json file:\r\n  - compilerOptions.target to be suggested value: es5 (this can be changed)\r\n  - compilerOptions.module must be esnext (for dynamic import() support)\r\n\r\n[ info ]  bundled successfully, waiting for typecheck results...\r\n[ event ] build page: /next/dist/pages/_error\r\n[ wait ]  compiling ...\r\n[ info ]  bundled successfully, waiting for typecheck results...\r\n[ ready ] compiled successfully - ready on http://localhost:3000\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'm wondering how I should configure my `tsconfig.json` to use both Nexus and Next.js together?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "nikolasburk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-02T10:36:48Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T02:43:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10780", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10783, "title": "Failed: static export + dynamic routes + a subpath (assetsPrefix)", "body": "Use case:\r\n\r\n- A site with dynamic routes (catch-all case). \r\n- Deployed onto a subpath on the production server.\r\n- Static export\r\n\r\n# Non-production builds (works)\r\n\r\nWith a non-production build, this is the exported path map:\r\n\r\n```\r\n{\r\n  '/grid.html': {\r\n    page: '/grid'\r\n  },\r\n  '/dyn/what/ever.html': {\r\n    page: '/dyn/[...slug]'\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n# Production builds (fails)\r\n\r\nHowever for production builds we add an assetsPrefix so the export path map ends like so:\r\n\r\n```\r\n{\r\n  '/subpath/grid.html': {\r\n    page: '/grid'\r\n  },\r\n  '/subpath/dyn/what/ever.html': {\r\n    page: '/dyn/[...slug]'\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\nThe first (regular) entry is fine, the second on (the dynamic one) fails with:\r\n\r\n```\r\nError occurred prerendering page \"/subpath/dyn/what/ever.html\" \r\nhttps://err.sh/zeit/next.js/prerender-error: \r\nError: The provided export path '/subpath/dyn/what/ever.html' doesn't match the '/dyn/[...slug]' page.\r\n```\r\n\r\nAny idea how this can be done?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Izhaki", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-02T12:02:00Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-07T18:46:04Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10783", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10784, "title": "Do you still support zones?  Is this feature gonna be continued? ", "body": "I was willing to try the usage of zones. But I realised they are not present on the most recent docs. Please, are they gonna be discontinued? ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "brunoreis", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-02T12:35:46Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T17:39:00Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10784", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10789, "title": "How to mutate apollo context?", "body": "I've looked in to [api-routes-apollo-server-and-client-auth](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/api-routes-apollo-server-and-client-auth) example and not sure how can i mutate context both on ssr and client request.\r\n\r\nI want for every graphql resolver (using api) have access to ctx.user object with preparsed JWT token. But where should i parse it?\r\n\r\nIf i parse it here:\r\n```\r\nconst apolloServer = new ApolloServer({\r\n  schema,\r\n  context(ctx) {\r\n    ctx.user = '123'\r\n    return ctx\r\n  }\r\n})\r\n\r\nexport default apolloServer.createHandler({ path: '/api/graphql' })\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen ctx.user will be undefined on SSR request, working only on client request.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ziimakc", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-02T15:07:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-23T23:55:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10789", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10806, "title": "can we redirect to another page declaretively?", "body": "I need to redrect to login page if  a 401 error is catched. I did this with React error boundary.  the `      Router.push` is not supposed to be used imperatively. \r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nclass ErrorBoundary extends React.Component<any> {\r\n  state = {error: null};\r\n\r\n  componentDidCatch(error) {\r\n    this.setState({error});\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  render() {\r\n    const {children, fallback, router} = this.props;\r\n    const { asPath } = router;\r\n\r\n    const {error} = this.state;\r\n    if (error instanceof RRNLRequestError && error.res.status == 401) {\r\n      Router.push(`/login?redirect=${asPath}`); \r\n      return;  // <Redirect to={`/login?redirect=${asPath}`} />\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if (error) {\r\n      return React.createElement(fallback, {error});\r\n    }\r\n    return children;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "videni", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-03T13:07:57Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-17T11:08:22Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10806", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10808, "title": "How to trigger a Next.js build in Now using webhooks", "body": "What's the recommended way to trigger a next.js build using webhooks in Now? For context, I'd like to deploy a CMS-powered next.js jamstack site to Now.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "adamsoffer", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-03T16:10:17Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T14:28:01Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10808", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10809, "title": "Is it possible to listen for when getInitialProps is called", "body": "I use [NProgress](https://github.com/rstacruz/nprogress) (a fake progress bar on top of the page) for user feedback while they wait for page transitions. \r\n\r\nI use it like this right now\r\n```\r\nRouter.events.on('routeChangeStart', NProgress.start)\r\nRouter.events.on('routeChangeComplete', NProgress.done)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThing is I only need it when I go to a dynamic page since navigating to statically optimized pages is instant anyways. \r\n\r\nIs there a way I can listen to either the start/end of `getInitialProps` or otherwise recognize if the page I'm navigating to is a dynamic page?\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "csenio", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-03T16:22:13Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-10T21:27:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10809", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10810, "title": "Sub tracking navigation possible using SSR?", "body": "\r\nIs sub navigation even possible in NextJS and this or am I missing something? I cannot figure out how to have a menu like this:\r\n\r\nHome\r\n--> Subpage\r\n--> Subpage\r\n-->-->SubSubpage\r\nMain Page\r\nContact Page\r\n\r\nI want to be able to track which option is pressed for example if subpage is selected then Home would be highlighted because it is the parent. Is this possible? If so how?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "matthewcoxx", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-03T16:41:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-01T15:04:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10810", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10815, "title": "How to show a progress bar while route changes using React concurrent mode? ", "body": "I am using React concurrent mode, when route changes, I'd like to stay at current page for a while and also show a progress bar at the top of the web page. any way to use `Router.push` with `useTransition`\r\n```\r\nconst useTransition = React.useTransition;\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "videni", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-04T02:45:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T21:29:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10815", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10817, "title": "Nested routes", "body": "Hi everyone. I came to next.js after nuxt.js and I have question about nested routes.\r\nFor example I have structure pages:\r\n```\r\n--pages\r\n----article\r\n------[id]\r\n---------index.js -> /article/[id]/\r\n---------edit.js -> /article/[id]/edit\r\n---------statistic.js -> /article/[id]/statistic\r\n```\r\nAnd I need common component for article (where I get article data) and it will be parent component for pages (index, edit, statistic) and will not reload after I change router inside (example:  `/article/[id]/ -> /article/[id]/edit`). I know about I can use common parent component but it will be reload when I change inside router.\r\n\r\nI need some like this https://nuxtjs.org/guide/routing#nested-routes\r\n\r\n```\r\n--pages\r\n----article\r\n------[id]\r\n---------index.js -> /article/[id]/\r\n---------edit.js -> /article/[id]/edit\r\n---------statistic.js -> /article/[id]/statistic\r\n------[id].js -> common component (for example how its working in nuxt).\r\n```\r\n\r\nHow can I do functional like this?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Jexim", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-04T09:01:17Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-20T08:06:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10817", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10820, "title": "[static-fun] How to exporting a static site by subdomain", "body": "Hi, I want apply feature static exporting for static-fun project (https://github.com/zeit/static-fun)\r\n\r\nEx: I have a add a button \"Deploy Page\"  in static-fun backend\r\n\r\nPageList:\r\n\r\n1. subdomain1.static.fun  |  Deploy Page\r\n1. subdomain2.static.fun |  Deploy Page\r\n1. subdomain3.static.fun |  Deploy Page\r\n\r\nWhen click DeployPage button on subdomain1.static.fun system will run a service to export static for subdomain1\r\n\r\nWhen click DeployPage button on subdomain2.static.fun system will run a service to export static for subdomain2\r\n\r\nAfter, when user open subdomain1.static.fun on browse. if it static file exits page will load from this. else page will get page info from database.\r\n\r\nHow i cant do this. Thank you so much!\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "flutterforsale", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-04T09:56:13Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T14:28:11Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10820", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10829, "title": "NextJS PostCSS + Electron", "body": "Heya. I'm using NextJS + Electron and trying to follow https://nextjs.org/docs/advanced-features/customizing-postcss-config but as soon as I add purgeCSS in production mode, things start to break. \r\n\r\nDid anyone have luck using it with Electron? Specifically following this example: https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-tailwindcss/postcss.config.js", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "assimovt", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-04T17:37:20Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T12:30:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10829", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10832, "title": "How do I keep pages from re-rendering completely in Next.js?", "body": "E.g.\r\n\r\nif I have a page called a.js, b.js, and c.js\r\n\r\nin each file it looks like this\r\n\r\n**a.js**\r\n```js\r\nimport Progress from \"../components/progress\";\r\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\r\n\r\nexport default () => {\r\n  return (\r\n    <div>\r\n      <Progress value={0} />\r\n      <Link href=\"/b\" shallow>\r\n        <a>Next</a>\r\n      </Link>\r\n    </div>\r\n  );\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n**b.js**\r\n```js\r\nimport Progress from \"../components/progress\";\r\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\r\n\r\nexport default () => {\r\n  return (\r\n    <div>\r\n      <Progress value={0.5} />\r\n      <Link href=\"/c\" shallow>\r\n        <a>Next</a>\r\n      </Link>\r\n    </div>\r\n  );\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n**c.js**\r\n```js\r\nimport Progress from \"../components/progress\";\r\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\r\n\r\nexport default () => {\r\n  return (\r\n    <div>\r\n      <Progress value={1} />\r\n      <Link href=\"/a\" shallow>\r\n        <a>Next</a>\r\n      </Link>\r\n    </div>\r\n  );\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\neach page will fully reload regardless of the `shallow` prop on the link and the state is lost. I would assume pages should be diffed so how can I get this behavior to work?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 15, "reactions_total": 22, "reactions_plus": 22, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "lifeiscontent", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-04T18:38:35Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-30T19:45:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10832", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10835, "title": "How to import .css file on a per page/component basis?", "body": "I understand that we can import global CSS in `_app` and the built-in CSS module support. \r\n\r\nBut neither of these approaches solve my problem: I'm using a third party image gallery and it requires to import a `.css` file, but it's not good practice to import it in `_app` because the gallery is only used on one page. What's the best way to import it just for that page?\r\n\r\nRelated to #10665 ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 9, "reactions_total": 4, "reactions_plus": 4, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "chhuang", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T05:22:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-18T09:38:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10835", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10839, "title": "Where to store server data for every single request", "body": "Hello, guys!\r\nI want to store some data for every single request, so that I can using these data at other files, currently I stored at ctx\uff0cbut data crossover occurs when concurrent requests\uff0cso where is the best place to stay independent while concurrent requests?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Huai-Kang", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T09:27:43Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10839", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10841, "title": "dev, staging and prod on now", "body": "Hey, \r\nwhat is the best solution to get a setup where I can set different env variables for the different environments and can deploy them to different urls such as http://dev.my-domain.com, http://staging.my-domain.com...\r\n\r\nIdeally if i push the dev branch into the staging branch it will push it to http://staging.my-domain.com\r\n\r\nHow do you guys do it?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Manubi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T10:50:31Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-30T10:15:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10841", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10846, "title": "Next.js get started example is broken", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nI was attempting at following along with the next.js get started example located at https://nextjs.org/learn/basics/getting-started. However, the project cannot run or build due to \"Module Parse failed.\"\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nFollow along with the next.js get started example. I followed along exactly several different times and each time I received the same error\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nI expect the example index.js file to be served up correctly\r\n\r\n## Screenshots\r\n```npm run dev\r\n\r\n> tabs@1.0.0 dev C:\\Users\\forwein\\Documents\\localVenv\\tabs\r\n> next\r\n\r\n[ wait ]  starting the development server ...\r\n[ info ]  waiting on http://localhost:3000 ...\r\n[ ready ] compiled successfully - ready on http://localhost:3000\r\n[ wait ]  compiling ...\r\n[ ready ] compiled successfully - ready on http://localhost:3000\r\n[ event ] build page: /\r\n[ wait ]  compiling ...\r\n[ error ] C:/Users/forwein/OneDrive - CDW/Automation Resources/Python Scripts/venv/tabs/pages/index.js 3:6\r\nModule parse failed: Unexpected token (3:6)\r\nYou may need an appropriate loader to handle this file type, currently no loaders are configured to process this file. See https://webpack.js.org/concepts#loaders\r\n| export default function Index() {\r\n|     return (\r\n>       <div>\r\n|         <p>Hello Next.js</p>\r\n|       </div>\r\n[ event ] build page: /next/dist/pages/_error\r\n[ wait ]  compiling ...\r\n[ error ] C:/Users/forwein/OneDrive - CDW/Automation Resources/Python Scripts/venv/tabs/pages/index.js 3:6```\r\n\r\n```npm run build\r\n\r\n> tabs@1.0.0 build C:\\Users\\forwein\\Documents\\localVenv\\tabs\r\n> next build\r\n\r\nCreating an optimized production build\r\n\r\nFailed to compile.\r\n\r\n3:6\r\nModule parse failed: Unexpected token (3:6)\r\nYou may need an appropriate loader to handle this file type, currently no loaders are configured to process this file. See https://webpack.js.org/concepts#loaders\r\n| export default function Index() {\r\n|     return (\r\n>       <div>\r\n|         <p>Hello Next.js</p>\r\n|       </div>\r\n\r\n> Build error occurred\r\nError: > Build failed because of webpack errors\r\n    at build (C:\\Users\\forwein\\OneDrive - CDW\\Automation Resources\\Python Scripts\\venv\\tabs\\node_modules\\next\\dist\\build\\index.js:12:900)\r\nnpm ERR! code ELIFECYCLE\r\nnpm ERR! errno 1\r\nnpm ERR! tabs@1.0.0 build: `next build`\r\nnpm ERR! Exit status 1\r\nnpm ERR!\r\nnpm ERR! Failed at the tabs@1.0.0 build script.\r\nnpm ERR! This is probably not a problem with npm. There is likely additional logging output above.\r\n\r\nnpm ERR! A complete log of this run can be found in:\r\nnpm ERR!     C:\\Users\\forwein\\AppData\\Roaming\\npm-cache\\_logs\\2020-03-05T13_11_21_290Z-debug.log```\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: Windows 10\r\n- Browser (if applies) Node.js\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.2.2\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nI am just trying to follow along on the get started example and am running into this error. Some assistance would be greatly appreciated.\r\n\r\nThank you\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "LongBeachHXC", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T13:20:41Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T14:32:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10846", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10847, "title": "Can't run my nextjs app in Node server -- Unexpected identifier", "body": "I'm deploying a next app, but when I try to run it I get the following error:\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\r\n       ^^^^^\r\n\r\nSyntaxError: Unexpected identifier\r\n```\r\nIt seems as if Node is not identifying the code. I understand that that is why we have to use Babel, so Node can understand ES6 sintax. I don't think I have to add Babel configuration since I didn't have to do that when I was running my NextJS project locally, but I actually tried that anyway and got an error when trying to build my app.\r\n\r\nThis seems to be an issue related with the Node environment itself and not Next, but I was wondering if somebody has encounter something like this.\r\n\r\nI am deploying this site using Plesk, and it seems as if Plesk uses PhusionPassenger to serve the app. ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "cguidog", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T14:57:21Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-23T18:45:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10847", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10848, "title": "How to I use add a class to a react-spring animated component", "body": "I have tried to add a style to react spring animated component, but the classname was not transformed.\r\n\r\n<img width=\"256\" alt=\"Screenshot 2020-03-05 at 11 04 06 PM\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/990517/75994254-a5ee8880-5f35-11ea-8d72-6fd8ef4c67ab.png\">\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\nimport React from \"react\";\r\nimport { animated } from \"react-spring\";\r\n\r\nconst Test = () => {\r\n  return (\r\n    <div>\r\n      <animated.div className=\"container\">\r\n        <animated.div className=\"card\">a</animated.div>\r\n        <animated.div className=\"card\">b</animated.div>\r\n        <animated.div className=\"card\">c</animated.div>\r\n      </animated.div>\r\n      <style jsx>{`\r\n        .container {\r\n          position: fixed;\r\n          top: 0;\r\n          left: 0;\r\n          width: 100vw;\r\n          height: 100vh;\r\n          will-change: transform;\r\n          display: flex;\r\n          align-items: center;\r\n          justify-content: center;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        .card {\r\n          background-color: white;\r\n          background-size: auto 85%;\r\n          background-repeat: no-repeat;\r\n          background-position: center center;\r\n          width: 45vh;\r\n          max-width: 300px;\r\n          height: 85vh;\r\n          max-height: 570px;\r\n          will-change: transform;\r\n          border-radius: 10px;\r\n          box-shadow: 0 12.5px 100px -10px rgba(50, 50, 73, 0.4),\r\n            0 10px 10px -10px rgba(50, 50, 73, 0.3);\r\n        }\r\n      `}</style>\r\n    </div>\r\n  );\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport default Test;\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "andyto", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T15:04:46Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T06:56:22Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10848", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10851, "title": "catch all routes returns 404 in root", "body": "In the catch all [example](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/catch-all-routes) you have a structure like this `post/[...slug].js` and it says it can capture urls like `post/1`, `post/2`, but if you try to access `post/` directly as a root you get 404, I have a similar scenario like this and I want to be able to handle the root `post/` with the catch all route, I tried adding an `index.js` that is exporting `[...slug].js` but the issue with this is that generates 2 separate lambdas one for `index.js` and one for `[...slug].js` as well. How can I handle this?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "yanv1991", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T16:09:18Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-11T13:00:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10851", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10853, "title": "Add a flag that allows us to detect while a dynamic imported component is being retrieved", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\nAdd a static boolean to indicate whether a component is being dynamically retrieved or not. Even though there's a placeholder to render when the component is being retrieved, this information might be useful to transition other parts of the application, outside of that component\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nYes. I asked a similar question in [StackOverflow](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/60301416/detect-if-dynamically-imported-component-has-loaded-in-nextjs) and in [Spectrum](https://spectrum.chat/next-js/general/detect-if-dynamically-imported-component-has-loaded-in-nextjs~bbf084de-7bc6-4bb6-b9e8-bbb9e7f949a8) without any response\r\n\r\nMy specific problem is that I'm dynamically retrieving a component that renders, among other things, a google map component (this map can be toggled on and off). The layouts differ completely when the map is shown and not. When it is shown, some components are positioned on top of the map. When it's not shown, the layout is a regular css grid\r\n\r\nWhile I'm loading the map component, a spinner is shown (as part of the `loading` prop that `dynamic` offers), but given the layout changes, I see the components that are supposed to be on top of the map on top of the spinner. I would like to hide them while the map is being loaded.\r\n\r\nA more general problem is something like this\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport React, { useState } from 'react';\r\nimport dynamic from 'next/dynamic';\r\n\r\nconst DynamicImportedComponent = dynamic(() => import('Foo/baz'), {\r\n  ssr: false,\r\n  loading: () => <p>loading..</p>\r\n});\r\n\r\n\r\nconst MyComponent: React.FC = () => {\r\n  const [show, setShow] = useState(false)\r\n\r\n  const variableComponent = <SomeStyledComponent styled={show}>value of show is {show.toString()}</SomeStyledComponent>\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n    {variableComponent}\r\n    { show && <DynamicImportedComponent />}\r\n    <button onClick={() => setShow(!show)}>toggle</button>\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI would like to hide `variableComponent` while `DynamicImportedComponent ` is being retrieved (that is, while the component is being loaded, but not when it hasn't started to load, nor when it  has loaded).\r\n\r\nThat is because there's a style for `SomeStyledComponent` for when `show` equals false, and another for when `show` equals true and `DynamicImportedComponent` has loaded, but there's no sense on showing it while it's being retrieved \r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nA boolean flag that tells whether the component is being retrieved or not. That way I could just do\r\n\r\n```\r\n{(DynamicImportedComponent.loaded || !DynamicImportedComponent.isLoading) && variableComponent}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nSo far I haven't, I'm rendering the components on top of the spinner.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nSome screenshots of my specific problem\r\n\r\n#### When no map is shown\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/352474/76007678-2fc63380-5eed-11ea-8b8b-fb54bda4ac40.png)\r\n\r\n\r\n#### Map is being loaded - I'd like those cards in the bottom to be hidden in the meantime! (that football/soccer ball is my spinner while the map is being loaded \ud83d\ude04 )\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/352474/76007738-4a98a800-5eed-11ea-8c90-f21eb7fb6a9c.png)\r\n\r\n#### Map has loaded - we're good to go\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/352474/76007770-54baa680-5eed-11ea-89b6-251879d1e59a.png)\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 10, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "gndelia", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-05T17:29:40Z", "updated_at": "2023-12-26T09:12:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10853", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10854, "title": "getInitialProps throws error when my app launches", "body": "`getInitialProps` thows an error when my app launches. I am running a node server and nextjs app in a docker container. Here is my get func\r\n```\r\nIndex.getInitialProps = async(ctx)=> {\r\n  console.log('get initial props')\r\n\tconst API_URL = process.env.API_URL;\r\n\tconst INDEX_ENDPOINT = `${API_URL}/data/page/Welcome`;\r\n\tlet response = {};\r\n\ttry {\r\n\t\tresponse = await axios({\r\n            method: 'get',\r\n            responseType: 'json',\r\n            url: INDEX_ENDPOINT\r\n    });\r\n    if(response.status == 200){\r\n    console.log(response);\r\n    return { data: response.data[0] }     \r\n    }\r\n        \r\n  } catch(error){\r\n        return { error }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\nexport default withStyles(styles)(Index);\r\n```\r\nI only get data when I navigate from a `Link` component otherwise I get this error\r\n```\r\nget initial props\r\nfrontend    | {\r\nfrontend    |   classes: { root: 'Index-root-1' },\r\nfrontend    |   error: Error: connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:3018\r\nfrontend    |       at TCPConnectWrap.afterConnect [as oncomplete] (net.js:1137:16) {\r\nfrontend    |     errno: -111,\r\nfrontend    |     code: 'ECONNREFUSED',\r\nfrontend    |     syscall: 'connect',\r\nfrontend    |     address: '127.0.0.1',\r\nfrontend    |     port: 3018,\r\nfrontend    |     config: {\r\nfrontend    |       url: 'http://localhost:3018/data/page/Welcome',\r\nfrontend    |       method: 'get',\r\nfrontend    |       headers: [Object],\r\nfrontend    |       transformRequest: [Array],\r\nfrontend    |       transformResponse: [Array],\r\nfrontend    |       timeout: 0,\r\nfrontend    |       adapter: [Function: httpAdapter],\r\nfrontend    |       responseType: 'json',\r\nfrontend    |       xsrfCookieName: 'XSRF-TOKEN',\r\nfrontend    |       xsrfHeaderName: 'X-XSRF-TOKEN',\r\nfrontend    |       maxContentLength: -1,\r\nfrontend    |       validateStatus: [Function: validateStatus],\r\nfrontend    |       data: undefined\r\nfrontend    |     },\r\nfrontend    |     request: Writable {\r\nfrontend    |       _writableState: [WritableState],\r\nfrontend    |       writable: true,\r\nfrontend    |       _events: [Object: null prototype],\r\nfrontend    |       _eventsCount: 2,\r\nfrontend    |       _maxListeners: undefined,\r\nfrontend    |       _options: [Object],\r\nfrontend    |       _redirectCount: 0,\r\nfrontend    |       _redirects: [],\r\nfrontend    |       _requestBodyLength: 0,\r\nfrontend    |       _requestBodyBuffers: [],\r\nfrontend    |       _onNativeResponse: [Function (anonymous)],\r\nfrontend    |       _currentRequest: [ClientRequest],\r\nfrontend    |       _currentUrl: 'http://localhost:3018/data/page/Welcome',\r\nfrontend    |       [Symbol(kCapture)]: false\r\nfrontend    |     },\r\nfrontend    |     response: undefined,\r\nfrontend    |     isAxiosError: true,\r\nfrontend    |     toJSON: [Function (anonymous)]\r\nfrontend    |   }\r\nfrontend    | }\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "webface", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T18:29:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-23T18:31:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10854", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10855, "title": "Static asset requests to next.js trigger url slugs", "body": "I have an application that uses multiple url slugged routes i.e. `localhost:3000/[id]` and `localhost:3000/[id]/[otherId]` for example. \r\n\r\nI take these url slugs and make requests to my server with them. I'm getting a ton of server requests where the slugs are static assets so `id` for example will be `static` and `otherId` will be `favicon.ico`.\r\n\r\n**Logs in `_app.js`:**\r\n<img width=\"244\" alt=\"Screen Shot 2020-03-05 at 11 30 01 AM\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13311268/76018252-c4706780-5ed4-11ea-93bd-4eece0b38e93.png\">\r\n\r\n**Console output:**\r\n<img width=\"158\" alt=\"Screen Shot 2020-03-05 at 11 26 13 AM\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13311268/76018099-912dd880-5ed4-11ea-9f5c-d1b0a9850197.png\">\r\n\r\nI apologize in advance for the limited info, this is a closed source project but please let me know if you need more information", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Chrischuck", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-05T19:32:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T21:06:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10855", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10868, "title": "Optimizing app/reduce first, non-cached load time for mobile", "body": "As I'm relative new to react/nextjs, I'm wondering what options do I have to optimize my app, so any help is really apperciated, I'm happy to learn new stuff and things ;-) \r\n\r\nMy target groups are currently mobile users.\r\nAs I have a lot of repeating stuff over the pages, I am using the `next-redux-wrapper` and limited everything to the data that is required to be loaded.\r\nTo handle network issues, I am using additionally `next-offline` to provide a service worker.\r\n\r\nMy [website](https://shokz.grief.dev/) and the related [source code](https://gitlab.com/shokz.tv/web-v2/app)\r\n\r\nMy only concern is the first, non-cached, page load. For desktop everything appears to be fine, but looking over on mobile devices the page appears to be slow. I have frequently 4s+ load times as the load on the parsing/main-thread work takes too long.\r\n\r\nI have reduced the bundle size by over 50% the recent two days just by upgrading antd and removing any icons and replaced them with inline svg's. I have still a lot of weird stuff in my bundle that ships with antd but I am not using (I am using tree shacking), but I think that further reducing will not resolve the issue completely.\r\n\r\nI'am mostly wondering whether the opt out from `Automatic Static Optimization` is causing this issue? Is the optimization that good, that it can change over 4 seconds load time?\r\n\r\nI have another page which is using it (no redux) and it has skyrocking performance on mobile devices comparing to this website (I'm only talking about the first, non-cached load).\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "michaelketzer", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-06T14:46:57Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T09:58:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10868", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10870, "title": "`Syntax error in regular expression` on IE11 using polyfillsOptimization experimental flag", "body": "I have turned on the experimental feature `polyfillsOptimization`, and on IE11 the app crashes with the following exception:\r\n\r\n```\r\nSyntaxError: Syntax error in regular expression\r\n   at ln (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/runtime/polyfills.js?ts=1583511926236:10:21259)\r\n   at Anonymous function (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/runtime/polyfills.js?ts=1583511926236:10:26773)\r\n   at Anonymous function (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/runtime/polyfills.js?ts=1583511926236:10:23750)\r\n   at Anonymous function (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/runtime/polyfills.js?ts=1583511926236:10:26518)\r\n   at Anonymous function (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/development/pages/_app.js?ts=1583511926236:12117:3)\r\n   at ../../node_modules/next-i18next/node_modules/core-js/modules/_fix-re-wks.js (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/development/pages/_app.js?ts=1583511926236:12112:1)\r\n   at __webpack_require__ (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/runtime/webpack.js?ts=1583511926236:798:13)\r\n   at fn (http://10.0.2.2:3000/reise/_next/static/runtime/webpack.js?ts=1583511926236:151:13)\r\n   at ../../node_modules/next-i18next/\r\n```\r\n\r\nNot sure how I can help out debugging this, but I can provide more information if needed.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "stigkj", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-06T17:06:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-17T21:25:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10870", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10873, "title": "Differentiation between requests send from next server and from the browser", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nWhen running the application in docker, it's common to access the container using their names, either by having them on the same network or using the link option. \r\n\r\nFor the requests that don't go through SSR, the value of the environment variable defined with `publicRuntimeConfig` will also be used even when requests don't go through Nodejs. Not always the value defined there (for example, a backend URL) will be accessible from the host.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI would like to split the `publicRuntimeConfig` configuration option (or introduce a new) that allows different values to be configured, depending on if the request is going to be sent from the browser or from the next server for SSR.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\n- Use `docker inspect` on the container and pass as environment variable the IP, which is the same either from inside another container or from the host.\r\n- Add an alias to my container name on my `/etc/hosts` file\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "fabiocarneiro", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-06T18:34:51Z", "updated_at": "2021-03-09T21:49:47Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10873", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10874, "title": "_app and getServerSideProps", "body": "**Update:** We\u2019re excited to announce [Next.js 13](https://nextjs.org/13), which includes the new `app/` directory (beta) with support for nested layouts, colocation of data fetching, streaming, Server Components by default, and much more.\r\n\r\nInside the `app/` directory, there is a powerful new way to fetch data with React\u2019s `use()` hook and the extended Web `fetch()` API. This allows you to colocate data fetching directly inside components for the most flexibility, including inside layouts.\r\n\r\nYou can leave feedback here: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/41745", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 118, "reactions_total": 150, "reactions_plus": 98, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 8, "reactions_rocket": 15, "reactions_eyes": 29, "author": "unbelivr", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-06T18:45:12Z", "updated_at": "2023-10-05T20:38:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10874", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10879, "title": "API calls inside getInitialProps even it's static build <= not 100% static site??", "body": "Hi there,\r\n\r\nIn my front page, I have made query data from CMS and passed to the front page:\r\n\r\nBelow is the piece of code in my exportPathMap function:\r\n\r\n```\r\n        const res = await fetch(SOME_ENDPOINT);\r\n        const data = await res.json();\r\n        const paths = {\r\n            '/': { page: '/', query: { data  } }\r\n        };\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd in my Index.getInitialProps() function, I did the same API call and return it as the initial props:\r\n\r\n```\r\nIndex.getInitialProps = async (ctx) => {\r\n        const res = await fetch(SOME_ENDPOINT);\r\n        const data = await res.json();\r\n        return { data }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nCoz I have followed the tutorial from here: Fetching Data for Pages\r\nhttps://nextjs.org/learn/basics/fetching-data-for-pages\r\n\r\nI've checked the server request logs and found that during the page is rendered in server side, the API will be requested. It's fine. When jumping to other page and going back to the front page (using &lt;Link&gt; not &lt;a&gt;), the Index.getInitialProps() gets called again and so does the API endpoint call.\r\n\r\nIs this the normal usage? I think it's not 100% static. Suppose the API end point cannot be accessed for the public. It must be used during the build and export phase.\r\n\r\nThe index.html must be generated in early time and content should be generated to meet the SEO purpose. So what is the recommended and correct usage in my case?\r\n\r\nThanks!!!!!\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "codyng", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-07T04:14:41Z", "updated_at": "2026-02-05T17:41:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10879", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10886, "title": "Prod build not picking up new files in /public?", "body": "Sorry if I'm misusing nextjs for this: I got dynamically uploaded images in the /public folder but it looks the server needs a rebuild to deliver them (all works fine in dev). Any idea?\r\n\r\n**Version of Next.js**: 9.2.2", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "PlopTheReal", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-08T10:39:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-26T21:26:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10886", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10887, "title": "Caching api ", "body": "So how to do caching on api routes with nextjs. Currently i'm using apicache for my api routes but i dont think it's a good pratices. Is there anyway or documents how to cache the return of the api routes ?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Sangdt", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-08T11:08:05Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-17T16:17:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10887", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 25601, "title": "Ability to disable body parsing by default", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nCurrently it is not possible to disable body parsing by default.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nThe ability to add an option in `next.config.js` to disable body parsing for all API routes.\r\n\r\nOne of the possible solutions could be to add a boolean option named `parseBodyByDefault` to specify whether *Next.js* should parse the body of all requests or not.\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// next.config.js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  parseBodyByDefault: false\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nRight now the only alternative that I have found is to add the following code to all files under `pages/api`.\r\n\r\n```js\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    bodyParser: false\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 16, "reactions_plus": 16, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "agmm", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-08T18:01:17Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T10:38:11Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/25601", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10898, "title": "Issue of nextjs + material-ui generate different class names", "body": "Hi,\r\nI don't know what is the problem.\r\nWhen I use material-ui in _app.js with:\r\n```\r\nconst generateClassName = createGenerateClassName({\r\n    disableGlobal: true,\r\n    productionPrefix: 'someprefix'\r\n});\r\n<StylesProvider generateClassName={generateClassName}>......</StylesProvider>\r\n```\r\n\r\nand with Next.js's MyDocument.getInitialProps():\r\n\r\n```\r\n    const sheets = new ServerStyleSheets({\r\n        serverGenerateClassName: generateClassName\r\n    });\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt is because I have lots of pages defined in exportPathMap.\r\nI found that the classes name, e.g. the class name used in the header bar <header> is firstly kept the same even I browse another page. However, when I refresh the page (Cmd + R), that class name is different and so the page style is crashed. The incremented number is very very large. From e.g. someprefix5 -> someprefix245.\r\n\r\nWhat is the problem inside? Is it the configuration issue? I don't know if I need to configure it in material-ui side or next.js side.\r\n\r\nAny help is highly appreciated! Thanks!\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "codyng", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-09T14:49:46Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T10:22:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10898", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10905, "title": "Getting SSG circular reference errors starting in 9.3", "body": "I'm currently using Next 9.2.3 with `unstable_getStaticProps` with Contentful without any issues. I attempted to upgrade to 9.3 now that `getStaticProps` has been officially released.\r\n\r\nDoing so, however, gives me a `Reason: Circular references cannot be expressed in JSON.` without any code changes. My data does have some circular references in it, but I go out of my way using `safe-stringify` to remove them when the data is fetched from Contentful. \r\n\r\nWhat changed between 9.2.3 and 9.3? Is it possible to tell Next to ignore the result of the circular reference test if I'm sure the data is serializable, as it was in 9.2.3?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "sdornan", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-09T20:42:31Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-25T03:35:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10905", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10906, "title": "SCSS vars", "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nI just updated to 9.3 and I am testing.\r\nI want to share variables between global styles and components. I don't which is the right way and how the CSS will be generated.\r\n\r\nHere is the structure\r\n```\r\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 components\r\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Button.scss\r\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 styles\r\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 _variables.scss\r\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 index.scss\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd here is the file contents\r\n\r\n```scss\r\n// index.scss\r\n@import \"variables.scss\";\r\n```\r\n\r\n```scss\r\n// _variables.scss\r\n$color: blue;\r\n```\r\n\r\n```scss\r\n// Button.scss\r\n@import ../styles/variables.scss\r\n\r\nButton {\r\n  color: $color\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd I get the following error :\r\n\r\n```\r\nSassError: File to import not found or unreadable: ../styles/variables.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThanks for help", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "JiDai", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-09T21:13:21Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-03T17:36:55Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10906", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10908, "title": "Page is not hydrated after dynamic import", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\nI am trying to import a custom element written in Preact using dynamic import. The custom element is loaded into the DOM but the page does not seem to get hydrated.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\nIf you open the sandbox the page gets loaded, but it should print \"mounted\" in the console. It should also console if you Click on \"Click me\".\r\nhttps://codesandbox.io/s/nextjs-dynamic-import-issue-ylvsy\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\nLoading custom and javascript gets hydrated without errors.\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n- Version of Next.js: 9.2.3-canary.28 (also tried 9.2.2)\r\nPreact: 10.3.3\r\nReact: 16.13.0\r\n\r\nHow do you recommend loading an external custom element inside a .js/.ts file?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "secretlifeof", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-09T21:25:05Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-03T16:02:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10908", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10909, "title": "Where to statically fetch site-wide data?", "body": " (9.3.0) I have data that my entire site relies upon (nav, footer) that I really only want to fetch at build time. Is there a way to do this within _app.js. From what I understand, `getStaticProps` doesn't work at the component level or within `_app`", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "closed", "state_reason": "resolved", "comments": 15, "reactions_total": 25, "reactions_plus": 25, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tylermcrobert", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-09T21:30:31Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-04T02:05:14Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10909", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10911, "title": "Combine SSG with Current Data Fetching?", "body": "I\u2018m thinking about a use case where I want to use SSG for high-traffic landing pages for SEO and for reducing the server load, but still want current data to be used after hydration, and on client side routing to this page. Would that be possible?\r\n\r\nSo basically on client side routing to this page, the behavior should be like getInitialProps (current data is fetched before the page becomes visible). And on server side routing to this page, the static html should be served and hydrated, and then (optionally) some api responses fetched to update some data on the page.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "fabb", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-09T22:04:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-22T09:32:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10911", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10913, "title": "How to get Docker working with Next.js + HMR for local development?", "body": "I can't for the life of me figure out the proper setup for running `next` in Docker, so that file changes are actually watched and reloaded. Has anyone manged to get this working?\r\n\r\nFor reference, I have the Docker setup below:\r\n\r\n**docker-compose.yaml**\r\n```\r\nversion: '3.6'\r\n\r\nservices:\r\n  postgres:\r\n    image: postgres:12\r\n    restart: always\r\n    volumes:\r\n      - db_data:/data\r\n    env_file:\r\n      - .env\r\n\r\n  graphql-engine:\r\n    image: hasura/graphql-engine:v1.2.0-beta.2\r\n    ports:\r\n      - '8080:8080'\r\n    depends_on:\r\n      - 'postgres'\r\n    restart: always\r\n    env_file:\r\n      - .env\r\n\r\n  nextjs:\r\n    container_name: nextjs\r\n    build: .\r\n    ports:\r\n      - '3000:3000'\r\n    restart: always\r\n    env_file:\r\n      - .env\r\n    volumes:\r\n      - ./nextjs:/app\r\n      - /app/node_modules\r\n\r\nvolumes:\r\n  db_data:\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Dockerfile**\r\n```\r\nFROM node:12.16.1-stretch\r\n\r\nENV APP_HOME=/app\r\n\r\nRUN mkdir $APP_HOME\r\nWORKDIR $APP_HOME\r\n\r\nCOPY . /app\r\nRUN yarn\r\n\r\nEXPOSE 3000\r\n\r\nCMD [\"yarn\", \"next-dev\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd get this error log:\r\n\r\n```\r\nnextjs            | yarn run v1.22.0\r\nnextjs            | error Couldn't find a package.json file in \"/app\"\r\nnextjs            | info Visit https://yarnpkg.com/en/docs/cli/run for documentation about this command.\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "xndyz", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-09T22:27:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T11:22:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10913", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10914, "title": "Offtopic - What are GitHub community \"discussions\"?", "body": "What is this new \"discussions\" tab? I've never seen this github feature, it seems to be a beta feature, would you have any github official documentation about it? (I'm interested for other projects)\r\n\r\nAlso, is it meant to replace Spectrum community? Or are you just trying things out?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Vadorequest", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-09T22:41:34Z", "updated_at": "2023-06-02T22:50:01Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10914", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10915, "title": "SASS Include Paths 9.3.0", "body": "Previously it was possible to include paths for sass like this:\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst withSass = require('@zeit/next-sass');\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = withSass({\r\n  cssModules: true,\r\n  sassLoaderOptions: {\r\n    includePaths: ['./src/styles']\r\n  }\r\n});\r\n```\r\nHow can that be done now with 9.3.0?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ivoilic", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T00:48:51Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-29T09:51:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10915", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10916, "title": "Best practice to deploy/release changes with SSG & SSR on zeit/now", "body": "Hi Guys,\r\n\r\nI read through the new exciting 9.3 release and I am wondering how I would get these goodies into my current SSR rendered website kit (Storyblok CMS + NextJS). What would be the best practice to get this seamlessly integrated into project - for example to run a `build` task if a CMS admin decides to bring changes online and hit a `ready to release` button somewhere in the headless CMS. I want to continue using zeit/now for my websites so I am curious how that would look like.\r\n\r\nThanks for any hints!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dohomi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T01:33:18Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T11:41:08Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10916", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10917, "title": "why webpack alias doesn't work as expected in nextjs?", "body": "I add an alias @ to the dir `src`, but can't import files like `import {Router} from \"@/routes\"`, can anybody help me please? here is a quick [demo](https://github.com/videni/nextjs-examples.git).\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst Dotenv = require('dotenv-webpack');\r\nconst path = require('path');\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  useFileSystemPublicRoutes: false,\r\n  webpack: (config, options) => {\r\n    config.plugins.push(\r\n      new Dotenv({\r\n        path: path.join(__dirname, '.env'),\r\n        systemvars: true,\r\n      }),\r\n    );\r\n\r\n    config.resolve.alias = {\r\n      ...config.resolve.alias,\r\n      '@': path.resolve(__dirname, 'src'),\r\n    };\r\n\r\n    return config;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n'@' doesn't work in src/server/index.ts, because this file is not packed by webpack.how can we make this work?\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport { createServer } from 'http';\r\nimport next from 'next';\r\nimport routes from '@/routes';\r\n\r\nconst port = parseInt(process.env.PORT, 10) || 3000;\r\nconst dev = process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production';\r\nconst app = next({ dev });\r\nconst handler = routes.getRequestHandler(app);\r\n\r\napp.prepare().then(() => {\r\n  createServer(handler)\r\n    .listen(port, err => {\r\n      if (err) throw err;\r\n      console.log(`> Ready on http://localhost:${port}`);\r\n    });\r\n});\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n''@\" also doesn't work  in src/pages/_app.tsx which is supposed to be packed by webpack, why \"@\" can't be resolved ?\r\n```\r\n\r\nimport { AppProps } from 'next/app'\r\nimport { RelayEnvironmentProvider } from 'relay-hooks';\r\nimport createEnvironment from '@/relay/createEnvironment';\r\n\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "videni", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T02:09:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-16T22:55:48Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10917", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10920, "title": "getServerSideProps: more server load than getInitialProps?", "body": "From the documentation I understand that `getServerSideProps` causes more requests to the next server:\r\n\r\n> When you request this page on client-side page transitions through next/link (documentation), Next.js sends an API request to server, which runs getServerSideProps. It\u2019ll return a JSON that contains the result of running getServerSideProps, and the JSON will be used to render the page.\r\nhttps://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/data-fetching\r\n\r\nI would need to measure, but it sounds like this could make scaling up the next servers necessary on high-traffic websites, going along with higher costs.\r\n\r\nI understand that it provides different benefits, like smaller response payloads, and not having to have isomorphic code in that function. In the end it\u2018s a tradeoff.\r\n\r\nSince [`getInitialProps` is now discouraged](https://nextjs.org/docs/api-reference/data-fetching/getInitialProps#recommended-use-getstaticprops-or-getserversideprops-instead), will users of the framework be able to decide which end of the tradeoff they want to use in the future?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "fabb", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-10T05:39:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-23T13:18:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10920", "classification": "need-related"}
