{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10924, "title": "Scss variables in custom webpack configuration", "body": "Hi, \r\nfor my project i needed to pass a variable from webpack to scss which i managed to do in v9.2.2 but since updating to 9.3.0 it doesn't seem to work. This is my current code:\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\nwebpack(config) {\r\n\r\n        let isDev = ENVIRONMENT !== 'production';\r\n\r\n        config.module.rules.forEach(rule => {\r\n\r\n            if (rule.test && rule.test.toString().includes('.scss')) {\r\n\r\n                rule.use.forEach(el => {\r\n\r\n                    if (el.loader && el.loader === 'sass-loader') {\r\n\r\n                        el.options.data = `$isDev: ${isDev};`;\r\n                    }\r\n                })\r\n\r\n            }\r\n        });\r\n\r\n        return config;\r\n    }\r\n</pre>\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tankpower1", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T07:44:22Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-18T17:59:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10924", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10925, "title": "How to use getServerSideProps in a HOC for authentication", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nThere are examples of how to perform authentification using cookies for one page (like the example [with-cookie-auth](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-cookie-auth/pages/profile.js)), however, in the real world, when we use auth we also use it on many different pages.\r\n\r\nI tried to play with the _app to catch getServiceSideProps (but that's not possible) or even the getInitialProps (never get called) with today's release of NextJS, but can't find any solution.\r\n\r\nIdeally, I would love to do this in a HOC. I would use it only for my pages requiring authentification. That HOC would also update my global state. My problem is that I can find a way to make a HOC on which the getServiceSideProps get called. Is there a way?\r\n\r\nThanks a lot :)", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 69, "reactions_total": 115, "reactions_plus": 53, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 2, "reactions_hooray": 2, "reactions_confused": 2, "reactions_heart": 2, "reactions_rocket": 6, "reactions_eyes": 48, "author": "jordymeow", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T08:24:28Z", "updated_at": "2023-07-24T16:53:28Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10925", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10926, "title": "React ssr(next)-getInitialProps insert a reactNode to props render", "body": "In next(react ssr), I try to render a page use like this\r\n```javascript\r\n// this common element, so much project use this, in my private node_modules\r\nconst Header = ({ result }) => <div>{result}</div>;\r\n\r\nconst renderComponent = async () => {\r\n  // mock some request\r\n  const result = await Promise.resolve(10);\r\n  return <Header result={result} />;\r\n};\r\n\r\nstatic getInitialProps = async ctx => {\r\n\r\n  return {\r\n    Element: await renderComponent()\r\n  }\r\n\r\n}\r\n\r\nrender({Element}){\r\n  console.log(Element)\r\n  return Element\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n* In server side, correct render and console is\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n{ '$$typeof': Symbol(react.element),\r\n  type: [Function: Header],\r\n  key: null,\r\n  ref: null,\r\n  props: { result: 10 },\r\n  _owner: null,\r\n  _store: {} }\r\n```\r\n\r\n* In webside dom render correct one time, but soon throw error to empty page, console is\r\n```javascript\r\n{key: null, ref: null, props: {\u2026}, _owner: null, _store: {\u2026}}\r\nkey: null\r\nref: null\r\nprops: {result: 10}\r\n_owner: null\r\n_store: {}\r\n__proto__: Object\r\n```\r\nso, how to keep correct to render the common element.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "li2568261", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T08:29:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-15T06:02:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10926", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10928, "title": "How to include components when rendering on the server?", "body": "**Qquestion description**:\r\n\r\n  If I'm using a **third-party component** with a global style, All third-party components are bundle into **chunks**.\r\n\r\n<br/>\r\n\r\n**Example**:\r\n\r\n  1. I have a component library [styled-jsx-lib-app](https://github.com/unix/styled-jsx-lib-app) (demo)\r\n\r\n```jsx\r\nconst BaseCssComponent = () => {\r\n  return (\r\n    <div>\r\n      <style global jsx>{`\r\n        html {\r\n          font-size: 30px;\r\n        }\r\n      `}</style>\r\n    </div>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n  2. After running `next dev`, you can see that the **global style** is directly rendered:\r\n\r\n![styled-jsx-lib-rendered](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/11304944/76296371-047a8480-62f1-11ea-9d5b-a1a9b8c46db6.png)\r\n\r\n\r\n  3. When I publish the library to NPM and use it in another project: [styled-jsx-user-app](https://github.com/unix/styled-jsx-user-app):\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport { BaseCssComponent } from 'styled-jsx-lib-app'\r\n\r\nconst Home = () => (\r\n  <div>\r\n    hello, I am using library `styled-jsx-lib-app`.\r\n    <BaseCssComponent />\r\n  </div>\r\n)\r\n```\r\n  4. This third-party library will always be in the chunk:\r\n\r\n![styled-demo-2](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/11304944/76296789-b4e88880-62f1-11ea-8774-dda21bd11a29.png)\r\n\r\n<br/>\r\n\r\n**I Want**:\r\nI want to include this **base style component** when rendering on the server,  what should I do now?\r\n\r\n<br/>\r\n\r\n**Some of the solutions I've tried**:\r\n  \r\n  1. Publish `.css` file to npm, and import in the `_app.js` of `next.js`. This works, but I still want to publish a **component** if possible.\r\n\r\n  2. Use `flush` from `styled-jsx/server` in `_document.jsx`. Not work, `flush` always return `[]`.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "unix", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T09:18:35Z", "updated_at": "2023-07-19T18:14:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10928", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10930, "title": "NextJS v9.3 performance of getServerSideProps vs getInitialProps", "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nSo I've created a little demo to show-case all the different ways NextJS can do page-rendering: [csr, ssr, ssg].\r\nYou can find that demo here: https://static-next.willemliu.now.sh/\r\nCode here: https://github.com/willemliu/static-next\r\n\r\nIn the NextJS documentation the following recommendation can be found:\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/974906/76304751-332e3600-62c4-11ea-8b85-881134dd967a.png)\r\n\r\nHowever I've found a significant performance penalty in using `getServerSideProps` vs `getInitialProps`\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/974906/76304863-653f9800-62c4-11ea-912f-be2670c0be19.png)\r\n\r\nYou can check for yourself by navigating between `SSR` and `Hybrid-SSR` in the demo mentioned above. `SSR` implements `getServerSideProps` and `Hybrid-SSR` implements `getInitialProps`.\r\nBoth do exactly the same.\r\n\r\n```\r\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context: any) {\r\n    const res = await fetch(\r\n        \"https://static-next.willemliu.now.sh/api/test\"\r\n    ).then((res) => res.json());\r\n    console.log(\"getServerProps\", res, context.params, context.query);\r\n    return {\r\n        props: { ...res, name: context.query.slug }\r\n    };\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\nIndex.getInitialProps = async (context: any) => {\r\n    const res = await fetch(\r\n        \"https://static-next.willemliu.now.sh/api/test\"\r\n    ).then((res) => res.json());\r\n    console.log(\"getInitialProps\", res, context.params, context.query);\r\n    return { ...res, name: context.query.slug };\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt seems to me that the penalty comes from the extra roundtrip to the lambda `getServerSideProps` of that page upon navigation. Where with `getInitialProps` the client-side code runs `getInitialProps` and does the data fetching directly.\r\n\r\nKnowing this, my question is what are the benefits of implementing `getServerSideProps` vs `getInitialProps`?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 29, "reactions_total": 25, "reactions_plus": 23, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 2, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "willemliu", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T10:48:55Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T07:23:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10930", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10931, "title": "How to stop page reload errors (_error.jsx)", "body": "Hello, is it possible to display _error.jsx without reloading the entire page? Apparently it has been patched, but I still have this problem.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "youhosi", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-10T12:53:04Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-13T21:41:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10931", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 10935, "title": "How can I use Next.js over HTTPS instead of HTTP?", "body": "I want to make a request to an API, however,  it must be done over HTTPS. I want to configure next so that the app is running at https://localhost:3000 and not http://localhost:3000. Can someone help me with this?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 187, "reactions_total": 135, "reactions_plus": 135, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "m-abdelwahab", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-10T15:25:23Z", "updated_at": "2025-08-07T17:05:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/10935", "classification": "need-related"}
