{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11027, "title": "navigator is not defined", "body": "When trying to import `@uppy/webcam` in next.js, so React with SSR, I get the following error: `ReferenceError: navigator is not defined`\r\n\r\nThis is my code\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport Uppy from '@uppy/core'\r\nimport { Dashboard } from '@uppy/react'\r\nimport Webcam from '@uppy/webcam'\r\nimport { FC } from 'react'\r\n\r\nconst Upload: FC<Props> = ({ name }) => {\r\n  const uppy = Uppy().use(Webcam)\r\n\r\n  return <Dashboard uppy={uppy} plugins={['Webcam']} />\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default Upload\r\n\r\ninterface Props {\r\n  name: string\r\n}\r\n\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "BjoernRave", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-13T00:05:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-30T23:54:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11027", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11036, "title": "http-proxy-middleware can't handle POST request in next.js", "body": "> next.js version: 9.3.1-canary.1\r\n\r\nI want to build middle tier server with next.js, so it must proxy all request with `api/*`.\r\n\r\nTo achieve this, I add file `pages/api/[...args].js` to an empty project, and write these simple code:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nimport { createProxyMiddleware } from \"http-proxy-middleware\";\r\nexport default createProxyMiddleware({\r\n  target: \"http://yuanjin.tech:5100\", //the data server\r\n  changeOrigin: true\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter testing, all get requests works well, but all POST requests has problems\r\n\r\n```shell\r\n# get request workds well\r\nGET http://localhost:3000/api/movie\r\n```\r\n\r\n```shell\r\n# post request has problems\r\nPOST http://localhost:3000/api/user/login\r\nBODY loginId=xxx&loginPwd=xxx\r\n\r\n# console info\r\nAPI resolved without sending a response for /api/user/login, this may result in stalled requests.\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen this error occurs, my data server does not actually receive any requests\r\n\r\nThen i have found any request with body has same problem, even GET request\r\n\r\nI don't know why the proxy request can't reach the data server, did i miss something?\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 15, "reactions_total": 10, "reactions_plus": 10, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "yjisme", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-13T04:23:28Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-16T16:39:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11036", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11043, "title": "MDX Improvements", "body": "Hey,\r\n\r\nI've built (and building) some fairly large static documentation websites which have both manual and auto generated API reference content. Right now I'm using Gatsby, which involves GraphQL - it's a pain to work with due to the complex nature of the auto-generated content & poses some limitations.\r\n\r\nI believe the latest Next update could get around those issues, however from my research, I don't believe the MDX aspect to quite powerful enough:\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n1)\r\n\r\nI use `gatsby-plugin-mdx` which allows me to convert the Markdown, however unlike NextJS, I can manually inject the content into a component using the `MDXProvider`, e.g:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n<MDXProvider components={components}>\r\n  <MDXRenderer>{mdx.body}</MDXRenderer>\r\n</MDXProvider>\r\n```\r\n\r\n`mdx.body` is the MDX parsed string from the plugin, and `components` is my custom object of global components which the MDX uses to render into HTML.\r\n\r\nIs there a way of having such functionality with NextJS? I'd like to take the content of the MDX output and use it inside of a React component like above, rather than Next automatically generate pages.\r\n\r\nI'm personally not a fan of putting layout inside of my MDX, hence the need to pass it as a prop to a custom component.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n2) \r\n\r\nI take advantage of frontmatter quite a lot, however currently it seems this is lost. I realise I could export an object, however to me this doesn't feel \"markdown\" like, especially considering the content can be edited by the public.\r\n\r\nUsing Gatsby, I can access the frontmatter as an object during the creation of the pages.\r\n\r\nI've seen https://github.com/hashicorp/next-mdx-enhanced, which I need to look into more, however I'm not sure whether it addresses point 1 still.\r\n\r\nCheers", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Ehesp", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-13T09:56:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T13:09:08Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11043", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11045, "title": "How to export static /pages/page.js to be served as /page.html (not /page)", "body": "I am trying to export static site to a set of `*.html` files. They can later be served by any HTTP server. The problem is that from a client view everything works, and requests to `/somepage` result in fetching `/somepage.html`. But if I refresh such page to get it from server directly, I have 404 error unless I add the html extension like `/somepage.html`.\r\n\r\nIn same cases it is possible to instruct a server to try known extensions like `html` or `htm` for requests without extension (MultiViews for Apache). Sometimes it is not possible. Is there any recommended way to refer to pages with full names (with `.html` extensions) when exporting a static site?\r\n\r\nPS. Sufficient option could be to generate files without extension, that is, not `page.html`, but just `page`. How?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "osnwt", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-13T11:28:26Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-22T10:47:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11045", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 64630, "title": "Caching headers are presented in Dev mode while missing in Prod mode", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n`Cache-control` header is presented in response when working in `dev` mode. In `prod` mode this header is missing. I was also trying to set headers manually using custom express server before sending a response `res.set(\"Cache-control\", \"no-store, must-revalidate\")` but it didn't make any change.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\nSteps to reproduce the behavior:\r\n1. `yarn create next-app`\r\n2. `yarn dev` - check response headers\r\n3. `yarn build`, `yarn start` - check response headers\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n`Cache-control` header is presented in both `dev` and `prod` environments.\r\n\r\n## Screenshots\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13063421/76617831-c7500580-652f-11ea-9ff6-76a19bcab503.png)\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13063421/76617984-1eee7100-6530-11ea-8e1e-eeada3cf1671.png)\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "SZharkov", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-13T11:44:03Z", "updated_at": "2024-04-17T11:53:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/64630", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11052, "title": "How is the build optimized with `getStaticPaths` and `getStaticProps`?", "body": "If I use graphQL for instance inside the `getStaticPaths` only, will it get bundled into the production anyways even if it's never used somewhere else at runtime?\r\nThe same thing for  `getStaticProps` ofc, with the exception, that modules should get loaded when preview mode is entered, right?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "closed", "state_reason": "outdated", "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "josias-r", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-13T13:29:34Z", "updated_at": "2023-05-20T13:24:57Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11052", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11056, "title": "Tutorial, getInitialProps, and HOC", "body": "Hi all,\r\n\r\nI'm following along the NextJS tutorial and I'm trying to make sense of getInitialProps.  I have an HOC/Layout file that looks like this:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst withLayout = Page => {\r\n    return () => (\r\n    <div style={layoutStyle}>\r\n        <Header />\r\n        <Page/>\r\n    </div>\r\n)};\r\n\r\n\r\nexport default withLayout;\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd an index page that looks like this:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst Blog = (props) => {\r\n    return (\r\n        <>\r\n            <h1>Batman TV Shows</h1>\r\n            <ul>\r\n                {props.shows.map(show => (\r\n                    <li key={show.id}>\r\n                        <Link href=\"/p/[id]\" as={`/p/${show.id}`}>\r\n                            <a>{show.name}</a>\r\n                        </Link>\r\n                    </li>\r\n                ))}\r\n            </ul>\r\n        </>\r\n    );\r\n};\r\n\r\nBlog.getInitialProps = async function () {\r\n    const res = await fetch('https://api.tvmaze.com/search/shows?q=batman');\r\n    const data = await res.json();\r\n\r\n    console.log(`Show data fetched. Count: ${data.length}`);\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n        shows: data.map(entry => entry.show)\r\n    };\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default withLayout(Blog);\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis returns an error that key shows doesn't exist in the props object. I'm sure it's something to do with the HOC but i'm not sure what the fix is. ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "awestbarrett", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-13T16:27:27Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-11T12:14:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11056", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11058, "title": "core-js@<3 is no longer maintained and not recommended for usage due to the number of issues", "body": "I just installed Next.js and got this warning. Can I just ignore it? Is it planed to update this in an upcoming version?\r\n\r\nThank you.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "theprobugmaker", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-13T17:09:29Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-16T13:47:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11058", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11060, "title": "How to test metadata using jest and react library test", "body": "Hi, \r\n\r\nWe are using Next-SEO for metadata and structured data in our project, and I was wondering if anyone could help me to write a unit test to verify those elements.\r\n\r\nCheers,", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 31, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "najandz", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-13T20:23:38Z", "updated_at": "2023-10-25T12:34:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11060", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11072, "title": "Can't find a way to properly redirect in 9.3", "body": "I'm attempting to build this flow with Next.js 9.3, and am unable to find a solution:\r\n\r\n1. A user logs in, and a non-HTTP-only cookie is set\r\n2. When the user clicks a logout link or visits /logout, the cookie is cleared, and the user is redirected\r\n\r\nThe login part works fine, as does clearing the cookie. My first implementation was adding this to my otherwise non-rendering `LogoutPage`:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nexport async function getServerSideProps(ctx) {\r\n  ctx.res.setHeader('set-cookie', 'token=; max-age=0')\r\n  ctx.res.writeHead(302, {Location: '/login'}).end()\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis actually works fine, and the cookie is cleared and a redirect occurs, but every time it runs, there's an error in the console `TypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object` caused by [this line](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/packages/next/next-server/server/render.tsx#L576) which expects `getServerSideProps` function to return an object containing a `props` object.\r\n\r\nIs there a better way to write this flow? My current workaround is just to use a custom server, which feels like overkill for such a minor thing, and it requires a real page refresh. Is that what I should be doing?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jclem", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-14T14:21:07Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T12:54:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11072", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11073, "title": "[Styled Components] How can we leverage the new SSG methods to export static style sheets at build time? ", "body": "The current way to do server side rendering in Next js requires creating a _document.jsx file in the pages directory, it uses Next's getInitialProps method which runs at each request.\r\n\r\nCan we use the new functionality in 9.3 to create our <styles> at build time.\r\n\r\nThis would allow the app to render for the first time without any flickers(the app initially loads without any styles and CSS is injected later).", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "heyitsarpit", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-14T17:47:46Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-05T06:56:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11073", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11079, "title": "Advantages & disadvantages of automatically concatenating global css into a single .css", "body": "I was glancing through css documentation for global css https://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/built-in-css-support#adding-a-global-stylesheet and there is a section there mentioning following:\r\n\r\n> In production, all CSS files will be automatically concatenated into a single minified .css file.\r\n\r\nI wanted to start discussion and ask why this is done, as I was under assumption that due to http2 it is better to keep them split into more files. Or is my assumption wrong and for css it makes sense to keep it as one file?\r\n\r\nAlso, I wanted to check if we already have some sort of setting that can disable this and keep css files as separate, if not, should this be considered?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "xzilja", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-15T06:20:30Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-31T14:40:26Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11079", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11080, "title": "Image pre-processing with SSG", "body": "Gatsby has pretty great support for pre-processing images at build time in order to eg use SVG traces or convert to better compression formats (webp):\r\nhttps://www.gatsbyjs.org/docs/preprocessing-external-images/\r\n\r\nI wrote a quick and dirty example of how to do this in Next.js at build time through `getStaticProps`. \r\nhttps://github.com/wbunting/with-image-preprocessing\r\nhttps://with-image-preprocessing.now.sh/\r\n\r\nIt takes an unsplash image url and saves it to a temp directory where it can run some conversions / optimizations, then stores the result webp in the `public/` directory and inlines the svg by passing through static props. \r\n\r\nThis also avoids having to add any specific webpack loaders for images since you just generate everything statically at build time. \r\n\r\nIt could use some cleaning up, but wanted to see if folks though it was useful, and if so if we should clean up / move into the `examples` repository. ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 20, "reactions_plus": 8, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 2, "reactions_confused": 1, "reactions_heart": 5, "reactions_rocket": 2, "reactions_eyes": 2, "author": "wbunting", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-15T07:12:49Z", "updated_at": "2025-01-05T14:52:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11080", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11086, "title": "Up to date guide for using AWS Amplify?", "body": "Hi,\r\nDoes anyone know of an up to date guide to using AWS Amplify and NextJS? Is it even possible with GraphQL and (Cognito) Auth. I've gotten a variety of errors from css issues with aws-amplify-react to not being able to figure out how to have public and logged in pages. \r\n\r\nAny guides, tips, pointers are appreciated.\r\n\r\nThanks.\r\n\r\nJulio\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "JulioBarros", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-15T19:35:21Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-25T16:45:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11086", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11087, "title": "How does the releases work for Next.js?", "body": "I saw that it has a lot of pre-release for a minor release (v9.3.1 for example), I was wondering how the releases work for Next.js", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "theprobugmaker", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-15T20:15:01Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-07T02:08:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11087", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11088, "title": "Instant Page Navigation", "body": "I have a next.js project with quite a lot of pages. My homepage fetches some events' json and renders them (~ 10 cards), all fetched client-side.\r\n\r\nI have this annoying problem: When I navigate through `next/link` from homepage to another page, say \"profile.js\" page, It's almost fast and OK. But when I click on home link, it gets a little stuck and takes some time to render the page.\r\n\r\nFirst it hangs (about 2 seconds), Then it starts showing the loading, then it shows the page.\r\nI can somehow manage this by clearing the state of redux on homepage's unmount. But that's not what I want. Because I really want the \"App-like\" experience.\r\n\r\nHow can I achieve this?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "pooriamo", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-15T20:26:26Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T16:01:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11088", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11090, "title": "Why the socket.IO example uses socket on the _app.js?", "body": "I installed the socket.IO example and I noticed that the socket instance is made in two places, on the hook and also on the `_app.js` the thing is that whenever I import the hook it will make a connection automatically and it will also make a connection because of `_app.js` (the server receives 2 connections), what is the reason to use in both places?\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "theprobugmaker", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-15T23:07:47Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-30T13:32:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11090", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11093, "title": "YC W20 company needs help! Deployments to Google Cloud fail with multiple instances.", "body": "I'm deploying a next.js to Google Cloud app engine. Whenever I deploy the project to AE with multiple instances, it throws this error: net::err_aborted 404 for one of the core files.\r\n\r\nAny advice? This is a huge problem.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "brennanerbz", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T02:44:27Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-20T14:43:19Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11093", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11099, "title": "Serve automatically generated static files with Nginx", "body": "Hi\r\n\r\nUsing `next build`, the automatically generated static files reside in `/project/.next/server/static/${BUILD_ID}/page.html`. \r\n\r\nThe great thing is that we can write `try_files` for this file and serve it if present, But the problem is that the `BUILD_ID` is varied build to build and nginx doesn't know about it.\r\n\r\nIs there a good way to handle this? \r\nIs using `try_files` the best option for this?\r\n\r\nThanks ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 23, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "pooriamo", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T10:08:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-06T10:04:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11099", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11101, "title": "How to import per page css without modules", "body": "Hi, so currently i have an issue with importing css from node_modules only where i need it.\r\nIn my `_app.js` i import the global css, but i need to import a css file from a package in node_modules only in a certain page which is rarely opened. Without using modules i have no other way but to import it also globally in `_app.js` and thats a problem since the css is quite big and loads on every page. So my question is how can i `import 'node_modules/package/index.css'` only on the page i need it to without using modules. Using next-css works but breaks some of the features of the canary release im on (9.3.1-canary.6).\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 5, "reactions_plus": 5, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tankpower1", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T11:56:16Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11101", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11102, "title": "Static assets list", "body": "Hello all, I've been searching on the docs and couldn't find anything related to this so decided to open a question, so please consider the following scenario:\r\n\r\nIf I wanted to create a endpoint on my next.js app that exposes the assets the apps is using, on a per build basis, what would be the recommend way to do this? \r\n\r\nI noticed - correct me If I'm wrong - that all the assets used on client side are under the `.next/static` folder, and that there is a file `build-manifest.json` that list some of the js used on client side. However, since this is not considered a public facing API, I feel like going for an approach where we sniff this out might create problems in the future, if for however reason this structures changes.\r\n\r\nPlease advise if there is an official way to do this, and if not, is it something I can create a feature request for?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "reabreu", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T12:05:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-08T13:26:14Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11102", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11104, "title": "Next 9.3 Locale getStaticPaths", "body": "Hi all\r\n\r\nI'm trying to figure out how to implement `getStaticPaths` with an CMS  where all urls is something i can get from the CMS so i can use benefits of `getStaticPaths` and catch all route. But i am struggling with how to set this up.\r\n\r\nWhat i want is all urls like this to hit the same file in the pages folder `/pages/[...slug].js`:\r\n```\r\n/    (this should somehow redirect to the correct language /en or /de url)\r\n/en\r\n/en/somepage\r\n/en/somepage/otherpage\r\n/de\r\n/de/somepage\r\n/de/somepage/otherpage\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js \r\n// file: pages/index.js:\r\n\r\nexport { default } from './[...slug]'\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js \r\n// file: pages/[...slug].js:\r\n\r\nexport async function getStaticPaths() {\r\n  // These params will be fetched from CMS, but for now they are hardcoded\r\n  return {\r\n    paths: [\r\n      { params: { slug: ['en'] } },\r\n      { params: { slug: ['en', 'somepage'] } },\r\n      { params: { slug: ['en', 'somepage', 'otherpage'] } },\r\n      { params: { slug: ['de'] } },\r\n      { params: { slug: ['de', 'somepage'] } },\r\n      { params: { slug: ['de', 'somepage', 'otherpage'] } },\r\n    ],\r\n    fallback: false,\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\nIf i do this on localhost, it actually works. But i keep getting error 500 and the page is full reloading when changing pages.\r\n\r\n![Screen Recording 2020-03-16 at 13 54 36](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/4134473/76760460-ff548400-678d-11ea-9741-974c90ff4da9.gif)\r\n\r\nWhen building it seems like it thinks the frontpage (/) and the [...slug] page is 2 different pages. But else everything looks fine\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/4134473/76760519-172c0800-678e-11ea-9288-90b4a5996ede.png)\r\n\r\n\r\nCan anyone link me to examples where something like this is working with v9.3?` And if you have a solution to the redirect to correct language i will be glad :) \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "TommySorensen", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T13:06:42Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-08T06:52:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11104", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11105, "title": "Persistent layouts", "body": "A while back @timneutkens alluded to forthcoming guidance from @Timer on the proper way to do persistent layouts (https://twitter.com/timneutkens/status/1185174317883105280). Has that been published somewhere?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 4, "reactions_plus": 4, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "1derwood", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T13:13:46Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T15:14:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11105", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11106, "title": "[RFC] .env environment support", "body": "## Goals\r\n\r\n- Add dotenv (`.env`) support with the same `.env` file loading semantics as Create React App: https://create-react-app.dev/docs/adding-custom-environment-variables/#what-other-env-files-can-be-used\r\n- Provide a way to easily expose environment variables to the browser\r\n\r\n## Background\r\nOver the last few months we've had a lot of feedback from companies running into confusion around how to handle environment variables.\r\n\r\nThe main feedback has been mostly:\r\n\r\n- It's unclear how / where to load environment variables in development.\r\n    - For this reason most tend to fall back to using `dotenv` (almost 50% of Next.js apps).\r\n- It's unclear when / if a environment variable is exposed to the browser\r\n- It's unclear how to expose environment variables to the browser\r\n\r\n## Proposal\r\n\r\n### Node.js-only environment variables\r\nCurrently you can already access `process.env` in the Node.js environment only. Meaning that environment variables are private by default. Next.js never inlines environment variables unless you specify to do so under the `env` key.\r\n\r\nAn example of this is:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// pages/index.js\r\nimport fetchData from '../lib/api'\r\n\r\nexport async function getStaticProps() {\r\n  const data = await fetchAPI('https://example.com/api/v1/posts', {\r\n    token: process.env.ACCESS_TOKEN\r\n  })\r\n  return {\r\n    props: {\r\n      data\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default function HomePage({ data }) {\r\n  return <div>\r\n    <h1>{data.title}</h1>\r\n  </div>\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis would also cover the Server \u2192 Browser handoff, as an environment variable could be returned in the data fetching methods to expose it:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// pages/index.js\r\nimport fetchData from '../lib/api'\r\n\r\nexport async function getStaticProps() {\r\n  return {\r\n    props: {\r\n      token: process.env.ACCESS_TOKEN\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default function HomePage({ token }) {\r\n  return <div>\r\n    <h1>This value was exposed to the browser {token}</h1>\r\n  </div>\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// pages/api/api-route.js\r\nexport default function ApiRoute(req, res) {\r\n  console.log(process.env.ACCESS_TOKEN) // Logs out the ACCESS_TOKEN variable \r\n  return res.json({ hello: 'world' })\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n### .env loading\r\nEnvironment variables will be loaded from `.env` files, using the same loading mechanism as Create React App: https://create-react-app.dev/docs/adding-custom-environment-variables\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n```\r\n# .env.local\r\nACCES_TOKEN=abcdefgh\r\n```\r\n\r\nWill set `ACCESS_TOKEN` to `abcdefgh` \r\n\r\n### Global public environment variables\r\nSometimes you need to configure something globally. A good example of this is the Sentry DSN key. To allow for this you can prefix global environment variables with `NEXT_PUBLIC_` in order to make it globally available in both node.js and the browser. Note that this will expose the variable to the browser when used, so it can't be used for secrets.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nOverall these changes should simplify usage of environment variables for the majority of use cases.", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 85, "reactions_total": 87, "reactions_plus": 59, "reactions_minus": 2, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 13, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 3, "reactions_rocket": 4, "reactions_eyes": 6, "author": "timneutkens", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-16T13:55:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T08:03:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11106", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11108, "title": "How to better organize (GET/POST/PUT) in NextJS API Routes?", "body": "This is how I am currently accounting for different types of API requests.\r\n\r\n/pages/api/user.js\r\n```js\r\nexport default (req, res) => {\r\n  if (req.method === 'POST') {\r\n    // Process a POST request\r\n  } else {\r\n    // Handle any other HTTP method\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt works fine but the file can get quite large after adding a bunch of stuff, most of the time unrelated.\r\n\r\nHas anyone been working with a better organizational structure? ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Gr8z", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T15:02:19Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-12T17:25:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11108", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11110, "title": "MDX and <Link>", "body": "It seems next-mdx does not turn `[]()` into <Link> to facilitate client-side transitions.\r\n\r\nIt also seems to be causing a bug where any link that's clicked loads the `/` page, even though the address bar contains the correct desired route.\r\n\r\nIs this a misconfiguration, or a bug, or is there something I'm missing?\r\n\r\nUsing latest 9.3, reduced everything to a simple index page and the issue persists.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Southclaws", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T15:49:30Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-11T12:19:47Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11110", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11112, "title": "Not understanding AMP-first unoptimized version", "body": "In https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/docs/api-reference/next/amp.md#L47 it is stated, that \"The page has an user-accessible (optimized) version of the page and a search-engine indexable (unoptimized) version of the page\".\r\n\r\nHow can I access the unoptimized version? How does this work? Is it dependant on user-agent or just not having JS enabled?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tjwelde", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T16:25:12Z", "updated_at": "2021-03-15T11:06:08Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11112", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11113, "title": "How to ignore a folder when running \"next build\"", "body": "When we run \"next build\" in our production server, it takes +20mins for the process to finish.\r\nThe problem for this delay is that we have a folder in the public/ directory called \"uploads\" and it has about 30,000 folders and files. This is our project hierarchy:\r\n\r\n- ... other folders\r\n- public/\r\n   - ... other folders\r\n   - uploads/     #### << here\r\n      - ... (huge number of files)\r\n- pages/\r\n\r\nis there a way to prevent the build command from going through each folder and file inside the uploads folder because they are only used for serving static pictures and assets.\r\n\r\nmaybe adding an argument inside \"next.config.js\" to exclude specific folders?\r\n\r\nthank you for your time!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 46, "reactions_total": 32, "reactions_plus": 21, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 11, "author": "andy-asi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T16:50:55Z", "updated_at": "2025-07-29T03:23:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11113", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23936, "title": "Allow setting custom permalink", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nUsing Next and Next Export is a great way to generate a static site. Unfortunately, it is not possible to generate non HTML static assets. Adding a custom permalink would allow non important non HTML files. Examples include:\r\n\r\n- RSS (feed.xml)\r\n- robots.txt\r\n- Sitemap (sitemap.xml)\r\n- Netlify CMS Config (admin/config.yml)\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nAdd a function or parameter to set the custom permalink for a page. We could also consider making the render function of the React component return a string for the body.\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nYou can generate these files outside of the Next.js build ecosystem but that seems like a lot of extra complexity to the developer experience.\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nThis idea was discussed in #9051 ", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 9, "reactions_plus": 9, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "pspeter3", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T17:00:41Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-07T17:58:08Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23936", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11116, "title": "SWR and getserversideprops", "body": "\r\n\r\nGoal: SSR page that also works as a SPA page that responds to a next/link. Is it good practice to use SWR with getsserversideprops (as it was with getinitialprops) or how?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 11, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jesperordrup", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T17:33:34Z", "updated_at": "2024-05-01T19:41:44Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11116", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11119, "title": "Debugging serverless functions in \"next dev\"", "body": "```\r\nNODE_OPTIONS=\"--inspect\" node_modules/.bin/next dev\r\n```\r\n\r\nI have `.ts` modules in `pages/api` and `next dev` (v9.3.0) is evaluating them as expected, but breakpoints are not being respected. Has anyone else figured this out?\r\n\r\nMight need to tweak the webpack config to support source maps for the compiled serverless functions? **edit:** Hmm, [guess not](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/packages/next/build/webpack-config.ts#L290).", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aleclarson", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-16T19:13:11Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-21T14:18:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11119", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11122, "title": "Why does the custom app and document examples not use the new SGG methods?", "body": "So in your custom app and document examples, the getInitialProps method is used. But the two new methods (getStaticProps and getServerSideProps) is preferred instead of getIntialProps when using next js version 9.3 or newer. So what is the \"new right\" way to implement server-side data fetching in the custom document and app component using next js 9.3?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Axedyson", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-16T20:41:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-23T15:54:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11122", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23929, "title": "Override which query parameter determines \"amp mode\"", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\nCurrently, for hybrid pages, I *think* `?amp=1` is the only way to tell next to do all the amp goodness.  Wondering if it's a good idea to be able to override this behavior.  If there is another way please let me know!  Is there somewhere we can rewrite query params more generally?\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nWe have various apps that are all behind the same CDN / Origin that use a different URL parameter to know whether to render amp or non amp.  Not all of these applications use next and in general we'd like to be able to customize the behavior of next to continue using our internal conventions that differ from `?amp=1` without handling nextjs traffic too differently.\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nNew to looking at this code in too much depth, but it looks like this bit here would have to change.  I'm not sure whether this is best to be configured in the userConfig (`next.config.js`) or on the pageConfig.\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/packages/next/next-server/server/render.tsx#L458\r\n\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nI think we could probably solve this problem by doing url rewrites etc. at our cdn / nginx or whatever to hide this bit of nextjs behavior from the rest of our stack but I'd like to be able to use the same url scheme's in development that we do in production without jumping through hoops.\r\n\r\nWilling to try PR'ing this if it sounds like a good idea.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dan-weaver", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-16T22:54:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-03T14:49:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23929", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11125, "title": "Trying to find with-jest-react-testing-library example", "body": "Seems like it once existed from a merge:\r\nhttps://github.com/zeit/next.js/commit/<HASH_REDACTED>#diff-<HASH_REDACTED>\r\n\r\nbut now I don't see it in the repo anymore.\r\n\r\nI see it in this forked repo: https://github.com/Megoos/next.js/tree/update-with-jest/examples/with-jest-react-testing-library\r\n\r\nIs there a reason it was removed from next.js repo?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "apardo04", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T03:56:24Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-12T16:49:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11125", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11127, "title": "Static 404 errors in different languages.", "body": "Although I see well that you force to always generate 404.html statically, sometimes as in my case I need it to be dynamic because the content depends on the language, do you know any way to do it easily?\r\n\r\nAnother thing, I have realized that it is generating two `404.html` files, one in the `out `root and another in the `out/404/404.html` folder, do you know what is due?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "chiqui3d", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T06:39:40Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-27T06:28:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11127", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11132, "title": "Exported modules returns undefined when used react's syntax", "body": "I'm using **next.js** and **Typescript**, everything compiles fine, when I try to follow any page I see in console (*SSR* from next) errors which occurred by undefined imports.\r\n\r\nI got that any *.tsx* file that contains react-syntax (`<div />`) returns undefined if imported as module. And even if I don't export the component and try to export something else (like `string`) it still returns undefined.\r\n\r\nIf I use statefull component and return `null` from renderer - it works fine and module returns function.\r\n\r\nMy **.babelrc**:\r\n\r\n    {\r\n      \"presets\": [\r\n        \"next/babel\",\r\n        \"@zeit/next-typescript/babel\"\r\n      ],\r\n      \"plugins\": [\r\n        \"babel-plugin-styled-components\",\r\n        [\r\n          \"@babel/plugin-proposal-decorators\",\r\n          { \"legacy\": true }\r\n        ],\r\n        [\r\n          \"@babel/plugin-proposal-class-properties\",\r\n          { \"loose\": true }\r\n        ],\r\n        [\r\n          \"module-resolver\",\r\n          {\r\n            \"alias\": {\r\n              \"Components\": \"./build/components\",\r\n              \"UI\": \"./build/uikit\",\r\n              \"Utils\": \"./build/utils\",\r\n              \"Hooks\": \"./build/hooks\",\r\n              \"Contexts\": \"./build/contexts\"\r\n            }\r\n          }\r\n        ]\r\n      ],\r\n      \"env\": {\r\n        \"test\": {\r\n          \"plugins\": [\r\n            \"@babel/plugin-transform-typescript\",\r\n            [\r\n              \"module-resolver\",\r\n              {\r\n                \"alias\": {\r\n                  \"Components\": \"./src/components\",\r\n                  \"UI\": \"./src/uikit\",\r\n                  \"Utils\": \"./src/utils\",\r\n                  \"Hooks\": \"./src/hooks\",\r\n                  \"Contexts\": \"./src/contexts\",\r\n                  \"Models\": \"./src/models\",\r\n                  \"Mocks\": \"./src/__mocks__\"\r\n                }\r\n              }\r\n            ]\r\n          ]\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n\r\nMy **tsconfig.json**:\r\n\r\n    {\r\n        \"compilerOptions\": {\r\n            \"typeRoots\": [\"./node_modules/@types\", \"./src/**/*.d.ts\"],\r\n            \"lib\": [\"dom\", \"dom.iterable\", \"esnext\"],\r\n            \"target\": \"es5\",\r\n            \"module\": \"commonjs\",\r\n            \"moduleResolution\": \"node\",\r\n            \"outDir\": \"./build\",\r\n            \"emitDecoratorMetadata\": true,\r\n            \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\r\n            \"esModuleInterop\": true,\r\n            \"sourceMap\": true,\r\n            \"jsx\": \"preserve\",\r\n            \"skipLibCheck\": true,\r\n            \"pretty\": true,\r\n            \"baseUrl\": \"./\",\r\n            \"paths\": {\r\n                \"Components/*\": [\"./src/components/*\"],\r\n                \"Utils/*\": [\"./src/utils/*\"],\r\n                \"Hooks/*\": [\"./src/hooks/*\"],\r\n                \"UI/*\": [\"./src/uikit/*\"],\r\n                \"Contexts/*\": [\"./src/contexts/*\"],\r\n                \"Models/*\": [\"./src/models/*\"],\r\n                \"Mocks/*\": [\"./src/__mocks__/*\"]\r\n            },\r\n            \"allowJs\": true,\r\n            \"strict\": true,\r\n            \"forceConsistentCasingInFileNames\": true,\r\n            \"noEmit\": false,\r\n            \"resolveJsonModule\": true,\r\n            \"isolatedModules\": true\r\n        },\r\n        \"include\": [\"./src/**/*\"],\r\n        \"exclude\": [\"./node_modules\"]\r\n    }\r\n\r\n**next.config.js** is regular one, nothing used to extend functionality.\r\n\r\nPreviously everything worked fine, but I've integrated eslint instead of tslint and had much corrections.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aezakmi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T09:46:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T03:48:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11132", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11133, "title": "CSS and JS file per page", "body": "When I generate my static html pages, I see that both on the home page and in the privacy policy, it is adding the same js and css files, is there any way to control that?\r\n\r\nI apologize if this is a very basic question.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "chiqui3d", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T11:12:28Z", "updated_at": "2023-04-16T11:51:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11133", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11134, "title": "getStaticProps called at runtime for the first time?", "body": "According to the docs:\r\nhttps://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/data-fetching#when-should-i-use-getstaticprops\r\n\r\n> The data required to render the page is available at build time ahead of a user\u2019s request.\r\n\r\nIt sounds like that `getStaticProps` should fetch the data at build time.\r\n\r\nIn the beginning, there is no corresponding data (json) in the `build` folder for a page. On the first request (while running with `next start`), I go and fetch it from my data source first, populate a file then only after that, the subsequent requests will use this file.\r\n\r\nI have more details on the issue, but first I would like to clarify if this is intentional behavior, or I do something wrong? In the latter case, I can try creating a reproduction example.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "balazsorban44", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-17T13:30:38Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-27T20:38:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11134", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11137, "title": "when does the getInitialProps of _app run?", "body": "I have an app that is using _app.js getInitialProps to fetch user info to render inside of a header. That works good on the first render of the app however if I logout of that user and login with a different user getInitialProps isn't run again so the previous users data is rendered. This seems like there is some sort of caching strategy with the custom app page that I haven't been able to find documented anywhere. Anyone know why this behavior is occuring?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tylerthehaas", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T14:27:22Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-03T13:33:01Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11137", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11138, "title": "undefined req.cookies", "body": "I'm trying to make a request to the express/nodejs backend using nextjs\r\n\r\nin pages/reader.js, I have\r\n\r\n```\r\nReader.getInitialProps = async ({query}) => {\r\n    const res = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/books/reader/' + query.id);\r\n    const json = await res.json();\r\n    return {book: json}\r\n};\r\n```\r\nUnfortunately, that overwrites the cookies stored in the request object on the backend. When I do a `console.dir(req.cookies)` in the backend node js, express code, I get undefined in book.js where the reader code is.\r\n\r\nHow can I fetch without overwriting the request object in the express backend?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "naissa12", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T14:37:01Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-30T20:27:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11138", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11140, "title": "Does anyone miss Classic MVC here  ?", "body": "\r\nHello guys.\r\nI found ` getServerSideProps` allows us to conveniently use next.js as a view engine. \r\n\r\nDo you think this is useful?\r\n\r\n``` js\r\n// fastify server.js\r\nconst fastify = require('fastify')({ logger: { level: 'error' } })\r\nconst Next = require('next')\r\n\r\nconst port = parseInt(process.env.PORT, 10) || 3000\r\nconst dev = process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production'\r\n\r\nfastify.register((fastify, opts, next) => {\r\n  const app = Next({ dev })\r\n\r\n  fastify.decorateReply('view', async function(template, data) {\r\n    this.res.viewdata = data\r\n    return await app.render(\r\n      this.request.req,\r\n      this.res,\r\n      template,\r\n      this.request.query\r\n    )\r\n  })\r\n\r\n  app\r\n    .prepare()\r\n    .then(() => {\r\n      // use next.js as a view engine\r\n      fastify.get('/a', async (req, res) => {\r\n        const data = { msg: 'Make MVC Great Again.' }\r\n        return await res.view('/a', data)\r\n      })\r\n\r\n      if (dev) {\r\n        fastify.get('/_next/*', (req, reply) => {\r\n          return app.handleRequest(req.req, reply.res).then(() => {\r\n            reply.sent = true\r\n          })\r\n        })\r\n      }\r\n\r\n      fastify.all('/*', (req, reply) => {\r\n        return app.handleRequest(req.req, reply.res).then(() => {\r\n          reply.sent = true\r\n        })\r\n      })\r\n\r\n      fastify.setNotFoundHandler((request, reply) => {\r\n        return app.render404(request.req, reply.res).then(() => {\r\n          reply.sent = true\r\n        })\r\n      })\r\n\r\n      next()\r\n    })\r\n    .catch(err => next(err))\r\n})\r\n\r\nfastify.listen(port, err => {\r\n  if (err) throw err\r\n  console.log(`> Ready on http://localhost:${port}`)\r\n})\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// next.js page\r\nimport React from 'react'\r\n\r\nexport const getServerSideProps = ({ res }) => {\r\n  return { props: res.viewdata || {} }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default ({ msg }) => <h3>{msg}</h3>\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "timedtext", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T15:18:35Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T05:50:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11140", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11142, "title": "How to create many static pages that have client side functionality and update them?", "body": "I have about 40,000 movie. each movie has it's own dedicate page. with url like this:\r\nhttps://example.com/movie/1\r\nthat dedicated to movie number one.\r\nmost data of this page are static. title, image, movie info, people comments and ... .but some data are about user like: user name, profile image, is he/she liked this movie?, is he/she bought this movie? and so on.\r\nI have 2 big challenge here:\r\n\r\n1.  how to create static pages that have static info (movie info) and then get user info in client side.\r\n2. if one movie added or one movie updated, how to add or update only that page? and not build all of them?\r\n\r\nthanks for your patience.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "MohammadPCh", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T15:41:38Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11142", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11143, "title": "Next.config.js not loaded in Storybook (and probably in other 3rd party tools)", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nNext.config.js `env` and runtime configs are not taken into account in Storybook stories. It makes code dependant on `next/config` difficult to test.\r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\n\r\nFull repro here: https://github.com/eric-burel/with-storybook-config-repro\r\n\r\n- init storybook example\r\n```sh\r\nnpm init next-app --example with-storybook with-storybook-app\r\n```\r\n- add `next.config.js` at root\r\n```js\r\nmodule.exports = (nextConfig = {}) => {\r\n  return {\r\n    env: { \"foo\":\"bar\"},\r\n    publicRuntimeConfig: { \"hello\":\"world\"}\r\n };\r\n};\r\n```\r\n- add following code in the demo story, eg welcome\r\n```js\r\nimport getConfig from \"next/config\"\r\nconsole.log(\"getConfig\", getConfig())\r\nconsole.log(\"process.env.foo\", process.env.foo)\r\n```\r\n- run storybook\r\n```\r\nyarn run storybook\r\n```\r\n- see browser console, variables are undefined\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\n`next.config.js` should be somehow taken into account, at least for the relevant part.\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- Version of Next.js: 0.3\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\n\r\nI work with TypeScript and I've tried to add a `.babelrc` with next/babel config.\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "eric-burel", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-17T15:58:22Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-30T18:07:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11143", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11147, "title": "You can not use getInitialProps with getServerSideProps. Please remove getInitialProps.", "body": "Hi,\r\nI have a HoC named withAuth which is gonna check the authentication for some pages.\r\nfirst how can I write a hoc with getServerSideProps function? if can't, so what's the solution to have hoc which uses getInitialProps that wraps a page has got  getServerSideProps?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 17, "reactions_plus": 17, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "saeedPadyab", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-17T17:47:32Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-30T18:30:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11147", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11156, "title": "window is undefined", "body": "I have the following page\r\n```\r\n\r\nif (document.cookie) {\r\n   loginLink = \"xxxx\";\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default () => (\r\n    <div className=\"header\" id=\"header\">\r\n        <a href=\"/\" className=\"logo\"></a>\r\n        <a href=\"/books\">Explore</a>\r\n        <a href=\"/search\">Search</a>\r\n        <a href=\"/pricing\">Upgrade to Premium</a>\r\n        <a href=\"/teams\">Teams</a>\r\n        <a href=\"/blog\">Blog</a>\r\n        {loginLink}\r\n        <a href=\"#\" className=\"icon\" onClick={toggleMobile.bind(this)}>\r\n            <div className=\"line\"></div>\r\n            <div className=\"line\"></div>\r\n            <div className=\"line\"></div>\r\n        </a>\r\n        <div className=\"clear\"></div>\r\n    </div>\r\n)\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nI get an error that document.cookie when creating Next.js pages. Same for window.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "naissa12", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T01:17:42Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-03T05:42:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11156", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11158, "title": "Is there a way to fetch static / local data at build time on each page (_app / _document)?", "body": "I want to make use of the static optimization to generate a full static website (without SSR). I have some function that loads some data and I would like to have it available on every page (or maybe mutate some context?). Is there a way to do that in nextjs? `getStaticProps` does not seem to work on _app or _document.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ConsoleTVs", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T01:44:38Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-09T15:26:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11158", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11162, "title": "How to export multiple withCss withPwa, etc.. in next.config.js", "body": "```javascript\r\nconst nextConfig = withPwa(\r\n  {\r\n    pwa: {\r\n      dest: \"public\"\r\n    }\r\n  },\r\n  withCSS(\r\n    withSass({\r\n      webpack(config, options) {\r\n        config.module.rules.push(\r\n          {\r\n            test: /\\.(png|jpg|gif|svg|eot|ttf|woff|woff2)$/,\r\n            use: {\r\n              loader: \"url-loader\",\r\n              options: {\r\n                limit: 100000\r\n              }\r\n            }\r\n          },\r\n          {\r\n            test: /\\.(ogg|mp3|wav|mpe?g)$/i,\r\n            loader: \"file-loader\",\r\n            options: {\r\n              name: \"[path][name].[ext]\"\r\n            }\r\n          }\r\n        );\r\n        return config;\r\n      }\r\n    })\r\n  )\r\n);\r\nmodule.exports = nextConfig;\r\n```\r\n\r\nhow can I export multiple statements like this, but with some configuration between them?\r\n\r\nfor example, I wanna use all these HOF and export them, but also I wanna add an object option in withPwa(), how should I do that? Compose those plugins!!!! without 3rd party libraries\r\n\r\nThanks in advance!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "yaohuangguan", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T04:05:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T08:57:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11162", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11171, "title": "SEO optimizable routes with dynamic routing", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nWhat I'm trying to accomplish is with dynamic routing have a substructure as so:\r\n\r\nFull path - for each subroute we would need a seperate page (specific page component as well)\r\n`/[lang]/[overview-slug]/[brand]/[model]/[id]`\r\n- Note that overview-slug is language specific\r\n\r\n`/nl/overzicht`\r\n`/nl/overzicht/audi`\r\n`/nl/overzicht/audi/a3`\r\n`/nl/overzicht/audi/a3/12430213-123-123123-123`\r\n\r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nprevious question\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nUsed to work with `next-routes`, but currently unable to export\r\n\r\n## Additional context\r\nBasic repository for reproduction: \r\nhttps://github.com/lizy-lab/lizy-basic-project\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "BramDecuypere", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T13:07:19Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-11T09:07:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11171", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11172, "title": "How to use Lingui with SSR?", "body": "Related to #11147 \r\n\r\nGiven the following requirements:\r\n- Fetch API data before build to enable SSR\r\n- Translate static content based on currently selected language\r\n\r\nHow can I make Lingui work in this scenario? \r\n\r\nThe official example [with-lingui](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/master/examples/with-lingui) works by wrapping a HOC around static routes. However, I need to use `getServerSideProps` which doesn't work with either `getInitialProps` or `getStaticProps`, as noted in #11147 above.\r\n\r\nI tried moving the catalog import to `getServerSideProps`, serializing the data (getInitialProps serializes by default), parsing it again and then passing it to a local `I18nProvider`, but the translations stop working because the data's not actually unserialized correctly.\r\n\r\nCode sample:\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport React from 'react'\r\nimport { GetServerSideProps } from 'next'\r\nimport { I18nProvider } from '@lingui/react'\r\nimport { Trans } from '@lingui/macro'\r\n\r\n// API\r\nimport fetch from 'isomorphic-unfetch'\r\n\r\n// Types\r\nimport { Catalogs } from '@lingui/core'\r\n\r\ntype TLanguage = 'en' | 'fr' | 'nl'\r\n\r\ninterface IProps {\r\n  data: any\r\n  language: TLanguage\r\n  catalogs: Catalogs\r\n}\r\n\r\nconst TestPage: React.FC<IProps> = ({ data, language, catalogs }) => {\r\n  console.log('data', data)\r\n  console.log('language', language)\r\n  console.log('catalogs', catalogs)\r\n\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <I18nProvider language={language} catalogs={JSON.parse(catalogs)}>\r\n        <Trans>Some random text</Trans>\r\n        <Trans id=\"some.random.id\" />\r\n      </I18nProvider>\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport const getServerSideProps: GetServerSideProps = async ctx => {\r\n  const queryLang =\r\n    ctx.query && typeof ctx.query.lang === 'string' && ctx.query.lang\r\n\r\n  const language = queryLang || 'nl'\r\n  const catalog = await import(`../src/locales/${language}/messages.po`).then(\r\n    m => m.default\r\n  )\r\n\r\n  const url = 'https://swapi.co/api/people/1'\r\n  const res = await fetch(url)\r\n  const data = await res.json()\r\n\r\n  return {\r\n    props: {\r\n      data,\r\n      language,\r\n      catalogs: {\r\n        [language]: JSON.stringify(catalog)\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default TestPage\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nI've been going in circles for hours and can't really find any examples of how it's supposed work. Any help would be appreciated, thank you!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "wolfpilot", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T13:08:29Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-17T23:13:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11172", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11174, "title": "Pipe `next dev` into `pino-pretty` and keep colorized output?", "body": "We have a `yarn dev` script like this:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"scripts\": {\r\n    \"dev\": \"next | pino-pretty -t -c -s \\\"level > '30'\\\"\"\r\n  }\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `-c` flag of pino-pretty means \"colorize output\", but I still don't see the colors:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"657\" alt=\"Screenshot 2020-03-18 at 16 08 22\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/101152/76975510-edf8ac80-6932-11ea-9f9c-89893c053794.png\">\r\n\r\n\r\nVs. plain `next dev` which is colorful:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"657\" alt=\"Screenshot 2020-03-18 at 16 09 42\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/101152/76975518-f18c3380-6932-11ea-9d23-20fae400308d.png\">\r\n\r\nIs it `next` CLI that's not sending the escape sequences when piped into `pino-pretty`?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "borekb", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-18T15:12:08Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T11:53:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11174", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11175, "title": "Dynamic Page with persisted Redux", "body": "Thanks for an amazing library!\r\nI'm new to next.js so I'm looking to start a discussion for what's the best way to solve this:\r\n\r\nI'm using persisted redux using `next-redux-wrapper` with localStorage and until now most components connected to redux with `mapStateToProps` and selected data using `reselect`.\r\n\r\nWhat I have now is a dynamic article page which should be SEO friendly but which can also show client specific info if the user is logged in like _save article_ / _article already saved_.\r\n\r\n1. How can I properly return the page info using `getServerSideProps` when landing on the page, but when using transitions from other pages use the data that is already available in redux store (get articleId from query and use a selector for getting data and pass it to props with `mapStateToProps`) and not let next.js make a new request like it is mentioned in the [docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/data-fetching#only-runs-on-server-side)?\r\n1.1 Also if the user has already logged in and the data is persisted in localStorage, how can I get that data instead of making a new request on server side?\r\n2. If I have thousands of article pages and also if I want to create client side features (_save article_) is it feasible to use SSG (getStaticProps and getStaticPaths)?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "vladfulgeanu", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T15:30:02Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-06T21:04:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11175", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11178, "title": "Dynamic Clean URL shows a 404 error on Reload", "body": "As the title says, the dynamic URL works when redirected after a click, but when the dynamic page is reloaded, it shows a 404 error(after deployment on Netlify).\r\n\r\nTried deploying it on Zeit, and it works!\r\nI guess I need to configure something for proper deployment in Netlify. Kindly help :)\r\n\r\nThe Clean URL defeinition:\r\n<img width=\"369\" alt=\"ScreenShot_20200318225203\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/47635607/76988841-69765000-696b-11ea-8d9e-d3ec51b88636.png\">\r\n\r\nThe dynamic page:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"268\" alt=\"ScreenShot_20200318225139\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/47635607/76988898-81e66a80-696b-11ea-9e5f-e1174b47cdc2.png\">\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Xtremilicious", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T17:32:44Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T20:33:14Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11178", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11179, "title": "SSG: Consume internal API routes inside getStaticProps", "body": "Is it possible the consume an internal api of a Next.js project (e.g.: `pages/api/post.js`) inside `getStaticProps`? If yes; how?\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n\r\n```js\r\nexport async function getStaticProps(context) {\r\n  // The following url must be absolute\r\n  // but what is the correct origin/port? \r\n  const url = '/api/post'\r\n\r\n  return {\r\n    props: await fetch(url).then(res => res.json())\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nBefore (in `getInitialProps`) we had the `Request` object of node (`ctx.req`) that allowed us the retrieve this URL from some headers. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "HaNdTriX", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-18T17:45:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T05:48:15Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11179", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11180, "title": "Handle data fetching errors in getStaticProps/getStaticPaths", "body": "I have the following `getStaticProps` function:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nexport const getStaticProps = async (ctx) => {\r\n    try {\r\n        const {slug} = ctx.params\r\n        const url = Array.isArray(slug) ? slug.join(\"/\") : slug\r\n        const token = await getToken()\r\n        const result = await getData(url, token)\r\n\r\n        if (result.errors) {\r\n            throw result.errors\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        if (!result.data) {\r\n            throw {message: \"No data\"}\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        return ({props: result.data})\r\n\r\n    } catch (error) {\r\n        error.ctx = ctx\r\n        logErrorToExternalLoggingService(error, \"Fetching initial props failed\")\r\n        return ({props: {}})\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn addition, in `getStaticPaths`, I set `fallback` to `true`, so as discussed in #11134, `getStaticProps` should first run at runtime, not at build time.\r\n\r\nNow if the API fails this first time, the way I handle it now, props will not be populated correctly, and all subsequent requests will receive an empty object. Is there a way to get around this? Somehow tell getStaticProps that if something failed at the first request, the next time it should try again fetching the data, instead of returning the empty object?\r\n\r\nCan you recommend any other way to solve this?\r\n\r\nIf it has any importance, when a request comes, I create an ApolloClient instance to fetch the data using, the `url`.\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 10, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "balazsorban44", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-18T18:55:40Z", "updated_at": "2023-09-04T19:26:00Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11180", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11183, "title": "app_.getInitialProps with page getServersideProps", "body": "I know that `_app.getServersideProps` doesn't exist yet, but is there a way to get `_app.getInitialProps` to correctly pass props to the `App` if a page defines `getServersideProps`? Simple illustration:\r\n\r\n```tsx\r\n// _app.tsx\r\nconst MyApp = ({ Component, pageProps }: AppProps) => {\r\n  const { viewer } = pageProps\r\n  console.log('viewer', pageProps)\r\n\r\n  return (\r\n    <UserProvider viewer={viewer}>\r\n      <Component {...pageProps} />\r\n    </UserProvider>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n\r\nMyApp.getInitialProps = async (appContext: AppContextType<Router>) => {\r\n  const appProps = await App.getInitialProps(appContext)\r\n  appProps.pageProps.viewer = { id: 'monkey' }\r\n  return { ...appProps }\r\n}\r\n\r\n// index.tsx\r\nconst IndexPage: NextPage = ({viewer}: IndexPageProps) => {\r\n  return (\r\n    <h1>OK</h1>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\nThis prints `viewer { id: 'monkey' }` as expected. However, if I add getServersideProps to IndexPage:\r\n\r\n```tsx\r\n// index.tsx\r\nexport const getServerSideProps: GetServerSideProps = async () => {\r\n  return { props: {} }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt now prints `viewer undefined`\r\n\r\nI presume the result of `getServersideProps` is being used as the page props, but I don't understand the order in which things execute, and/or whether something like this is possible. I don't want to use `getInitialProps` on the page, because `getServersideProps` does exactly what I want in terms of direct db access etc. (Nice capability upgrade, thank you!)\r\n\r\nWhat I'm trying to achieve:\r\n\r\n* load a user from a cookie on the server side\r\n* use that user to set an initial client side value for a viewer/setViewer state on a context Provider component high up in the react tree.\r\n* add the viewer to the cache key for SWR, so that data is automatically refetched when we call setViewer and the viewer changes (on login/logout without redirect).\r\n* still have all this server side rendered.\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 26, "reactions_total": 14, "reactions_plus": 14, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "majelbstoat", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T19:54:42Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-07T04:18:04Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11183", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11185, "title": "Is top level await supported?", "body": "Hello all,\r\n\r\nWhen I try to use top-level `await` with Typescript 3.8, and appropriate `tsconfi.json` file, I get a Syntax Error. I can be more thorough in my error logs if it turns out it is supported. But, I have this feeling that there is something in the middleware of Nextjs that would need to be updated before it's supported, but I'm not sure.\r\n\r\nSo, is top-level `await` supported yet?\r\n\r\nThanks you!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 31, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aegatlin", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-18T20:07:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-30T15:04:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11185", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11187, "title": "Implementing /app/[slug]/dashboard, /app/[slug]/settings in an app", "body": "Hi there, I essentially want to know how would you implement a statically generated website/app where you would have routes like:\r\n- /app/[slug]/dashboard\r\n- /app/[slug]/settings\r\n- /app/[slug]/team\r\n\r\nKnowing that dashboard/settings/team all share the same layout/nav and only the content changes. I am trying to wrap my head around the right solution but unsure if it should be:\r\n- app/[slug]/dashboard.js and app/[slug]/settings.js\r\nor\r\n- app/[...slug].js where in slug.js I would switch/case\r\n\r\nThen for the layout itself, in both solutions, not sure what to apply.\r\n\r\nLet me know!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 11, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "vvo", "author_association": "MEMBER", "created_at": "2020-03-18T20:52:30Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-07T09:21:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11187", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11189, "title": "styled-jsx server side work with 9.3.1 but not with 9.3", "body": "Hi, after dealing with the #10148 error using css modules with a global style, we moved trying styled-jsx (with sass).\r\n\r\nI noticed that in production mode using the 9.3 version the style tag rendered in the head missing, applying the style only after rehydrate the page with a terrible initial effect... upgrading to 9.3.1 all start working fine but I can't understand why \ud83e\udd14", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "valse", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-19T05:15:10Z", "updated_at": "2023-07-25T01:31:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11189", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11190, "title": "with SSG/SSR how to render a deep nested component which requires fetch to render", "body": "Hi Guys\r\n\r\nI am using currently SSR but might will switch to SSG. I have a few deep nested components which requires a fetch call to an API. How could I solve this? For SEO reasons it would be important that the NextJS render waits till all data is present. Inside of pages I make use of initialPageProps but how to do this outside of the pages?\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dohomi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-19T07:03:29Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T13:12:59Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11190", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11191, "title": "React profiler", "body": "Is there a way to implement the react profiler api?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tankpower1", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-19T07:46:50Z", "updated_at": "2024-02-04T06:31:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11191", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11192, "title": "Redux toolkit with getStaticProps and preloadedState how", "body": "Does anyone have a good example of Next used with `getStaticProps` and Redux hooks together with a `preloadedState`?\r\nThis example does not use `getStaticProps` https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-redux\r\n\r\nI have tried with this. It does set the preloaded state, but the client have a different state, so i end up 2 different states. I must be missing something.\r\n```js\r\n// store.js\r\n\r\nexport default function configureStore(preloadedState) {\r\n  return configureStore({\r\n    reducer,\r\n    middleware,\r\n    preloadedState,\r\n  })\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// pages/_app.js\r\n\r\nimport store from './../store'\r\nclass MyApp extends App {\r\n  render() {\r\n    const { Component, pageProps } = this.props\r\n\r\n    return (\r\n      <ReduxProvider store={configureStore(pageProps)}>\r\n          <Component {...pageProps} />\r\n      </ReduxProvider>\r\n    )\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is also related to https://github.com/zeit/next.js/pull/11103 just with redux toolkit", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 29, "reactions_total": 21, "reactions_plus": 20, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "TommySorensen", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-19T08:10:49Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T18:25:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11192", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11199, "title": "Can the \"out\" folder contents be safely cached forever after `next-export` ?", "body": "Hey guys!\r\n\r\nSome background: we use next.js with a custom express server & have cloudfront configured to hit the next.js app. \r\n\r\nWas wondering if the contents of the \"out\" folder after doing `next build && next export` can safely be cached \"forever\"?\r\n\r\nHad some concerns since I don't see a hash on some of the files..\r\n\r\nFor example: the `[0-3].js` chunks:-\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6426069/77070426-36d26300-6a10-11ea-811f-f8b84e8f1bd1.png)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThe *specific* issue we're having is that after a new deployment, some users start seeing 404s on the old server's assets (since that server is brought down).\r\n\r\nCame across [this gist](https://gist.github.com/rbalicki2/<HASH_REDACTED>) but not sure if this is up to date!\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "rheaditi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-19T13:07:11Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11199", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11200, "title": "Same app with multiple domains on Zeit Now", "body": "My use case is this: I have one app that serves multiple domains. The app fetches different data based on the domain (for example translations and CMS pages).\r\n\r\nCurrently I need to use `getInitialProps` or `getServerSideProps` because that's the only place where the `req.headers.host` is available. But theoretically all this information can be provided at build time via the correct environment as translations and pages don't change that often and don't need to be fetched on every request so that all pages could be generated statically.\r\n\r\nSome ideas that came to mind:\r\n- Using different branches and deploying each branch as a separate app (very error prone as each branch has to be kept up-to-date)\r\n- Using multiple build commands in `package.json` (don't know how to use this with Zeit Now)\r\n\r\nBut all this feels not quite right. Is there a go-to route for a project structure like this?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 5, "reactions_plus": 5, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tiwac100", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-19T14:18:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T16:53:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11200", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11202, "title": "Dynamic Routes - 404 error in production mode", "body": "We have created dynamic routes using express in custom server.js file. our url have dynamic parameters. For example below is the url.\r\n\r\n**https://www.mydomain.com/en-kw/men/fashion-tech/2751/c\r\n\r\nen-kw ( language and country )\r\nmen ( gender)**\r\n\r\nBut when we build and run on production, I am getting lot of 404 errors in console.\r\n\r\n**GET https://www.mydomain.com/_next/static/1584607820331/pages/en-kw/men.js net::ERR_ABORTED 404**\r\n\r\nPlease let me know how we can fix this as this is highly customized ?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "huzefahusain", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-19T14:54:31Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T09:16:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11202", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11204, "title": "Renaming All CSS/SCSS for Modules Support", "body": "If you're like me managing a large project with lots of css module files, want to upgrade to Next CSS/SCSS modules support, but don't want to manually go through and rename every file, here's a one* liner you can use on OSX. I'm far from a command line pro and as such this took me a while to piece together, so I figured I'd share my result. This assumes you're using homebrew and don't already have the rename package installed.\r\n\r\n`brew install rename`\r\n`find . -name '*.scss' | xargs -t rename -x -a .module.scss`\r\n\r\nI also needed to run `git add -A` afterwards, because all these files were no longer tracked\r\n\r\nnote this will rename all scss files in your node_modules too, so you'll probably need to run `npm ci` after", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 2, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 4, "reactions_rocket": 2, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "davidgolden", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-19T16:39:39Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T05:01:15Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11204", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11208, "title": "Route prioritization", "body": "Hello folks, I wasn't sure if this is a bug, feature request or a clarification on the behavior, so I thought I'd try this.\r\n\r\nIn my app that I'm porting to Next, we have two special route cases - \r\n`https://website.com/@username` & `https://website.com/projectname`\r\n\r\nSo, I figured that in Next world, I would try the following:\r\n```\r\npages\r\n...\r\n- [projectname].js\r\n- @[username].js\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut then, it seems like every time I try something like `https://website.com/@beyonce`, it still renders `[projectname].js` component. \r\n\r\nFWIW, in the React Router 5 world, this could work by just the order of the components and in 6, this works out of the box.\r\n\r\nAny thoughts on if this behavior is even possible, and if so, how?\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "oyeanuj", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T01:48:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-19T08:50:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11208", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11209, "title": "getServerSideProps error: \"Reason: `undefined` cannot be serialized as JSON. Please use `null` or omit this value all together.\"", "body": "The `undefined` type is convenient to use in Typescript, specially because of the optional-attributes feature.\r\n\r\nHowever, in the code below:\r\n```typescript\r\nimport React from 'react';\r\nimport { NextPage, GetServerSideProps } from \"next\";\r\n\r\nconst getA = (): number => 3;\r\n\r\nconst Test: NextPage<{a?: number}> = ({ a }: {a?: number}): JSX.Element => {\r\n  return (<>\r\n    <p>{a}</p>\r\n  </>);\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport const getServerSideProps: GetServerSideProps<{a?: number}> = async ()  => {\r\n  const a = getA();\r\n  return { props: { a: a > 5? a: undefined} };\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default Test;\r\n```\r\nWe get this error :\r\n```\r\nError serializing `.a` returned from `getServerSideProps` in \"/test\". Reason: `undefined` cannot be serialized as JSON. Please use `null` or omit this value all together.\r\n\r\nError: Error serializing `.a` returned from `getServerSideProps` in \"/test\".\r\nReason: `undefined` cannot be serialized as JSON. Please use `null` or omit this value all together.\r\n```\r\nI can imagine this kind of validation was added for a good reason: to avoid lost hours debugging why a prop that is not JSON-serializable was not received by the component.\r\n\r\nHowever, it would be interesting to allow `undefined`, and omit it from the resulting JSON.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 46, "reactions_total": 159, "reactions_plus": 159, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jgpacker", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T01:49:32Z", "updated_at": "2024-05-14T04:21:57Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11209", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11210, "title": "How can I use one API route from within another one?", "body": "I have a working API route (confirmed with postman) at \"/api/auth/emailAvailable\" \r\n\r\nI want to call this API route from another API route, \"/api/auth/register\", so I did this:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nhandler.use(async (req, res) => {\r\n    axios.post(process.env.ROOT_URL + \"/api/auth/emailAvailable\", {\r\n        email: req.body.email || '  '\r\n    }).then(subRes => res.json(subRes)).catch(e => res.json(e))\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor some reason the response is always either a 400 with error, or an empty object. I also get this message in the console\r\n```\r\nAPI resolved without sending a response for /api/auth/register, this may result in stalled requests.\r\nAPI resolved without sending a response for /api/auth/emailAvailable, this may result in stalled requests.\r\n```\r\n\r\nAny idea what could be wrong?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ajones55555", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T01:58:13Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T13:19:26Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11210", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11213, "title": "Confused about SSR and Static Generation", "body": "Recently I need to make a static official website with nextjs\uff0cand I am asked to do with SSR for a better SEO.    But nextjs tell us that Static Generation  pre-rendering is faster than SSR .   So how should i choose now ?  Anybody could help me to explain about these concepts ?  Thanks so much !\r\n\r\nMy Product Requirements\uff1a  a static official website,   SSR,  better SEO \r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/29164467/77134449-fde2ce80-6aa1-11ea-90ad-91de6905b629.png)\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "serena-ai-architect", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T03:59:18Z", "updated_at": "2024-08-10T00:36:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11213", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11216, "title": "JSX preserve", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nwhy does Next requires tsconfig option \"jsx\" set to \"preserve\"?\r\nDoes TypeScript not transpiles JSX and passes it to (some internal) Babel etc. or is is entirely different reason?\r\n\r\n:)", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 9, "reactions_plus": 9, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "pnemi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T09:57:15Z", "updated_at": "2023-05-03T19:13:15Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11216", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11218, "title": "Customer Server, useFileSystemPublicRoutes, and the pages folder", "body": "### A Few Things\r\n\r\n- I can't seem to find the documentation on the render() method anywhere. It's used in the custom server info but it doesn't actually say what the arguments are or how to use it. \r\n- The docs say to set useFileSystemPublicRoutes to false if you don't want to use `/pages`, but then how do you point the render method to the right folder/file? How do you actually route to places? \r\n\r\nI couldn't find documentation on either. My whole goal is to not use the `/pages` folder and to be able to write custom routes. ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "austinthetaco", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T10:15:16Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T04:32:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11218", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11219, "title": "Jest Tests fails after upgrading to 9.3, cannot find interopRequireDefault", "body": "I have a inherited next.js project. I've tried to upgrade to 9.3.1 yesterday but when i tried to run my tests it just fails straightway with this error message.\r\n\r\nCannot find module '@babel/runtime-corejs2/helpers/interopRequireDefault' from 'jest.setup.ts'\r\n\r\nI guess it has something todo with that performance optimizing thing that has been done? But i can't figure out what to do.\r\n\r\nAs i said the project is not mine from the beginning so I'm not 100% sure exactly how everything is setup but we are using Jest, Enzyme and React Test Render. \r\n\r\nThe full message is:\r\n\r\n`    Cannot find module '@babel/runtime-corejs2/helpers/interopRequireDefault' from 'jest.setup.ts'\r\n\r\n      1 | import { configure } from \"enzyme\";\r\n      2 | import Adapter from \"enzyme-adapter-react-16\";\r\n    > 3 | \r\n        | ^\r\n      4 | import {GlobalWithFetchMock} from \"jest-fetch-mock\";\r\n      5 | \r\n      6 | const customGlobal: GlobalWithFetchMock = global as GlobalWithFetchMock;\r\n\r\n      at Resolver.resolveModule (node_modules/jest-resolve/build/index.js:276:11)\r\n      at Object.<anonymous> (jest.setup.ts:3:30)`", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "olaj", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T10:16:40Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T07:49:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11219", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11226, "title": "Extracting API routes from `pages` directory`", "body": "Hi all,\r\n\r\nI have been working on a rather large NextJS application and wonder if this discussion has come up before. I have a _lot_ of different pages, and am interested in moving my API routes outside of the `pages` directory to help better organize the project. Is there any way currently to do this, perhaps through `next.config.js`?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "sejr", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T14:59:30Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-13T18:07:02Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11226", "classification": "random"}
