{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11238, "title": "Loading dynamic html into _document.js <Head> element", "body": "I'm writing a multi-tenant application that needs to support custom skins for each different property. One of the things that makes that skin work is custom CSS includes...\r\n\r\n```\r\n<link type=\"text/css\" href=\"...\" />\r\n```\r\n\r\nI can get the necessary content as part of `getInitialProps` in my `_document.js` file, but I can't figure out how to inject the content into the page. My first thought was to use a fragment inside the `<Head>` element, with a `__dangerouslySetInnerHtml` attribute, but as far as I can tell fragments don't support any attributes.\r\n\r\nIt's starting to feel like I'm barking up the wrong tree. I assume there's got to be a way to accomplish what I want to do. What's the next.js way to do it?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "atuttle", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T20:29:43Z", "updated_at": "2023-05-30T02:43:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11238", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11239, "title": "What's the best way to deploy with your own server?", "body": "I have a node.js server (which is primarily just responsible for authentication via `passport`. Running locally I use `next build` and I build my own app, which is in typescript, and everything works perfectly.\r\n\r\nI'm trying to deploy to IBM Cloud and no matter what I've tried, deploying the app has been a massive pain for me. Using `cf push` locally pushes all files (including `.next`) and tries to start the app but I always get the following error:\r\n```Error: Could not find a valid build in the '/home/vcap/app/next' directory! Try building your app with 'next build' before starting the server.```\r\n\r\nThe only workaround I could find was to hackily move all my `dev-dependencies` to `dependencies` and then add `next build` to my `start` script. That was working (albeit incredibly janky) but as of yesterday, I'm now getting a different error:\r\n```Error: /home/vcap/app/src/components/layout.tsx: transform-react-jsx: pragma has been set but pragmaFrag has not been set\t```\r\n\r\nWhat is the _right_ way to build my app and deploy? Was that first error being caused by something being different like the node version or the target (i.e., macOS to Linux)?\r\n\r\nThank you for the help!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "SpencerKaiser", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T20:51:56Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-28T12:32:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11239", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11240, "title": "How to work with router.isFallback on production builds?", "body": "I'm using next.js 9.3 and `getStaticPaths` with `fallback: true`. In my page component I'm checking for `isFallback` and rendering a spinner on fallback and an 404 error page if props are missing:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst Location: React.FC<LocationProps> = ({ location }) => {\r\n  const router = useRouter();\r\n\r\n  // Fallback\r\n  if (router.isFallback) {\r\n    return (\r\n      <Spinner />\r\n    );\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // No location found\r\n  if (!location) {\r\n    return <Error statusCode={404} />;\r\n  }\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhile development opening an uncached pages shows the spinner and after loading everything my component, but on production the 404 error page is shown instead of the spinner.\r\n\r\nHow can I make sure that my component is build with the spinner as default?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "gglnx", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T21:10:22Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T13:23:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11240", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11244, "title": "Error with babel plugin transform-react-jsx: pragmaFrag not set", "body": "```\r\ntransform-react-jsx: pragma has been set but pragmaFrag has not been set\r\n```\r\n\r\nI haven't ran into this before. I'm setting up a new project using next v 9.3.1, and I'm getting this error when trying to use React Fragments. It wasn't a problem on my last project in next v 9.1.1. Has something changed?\r\n\r\nI have no `.babelrc` file. I'm using typescript v 3.8.3 and react v 16.13.1.\r\n\r\nI can provide more context if yall think its an app setup issue. I just want to make sure this isn't \"expected\" or something.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aegatlin", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T22:46:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-30T22:34:04Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11244", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11247, "title": "Cloudflare CORS", "body": "Hi there,\r\n\r\nWe are using Cloudflare as our DNS manager and we are under a huge DDOS attack and when we do active the Cloudflare firewall on our site, then we receive CORS errors, but when we deactivate the firewall, then everything goes right.\r\n\r\nIs there anyone who knows how we can fix this problem?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ali-master", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-20T22:48:54Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T21:14:44Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11247", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11252, "title": "how to use env.js file in next.config.js file to read the variables", "body": "Hi ,\r\n\r\n I have created a file env.js with variable eg: API_URL = \"https://apiurl.com\"\r\n\r\nIn next.config.js, I have used as\r\n\r\nrequire('dotenv').config()\r\nconst path = require('path')\r\nconst Dotenv = require('dotenv-webpack')\r\n\r\n webpack(config) {\r\n      config.plugins = [\r\n        ...config.plugins,\r\n        new Dotenv({\r\n          path: path.join(__dirname, 'env.js')\r\n\r\n        })\r\n      ]\r\n    return config;\r\n  }\r\n});\r\nAfter that, \r\nwhen i do console, its not printing the variables defined in env.js\r\nconsole.log(process.env)\r\n\r\nCan someone help on this ?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kaleshasheik", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-21T05:17:14Z", "updated_at": "2023-06-07T12:10:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11252", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11254, "title": "Send Custom Data on Status 40x 50x", "body": "Hi how I can send a custom response and message on status 400,\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n\r\nexport const (req, res) {\r\n // external api call\r\n const response = await axios.post(...)\r\n //handle response\r\n res.status(200).json(response.data)\r\n // if has an error\r\n res.status(400).json(response.data)\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\non front-end, I don't receive the payload data if  not status 200\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\ntry {\r\n  let response = await.post(...)\r\n  console.log(response.data)\r\n} catch (error) {\r\n  console.log(error) // i cant get the payload passed here if not status 200\r\n}\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "blackmo18", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-21T12:16:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-11T03:48:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11254", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11257, "title": "Importing library that has css imports but has not been processed by a module bundler breaks build", "body": "Hey, running into an issue where importing css files within my nextApp works perfectly fine, however, installing a 3rd party package that is only transpiled (modules not compiled / bundled) breaks the build because one of the files has a css import, anyone have tips for solving this?\r\n\r\nThis also isn't really limited to CSS, the same would be the case with images, or any non JS modules it seems like for that matter.\r\n\r\nThe error is (FYI, this only happens in nextJS app, works fine with regular react):\r\n\r\n> Module not found: Can't resolve './style.css' in '<name_of_third_party_package>/lib/js/components'\r\n\r\nTo stick to this specific example, though, as a test I tried creating below file, but it still doesn't do it:\r\n\r\nnext.config.js\r\n```\r\nconst MiniCssExtractPlugin = require('mini-css-extract-plugin')\r\nconst cssLoaders = ['css-loader']\r\nconst sassLoaders = ['css-loader', 'sass-loader']\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  webpack: config => {\r\n    config.module.rules.push(\r\n      {\r\n          test: /\\.css$/,\r\n          loader: [\r\n              MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader,\r\n              ...cssLoaders\r\n          ]\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n          test: /\\.scss$/,\r\n          loader: [\r\n              MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader,\r\n              ...sassLoaders\r\n          ]\r\n      }\r\n    )\r\n    config.plugins.push(\r\n      new MiniCssExtractPlugin(),\r\n    )\r\n    return config\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "linasmnew", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-21T14:27:37Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-07T20:24:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11257", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11259, "title": "getServerSideProps not working and throws error on dev", "body": "I'd like to fetch the latest blog posts from an external API on an otherwise static page. I use `getServerSideProps` on my page component. On dev, it throws this error.\r\n\r\n```\r\nTypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object\r\n    at Function.keys (<anonymous>)\r\n    at Object.renderToHTML (/MY-PROJECT-DIR/node_modules/next/dist/next-server/server/render.js:318:40)\r\n```\r\n\r\nUsing `next` version 9.3.1.\r\n\r\nMy code: (also on [CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/s/test-getserversideprops-vi4vn))\r\n\r\n```js\r\nimport fetch from \"node-fetch\";\r\n\r\nconst endpoint = \"https://swapi.co/api/films\"; \r\n\r\nexport default function TestPage(props) {\r\n  return (\r\n    <div>\r\n      Hello World.\r\n      <section>\r\n        <h2>There should be a list of titles here</h2>\r\n        {typeof props.posts !== \"undefined\" &&\r\n          props.posts.map(film => (\r\n            <li key={film.id}>\r\n              <strong>{film.title}</strong>\r\n            </li>\r\n          ))}\r\n      </section>\r\n      <hr />\r\n    </div>\r\n  );\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport async function getServerSideProps() {\r\n  fetch(endpoint)\r\n    .then(postsResponse => {\r\n      return postsResponse.json();\r\n    })\r\n    .then(posts => {\r\n      console.log(\"PAYLOAD TITLE!!!\", posts.results[0].title || \"nothing?\");\r\n      return {\r\n        // props: { posts: posts.results }\r\n        props: { posts: [{ id: 666, title: \"Fake Post Title\" }] }\r\n      };\r\n    });\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n- The first item's title from the payload _does_ print to server (ie. the CLI), thus `getServerSideProps` is run and fetch does work.\r\n- It does not matter whether I return the \"fake\" data or actual data from the API.\r\n\r\nThis is very similar to #11009 but I'm not sure where else should I export `getServerSideProps` (as suggested in the solution)? Am I understanding this correctly--or should I use something else instead for this purpose?\r\n\r\nThanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ekafyi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-21T14:37:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T19:37:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11259", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11261, "title": "It's hard to create middleware for both API routes and getServerSideProps", "body": "Let's say I have a middleware:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// pages/api/example.js\r\nimport withBanana from '../../lib/withBanana'\r\n\r\nexport default withBanana((req, res) => {\r\n  res.json({ result: req.banana })\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// lib/withBanana.js\r\nexport default function withBanana(handler) {\r\n  return function(req, res) {\r\n    req.banana = 'yellow'\r\n    return handler(req, res)\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut now, let's say I also want to apply my middleware to the getServerSideProps:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// pages/hello.js\r\n\r\nexport default function Hello({ banana }) {\r\n  return <h1>Hello {banana}</h1>\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport async function getServerSideProps({ req, res }) {\r\n  // TODO: apply the middleware here\r\n  return { props: { banana: req.banana } }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo, how to do it?\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// pages/hello.js\r\n\r\nexport default function Hello({ banana }) {\r\n  return <h1>Hello {banana}</h1>\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport const getServerSideProps = withBanana(({ req, res }) => {\r\n  return { props: { banana: req.banana } }\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis will not work, because the signature of getServerSideProps function is different than signature of API route handler.\r\n\r\nI could modify the decorator (the function in lib/withBanana.js) to work with both signatures, but that gets pretty ugly.\r\n\r\nI would like to start discussion how/whether to solve this.\r\n\r\nI personally use such decorators (or higher-order functions) for having access to `req.user` or `req.session`. Or for catching errors from async handlers.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 13, "reactions_plus": 13, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "messa", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-21T20:20:16Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T08:45:07Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11261", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11263, "title": "404 with _app getInitialProps", "body": "This seems to be a new fatal error in the latest version?\r\n\r\n`pages/404` can not have getInitialProps/getServerSideProps, https://err.sh/zeit/next.js/404-get-initial-props\r\n\r\nIf you have getInitialProps in _app, is there a way to have a functioning 404 page?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "1derwood", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-21T21:31:20Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-16T19:08:15Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11263", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11267, "title": "CSS modules - converting class names to camelCase automatically", "body": "Is it possible to configure the new built-in CSS modules support to automatically convert `.kebab-case` class names to `camelCase` when importing? `css-loader` supports this through the [`localsConvention` option](https://github.com/webpack-contrib/css-loader#localsconvention), but I'm not sure if/how it's possible to configure specific configuration options for `css-loader`.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 38, "reactions_total": 60, "reactions_plus": 60, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "controversial", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-22T00:57:33Z", "updated_at": "2025-01-23T13:08:45Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11267", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11272, "title": "Pass fetched data from getStaticPaths to getStaticProps", "body": "_Edit by @leerob: see updated response [here](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11272#discussioncomment-5831496)._\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThe SSG documentation recommends to fetch the data for the list of path params in getStaticPaths and fetch data for the individual pages in getStaticProps.\r\n\r\nWhen using a GraphQL API for fetching data, this results in possibly redundant calls to the API: all the props needed for the individual pages can already be fetched in one single API call in getStaticPaths.\r\n\r\nBut as far as I know, there is no way to pass this extra data along to getStaticProps yet. If I add another key to paths, I get the error: `Additional keys were returned from getStaticPaths in page \"/posts/[id]\". URL Parameters intended for this dynamic route must be nested under the params key`\r\n\r\n## llustration\r\n\r\n### Current way (paraphrased from the docs)\r\n\r\n``` js\r\nexport async function getStaticPaths() {\r\n  const posts = await query(`\r\n    query {\r\n      posts {\r\n        id\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  `)\r\n  const paths = posts.map(post => ({\r\n    params: { id: post.id },\r\n  }))\r\n  return { paths, fallback: false }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport async function getStaticProps(context) {\r\n  const post = await query(\r\n    `query {\r\n      post(id: $id) {\r\n        title\r\n        body\r\n      }\r\n    }`,\r\n    { variables: { id: context.params.id }}\r\n  )\r\n  return {\r\n    props: { post },\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Hypothetical way in which all data is fetched at once\r\n\r\n``` js\r\nexport async function getStaticPaths() {\r\n  const posts = await query(`\r\n    query {\r\n      posts {\r\n        id\r\n        title\r\n        body\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  `)\r\n  const paths = posts.map(post => ({\r\n    params: { id: post.id },\r\n    // additional key for passing along data\r\n    content: {\r\n      ...post\r\n    } \r\n  }))\r\n  return { paths, fallback: false }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport async function getStaticProps(context) {\r\n  return {\r\n    props: { context.content },\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis would reduce boilerplate code and (potentially significant) numbers of API roundtrips when working with GraphQL, thereby reducing buildtime.\r\n\r\nIs this already possible and if not, are there any plans of making this possible in the future?\r\n\r\nThank you for your time!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "locked", "state_reason": "resolved", "comments": 140, "reactions_total": 264, "reactions_plus": 189, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 1, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 69, "reactions_rocket": 1, "reactions_eyes": 4, "author": "baukevanderlaan", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-22T08:26:54Z", "updated_at": "2023-05-07T18:21:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11272", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11273, "title": "[with-next-sass example] Use global sass variable from styles.scss inside hello-world.module.scss", "body": "Thanks a lot for the great example : [with-next-sass](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-next-sass).\r\n\r\nI was wondering if there is a way to use the global variable `$backgroundColor` (styles.scss) inside 'hello-world.module.scss' ?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "redaben", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-22T12:22:35Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-22T13:54:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11273", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11274, "title": "Static page with client-side routing", "body": "Hi, I have a dashboard section in my next app I want to serve as a static page.\r\n\r\nThe dashboard implements client-side routing so I have paths like\r\n`/dashboard/path/../to`\r\nSo my folder structure is `pages/dashboard/[...all].js` and that file handles the routing\r\n\r\nThe dashboard needs some data I am currently fetching with getInitialProps. But in this way the page is interpreted as server side rendered.\r\n\r\nSince the data is always the same (for every user) I am trying to use getStaticProps to serve the page as static.\r\nBut due to the folder structure next thinks I need the parameters to fetch data so I must provide a getStaticPaths function and since `[...all]` changes every time my page will be rendered again on each request.\r\n\r\nDoes anyone know how to solve the issue or some workaround?\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tonxxd", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-22T15:17:19Z", "updated_at": "2024-10-22T14:55:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11274", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11275, "title": "I can't seem to understand how to import a polyfill", "body": "Hello! \r\nFirst, thanks for this amazing project! It looks really awesome and I'm loving it!! \r\n\r\nHowever, I'm having lots of troubles setting up [adonisjs' websocket client library](https://adonisjs.com/docs/4.1/websocket-client)\r\n\r\nIn the documentation says\r\n```\r\nThe module build requires the regenerator-runtime polyfill (add it via Babel).\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut how do I do that? \r\n\r\nRight now I'm trying in .babelrc\r\n```\r\n{\r\n    \"presets\": [\r\n      [\r\n        \"next/babel\",\r\n        {\r\n          \"preset-env\": {},\r\n          \"transform-runtime\": {},\r\n          \"styled-jsx\": {},\r\n          \"class-properties\": {}\r\n        }\r\n      ]\r\n    ],\r\n      \"plugins\": [\"@babel/plugin-transform-regenerator\"]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut that throws me an error saying: \r\n`Cannot find module '/Users/mike/batcave/workolam/frontend/.next/server/static/development/pages/_document'`\r\n\r\nSo, how should I import it? \r\n\r\nQuestion number 2: once I resolve that... how do I import the library so it only loads in the client? \r\n\r\nI would really like to be able to use nextjs as a frontend with adonisjs as a backend. But this configuration error is tearing me down :( \r\n\r\nIf anyone could indicate me what steps I should follow, that'd be amazing! \r\nThanks in advance\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mikealche", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-22T15:37:00Z", "updated_at": "2023-08-26T22:17:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11275", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11277, "title": "Scheduled deployments", "body": "What is the recommended way for creating scheduled deployments (e.g. daily) for NextJS and Zeit?\r\n\r\nNB. did not find any official Zapier, Github actions integrations. ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "joostmeijles", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-22T17:23:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-24T02:01:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11277", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11279, "title": "How i can set target: 'serverless' in file next.config.js", "body": "Hi @ all,\r\n\r\ni have `next.config.js` file:\r\n```js\r\nconst withSass = require(\"@zeit/next-sass\");\r\nconst tailwindCss = require(\"tailwindcss\");\r\nmodule.exports = withSass({\r\n        webpack(config, options) {\r\n            const rules = [{\r\n                test: /\\.scss$/,\r\n                use: [\r\n                    {\r\n                        loader: \"postcss-loader\",\r\n                        options: {\r\n                            ident: \"postcss\",\r\n                            plugins: [tailwindCss(\"./tailwind.config.js\")]\r\n                        }\r\n                    },\r\n                    {loader: \"sass-loader\"}\r\n                ]\r\n            }\r\n            ];\r\n            return {\r\n                ...config,\r\n                module: {\r\n                    ...config.module,\r\n                    rules: [...config.module.rules, ...rules]\r\n                }\r\n            };\r\n        }\r\n    },\r\n);\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor serverless deployment me need to set:\r\n```js\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  target: \"serverless\"\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut how can i do it? By the way how i can debug the module.exports  output?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "approached", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-22T19:44:03Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-09T11:35:18Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11279", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11280, "title": "[postcss] how to split Css files for each route", "body": "Hey,\r\nI'm using `postcss`  and trying to bootstrap the project,\r\n\r\nI can see that all css files are added only via the map `_app.js`  which will include all styles all over the app in single .css file which may impact the performance.\r\n\r\nwhat I want to do here is to have css file loaded for each page to avoid unused css.\r\nhow can I do that ?\r\n\r\nThank you", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 12, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ahakem", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-22T20:13:44Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-09T11:00:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11280", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11281, "title": "Return redirect from getServerSideProps", "body": "For example to redirect from `/` to `/dashboard` if user is logged in, otherwise to `/login`.\r\n\r\n- When the `getServerSideProps` is called during SSR it should return a 3xx response with Location header.\r\n- When the `getServerSideProps` is called during client-side router transition it should perform `Router.push()`.\r\n\r\nWould be cool to be able to do this easily, for example by `return { redirect: '/login' }`.\r\n\r\nOr is it possible to do such thing now? I could not find any way except maybe handling both cases manually and doing `Router.push()` myself in the page component.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 61, "reactions_total": 81, "reactions_plus": 81, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "messa", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-22T21:39:59Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-08T16:50:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11281", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11285, "title": "How to think about SSG when API changes are part of the same PR", "body": "I'm currently rebuilding the api for my personal site. In doing so, I did the following (generally):\r\n- created a new `pages/api/graphql.ts` route\r\n- created a new `pages/index.tsx` page\r\n- in the view, use `getStaticProps` to fetch from `/api/graphql`\r\n\r\nI'm confused about how to think about the right way to deploy this. It seems that the `getStaticProps` would need to read data from an API route that doesn't exist yet, or is being simultaneously built. This caused me a lot of frustration as I basically had to comment out a ton of code, deploy the API Routes, then rebuild my client side data fetching.\r\n\r\nAny suggestions on this would be great!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "brianlovin", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T01:06:47Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-15T22:32:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11285", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11286, "title": "Page using getStaticProps does not seem to be fetching fresh data with `swr`", "body": "Hey team, I'm hitting some issues with `swr` not running on the initial mount of a Next.js application running with `getStaticProps` for SSG. \r\n\r\nHere's a video of this in action: https://www.dropbox.com/s/bchsi0qx1pcate6/swr-gsp.mp4?dl=0\r\n\r\nWhat I'm doing:\r\n- load https://brianlovin.com/bookmarks \u2013 initial render is old data that was computed at build time\r\n- see the (expired) first link\r\n- unfocus / refocus the tab and you can see `swr` kick off and get fresh data from the database\r\n\r\nMy question is why is `swr` not getting fresh data on the initial mount, and only on refocus? My impression is that it should run for _both_ events. \r\n\r\nURL where it's happening: https://brianlovin.com/bookmarks\r\nCode: \r\n\r\n- page: https://github.com/brianlovin/brian-lovin-next/blob/master/src/pages/bookmarks.tsx\r\n- fetcher: https://github.com/brianlovin/brian-lovin-next/blob/master/src/api/index.ts\r\n\r\nEdit: it seems entirely more simple to just migrate this specific page to use `getServerSideProps` \u2013 in fact, I'll probably make this change right now. But the question still stands, especially as static generation becomes more common in Next.js land, so I'll leave this post up for possible tips on making these two tools work better together!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "brianlovin", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T04:31:10Z", "updated_at": "2022-12-01T10:17:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11286", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11287, "title": "Custom _app.js imported from an external package errors when using global CSS", "body": "I have a custom _app used in numerous projects so I wanted to move it to an external package. All of my apps would look like this:\r\n\r\n_app.js\r\n```\r\nimport CustomApp from 'package';\r\nexport default CustomApp\r\n```\r\n\r\nI can't seem to get this working if I have global CSS inside of the CustomApp due to this error:\r\n```\r\nGlobal CSS cannot be imported from files other than your Custom <App>.\r\n```\r\n\r\nHas anyone been able to get around this? I found numerous related issues but not quite the same as this one. Thought I would ask here first.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T05:47:08Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-23T23:50:49Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11287", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11290, "title": "Can you smoke test production deploys with Github Actions?", "body": "I want to use Github Actions to try and run a smoke test against prod, triggered by a successful deployment to production. I know that Github Actions has a `on: deployment` and `on: deployment_status` trigger, but it only seems to run at the **beginning** of a deploy. I want a trigger that occurs on **deployment success**. Does anyone know how to do this? Are there any examples of how to do this?\r\n\r\n(Incidentally, in investigating, I noticed that Nextjs doesn't utilize the notion of smoke tests in their cicd github action :P)", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aegatlin", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T10:30:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T13:16:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11290", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11291, "title": "Dynamically import components depending on getStaticProps response", "body": "We have a CMS where we get all the components that a page has. The components are on the project's `/components/*` directory. Here is an example:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nimport React from 'react'\r\nimport dynamic from 'next/dynamic'\r\nimport fetch from 'isomorphic-fetch'\r\n\r\nconst Page = (props) => {\r\n  const {\r\n    id,\r\n    data,\r\n  } = props\r\n\r\n  const components = data.map((d) => dynamic(() => import(`../../components/${d}`)))\r\n\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <p>\r\n        Page {id} has the next components: {data.map((d) => `\"${d}\", `)}\r\n      </p>\r\n      {components.map((Component, i) => (\r\n        <div key={i}>\r\n          <Component />\r\n        </div>\r\n      ))}\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport const getStaticProps = async ({ params }) => {\r\n  const {\r\n    id,\r\n  } = params\r\n  const result = await fetch(`http://localhost:4000/get?id=${id}`)\r\n  // Here is an array with the components.\r\n  const data = await result.json()\r\n  return ({\r\n    props: {\r\n      id,\r\n      data,\r\n    }\r\n  })\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport const getStaticPaths = async () => {\r\n  const result = await fetch('http://localhost:4000/get/all')\r\n  const data = await result.json()\r\n  const paths = data.map((id) => `/p/${id}`)\r\n  return ({\r\n    paths,\r\n    fallback: false,\r\n  })\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default Page\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe problem is that when this page is exported, the components are not embedded into the exported HTML page. This is a normal behaviour because we import dynamically the components on render, because we depend on the data from `getStaticProps`.\r\n\r\n**The question is, there is away to make Next.js aware of what components we need in the `getStaticProps`? This way, when the page is exported based on `getStaticProps`, we could tell Next.js to include in the exported HTML the components needed on that page.**\r\n\r\n**IMPORTANT NOTE:** We would like to avoid importing all the components of the project in the page. This is a solution but we have a lot of components and each page does not use all of them, so, the exported HTML would be too large.\r\n\r\nThanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 23, "reactions_total": 22, "reactions_plus": 19, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 3, "author": "urko-pineda", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-23T12:02:47Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-17T21:26:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11291", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11292, "title": "next-pwa: API data not updating every-time. API not called.", "body": "- cache issue. No Update after next-pwa implementation. \r\n- Need every time api should hit get latest data and update.\r\n- After clear cache its called api.\r\n\r\nCan anyone help how I fix this issue with next-pwa?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "2manoj1", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T13:16:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-21T03:16:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11292", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11294, "title": "Why nesting `props` when returning from `getStaticProps` or `getServerSideProps`?", "body": "When using the `getInitialProps` method, we return the `props` directly like this\r\n```\r\nreturn { data: myData }\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is not the case with `getStaticProps` and `getServerSideProps` when we nest it like this:\r\n```\r\nreturn { props: { data: myData } }\r\n```\r\n\r\nI wonder what the reasons are for introducing a more verbose syntax :)", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tomdohnal", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-23T13:52:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-04T20:56:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11294", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11295, "title": "Client side caching with getServerSideProps", "body": "Hi, right now with `getInitialProps` I can avoid refetching data that is used on multiple pages like so:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nMyPage.getInitialProps = async () => ({\r\n  bigBlob: (isClient && bigBlobCache.get()) || await fetchBigBlob(),\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis means that `bigBlob` will be fetched during server render and in the first client navigation to a page that uses `bigBlob`, but not in subsequent client navigations to pages that also use `bigBlob`. Is there a way to optimize data fetching in this way using the new `getServerSideProps`? Client side data fetching would work, but then if the user does a client navigation to the first page that uses `bigBlob`, they would have to see a flash of a loading indicator.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 7, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "nitsky", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T15:28:31Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T18:27:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11295", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11299, "title": "Trying to incorporate styled-components and material-ui into nextjs with SSR using TypeScript", "body": "# It all started with a dream...\r\nI wanted to leverage the benefits of material-ui and styled-components and keep SSR benefits from nextjs intact.\r\n\r\n## Enter a random medium article...\r\nhttps://medium.com/javascript-in-plain-english/ssr-with-next-js-styled-components-and-material-ui-b1e88ac11dfa\r\n\r\n## Everything seems great... until I deploy to zeit\r\nOuch. build errors. Public repo here:\r\nhttps://github.com/jajourda/reproduce-zeit-build-error-_document.js\r\n\r\n## Zeit's response email:\r\nInteracting with Zeit's team I got this response email:\r\n\r\nHello Joshua,\r\n\r\nI am also unable to build this project locally:\r\nERROR in /home/paulogdm/Codes/tmp/reproduce-zeit-build-error-_document.js/pages/_document.tsx(13,23):\r\n13:23 Parameter 'App' implicitly has an 'any' type.\r\n11 | ctx.renderPage = () =>\r\n12 | originalRenderPage({\r\n\r\n13 | enhanceApp: App => props =>\r\n| ^\r\n14 | styledComponentSheet.collectStyles(\r\n15 | materialUiSheets.collect(<App {...props} />),\r\n16 | ),\r\n\r\nBuild error occurred\r\nError: > Build failed because of webpack errors\r\nat build (/home/paulogdm/Codes/tmp/reproduce-zeit-build-error-_document.js/node_modules/next/dist/build/index.js:12:900)\r\nnpm ERR! code ELIFECYCLE\r\nnpm ERR! errno 1\r\nnpm ERR! reproduce-zeit-build-error-_document.js@0.1.0 build: next build\r\nnpm ERR! Exit status 1\r\nnpm ERR!\r\nnpm ERR! Failed at the reproduce-zeit-build-error-_document.js@0.1.0 build script.\r\nnpm ERR! This is probably not a problem with npm. There is likely additional logging output above.\r\n\r\nnpm ERR! A complete log of this run can be found in:\r\nnpm ERR! /home/paulogdm/.npm/_logs/2020-03-23T12_40_31_129Z-debug.log\r\nPlease check the documentation here. After applying the changes suggested in the link, I was able to successfully build the app.\r\n\r\nSince your question is a Next.js-specific problem, we recommend the following channels:\r\nhttps://github.com/zeit/next.js/discussions\r\nhttps://spectrum.chat/next-js?tab=posts\r\nhttps://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/next.js\r\n\r\nBest Regards,\r\n~ Paulo De Mitri @ ZEIT\r\n\r\n# Left scratching my head in the mystery: \"how did you apply the changes in the suggested link?\"", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "joshualinog", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T19:08:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-05T08:37:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11299", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11301, "title": "SSG export query", "body": "I\u2019ve got a fairly simple app using getStaticPaths and Props from canary, it works beautifully locally but when I build and export, while all the files are created, the links in pages do not have the .html extension. Presumably, I\u2019ve missed something simple?? Hopefully.....\r\n\r\nThanks", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "1jmj", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T19:41:33Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11301", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11307, "title": "How to structure an app using GraphQL and Firebase Auth.", "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nI'm new to NextJS and am enjoying it so far but am finding it challenging to wrap my head around how to structure an app that has some static pages, regular SSR pages with GraphQL and uses GraphQL with Firebase/JWT auth for some of the dynamic pages (clients side rendering).\r\n\r\nIn particular, firebase needs to be initialized when someone logs in and I need to maintain that initialized app and JWT token across pages so that pages can render in anonymous or logged in mode. Ideally pages would be SSR for anonymous users and client side / dynamic for logged in users so they could edit and view changes right away.\r\n\r\nIn regular CRA apps I could use a context to track the auth state ... but with NextJS I seem to lose the context and user auth info when I don't expect to. I'm guessing because some pages end up being SSR ... but being new I don't have a good enough mental model to figure out how to address this and/or if I'll be able to do what I want/need with NextJS.\r\n\r\nI've looked at many of the examples and they are useful in isolation but don't really seem to address this particular problem/configuration and often don't explain their approaches well enough for me to grok.\r\n\r\nDoes anyone know of a good tutorial, article or documentation link that I could review for insight?\r\n\r\nAlso, is there a discord, slack, gitter, etc. for NextJS? This discussion feature is all I've found but it leaves a lot to be desired like search.\r\n\r\nThanks in advance.\r\n\r\nJulio\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "JulioBarros", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-23T21:39:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-04T03:18:59Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11307", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11310, "title": "Using Apollo's useQuery with getServerSideProps", "body": "I'm trying to change code with `getInitialProps` to use `getServerSideProps`. I have something like:\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst ObjPage = ({ objId }: Props): JSX.Element => {\r\n    const { loading, error, data } = useQuery(LoadObj, {\r\n        query: LoadObj,\r\n        variables: { objId}\r\n    });\r\n\r\n    if (loading) {\r\n        return <>Loading...</>;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if (!data || error) {\r\n        return <ErrorPage statusCode={500} />;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    ...go on to render the page\r\n};\r\n\r\nObjPage.getInitialProps = async (context: Context): Promise<Props> => {\r\n    const { query: { objId } } = context;\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n        objId: objId as string\r\n    };\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis works fine for both client and SSR. When I simply change the `getInitialProps` to `getServerSideProps` everything breaks because `objId` is now undefined. How do I get this to work?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "bwesner", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T01:33:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T09:06:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11310", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11313, "title": "Uncaught (in promise) Error: No router instance found on Jest test next/link", "body": "I want to test redirection of page when click on div wrap by next/link\r\n```\r\nimport Link from 'next/link'\r\n<Link href=\"/home\">\r\n     <div className=\"test\">Home</div>\r\n</Link>\r\n```\r\nmy test file looks like this\r\n```\r\nimport { mount } from 'enzyme'\r\n...\r\nwrapper = mount(<TestHeaderComponent />);\r\nawait wrapper\r\n            .find('. test')\r\n            .at(0)\r\n            .simulate('click');\r\n```\r\n\r\nim still not expect anything but it still this error\r\n\r\n> Error: Uncaught [Error: No router instance found.\r\n>     You should only use \"next/router\" inside the client side of your app.\r\n>     ]\r\n\r\n\r\nI'm looking for and appreciate your helps, thanks", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "iamhuynq", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T03:40:08Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-28T21:07:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11313", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11315, "title": "problems using preview mode", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nI started to convert my SSR project to SSG and try to implement the preview mode. Does the preview mode intend to work while in development mode as well? Because I can't see the cookie being set. I am using a very basic approach and deployed via Vercel:\r\n```js\r\n// pages/api/preview\r\nexport default (req: NextApiRequest, res: NextApiResponse) => {\r\n  if (!req.query.slug) {\r\n    return res.status(401).json({ message: 'Invalid slug' })\r\n  }\r\n  res.setPreviewData({})\r\n  res.writeHead(307, { Location: req.query.slug})\r\n  res.end()\r\n}\r\n```\r\nIf I log the value of `context.preview` inside of my getStaticProps it is undefined and thus the preview does not work.\r\n```\r\n[ event ] build page: /api/preview\r\n[ wait ]  compiling ...\r\n[ ready ] compiled successfully - ready on http://localhost:3000\r\ninside preview home // => this is home inside of api/preview\r\ninside index\r\npreview { index: [ 'api', 'home' ] } undefined undefined // preview slug is \"api/home\" and preview as well as preview data is undefined\r\n{ status: 404, url: '/cdn/stories/api/home' }\r\n\r\n```\r\nIs there any way how to debug this behaviour? Thanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 14, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dohomi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T05:33:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T14:20:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11315", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11317, "title": "Use getServerSideProps with Wrapper", "body": "The example [with-cookie-example](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-cookie-auth/utils/auth.js) uses getInitialProps, how to use getServerSideProps in that case? \r\n\r\n```\r\n// /components/withAuth.js\r\nexport const withAuthSync = WrappedComponent => {\r\n  const Wrapper = props => {\r\n    return <WrappedComponent {...props} />\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  Wrapper.getInitialProps = async ctx => {\r\n    const token = auth(ctx)\r\n\r\n    const componentProps =\r\n      WrappedComponent.getInitialProps &&\r\n      (await WrappedComponent.getInitialProps(ctx))\r\n\r\n    return { ...componentProps, token }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  return Wrapper\r\n}\r\n```\r\nMy page with this wrapper\r\n```\r\n// /pages/home.js\r\nimport React from 'react';\r\nimport {withAuthSync} from '../containers/hocs/withAuth';\r\n\r\nconst Home = (props) => {\r\n    return (\r\n        <div>\r\n            Home Page\r\n        </div>\r\n    );\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport async function getServerSideProps(ctx) {\r\n    const data = await doSomething();\r\n    return {\r\n        props: {...data}\r\n    };\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport default withAuthSync(Home);\r\n```\r\nI have this error \r\n\r\n> You can not use getInitialProps with getServerSideProps. Please remove getInitialProps. /home\r\n\r\n\r\nI do not want to write authentication logic on every page", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "happy-unicorn", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T06:10:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T14:35:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11317", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11318, "title": "SEO Optimization with Apollo", "body": "I am following [ with-apollo](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-apollo) example to make my web application, however the meta tags seem to be not rendered for the bots, i.e. if I debug my dynamic url say http://abc.com/item/[id]/[name]\r\nat https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/ this always gets the default meta tags that I have set inside _app, even though I am updating meta tags with props (received from getInitialProps) inside component using next/head. \r\nI have tried this using getServerSideProps as well.\r\nIs this something doable with nextjs?\r\nPlease help, I have googled alot and tried almost any example that seemed to be solution, but no luck so far. \r\nI am using Next 9.3 and apollo/client 3.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mshahbazm", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T07:16:02Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-02T10:24:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11318", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11321, "title": "Getting strange `;` at the end of HTML output", "body": "Anyone has any ideas why I am getting a mysterious semicolon at the end of the page?\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/10786558/77415839-00c12480-6dfe-11ea-953f-bcaa25b53dd6.png)\r\n\r\nNowhere to find it in any of the code though:\r\n```\r\n//document.ts\r\nexport default class CustomDocument extends Document {\r\n  static async getInitialProps(ctx: DocumentContext) {\r\n    const sheets = new ServerStyleSheets()\r\n    const originalRenderPage = ctx.renderPage\r\n\r\n    ctx.renderPage = () =>\r\n      originalRenderPage({\r\n        enhanceApp: App => props => sheets.collect(<App {...props} />)\r\n      })\r\n    const initialProps = await Document.getInitialProps(ctx)\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n      ...initialProps,\r\n      styles: [\r\n        ...React.Children.toArray(initialProps.styles),\r\n        sheets.getStyleElement()\r\n      ]\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  render() {\r\n    return (\r\n      <Html>\r\n        <Head />\r\n        <body>\r\n          <Main />\r\n          <NextScript />\r\n        </body>\r\n      </Html>\r\n    )\r\n  }\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\n//index.ts\r\n  return (\r\n    <>\r\n      <Head>\r\n        <title>233</title>\r\n        <meta charSet=\"utf-8\" />\r\n        <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"initial-scale=1.0, width=device-width\" />\r\n      </Head>\r\n      <Layout maxWidth='xl' background='none' headerSettings={{menus,asyncSearch:true,showFilter:false}}>\r\n       <div className={showContents?classes.visible:classes.invisible}>\r\n        {pagination}\r\n        <div\r\n          ref={container}\r\n          style={{\r\n            position: 'relative',\r\n          }}\r\n        >\r\n          {currentEntries.length > 0 && renderPostList()}\r\n        </div>\r\n        {pagination}\r\n        </div>\r\n      </Layout>\r\n    </>\r\n  )\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAny ideas? Thanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 14, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dukedhx", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T10:42:33Z", "updated_at": "2024-10-16T06:28:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11321", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11326, "title": "Abstract SSR & API Routes from Node.js", "body": "# Feature request\r\nUsing `next` for server-side rendering or API routes **must** be done in a Node.js runtime. This is because the `next` package is tightly bound to Node.js preventing using other runtimes like [Deno](https://deno.land/), [Electron](https://www.electronjs.org/) or even the browser (which now has things like [native filesystem](https://web.dev/native-file-system/) support).\r\n\r\nIt would be helpful if the core functionalities of `next` were abstracted from Node.js. This would be similar to the way a database abstraction layer would work. Effectively `next` would have \"adapters\" (or something like that) for different runtimes. The default adapter (and the only one in core) would be a Node.js adapter, but other adapters could be created in userland.\r\n\r\nThe `next` app would receive its necessary dependencies from the adapter. For instance it might get the parsed URL and a list of routes with regexes and callback functions to call to get the component for that route. The adapter might also provide a callback to determine the proper `_app.js` and the like.\r\n\r\nHaving a clear and documented interface for what an adapter should look like (and obviously a Node.js example) would be really helpful as well. :)\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\n1) Reimplementing all of next for a different environment seems like a ton of work. :(\r\n2) Perhaps shimming the Node.js APIs for a different environment, but that's also a can of worms.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 4, "reactions_plus": 4, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "davidbarratt", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T15:18:01Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-07T23:08:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11326", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11327, "title": "Pass props through Link", "body": "Is it possible to pass props to a component targeted by `next/link` in a similar way to React-Router's Link ?\r\n\r\nThey have an example here: https://tylermcginnis.com/react-router-pass-props-to-link/.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 5, "reactions_plus": 5, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "bleucitron", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T15:22:20Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T21:23:01Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11327", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11333, "title": "Firebase onAuthStateChanged user returns null in next build", "body": "https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/examples/with-firebase/context/userContext.js#L12\r\n\r\nFollowing the with-firebase example, userContext.js inits firebase and returns the user as expected when running in development mode with `next`, but when serving the output of `next build`, firebase inits, but user returns `null`. I'm using SSG for most pages, so not sure if that has some effect. \r\n\r\n<img width=\"198\" alt=\"Image 2020-03-24 at 8 04 52 PM\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/4154407/77466565-c0b46e80-6e0a-11ea-9f58-aa08528e35d6.png\">\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "stormfar", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T19:05:56Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-15T20:57:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11333", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11334, "title": "getStaticProps with Custom App", "body": "getInitialProps in _app.js is also called build-time when using getStaticProps - but how can Props from getStaticProps ever be passed to the Page? The custom app can only render the Page with the Props received from the getInitialProps of _app.js and the getInitialProps of the Page itself.\r\n\r\nWhat do i miss here?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 28, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "andreas1327250", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T19:30:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T09:06:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11334", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11335, "title": "How to disable 'x-powered-by' header", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nI'm having some problems trying to disable the 'x-powered-by' header.\r\nI don't have a custom server and i'm using next 9.3.1.\r\n\r\nnext.config.js file\r\n```\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  webpack: (config, { webpack }) => {\r\n    config.plugins.push(new webpack.IgnorePlugin(/\\/__tests__\\//));\r\n\r\n    return config;\r\n  },\r\n  poweredByHeader: false,\r\n  env: {\r\n    BFF_URL: process.env.BFF_URL,\r\n    BFF_TOKEN: process.env.BFF_TOKEN,\r\n    RECAPTCHA_KEY: process.env.RECAPTCHA_KEY,\r\n  },\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhen i do `npm run dev`\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5843706/77476330-239d0a00-6df9-11ea-88e3-03688c337834.png)\r\n\r\nwhen i do `npm run build` and `npm start`\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5843706/77476583-98704400-6df9-11ea-97e1-e0492b5781bb.png)\r\n\r\nany solution or idea?\r\nThanks.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "levelasquez", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T21:05:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T16:51:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11335", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11337, "title": "Dynamic app layout with wrapped component in HOC", "body": "Hi all,\r\n\r\nI'm trying to get **[dynamic app layout](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-dynamic-app-layout)** example working with my page wrapped in HOC. My page export is like this -`export default connect()(withAuth(Profile));`\r\n\r\nIn this case, I'm getting **WrappedComponent** instead of **Profile** which makes sense but how can I access the **Layout** property from the Profile component in _app.js? I'm looking for a generic solution which can work with any number of HOCs\r\n\r\nThanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "overide", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-24T21:18:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T00:58:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11337", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11340, "title": "Dynamic env variables", "body": "Is it possible to build the project once, but use different env variables upon starting the `nextjs` server?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Amurmurmur", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-25T04:13:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T22:06:48Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11340", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11341, "title": "How to update next.js to use new javascript function?", "body": "I has my project that use next.js since version 6.xx but now I upgrade next.js version to lastest and use it normally. \r\nbut I found little problem that i can't use new javascript function eg. .flat(), .flatMap(), optional chaining\r\n\r\nwhat I should update??\r\n\r\nthank you.\r\n\r\npackage.json\r\n```\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"fw-mobile-site\",\r\n  \"description\": \"Fastwork mobile site with Next.js\",\r\n  \"author\": \"Fastwork.co\",\r\n  \"version\": \"1.9.11\",\r\n  \"license\": \"ISC\",\r\n  \"scripts\": {\r\n    \"dev\": \"node server.js\",\r\n    \"build\": \"next build\",\r\n    \"start:prod\": \"node server.js\",\r\n    \"lint\": \"eslint '**/*.{ts,tsx}'\",\r\n    \"type-check\": \"tsc --noEmit\",\r\n    \"test\": \"LOCALE=th jest\",\r\n    \"test:watch\": \"LOCALE=th jest --watch\",\r\n    \"test:coverage\": \"LOCALE=th jest --coverage\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"dependencies\": {\r\n    \"@fastwork/turbine\": \"^0.1.19\",\r\n    \"@firebase/app-types\": \"^0.4.0\",\r\n    \"@firebase/util\": \"^0.2.20\",\r\n    \"@fortawesome/fontawesome-svg-core\": \"^1.2.19\",\r\n    \"@fortawesome/free-brands-svg-icons\": \"^5.9.0\",\r\n    \"@fortawesome/pro-light-svg-icons\": \"^5.9.0\",\r\n    \"@fortawesome/pro-regular-svg-icons\": \"^5.9.0\",\r\n    \"@fortawesome/pro-solid-svg-icons\": \"^5.9.0\",\r\n    \"@fortawesome/react-fontawesome\": \"^0.1.4\",\r\n    \"@intervolga/optimize-cssnano-plugin\": \"^1.0.6\",\r\n    \"@sentry/browser\": \"^4.4.2\",\r\n    \"@types/react-dates\": \"^17.1.7\",\r\n    \"@zeit/next-bundle-analyzer\": \"^0.1.2\",\r\n    \"@zeit/next-css\": \"^1.0.1\",\r\n    \"@zeit/next-sass\": \"^1.0.1\",\r\n    \"@zeit/next-source-maps\": \"^0.0.3\",\r\n    \"algoliasearch\": \"^3.32.0\",\r\n    \"babel-plugin-styled-components\": \"^1.10.0\",\r\n    \"babel-plugin-transform-imports\": \"^1.5.1\",\r\n    \"biomatic\": \"^0.3.12\",\r\n   