{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11393, "title": "Support Concurrent Mode introduces serious performance issues with native HTML drag and drop ", "body": "After upgrading to 9.3.1 from 8.x we noticed a performance issue with drag and drop. UI starts to flicker, scroll is not responding etc... Trying to get some data from Chrome performance tools slows browser tab to the point that entire Chrome UI becomes unresponsive. By going back and checking releases we found that issue was introduced with PR #[9026](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/pull/9026/files), Support Concurrent Mode in Loadable.\r\n\r\nI would like to understand what was gained by using `use-subscription` lib? According to their [website](https://www.npmjs.com/package/use-subscription), this lib has a limited use case and it's probably better to avoid it for frameworks like Next IMO.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 11, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kokipsiho", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-27T11:08:17Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T10:13:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11393", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11394, "title": "prefetch \"on-scroll\" instead of \"in-viewport\" to optimize user experience", "body": "Hi Guys,\r\n\r\ndue to https://nextjs.org/blog/next-9#production-optimizations I have on large websites a very high amount of prefetched data in my production build. It would be great if it would be possible to leverage the behaviour to change \"in-viewport\" to \"on-scroll\".\r\n\r\nI'm requesting this this mainly because I use intersection-observer for delaying images on my websites. Now the data of prefetched pages are requested before my images and this results in a worse user experience than to disable prefetching.\r\n\r\nIs there a solution to change that behaviour? Thanks for any input", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dohomi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-27T11:09:46Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-30T17:34:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11394", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11398, "title": "SCSS module with dynamically added classes", "body": "I'm using the 9.3 built-in support for SCSS modules.\r\n\r\nThe problem that I have is that after rendering (inside `useEffect()`) I want to dynamically add a class to an element using vanilla JS:\r\n`element.classList.add('error')`\r\n\r\nBut this doesn't work with the current built-in support for SCSS (same for CSS) as it only works when doing something like this:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n```\r\n// THIS WORKS\r\nimport styles from \"./Button.module.scss\";\r\n\r\nconst Button = ({ children }) => (\r\n  <button type=\"button\" className={styles.error}>\r\n    {children}\r\n  </button>\r\n);\r\n\r\nexport default Button;\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n```\r\n// THIS DOES NOT WORK\r\nimport \"./Button.module.scss\";\r\n\r\nconst Button = ({ children }) => (\r\n  <button type=\"button\" className=\"error\">\r\n    {children}\r\n  </button>\r\n);\r\n\r\nexport default Button;\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs there a way to make this work with component-level styles or will I have to use global stylesheets?\r\n ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "vladfulgeanu", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-27T12:59:33Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-04T11:19:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11398", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11399, "title": "Which apollo-server variant should be used on API routes?", "body": "In the [api-routes-graphql](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/api-routes-graphql) example, `apollo-server-micro` is used. I have also implemented this and it works perfectly fine as far as I can tell.\r\n\r\nHowever, recently the use of micro middlewares was discouraged in favor of using [Connect/Express middlewares](https://nextjs.org/docs/api-routes/api-middlewares#connectexpress-middleware-support), which adds the need to write some boilerplate code to promisify express-style middleware.\r\n\r\nNow, I'm wondering if in above's example `apollo-server-express` should be used or if there is a better solution?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jstcki", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-27T13:11:21Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11399", "classification": "random"}
