{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11424, "title": "Use getStaticProps and getServerSideProps together", "body": "As far as I understood the new data fetching strategies `getStaticProps` and `getServerSideProps` introduced with the RFC #9524 are mutually exclusive but I believe there are many cases where we could enjoy the benefits of both of these approaches.\r\n\r\nLet's say I want to build a page where most of the content is static and could be loaded at build-time, but there is a small portion of data that must be loaded at request-time and for SEO reasons the whole page must be rendered server-side. This could be the case of a homepage of multilanguage e-commerces or marketplaces where most of the content is static and could be fetched at build-time based on a provided language but the \"featured product\" section must always be loaded with the most updated content per request.\r\n\r\nRight now the only solutions I can think of to keep TTFB low and avoid fetching static content on each request are:\r\n\r\n1. Fetch static content at build-time running a customized script which will save data in a JSON file, which will be load later in `getServerSideProps` together with dynamic content or imported as a module\r\n\r\n2.  Fetch both static and dynamic content in `getServerSideProps` and use a custom cache layer to cache only static content for future requests.\r\n\r\nBoth solutions force developers to write custom caching logic and/or DevOps strategies to build and deploy this kind of application, while it could be easier if we could just use `getStaticProps` and `getServerSideProps` together. In this scenario, static content could be loaded at build-time using `getStaticProps` while dynamic content used for server-side rendering will be loaded per-request using `getServerSideProps`. In the case, we are not enjoying the full benefits of SSG pages but we can still reduce TTFB by loading static content only once at build-time.\r\n\r\nWhat do you think about this solution? Could it be a new proposal for an RFC? Are there any other solutions I haven't considered?", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "locked", "state_reason": "resolved", "comments": 110, "reactions_total": 279, "reactions_plus": 192, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 15, "reactions_hooray": 15, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 35, "reactions_rocket": 4, "reactions_eyes": 18, "author": "izziaraffaele", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-28T08:59:44Z", "updated_at": "2023-05-07T18:26:14Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11424", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11425, "title": "Error on load sass from import file in next js", "body": "I have this in next.config.js\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst withSass = require('@zeit/next-sass');\r\nconst withCSS = require(\"@zeit/next-css\");\r\nmodule.exports = withCSS(withSass({\r\n   webpack (config, options) {\r\n       config.module.rules.push({\r\n\r\n           test: /\\.(png|jpg|gif|svg|eot|ttf|woff|woff2)$/,\r\n           use: {\r\n               loader: 'url-loader',\r\n               options: {\r\n                   limit: 100000\r\n               }\r\n           }\r\n       });\r\n\r\n       return config;\r\n   }\r\n}));\r\n```\r\n\r\nI have this configuration but can't be loading CSS from saas files, for instance, I made import \"./main.scss\"; and is not loaded next js: 9.3\r\n`\r\n./components/landing/main.scss ModuleParseError: Module parse failed: Unexpected character '\ufffd' (1:0) You may need an appropriate loader to handle this file type, currently no loaders are configured to process this file. See https://webpack.js.org/concepts#loaders (Source code omitted for this binary file)`\r\n\r\ncan someone help with the right configuration?\r\n\r\nthanks in advance\r\nCarlos Vieira\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ShintaroNippon", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-28T13:57:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-04T02:15:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11425", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11427, "title": "How/when use getStaticProps", "body": "We have an e-commerce with Next.js since the last year. And after seeing the new optimizations of 9.3, we are thinking to migrate from a `getInitialProps` to `getStaticProps`. However, there is something that it's not very clear (at least for me)...\r\n\r\nThe idea with `getStaticProps` is that the data is fetched at build-time, isn't it? Then, is it needed some cron or some mechanism to re-build when this content change... ?? I looked at the docs but I don't understand how to do that. \r\n\r\nThen, if the content change constantly, so instead of keeping the static approach and doing re-builds, is it better to keep on a server, maybe with `getServerSideProps`? Where is the limit when a resource is considered to serve using `getStaticProps` or with `getServerSideProps`?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 8, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aralroca", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-28T16:00:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T21:23:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11427", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11428, "title": "Production deployment: Can I use a shared static cache for multiple nodes?", "body": "I\u2018m building a nextjs application which heavily used fallback rendering and the revalidate parameter to serve a big product catalog.\r\n\r\nI wonder what the best way would be for production deployments to handle this efficiently besides using Zeit Now. (Probably will use that anyways, but want to know that there are alternatives).\r\n\r\nIf I load balance multiple nextjs instances, each of them would rebuild the fallback pages/revalidate pages on their own which seems really inefficient for a large catalog.\r\nWould it be viable to let all of these instances share the same .next cache folder, or would that lead to issues if many of them access it simultaneously?\r\nAlso, as currently these multiple nextjs instances are just docker containers on the same host, that would work easily, but he same might not be the case if I have them on entirely separate hosts.\r\n\r\nIs load balancing multiple instances actually even a good approach? Most of the time I just want to serve static prerendered content, and would only need more computing power for the api routes and maybe for rendering the fallback or to\u2013be\u2013revalidated pages if many are requested on the same time.\r\n\r\nFor a serverless deployment I guess things will be easier as most of the time content could be served from one static source, and new functions would be called to render a page one time if certain criteria are met(doesn\u2019t exist yet/need to revalidate) but serverless nextjs doesn\u2019t support the new static functions yet.\r\n\r\nAll in all I\u2018m struggling to find a proper deployment example for this and wonder if there might be a more clever way than simply having several containers running nextjs and balance between them.\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "janus-reith", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-28T16:31:02Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T17:29:35Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11428", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11429, "title": "SSG/R and Hardcore SPAs", "body": "For a side project of mine I am using Next.js - mostly for the built-in routing and SSG capabilities.\r\n\r\nThe app has only one dynamic route that is `/pages/picture/[id].js` and 20 pictures - a tiny JSON payload which I am reading from the FS at build time. To speed up navigation (next/prev via arrow keys) the page uses the array of metadata and a cursor which is kept in local state and used to select the next/prev image. \r\n\r\nThis works fine but when the user stops navigating I would eventually like to update the URL. I've tried to use next-router but it seems that when I push the new route `\"/picture/\" + currentId` the page is re-rendered which makes totally sense I guess. \r\n\r\nIdeally I would like to avoid this and do a full page render only upon initial navigation. So I thought I can use the history API to push and handle `popostate` events but I was wondering if you'd suggest a different more Next.js-y approach.\r\n\r\nThanks in advance for your help!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "giuseppeg", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-03-28T16:45:41Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-26T20:55:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11429", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11431, "title": "Use getServerSideProps for a specific request once for multiple pages", "body": "I have two page that need to get store information from the api (I don't think I can use getStaticProps because I have hundreds of stores all on different domains all using the same page). I want the `/` and `/cart` pages to both request the store info.\r\n\r\nCurrently I have them both hitting an API endpoint in getServerSideProps, however even though its the exact same request, they both run it, and it delays render. Is it possible to only run one, and disable the call to the other if the client already has the data? Alternatively is there a way to do this efficiently without getServerSideProps or using getStaticProps with hundreds of stores using the same page?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 6, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "samschooler", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-28T17:05:05Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-21T06:31:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11431", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11437, "title": "How to solve ree-validate issue?", "body": "I'm working on debugging a Nextjs project which I have downloaded from Git. When I try to install its packages I get the error below:\r\n\r\n\" ./utils/validator.js Module not found: Can't resolve 'ree-validate' in '/project/path/utils' \" .\r\n\r\nI have tried Installing the \"ree-validate\" separately but I still get the error above. How can I fix this issue?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Elessar1996", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-03-28T18:43:17Z", "updated_at": "2024-05-14T06:03:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11437", "classification": "random"}
