{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11552, "title": "RFC: Incremental Static Regeneration", "body": "**Update:** This has been released. See [the docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/data-fetching#incremental-static-regeneration) for more information. The RFC will remain for historical context.\r\n\r\n## Summary\r\n\r\nFully automatic re-rendering of statically exported pages without a full rebuild.\r\n\r\nWith regards to static site generators this feature has been referred to in multiple ways, most commonly \"Incremental builds\" or \"Incremental rebuilds\".\r\n\r\nThis RFC exclusively discusses API additions. All new functionality is completely backwards compatible and can be incrementally adopted. This RFC introduces no deprecations.\r\n\r\n## Background\r\n\r\nNext.js recently introduced support for [next-gen Static Generation](https://nextjs.org/docs/basic-features/pages) (SSG) in v9.3.\r\n\r\nGenerating static pages in Next.js using `getStaticProps` and `getStaticPaths` provides numerous upsides:\r\n\r\n- **Faster Websites:** Generated files can be served from a global CDN close to your users\r\n- **Reduced Costs:** Request are not rendered on-demand for every user\r\n\r\nHowever static generation as it exists today has one downside that makes it hard to adopt for larger websites or applications:\r\n\r\nEvery time something changes in the database a full rebuild is required in order to see the change in one single page.\r\n\r\nAn example to illustrate this is when you make a change to a blogpost in a headless CMS, you could be only updating the title of one blog item. After you make the change the application would require a full rebuild of all pages, just to make that one change visible.\r\n\r\nIn order to make static sites scale we have to regenerate pages without needing a full build of the application.\r\n\r\nWhat if we could generate a new version of the page on-demand when a new request comes in, but without having the user wait for the re-rendering?\r\n\r\nEffectively re-rendering the page in the background while the previous version of the page keeps being served.\r\n\r\nThe background re-rendering could be triggered based on a timeout, similar to the way that `Cache-Control`'s `maxage` works, when the timeout is exceeded a background re-render is triggered when a request comes in.\r\n\r\nThis would look something like:\r\n\r\n- The page defines what timeout is reasonable, this could be as low as 1 second\r\n- When a new request comes in the statically generated page is served\r\n- When a new request comes in and the defined timeout is exceeded:\r\n  - The statically generated page is served\r\n  - Next.js generates a new version of the page in the background and updates the static file for future requests\r\n- When a new request comes in the updated static file is served\r\n\r\nThis model is pretty ideal for static generation:\r\n\r\n- Works in the background, users always get a static response\r\n- Pages get statically re-generated, including all changes from the CMS\r\n- It's fully automatic, no need to trigger a full rebuild\r\n\r\nThis allows for true incremental rendering of static pages in Next.js.\r\n\r\nAfter this RFC for incremental static generation is implemented we can extend it to do force revalidation, which would be useful for CMS providers that want to get the content updated as quickly as possible.\r\n\r\n## Implementation\r\n\r\nNext.js 9.3 introduced `getStaticProps`, in `getStaticProps` you'll be able to return a `revalidate` property in order to opt-in to automatically generating in the background when a request comes in:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// pages/blog/[slug].js\r\nexport async function getStaticProps({ params }) {\r\n  const post = await getBlogPostBySlug(params.slug);\r\n  return {\r\n    // Set the timeout for generating to 1 second\r\n    // This timeout could be longer depending on how often data changes\r\n    revalidate: 1,\r\n    props: {\r\n      post,\r\n    },\r\n  };\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nKeep in mind that the timeout defined is a timeout for when an incoming request will re-render in the background. Meaning that the first request after the timeout period will serve the previously rendered page and then the page is regenerated in the background.\r\n\r\nWhen you opt-in to `revalidate` you get:\r\n\r\n- Fully static rendering\r\n- Automatic re-generation in the background when a request comes in\r\n\r\nCo-authored with @rauchg @Timer\r\nSpecial thanks to @juancampa @dav-is\r\n", "category": "RFC", "category_slug": "rfc", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 453, "reactions_total": 899, "reactions_plus": 376, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 71, "reactions_hooray": 119, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 166, "reactions_rocket": 108, "reactions_eyes": 59, "author": "timneutkens", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-01T14:47:19Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-02T20:43:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11552", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11555, "title": "Redirecting to arbitrary route in auth0 callback", "body": "Hi folks,\r\n\r\nI'm building a small app, based on the auth0 sample. What I haven't found out how to do is how to redirect to an arbitrary route in the post-login callback. For example, assume there is a protected route `/account` that I redirect to `/api/login` as in the sample. I would like to pass a url parameter (e.g. `?redirect=/account`) that the `/api/callback` route takes and redirects me to `/account`. Is that possible? It's a pretty common pattern in many web apps.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aloukissas", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-01T15:43:48Z", "updated_at": "2023-06-27T05:07:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11555", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11556, "title": "NextJS Expert Developers For Contract Hire?", "body": "Whats the best place to find NextJS Experts interested in a few hours a week of project assistance? \r\n\r\nWe have development capabilities, recently transitioned to NextJS and are pushing the boundaries of whats possible and are looking to accelerate progress.  In short, a high hourly rate will be offered for efficient, on-demand bottleneck assistance as problems emerge.\r\n\r\nIf anyone reading this is interested, let me know!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "e-karma", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-01T15:49:39Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-26T20:45:51Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11556", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11560, "title": "Advanced Logging Level Option?", "body": "I wonder if y'all have considered adding config option for a noisier level of logging in development mode...Iwould be all for it.\r\n\r\nex: I has a Router.push that was silently cancelling (totally an error in our code), but we had to go in and add some more logging in our Router.events to see what was causing this to happen. \r\n\r\nMaybe the solution is for us to have a component that does noisier Router logging in development, but another interesting option is to enable and advanced level of logging of what's going on under the hood. I know for a fact I'd learn a bunch of things that I'm more than likely doing wrong. \r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "followbl", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-01T19:38:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-25T06:16:59Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11560", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11575, "title": "Help with loader in next.config.js please!", "body": "I cannot figure out why my loader is not working. I'm trying to render a pdf file via `react-pdf` using `url-loader`. I modified the next.config.js as the docs seemed to suggest, but no luck. Same result regardless.\r\nI am getting :\r\n```\r\n./components/PDF/sample.pdf 1:0\r\nModule parse failed: Unexpected token (1:0)\r\nYou may need an appropriate loader to handle this file type, currently no loaders are configured to process this file. See https://webpack.js.org/concepts#loaders\r\n```\r\n\r\n`next.config.js`\r\n```\r\nconst withPlugins = require(\"next-compose-plugins\");\r\nconst withTM = require(\"next-transpile-modules\");\r\nconst withOptimizedImages = require(\"next-optimized-images\");\r\nconst withFonts = require(\"next-fonts\");\r\nconst withCSS = require(\"@zeit/next-css\");\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  env: {\r\n    STRIPE_PUBLIC_KEY: \"your_stripe_public_key_here\",\r\n    API_URL: \"http://localhost:3001\"\r\n  },\r\n  webpack: (config, { buildId, dev, isServer, defaultLoaders, webpack }) => {\r\n    // Fixes npm packages that depend on `fs` module\r\n    if (!isServer) {\r\n      config.node = {\r\n        fs: \"empty\"\r\n      };\r\n    }\r\n    config.module.rules.push({\r\n      test: /\\.pdf$/,\r\n      use: {\r\n        loader: \"url-loader\",\r\n        options: {\r\n          limit: 10000000,\r\n          name: \"[name].[ext]\"\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }),\r\n      config.resolve.modules.push(__dirname);\r\n\r\n    config.resolve.alias = {\r\n      ...config.resolve.alias\r\n    };\r\n\r\n    return config;\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = withPlugins(\r\n  [\r\n    [\r\n      withTM,\r\n      {\r\n        transpileModules: [\"reusecore\", \"common\"]\r\n      }\r\n    ],\r\n    [\r\n      withOptimizedImages,\r\n      {\r\n        mozjpeg: {\r\n          quality: 90\r\n        },\r\n        webp: {\r\n          preset: \"default\",\r\n          quality: 90\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    ],\r\n    withFonts,\r\n    withCSS\r\n  ],\r\n  {\r\n    distDir: \"../../dist/functions/next\"\r\n  }\r\n);\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nNext.js version 9.3.0", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 9, "reactions_total": 9, "reactions_plus": 9, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "hatchli", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-01T23:27:53Z", "updated_at": "2024-03-26T09:35:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11575", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11577, "title": "Store themes on cookies", "body": "Can someone tell me how to switch light mode and dark mode themes with styled-components and persist it on cookies, I\u2019m trying to do this for my portfolio but it\u2019s not working, and I can\u2019t fix it, It would be great an example like this inside the examples folder.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "washington299", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T00:04:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T22:30:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11577", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11580, "title": "Default built-in postcss plugins dependency error in lastest nextjs 9.3.4", "body": "After updating to 9.3.4, the following dependency error occurred. As far as I know, plugins like postcss-flexbugs-fixes are built into nextjs by default, but should I explicitly install them now?\r\n\r\n```\r\n> Ready on http://localhost:3000\r\n[ ready ] compiled successfully\r\n[ event ] build page: /\r\n[ wait ]  compiling ...\r\n[ error ] ./assets/global.scss\r\nError: Loading PostCSS Plugin failed: Cannot find module 'postcss-flexbugs-fixes'\r\n```", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "gtolarc", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T01:34:52Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T12:52:55Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11580", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11588, "title": "About SSR, SSG, iSSG, please correct me if I'm wrong.", "body": "1. `getStaticProps()` with `fallback: true` works only with `target: \"server\"` build, not work with serverless build. Also require `next start` to bootstrap the server.\r\n2. `getStaticProps()` and `fallback: true`, require a server to work. And the generated static files store in the file system of the server. And I'm supposed to set a Nginx proxy to handle the requests to those static files.\r\n3. user navigate between pages with `<Link />` component also trigger `getStaticProps()` or `getServerSideProps()`, and the slow server response can potentially slow down the transition.\r\n4. In my case. I want to deploy my project to a serverless service (I really love Now, but I can't due to some reasons). And my project contains about 4,000 static pages whose data source is changed once a week. \r\n    1. Firstly, I gave up on exporting the 4,000 pages in the build time which is a lot work.\r\n    2. Since `getStaticProps()` along with `getSaticPaths()` and `fallout` came out, I found that it only work with a custom server while I don't want to maintain one.\r\n    3. However `getServerSideProps()` works great with serverless build, I don't think it is performant that every request to the pages require a backend API call, since they are relatively static.\r\n   4. So, I'd like to have a feature that output a `build function` in the serverless build which act like the `next start` with ordinary server build. When a request incoming, it initially calls this `build function` to check the existence of the generated static file (through a configured location, in my case, it is a url of the cloud storage), generates the files if needs to.\r\n\r\nI don't know if there are any misunderstandings above. Please correct me if there is anything wrong!\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "grepug", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T06:47:32Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-19T09:19:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11588", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11589, "title": "How easy is it to use Next.js with non-Node API server?", "body": "I'm interested in using Next.js for SSR, but I'm not a big fan of JavaScript for API code in general. \r\n\r\n1. How easy is it to use Next.js with an API server in Ruby or Flask?\r\n\r\n2. What functionality is lost by using my own API server?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Dehorser", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T08:26:40Z", "updated_at": "2023-04-27T09:17:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11589", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11590, "title": "Using getServerSideProps inside a component", "body": "I want to dynamically get data based on the query parameter. \r\n\r\nUsing `getServerSideProps` in pages work, but how can I do the same (getting data server side only) in components?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 10, "reactions_plus": 10, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mafshin", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T08:30:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T12:12:14Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11590", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11593, "title": "next build failed due to serverRuntimeConfig env vars missing", "body": "Hello guys and thanks a lot for the good effort put on NextJS! : )\r\n\r\nI tried to find answers for the following without much success.\r\n\r\nWe are working on a Dockerized NextJS application that is thought to be built once and deployed to several environments for which we will have different configuration. This configuration is to be set in the Docker container when deployed as environment variables.\r\n\r\nIn order to achieve this, we are using `next.config.js` file, splitting the vars on `serverRuntimeConfig` and `publicRuntimeConfig` as suggested [here](https://nextjs.org/docs/api-reference/next.config.js/runtime-configuration), and we are getting the values for the environment variables from `process.env`. i.e.:\r\n\r\n```\r\nmodule.exports = {\r\n  serverRuntimeConfig: {\r\n    mySecret: process.env.MY_SECRET,\r\n    secondSecret: process.env.SECOND_SECRET,\r\n  },\r\n  publicRuntimeConfig: {\r\n    staticFolder: process.env.STATIC_FOLDER_URL,\r\n  },\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe problem we have is that these variables are not set on build time ( when we run `next build` ), as they are environment specific and supposed to be set on deployment. Because of this, the build fails complaining about the missing variables.\r\n\r\nMaking a build per environment is not an option: as referred before, we want to build it once (with `next build`), put the output of the build in a Docker container, and use that Docker container to deploy in several environments.\r\n\r\nIs there any way to solve this so that the application builds without environment vars and we pass them afterwards on runtime ( deployment )?\r\n\r\nThanks in advance!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 6, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "JoseMartinC", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T09:53:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T15:00:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11593", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11605, "title": "Trying to log requested URL", "body": "Hi, I'm trying to log the request URL that's made that results in a 404 error. \r\n\r\nWhen I deploy my nextjs app with next start, I don't get any request logging, so if someone requests a missing page, all I can do is log from a custom 404 page. So I can find out when the 404 happens, but I want the actual URL that was requested.\r\n\r\nIf I use useRouter and log the request on the 404 page, of course the only path I can access is /404, as that is what they got routed to.\r\n\r\nI tried seeing if getInitialProps or getServerSideProps could tell me the actual requested URL, but these are not permitted on the /404 page.\r\n\r\nHow can I actually find out the actual requested URL for a 404 (or any other request for that matter)?\r\n\r\nOr does anyone know a way to increase some existing  logging that would tell me? \r\n\r\nThanks", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "RyanPridgeon", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T17:56:11Z", "updated_at": "2023-05-04T02:21:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11605", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11606, "title": "Distributing a next.js app via NPM", "body": "(related to and coppied from https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/11411)\r\n\r\nHello!\r\n\r\nWe are looking at distributing our next.js application via NPM, but are having trouble getting webpack to work properly when running next from /within/ `node_modules`.   We want to allow folks to run `next dev` and `next build` in our project so they can add plugins and modify behavior - meaning we can't run `next build` and ship that ourselves.  Our users would install peerDependencies which would be bundled into the next application. \r\n\r\nThe goal is to have something like this: \r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI've published [`next-subproject-example` (github)](https://github.com/evantahler/next-subproject-example) to npm which is a very simple next.js application with one page and no custom config. \r\n\r\n1. Make a new project with the following `package.json`, which aims to consume `next-subproject-example`\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"name\": \"my-project\",\r\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\r\n  \"description\": \"\",\r\n  \"main\": \"index.js\",\r\n  \"scripts\": {\r\n    \"dev\": \"cd node_modules/next-subproject-example && npm run dev\",\r\n    \"build\": \"cd node_modules/next-subproject-example && npm run prepare\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"dependencies\": {\r\n    \"next-subproject-example\": \"latest\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"license\": \"ISC\",\r\n  \"devDependencies\": {\r\n    \"@types/node\": \"^13.9.5\",\r\n    \"@types/react\": \"^16.9.26\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. `npm install`\r\n3. `npm run dev`\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 13, "reactions_total": 5, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 5, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "evantahler", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-02T17:57:26Z", "updated_at": "2026-01-01T12:25:49Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11606", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11607, "title": "Yarn 2 not working with basic setup", "body": "I'm looking for some guidance on this problem I'm having. I am looking into Yarn 2 with Next.js and I cannot get a basic example working. Here is my setup\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// package.json\r\n{\r\n    \"name\": \"testing-yarn-2\",\r\n    \"scripts\": {\r\n        \"dev\": \"next dev\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"dependencies\": {\r\n        \"next\": \"^9.3.4\",\r\n        \"react\": \"^16.13.1\",\r\n        \"react-dom\": \"^16.13.1\"\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter running `yarn` to install dependencies, I've tried running `yarn dev` as well as `yarn next`. Both output the following error message:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\nmy-app $ yarn next\r\n[ wait ]  starting the development server ...\r\n[ info ]  waiting on http://localhost:3000 ...\r\nError: A package is trying to access another package without the second one being listed as a dependency of the first one\r\n\r\nRequired package: strip-ansi (via \"strip-ansi\")\r\nRequired by: next@virtual:...\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n    <summary>Additional terminal output</summary>\r\n\r\n```js\r\n{\r\n    code: 'MODULE_NOT_FOUND',\r\n    pnpCode: 'UNDECLARED_DEPENDENCY',\r\n    data: {\r\n        request: 'strip-ansi',\r\n        issuer: '...virtual/next/dist/build/',\r\n        issuerLocator: {\r\n            name: 'next',\r\n            reference: 'virtual:....next-9.3.4.tgz'\r\n        },\r\n        dependencyName: 'strip-ansi',\r\n        candidates: [\r\n            'next',\r\n            '@ampproject/toolbox-optimizer',\r\n            '@babel/core',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-proposal-class-properties',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-proposal-nullish-coalescing-operator',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-proposal-numeric-separator',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-proposal-object-rest-spread',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-proposal-optional-chaining',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-syntax-bigint',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-syntax-dynamic-import',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-transform-modules-commonjs',\r\n            '@babel/plugin-transform-runtime',\r\n            '@babel/preset-env',\r\n            '@babel/preset-modules',\r\n            '@babel/preset-react',\r\n            '@babel/preset-typescript',\r\n            '@babel/runtime',\r\n            '@babel/types',\r\n            'babel-plugin-syntax-jsx',\r\n            'babel-plugin-transform-define',\r\n            'babel-plugin-transform-react-remove-prop-types',\r\n            'browserslist',\r\n            'css-loader',\r\n            'fork-ts-checker-webpack-plugin',\r\n            'jest-worker',\r\n            'loader-utils',\r\n            'mini-css-extract-plugin',\r\n            'native-url',\r\n            'pnp-webpack-plugin',\r\n            'postcss',\r\n            'prop-types',\r\n            'prop-types-exact',\r\n            'react',\r\n            'react-dom',\r\n            'react-is',\r\n            'resolve-url-loader',\r\n            'sass-loader',\r\n            'style-loader',\r\n            'styled-jsx',\r\n            'use-subscription',\r\n            'watchpack',\r\n            'webpack',\r\n            'webpack-sources'\r\n        ]\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n</details>\r\n\r\nAny guidance on this?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Kinbaum", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-02T18:16:00Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T21:58:27Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11607", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11622, "title": "Next.js application is looking for wrong \"_next\" directory after build & start", "body": "I'm running build & start with my Next.js application on my own server. The page content (HTML tags and text) is loading fine but nothing else loads (css, js etc). When I go to the console all the assets are returning 404 error because it's trying to search for a directory which doesn't exist, for example:\r\n\r\nhttps://mywebsite.com/_next/static/css/styles.a95cabed.chunk.css\r\n\r\nBut the problem is that **_next** doesn't exist on my server, I can see only **.next** directory. Why the build is generating different directories? Sorry if it's a newbie issue, I'm still learning React & Next.js. Locally everything works fine.\r\n\r\nThank you!\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\nBoth my laptop and server are Ubuntu 18.04\r\n\r\n\"dependencies\": {\r\n    \"@tanem/react-nprogress\": \"^3.0.19\",\r\n    \"@zeit/next-sass\": \"^1.0.1\",\r\n    \"leaflet\": \"^1.6.0\",\r\n    \"next\": \"9.2.2\",\r\n    \"next-i18next\": \"^4.2.0\",\r\n    \"node-sass\": \"^4.13.1\",\r\n    \"react\": \"16.12.0\",\r\n    \"react-dom\": \"16.12.0\",\r\n    \"react-icons\": \"^3.9.0\",\r\n    \"react-leaflet\": \"^2.6.3\",\r\n    \"react-reveal\": \"^1.2.2\"\r\n  }\r\n\r\nUPDATE:\r\n\r\nI will try to describe the steps I'm taking:\r\n\r\n1 - The directory on my server is empty\r\n2 - I upload the following files/directories from my PC:\r\npackage.json\r\ni18n.js\r\nyarn.lock\r\nnext.config.js\r\nserver.js\r\n/pages\r\n/styles\r\n/components\r\n/lib\r\n/contexts\r\n/public\r\n3 - On my server I run the command \"yarn\" to download the packages\r\n4 - I run \"yarn build\" (next build)\r\n5 - Then \"yarn start\" (NODE_ENV=production node server.js)\r\n6 - \"Ready on http://localhost:3000\" is displayed on the console, in my server the port 3000 is used proxy (Nginx) pointing to the test domain I'm using: https://node.alexandrepaiva.dev\r\n7 - No asset is loaded (You can see the browser console error) - Because it's trying to load from the directory that doesn't exist (that I explained originally on the post)\r\n\r\nNote: locally everything works fine\r\n\r\nI'd like to know what I'm doing wrong and what to do to fix it?\r\n\r\nThank you!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 17, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "alexandrepaiva-dev", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-02T23:51:31Z", "updated_at": "2025-03-25T08:57:16Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11622", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11625, "title": "Is shallow (Instagram-style) routing on dynamic routes possible?", "body": "One thing I've never been able to get working is the Instagram-style \"clicking a link opens a modal, hitting refresh shows a full page\" routing behavior when working with dynamic routes.\r\n\r\nHere's an example of a link from a demo project I was hacking on to try and get this working:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n<Link\r\n  href={`/artists/${artist.id}/?showAlbum=${album.id}`}\r\n  as={`/albums/${album.id}`}\r\n  scroll={false}\r\n  shallow\r\n>\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere's a live demo:\r\n\r\nhttps://headbangstagram-18x9bitrm.now.sh/artists/1\r\n\r\nNotice that clicking an album cover goes to a new page instead of doing a shallow navigation (which should open a modal)?\r\n\r\nHere's a direct link that includes the `showAlbum` query param, so you can see what should be happening:\r\n\r\nhttps://headbangstagram-18x9bitrm.now.sh/artists/1?showAlbum=1\r\n\r\n...and here's the source on GitHub for anyone interested:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/adamwathan/headbangstagram/blob/master/pages/artists/%5Bid%5D.js#L58-L63\r\n\r\nIs there any way to make this actually work with dynamic routes?\r\n\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 5, "reactions_plus": 5, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "adamwathan", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-03T01:42:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-21T12:31:49Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11625", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11631, "title": "Can't import named exports from an external NPM library with TypeScript", "body": "Hello friends,\r\n\r\nI'm unable to import named exports from an external component library I've created as it is described in the [official documentation](https://nextjs.org/docs/advanced-features/dynamic-import). The situation seems to be very niche as I struggle to find anything online that would bring me closer to the solution. Did anyone manage to get it to work?\r\n\r\nHere comes the context...\r\n\r\n## In my NPM library\r\n### Component\r\n\r\n**Modal component** (fragment)\r\n```ts\r\nconst Modal: React.FC<ModalProps> = ({ onCancelCallback, children }) => {...};\r\nexport default Modal;\r\n```\r\nAs you can see, I export it as a default from Modal.tsx but later re-export it as a named export in every folder's index.ts file like so:\r\n```ts\r\nexport { default as Modal } from './Modal';\r\n```\r\nthen\r\n```ts\r\nexport { Modal } from './Modal';\r\n```\r\nand, finally:\r\n```ts\r\nexport * from './components';\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Interface\r\n**ModalProps**\r\n```ts\r\nexport interface ModalProps {\r\n  onCancelCallback?: () => void;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n## In Next.js\r\nHere's how I'm importing my external \"Modal\" component in one of the Next.js components (not pages):\r\n\r\n**nextjscomponent.tsx**\r\n```ts\r\nimport dynamic from 'next/dynamic';\r\nconst Modal = dynamic(() => import('my-ui-kit').then((mod) => mod.Modal), {\r\n  ssr: false,\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n**TypeScript error**\r\n```sh\r\nArgument of type '() => Promise<React.ComponentClass<never, any> | React.FunctionComponent<never> | { default: React.ComponentType<never>; } | React.FC<ModalProps>>' is not assignable to parameter of type 'DynamicOptions<{}> | (() => LoaderComponent<{}>) | LoaderComponent<{}>'.\r\n  Type '() => Promise<React.ComponentClass<never, any> | React.FunctionComponent<never> | { default: React.ComponentType<never>; } | React.FC<ModalProps>>' is not assignable to type '() => LoaderComponent<{}>'.\r\n    Type 'Promise<ComponentClass<never, any> | FunctionComponent<never> | { default: ComponentType<never>; } | FC<ModalProps>>' is not assignable to type 'LoaderComponent<{}>'.\r\n      Type 'ComponentClass<never, any> | FunctionComponent<never> | { default: ComponentType<never>; } | FC<ModalProps>' is not assignable to type 'ComponentClass<{}, any> | FunctionComponent<{}> | { default: ComponentType<{}>; }'.\r\n        Type 'ComponentClass<never, any>' is not assignable to type 'ComponentClass<{}, any> | FunctionComponent<{}> | { default: ComponentType<{}>; }'.\r\n          Type 'ComponentClass<never, any>' is not assignable to type 'ComponentClass<{}, any>'.\r\n            Types of property 'getDerivedStateFromProps' are incompatible.\r\n              Type 'GetDerivedStateFromProps<never, any> | undefined' is not assignable to type 'GetDerivedStateFromProps<{}, any> | undefined'.\r\n                Type 'GetDerivedStateFromProps<never, any>' is not assignable to type 'GetDerivedStateFromProps<{}, any>'.\r\n                  Types of parameters 'nextProps' and 'nextProps' are incompatible.\r\n                    Type 'Readonly<{}>' is not assignable to type 'never'.ts(2345)\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Notes\r\n- I'm using Rollup to transpile the contents of \"src\" folder with component and index files into \"dist\" folder which ends up with index.cjs.js (for CommonJS), index.esm.js (for ES modules) and a bunch of automatically generated .d.ts files.\r\n- I'm using Yarn link to test the integration of my external library into my Next.js project locally.\r\n\r\nAny help is highly appreciated. Thank you in advance.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 5, "reactions_plus": 5, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "malikalimoekhamedov", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-03T10:59:20Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T04:31:16Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11631", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11632, "title": "Next.js source maps with typescript on Sentry", "body": "I have a simple setup for a project that imitates the [Next JS sentry (simple) example](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/tree/canary/examples/with-sentry-simple)\r\n\r\nThe problem is without sentry `Enable JavaScript source fetching` feature on, I cannot get the source maps to report correctly to sentry\r\nexample: [![enter image description here][1]][1]\r\n\r\nwith the `Enable JavaScript source fetching` it shows correctly\r\nexample (of the same error): [![enter image description here][2]][2]\r\n\r\nHere is the configuration files used:\r\n```\r\n// next.config.js\r\nconst { parsed: localEnv } = require(\"dotenv\").config();\r\nconst webpack = require(\"webpack\");\r\nconst TsconfigPathsPlugin = require(\"tsconfig-paths-webpack-plugin\");\r\n\r\n// Package.json => \"@zeit/next-source-maps\": \"^0.0.4-canary.1\",\r\nconst withSourceMaps = require(\"@zeit/next-source-maps\")({ devtool: \"source-map\" });\r\n\r\nmodule.exports = withSourceMaps({\r\n  target: \"serverless\",\r\n  env: {\r\n    // Will be available on both server and client\r\n    // Sentry DNS configurations\r\n    SENTRY_DNS: process.env.SENTRY_DNS,\r\n  },\r\n  poweredByHeader: false,\r\n\r\n  webpack(config, options) {\r\n    config.plugins.push(new webpack.EnvironmentPlugin(localEnv));\r\n    config.resolve.plugins.push(new TsconfigPathsPlugin());\r\n    config.node = {\r\n      // Fixes node packages that depend on `fs` module\r\n      fs: \"empty\",\r\n    };\r\n\r\n    if (!options.isServer) {\r\n      config.resolve.alias[\"@sentry/node\"] = \"@sentry/browser\";\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    return config;\r\n  },\r\n});\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `src/pages/_app.tsx` and `src/pages/_error.tsx` follow the example mentioned in the repo.\r\n\r\n```\r\n// tsconfig.json\r\n{\r\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\r\n    \"allowJs\": true,\r\n    \"allowSyntheticDefaultImports\": true,\r\n    \"baseUrl\": \".\",\r\n    \"declaration\": false,\r\n    \"emitDecoratorMetadata\": true,\r\n    \"esModuleInterop\": true,\r\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\r\n    \"forceConsistentCasingInFileNames\": true,\r\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\r\n    \"lib\": [\r\n      \"dom\",\r\n      \"dom.iterable\",\r\n      \"esnext\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"module\": \"esnext\",\r\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"node\",\r\n    \"noEmit\": true,\r\n    \"noImplicitAny\": true,\r\n    \"noImplicitReturns\": true,\r\n    \"paths\": {\r\n      \"@src/*\": [\"./src/*\"],\r\n      \"@components/*\": [\"./src/components/*\"],\r\n      \"@services/*\": [\"./src/services/*\"],\r\n      \"@utils/*\": [\"./src/utils/*\"]\r\n    },\r\n    \"removeComments\": false,\r\n    \"resolveJsonModule\": true,\r\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\r\n    \"sourceMap\": true,\r\n    \"sourceRoot\": \"/\",\r\n    \"strict\": true,\r\n    \"target\": \"es6\",\r\n    \"jsx\": \"preserve\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"exclude\": [\r\n    \"node_modules\",\r\n    \"cypress\",\r\n    \"test\",\r\n    \"public\",\r\n    \"out\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"include\": [\r\n    \"next-env.d.ts\",\r\n    \"**/*.ts\",\r\n    \"**/*.tsx\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"compileOnSave\": false\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe source maps are uploaded to sentry during CI build process\r\nUsing this script (after `next build` and `next export`)\r\n\r\n```\r\nconfigure-sentry-release.sh\r\n#!/bin/bash\r\nset -eo pipefail\r\n\r\n# Install Sentry-CLI\r\ncurl -sL https://sentry.io/get-cli/ | bash\r\n\r\nexport SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=\"production\"\r\nexport SENTRY_RELEASE=$(sentry-cli releases propose-version)\r\n\r\n# Configure the release and upload source maps\r\necho \"=> Configure Release: $SENTRY_RELEASE :: $SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT\"\r\nsentry-cli releases new $SENTRY_RELEASE --project $SENTRY_PROJECT\r\nsentry-cli releases set-commits --auto $SENTRY_RELEASE\r\nsentry-cli releases files $SENTRY_RELEASE upload-sourcemaps \".next\" --url-prefix \"~/_next\" --rewrite --verbose --validate\r\nsentry-cli releases deploys $SENTRY_RELEASE new -e $SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT\r\nsentry-cli releases finalize $SENTRY_RELEASE\r\n```\r\n\r\nI can verify that the source maps are uploaded to sentry (for each build -example log below-)\r\n```\r\n> Uploading release files...\r\n\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2588\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591\u2591  705.36KB/1.94MB (1s)\r\n> Uploaded release files to Sentry\r\n\u2809 Processing files...\r\n> File upload complete\r\n\r\nSource Map Upload Report\r\n  Scripts\r\n    ~/_next/serverless/init-server.js\r\n    ...\r\n\r\n  Minified Scripts\r\n    ~/_next/static/47Aa7MafJzmEcVJNWA4bF/pages/_app.js (sourcemap at _app.js.map)\r\n    ~/_next/static/47Aa7MafJzmEcVJNWA4bF/pages/_error.js (sourcemap at _error.js.map)\r\n    ~/_next/static/47Aa7MafJzmEcVJNWA4bF/pages/demo.js (sourcemap at demo.js.map)\r\n    ~/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.d1e174b5998c49a0d977.js (sourcemap at <HASH_REDACTED>.d1e174b5998c49a0d977.js.map)\r\n    ~/_next/static/chunks/commons.9c904bb4eb4d53773228.js (sourcemap at commons.9c904bb4eb4d53773228.js.map)\r\n    ... \r\n    \r\n  Source Maps\r\n    ~/_next/serverless/init-server.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/serverless/on-error-server.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/serverless/pages/_error.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/serverless/pages/demo.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/serverless/pages/index.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/static/47Aa7MafJzmEcVJNWA4bF/pages/_app.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/static/47Aa7MafJzmEcVJNWA4bF/pages/_error.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/static/47Aa7MafJzmEcVJNWA4bF/pages/demo.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/static/47Aa7MafJzmEcVJNWA4bF/pages/index.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.d1e174b5998c49a0d977.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/static/chunks/commons.9c904bb4eb4d53773228.js.map\r\n    ~/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.bffb6f5b8a05970a450a.js.map\r\n    ...\r\nFinalized release <HASH_REDACTED>.\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs there anyway to get the source maps to work with sentry (without the `Enable JavaScript source fetching` and without leaving the source map publicly available on the server)?\r\n\r\n  [1]: https://i.stack.imgur.com/P6CPV.png\r\n  [2]: https://i.stack.imgur.com/CxPHX.png", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "a14m", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-03T11:53:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-02T14:07:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11632", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11633, "title": "How to load polyfills based on usage?", "body": "I'm trying to load polyfills based on usage, ie. babel preset-env useBuiltIns: usage option. I have a page like this:\r\n\r\n```\r\nexport default () => {\r\n  console.log(Object.fromEntries([]));\r\n  return null;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen I compile that JS file with babel cli and the babel config `[\"@babel/preset-env\", { useBuiltIns: \"usage\", corejs: \"3\" }]`, then the file that is generated includes all the necessary polyfills. However when I configure next/babel preset-env with the same options, then the polyfills are not loaded.\r\n\r\nEssentially: how do I make this not crash in Edge 17: https://codesandbox.io/s/next-polyfill-howto-lqy3x (btw, the useEffect is there because CSB runs an older version of node.js).", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "wereHamster", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-03T13:30:41Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T16:44:59Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11633", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11634, "title": "upload file and save it to public directory", "body": "I've been looking example for uploading multiple files to the nextjs api. So I come up with my own solution here. \r\n\r\nhere the request from client\r\n```js\r\nconst onSubmit = values => {\r\n    const { userfile } = values;\r\n    const data = new FormData();\r\n    data.append(\"file\", userfile[0]);\r\n    fetch(\"/api/upload\", {\r\n      method: \"POST\",\r\n      // headers: {\r\n      //   \"Content-Type\": \"multipart/form-data\"\r\n      // },\r\n      body: data\r\n    })\r\n      .then(response => response.json())\r\n      .then(data => {\r\n        console.log(data);\r\n      })\r\n      .catch(error => {\r\n        console.error(error);\r\n      });\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nthis is the `api/upload.js` content\r\n```js\r\nimport Formidable from \"Formidable\";\r\nconst fs = require(\"fs\");\r\n\r\nexport const config = {\r\n  api: {\r\n    bodyParser: false\r\n  }\r\n};\r\n\r\nconst uploadForm = next => (req, res) => {\r\n  return new Promise(async (resolve, reject) => {\r\n    try {\r\n      const form = new Formidable.IncomingForm({\r\n        multiples: true,\r\n        keepExtensions: true\r\n      });\r\n      form.once(\"error\", console.error);\r\n      form\r\n        .on(\"fileBegin\", (name, file) => {\r\n          console.log(\"start uploading: \", file.name);\r\n        })\r\n        .on(\"aborted\", () => console.log(\"Aborted...\"));\r\n      form.once(\"end\", () => {\r\n        console.log(\"Done!\");\r\n      });\r\n      await form.parse(req, async (err, fields, files) => {\r\n        if (err) {\r\n          throw String(JSON.stringify(err, null, 2));\r\n        }\r\n        console.log(\r\n          \"moving file: \",\r\n          files.file.path,\r\n          \" to \",\r\n          `public/upload/${files.file.name}`\r\n        );\r\n        // await fs.rename(\r\n        //   files.file.path,\r\n        //   `public/upload/${files.file.name}`,\r\n        //   err => {\r\n        //     if (err) throw err;\r\n        //   }\r\n        // );\r\n        fs.renameSync(files.file.path, `public/upload/${files.file.name}`);\r\n        req.form = { fields, files };\r\n        return resolve(next(req, res));\r\n      });\r\n    } catch (error) {\r\n      return resolve(res.status(403).send(error));\r\n    }\r\n  });\r\n};\r\n\r\nfunction handler(req, res) {\r\n  try {\r\n    if (req.method === \"POST\") {\r\n      res.status(200).send(req.form);\r\n    } else {\r\n      throw String(\"Method not allowed\");\r\n    }\r\n  } catch (error) {\r\n    res.status(400).json({ message: JSON.stringify(error, null, 2) });\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default uploadForm(handler);\r\n```\r\n\r\n I just do simple image upload, it is working but the file is in incomplete shape/form. What do you guys think that I missed?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 25, "reactions_total": 25, "reactions_plus": 20, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 5, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "muhaimincs", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-03T14:13:50Z", "updated_at": "2026-04-07T15:21:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11634", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11640, "title": "How to make next.js cancel long running processes", "body": "Every now and then, our next.js app running on heroku will go down for no apparent reason. We reached out to Heroku and the gist of what they said is that:\r\n\r\n1. Our application most likely had a long running task going\r\n2. The heroku router ended that request\r\n3. Dyno kept working on that task\r\n4. The router sent another request to the stuck dyno\r\n5. Compounding effect occurred\r\n\r\nThe heroku rep suggested the express `timeout` library which would help. Without having to use our own express server, is there a way to tell next.js \"Cancel any server side processes that take longer than X seconds and respond with a 408\"?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Chrischuck", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-03T18:44:22Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-30T15:17:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11640", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11641, "title": "Support for Lerna default symlinks in pages/", "body": "# Bug report\r\n\r\n## Describe the bug\r\n\r\nA clear and concise description of what the bug is.\r\n\r\n[Similar issues](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/3728) have been extensively discussed, but I haven't found a concise answer for my particular quandary yet.\r\n\r\nI am managing [this Next.js project](https://github.com/tutorbookapp/covid-tutoring) and want to use [Lerna](https://lerna.js.org) to keep each component as a separate `npm` package within the `src/` directory:\r\n```\r\n.                      \r\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 form\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 index.module.scss\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 index.tsx\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 node_modules\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2514\u2500\u2500 @tutorbook\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 animated-checkmark-overlay -> ../../../animated-checkmark-overlay\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 arrow-btn -> ../../../arrow-btn\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 covid-fonts -> ../../../fonts\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u2514\u2500\u2500 spinner -> ../../../spinner\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 package.json\r\n\u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2514\u2500\u2500 package-lock.json\r\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 pages       \r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 about.tsx   \r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 _app.tsx    \r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 index.tsx        \r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 node_modules\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0 \u2514\u2500\u2500 @tutorbook\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 covid-about -> ../../../about\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 covid-footer -> ../../../footer\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 covid-header -> ../../../header\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 covid-pupil-form -> ../../../pupil-form\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 covid-styles -> ../../../styles\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u251c\u2500\u2500 covid-tutor-form -> ../../../tutor-form\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2502\u00a0\u00a0     \u2514\u2500\u2500 next-firebase -> ../../../firebase\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 package.json                         \r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 package-lock.json\r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u251c\u2500\u2500 pupils.tsx       \r\n  \u00a0\u00a0 \u2514\u2500\u2500 tutors.tsx\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nA clear and concise description of what you expected to happen.\r\n\r\nWe should be able to ignore\r\n\r\n## Screenshots\r\n\r\nIf applicable, add screenshots to help explain your problem.\r\n\r\nThose Lerna-generated symlinks have some issues though as users can access the individual components:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/20798889/78394579-bd567b00-75a0-11ea-85ce-e86dfe324e3d.png)\r\n\r\n## System information\r\n\r\n- OS: Ubuntu 18.04.2\r\n- Version of Next.js: `^9.3.4`\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "nicholaschiang", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-03T18:47:04Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-24T14:34:35Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11641", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11649, "title": "How to remove the viewport tag of head?", "body": "Hi! Is there a way to remove the vieport tag of head. I find the `viewport` tag will antomatically be added to the page No matter what way I use.  So, is there a way to remove the viewport tag of page? Thanks!", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "wangyi7099", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T01:57:05Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-01T14:27:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11649", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11650, "title": "Router.on.events not firing", "body": "I currently have a custom server in a test app \r\n```\r\n// import App from 'next/app'\r\n\r\nimport { useEffect } from \"react\"\r\nimport Router from 'next/router'\r\n\r\nfunction MyApp({ Component, pageProps }) {\r\n    useEffect(() => {\r\n        const handleRouteChange = url => {\r\n          console.log('App is changing to: ', url)\r\n        }\r\n      \r\n        Router.events.on('routeChangeStart', handleRouteChange)\r\n        return () => {\r\n          Router.events.off('routeChangeStart', handleRouteChange)\r\n        }\r\n    }, [])\r\n\r\n\r\n    return <Component {...pageProps} />\r\n  }\r\n  \r\n  // Only uncomment this method if you have blocking data requirements for\r\n  // every single page in your application. This disables the ability to\r\n  // perform automatic static optimization, causing every page in your app to\r\n  // be server-side rendered.\r\n  //\r\n  // MyApp.getInitialProps = async (appContext) => {\r\n  //   // calls page's `getInitialProps` and fills `appProps.pageProps`\r\n  //   const appProps = await App.getInitialProps(appContext);\r\n  //\r\n  //   return { ...appProps }\r\n  // }\r\n  \r\n  export default MyApp\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhich is nearly identical to the example that was given in the documents. When I go from \"/\" to \"/test\" on the browser, which is another page I've created in my test app, I can never see the console.log fire which seems to indicate that the events are never firing for whatever reason. \r\n\r\nI'm not sure if I\"m doing something wrong or if this is a bug", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 9, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "clarencejychan", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T07:45:23Z", "updated_at": "2024-01-22T20:17:44Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11650", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11654, "title": "Where is application error logs stored? ", "body": "Some of my website's users reported a bug telling me they keep getting  `\"An unexpected error has occurred\"` error.I couldn't find any way to get exception detail from their devices.The only thing come to mind is checking application error log file to get stack trace, but couldn't find `nextjs` error log file either.could you please tell me how can i debug my application? ", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "B-Esmaili", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T08:27:26Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-06T14:17:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11654", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11656, "title": "PORT from environment in doker-compose.yml", "body": "It would be possible that Next.js in dev mode could obtain the `port `from the environment if it existed and was not indicated by the `-p`?\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\ndocker-compose.yml\r\n```yml\r\nclient:\r\n    environment:\r\n      PORT: ${PORT_CLIENT}\r\n      NODE_ENV: ${NODE_ENV}\r\n```\r\npackage.json\r\n\r\n`\"dev\": \"next\",`", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "chiqui3d", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T10:21:09Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:55Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11656", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11661, "title": "Synchronously execution tasks in API routes", "body": "I want to send an email when a API route is hit, but I don't want to wait till the email is send to send a response. But when I don't await the response I get this error: Client network socket disconnected before secure TSL connection was established. I am using SendGrid/mail for this and I basically to this https://zeit.co/guides/deploying-nextjs-nodejs-and-sendgrid-with-zeit-now without await the response and it's a GraphQL API using Nexus.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "enricoschaaf", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T13:52:26Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-24T01:27:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11661", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11663, "title": "Memory leak in next/head ", "body": "Hello awesome nextjs team,\r\n\r\nIt took us few weeks to conclude that instability of our production app is due to next/head memory leak. \r\nwe have memory & cpu profiling log(can be loaded into chrome debugger) if someone want to check. \r\n\r\nThis issue is already reported few times ref: 1) [fatal CPU usage growth with webpack externals & next/head](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/6914)  , 2) [next/head has a memory leak issue](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/6955) , 3)[next/side-effect is not thread-safe ](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/5525) and few more may be marked duplicated.\r\nAll these issues were goingto be fixed by  [[RFC] Deprecate next/head](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/8981) but this has been put on halt(close) with following comment\r\n\r\n\"Closing as AFAIK we're not sure next/head deprecation is required (per last meeting). @devknoll: let me know if we should reopen this.\" https://github.com/zeit/next.js/pull/9084#issuecomment-576423235 . \r\n\r\nOur website has on average 8k-9k daily page visits and not to mention tens of thousands of google crawler calls. And with this memory leak it is becoming a showstopper for us as it requires a reboot after few hours. \r\nAs the root cause of this problem has already been found could someone please suggest a way forward for people in situation like us, may be an example of correctly using react-helment-async with latest Nextjs would unblock us.  The official example in react-helment-async docs is still using `next/head` so really we need an example which does not use `next/head`. \r\n  \r\nbest regards", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "asim-basraa", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T15:24:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T18:24:18Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11663", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11664, "title": "How to use a react package in nextjs that contains css", "body": "Hello is been some days now that am trying to use a react-package in my next project but each time, time i wish to import  i get this error\r\n```\r\nGlobal CSS cannot be imported from within node_modules.\r\nRead more: https://err.sh/next.js/css-npm\r\nLocation: node_modules/react-images-upload/compiled.js\r\n```\r\nPlease can someone tell me how i can go about using this component with having this error ?", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "lewatt23", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T15:35:21Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T06:33:08Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11664", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11668, "title": "How does session management work with firebase-auth-serverless?", "body": "In the [serverless firebase auth example](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-firebase-authentication-serverless), there is an instruction to store the authentication token with something like `express-session`. However, `express-session` would only make sense in a scenario where there is a server, right? Not in a serverless environment.\r\n\r\nI suppose we can use an external store, like [connect-dynamodb](https://www.npmjs.com/package/connect-dynamodb), but, won't that mean every lambda will have to import code to query the external database?\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/examples/with-firebase-authentication-serverless/pages/api/login.js#L12", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "vedantroy", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-04T18:03:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-29T05:11:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11668", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11672, "title": "Pass data from _app.getInitialProps to page getInitialProps", "body": "Hi all, I am trying to migrate a large system of websites to Next.js, and I have a quick question. The site is currently using redux to store fetched server data (we will move away from this eventually, but this is how it is for now). I initially build the store in my `_app.getInitialProps` and provide the initial state to the `_app` component, which then passes it down the tree in context. \r\n\r\nHowever, it is my understanding that tree context is not available in a page's `getInitialProps`. In this function I need to perform some data fetching and update the store with the result. How do you suggest I access the store that I created in `_app`? I have tried passing it directly when calling the page's `getInitialProps` from within `_app`, but despite providing it to the function, it doesn't seem to be passed. See below:\r\n```js\r\n// pages/_app.tsx\r\nfunction MyApp({\r\n  Component,\r\n  pageProps,\r\n  store\r\n}: AppProps) {\r\n  return (\r\n    <StoreProvider\r\n      store={store}\r\n    >\r\n      <Component {...pageProps} />\r\n    </ContextProvider>\r\n  );\r\n}\r\n\r\nMyApp.getInitialProps = async appContext => {\r\n  const store = getOrCreateStore(pathFor);\r\n  /**\r\n   * call the page's getInitialProps, which may further update the redux store.\r\n   */\r\nconst pageProps = await App.getInitialProps({\r\n    ...appContext,\r\n  // the store here doesn't actually get passed to getInitialProps\r\n    store,\r\n  });\r\n  /**\r\n   * Pass updated redux state to the app component to be used to rebuild\r\n   * the store on client side with pre-filled data from ssr\r\n   */\r\n  const initialState = store.getState();\r\n\r\n  return {\r\n    initialState,\r\n    pageProps\r\n  };\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nI have looked at the examples, but they don't seem to initialize the store in the `_app` component, so I don't think they fit my use case.\r\n\r\nI also will be moving away from `getInitialProps` to `getServerSideProps` as soon as I can, but I'm not sure how that is possible for my setup currently.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kristojorg", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-05T00:42:52Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-23T20:05:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11672", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11673, "title": "Not clear on the use of getStaticProps and getStaticPaths with dynamic routes", "body": "I am finding the docs a little ambiguous. Given a list franchises, we would like to render out the relevant franchise detail pages at build time to avoid hitting the CMS/API at run time as these don't change frequently.\r\n\r\nHowever, it seems that even if the relevant pages are generated at build time, they still require the calls in `getStaticProps` to be executed at runtime. This defeats the purpose of generating static pages.\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport {withLayout} from '../../components/layout';\r\nimport {getFranchises} from '../api/franchises';\r\n\r\nconst Franchise = (props) => {\r\n    console.info(props);\r\n\r\n    return (\r\n        <>\r\n            <h1>{props.name}</h1>\r\n        </>\r\n    );\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport async function getStaticProps({params}) {\r\n    let data = await getFranchises();\r\n\r\n    let franchise = data.find(x => x.id === params.id);\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n        props: franchise\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport async function getStaticPaths() {\r\n    let data = await getFranchises();\r\n\r\n    // Get the paths we want to pre-render based on posts\r\n    const paths = data.map(post => ({\r\n        params: {id: post.id},\r\n    }));\r\n\r\n    // We'll pre-render only these paths at build time.\r\n    return {paths, fallback: false}\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default withLayout(Franchise);\r\n```\r\n\r\nPerhaps, I am doing something wrong, but I am looking for some docs/demo on how to generate static pages at **build** time using data from API that do not require any further calls at **runtime** to populate props.", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "samuelgoldenbaum", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-05T03:31:51Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-09T11:34:58Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11673", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 23922, "title": "Make it easy to opt out of next/babel preset components", "body": "# Feature request\r\n\r\n## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\r\n\r\nSometimes the provided babel plugins conflict with what I want to do. For example, I would like to [use `class` instead of `className` and Preact with Next.js](https://dev.to/swyx/how-to-use-class-instead-of-classname-with-preact-and-typescript-2bjh). \r\n\r\nHowever, `styled-jsx`'s babel plugin is overwriting my classes:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6764957/78468866-23343580-774e-11ea-8c3a-e05701579a5b.png)\r\n\r\nsince I use `Tailwind` instead of `styled-jsx`, I would like to turn the styled-jsx plugin off.\r\n\r\nThere is an old issue to [opt out of styled-jsx](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/issues/2384), but this no longer works since plugins are no longer self identifying:\r\n\r\n[GitHub Source](https://github.com/zeit/next.js/blob/canary/packages/next/build/babel/preset.ts) \r\n\r\nYou cant simply do `myPreset.plugins.filter` any more because there is no identifying name for the plugins. You can't rely on order because the order varies depending on env.\r\n\r\n\r\nin order to do what i need i am having to [copy out the entire source of the babel plugin](https://prettier.io/playground/#N4Igxg9gdgLgprEAuEAdEBXAznABFmAJwEswZ0BuSKA3BANwF4AHQiMOLLAOge4DkA8gBEAogH1R-AGpVotYlgAKbACYYyxaIwaM9AclYR1m6Prk0YuRcLj04AGwjMAtghg6oTA6ruPnbrDm1ApYACqcHroG8ATmAPTxuABKnBAO9vgwAJ4OcKoAtABWWAAeuMwOGADmxDSoUABmGFCmUFm5+QBSZYLMMFo0ABTOA-IAlMDEjUMAhKODWJOEcDAYhFDAAL4UW9NzAIKEhACG2dyKR6fZI-2L3JU1dUvLq+vtC-K7nzQPVbU0Rg--3vSMKAYO8AH1-N53nAhCfvQEDEKouChgA-GBEFQUggH3twm7bru+5HiepDnheSpbC4xg-nAvClDue5YIwQyQsQArrhR2zjPCwD0CchC4OIwIACSsJwqyiF4Fi0KhZH7jhjDUc677AUMwnoYex6njhSq4tYh7AfYIESUh0naSBcIvrpABkhm6aiB7qcBynyFYigXsYwFQOJzDEJJQGEG+bnwgZUmEMZpm2fZGxWZYqkADInKUdm+BsjABdFUD-kMQwcYKNDcRyfFeECqVYHihgZTABQMPoZKMXo35--1c2F2A6wrLAuD8PZuAaVggwM0k0y4FAEB3aRudgAGDi41ggeD0d+RIA0+xDIo5nF8B-6KBEBDjMZcw7T1+2HcdM0DUN--DAYkIDt6X2qGSYk2TckrHyYU3RxSE4rCzDAMpxIzpJDCAQrAuBeyqFGR0r0mJ--oABecACgmggBAHUBRfAEBhmQdYJNeVJGgMPXmAwi14DLRWg0LQ2ijIDehFhhozFOrIPEKxvSGi5wdJSmG1KmmZtRbsnNAIzbZFgBFKta6iSkAKP2vc-SF35JZfS+IpyChOF7B6PIvj5T9MZVmpdMyjA7iwJ4l2JwuBcrjXuYgnB+lx3fZ+598bf1YBKv+bYGackovaeivZ8ov0vs8ffSVhUOSFVHj01Q+LsBwAAELUJgJdK0VKaXElmHPCyvlDL3sXXB5ddRZle0OQsmGsAZVwHxZAFYJQkAiwcDgYkGcHBuJI7O4kKxBr-VOMDQgM7Ya4ZwoRW2slyI4WMnUsBu9FVYFmAYP00ASiOw9JyKo8BkgtDbcI5BnAeDcPkA8KAzBoIQqbBsThSAWiVLqPkaOsdnH6AOUck5lmOOIuacvSITi6J5QIJ0QoUKyTRcWLFnIeQEsxrJHlOD-tIglWgoe-QcX0srNKN6XRuW4jjCQIV4r+RSvlccP0urqgeilD6HRW4MWitpfq4l8Y87oNMuPWy+IRJmAFBPEQYgDosZ-v5IoFQgr1zGVo-knL+y5kseOYso1aHU0QHsIh-24T5sDJWARew51ZV7nk3OiTQ2H30afWwJDDgCg-DfejVVUBqgHse3RopL3nAQFfe9iqudzYFEgLeTgg9fsfbcSeXc-TKML2o4Vx9CHQ7rouTEgEfT+RrbRUDhDz7QeeO-G4EgYBLnyxkyj8plSqY0OJPxnAtLuBHRodJPD5a8he35Id4CJBq3u2ALda0qBUB9TKNBTi8Ralsvdpj57ZPXsU-BxganO6cD0+R4QMpFS4BVJoWSGlkiKAgGJCAYEyBQCsTYFLJQrEEBYGQCAW8Uszju5tw6U4YA9QwAPPLeH1RkBUpt2aFwDh04Bk4KHuA5kaABmIPQAM2QPdgC4NbkAzx40qBOKCk4yB2dwBt+cvDAeg8Hk4aFHzpfRbl5AOcg8JM8gAEUMC9zgI3gTzf6c4EIB7uDufWB1BgKnCCdpkBdlDDbowOBU6nGYB7naC9c-Mh70ty0HuP0FH+vkfIueF2F+L33wTIAcD+gj+Jq-7e4Bd57xf5vx4HRT9UDPpAU4bee2ILnX7A6FwCED3TgCMXPXDbUB0d3PjJvG3egDaS6XwWAA8HFYgfoA4NWI8ToF-LYLYIAA) in order to delete the specific plugin I need.\r\n \r\n## Describe the solution you'd like\r\n\r\nI would like a simple way to opt out of the babel plugins. I think the best way is to simply attach the name of the plugin to each plugin, so that the user can filter in a babel.config.js:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// note: this is just my proposal - this doesnt actually work bc plugins arent self identifying\r\nconst nextbabel = require(\"next/babel\");\r\n\r\nconst wrapNextBabel = (api) => {\r\n  const temp = nextbabel(api);\r\n  temp.plugins = templ.plugins.filter(plugin => plugin.name !== 'styled-jsx')\r\n  return temp;\r\n};\r\nreturn {\r\n  presets: [wrapNextBabel],\r\n  plugins: [],\r\n};\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n(btw - Next.js' typescript fails when you use `babel.config.js` as oppose to `.babelrc` - i ended up routing `.babelrc` to a `babel-preset.js` in order to trick Next.js)\r\n\r\n## Describe alternatives you've considered\r\n\r\nmaintaining this ugly ass fork haha", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "swyxio", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2020-04-05T07:14:39Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-15T14:49:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/23922", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11676, "title": "Can't request upload files after update to 9.3.2", "body": "I had a function which will upload images from front-end and at the back-end will save the images to the static folder. After I updated next package from 9.1.2 to 9.3.2 front-end request the image file util I restart the next server", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "goalbased", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-05T07:51:48Z", "updated_at": "2020-12-01T22:04:57Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11676", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11677, "title": "is there any way to create dynamic route after /", "body": "# is there any way to create a dynamic route after /\r\n\r\n I have created dynamic route inside page directory like below \r\n\r\n- pages\r\n           - [username].js\r\n\r\n When someone visits http://mydomain.com/myusername.\r\n I get the username from get initial props and show them relevant content \r\n problem Is when I visit my other routes like /home/preview or /contacts it gives me the right page but [username].js page run as well I have some code that should only run for [username].js not for another route\r\n\r\nroutes structure\r\n\r\n- - pages\r\n\r\n\r\n      - contact.js\r\n      - inex.js\r\n      - [username].js\r\n      - home\r\n           - preview.js\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nwe cannot create dynamic routes for  after root \r\n\r\n## To Reproduce\r\nCreate a  directory structure like above\r\n1. create the file and create react component for each page\r\n2. console log the `query. username` inside get initial props of [username].js\r\n3. visit `localhost:3000/myusername `, you will see the log of `my username` there \r\n4. now visite another route like `localhost:3000/contact` it will result in you your contacts.js react component but it will also run the [username].js file\r\n5 likely you can also visit /home/preview and it will again run the file \r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nit should not run the [username].js file\r\n", "category": "Help", "category_slug": "help", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "rizwan92", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2020-04-05T07:55:55Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-25T12:10:21Z", "url": "https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/11677", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "vercel/next.js", "number": 11680, "title": "Static and Server Rendered HTML has no content", "body": "As the title says, this is the html I get. It has no content in it, just script tags. Anything I could be doing wrong?\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html>\r\n<head>\r\n  <meta charSet=\"utf-8\" />\r\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width\" />\r\n  <meta name=\"next-head-count\" content=\"2\" />\r\n  <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"/_next/static/hyqrwnOVVIXAj2Rp5EaPh/pages/index.js\" as=\"script\" />\r\n  <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"/_next/static/hyqrwnOVVIXAj2Rp5EaPh/pages/_app.js\" as=\"script\" />\r\n  <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"/_next/static/runtime/webpack-b65cab0b00afd201cbda.js\" as=\"script\" />\r\n  <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"/_next/static/chunks/framework.01fb8c9091ebbaaf6515.js\" as=\"script\" />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.1e4eaf0022bc1b80ca55.js\"\r\n    as=\"script\"\r\n  />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.0a3e12d8ae9e525fe73d.js\"\r\n    as=\"script\"\r\n  />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.1936b14f1cfe8eddd2a3.js\"\r\n    as=\"script\"\r\n  />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.0762fd42a872c84034af.js\"\r\n    as=\"script\"\r\n  />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.8f2c8ce3965d8eaa7007.js\"\r\n    as=\"script\"\r\n  />\r\n  <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"/_next/static/runtime/main-103ccbfe0f67d1d43118.js\" as=\"script\" />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.f762a188550ba0d9a710.js\"\r\n    as=\"script\"\r\n  />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static/chunks/<HASH_REDACTED>.3d186d3f72b53016ff27.js\"\r\n    as=\"script\"\r\n  />\r\n  <link\r\n    rel=\"preload\"\r\n    href=\"/_next/static