7:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-07T02:19:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9403", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9419, "title": "Clean cache", "body": "Is there any elegant way to clean cache before running program?\r\nOr to enforce deno to ensure that remote imports it is using in exec file, getting from its cache, are the most actual ones? If any of remote imports was updated, is it possible for deno to update the cache?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "GabRog", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-05T12:05:24Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T10:17:22Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9419", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9412, "title": "Extended Toolchain", "body": "Related to #8664 and a discussion on Discord https://discord.com/channels/684898665143206084/684911491035430919/807241638543753267\r\n\r\nI want to open up the discussion about the tooling included in the cli; does it make sense to put every tool in the cli? should we put some of them in std? should we have an \"extended toolchain\" similar to Golang's [tools](https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/tools)?\r\n\r\nI think at some point it would make sense to include the tools that are used 80% of the time in the cli, and the tools that are used for the remaining 20%, and that are more decoupled from the core in a set of curated tools officially supported. Whether that's in the deno.land/std or another directory like deno.land/tools I'm not sure.\r\n\r\nShould those tools have a special treatment from the cli, for example through a `deno tool` subcommand is another thing to consider.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "wperron", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-02-05T13:46:24Z", "updated_at": "2024-09-24T17:41:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9412", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9424, "title": "Import from Private Code/Module", "body": "What is the idiomatic way of working with private code in deno?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dc0d", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-06T13:59:33Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-06T19:08:47Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9424", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9435, "title": "Propose changes to the Deno.run() API", "body": "[Given unintuitive way of `Deno.run()` API usage](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/4568#issuecomment-772463496) I think we should consider changing that API for Deno 2.0.\r\n\r\nTokio provides a great abstraction for running subprocess by the means of [`Command`](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Command.html) and [`Child`](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Child.html) APIs\r\nespecially if user wants to just [run process to completion](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Command.html#method.status) or [run it to completion while collecting output](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.1.1/tokio/process/struct.Command.html#method.output).\r\n\r\nCurrently Deno APIs requires users to handle both situations manually; and I believe it's worth considering to add some helper methods.\r\n\r\nHereby I propose to align Deno's subprocess APIs to follow Tokio's API as closely as possible.\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\n\r\n// run process to completion\r\nconst command = new Deno.Command({\r\n    cmd: [\"ls\"],\r\n});\r\nconst status = await command.status();\r\n\r\n// run process to completion, collecting output (will configure stdout/stderr to be piped automatically)\r\nconst command = new Deno.Command({\r\n    cmd: [\"ls\"],\r\n});\r\nconst { status, stdout, stderr } = await command.output();\r\n\r\n// spawn a subprocess and return a handle to it\r\nconst command = new Deno.Command({\r\n    cmd: [\"ls\"],\r\n});\r\nconst subprocess = command.spawn();\r\n\r\n// wait for subprocess to complete\r\nconst status = await subprocess.wait();\r\n\r\n// check if subprocess has completed\r\nconst maybeStatus = subprocess.tryWait(); // rename to status() ?\r\n\r\n// wait for subproces to complete and collect output\r\nconst { status, stdout, stderr } = await subprocess.waitWithOutput();\r\n\r\n// force subprocess to exit\r\nawait subprocess.kill();\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith the proposed changes to the API all appropriate resources used to facilitate subprocess\r\nwould be automatically cleaned up (especially stdio resources), meaning that for the most popular\r\nuse cases users wouldn't have to remember to call `Deno.close()` on the resources. \r\n\r\nFor more advanced use cases (eg. streaming data from stdout/stderr) there wouldn't be much difference\r\nin the API usability besides changing API names.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 24, "reactions_total": 11, "reactions_plus": 11, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "bartlomieju", "author_association": "MEMBER", "created_at": "2021-02-07T15:06:14Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-16T00:20:26Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9435", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9487, "title": "Allow /cli to be built as a static library", "body": "I've had a few conversations with some other users/forkers - I think it would be useful to allow deno cli to be build as a static (or even shared) library via Cargo to allow for applications to embed more Deno functionality. Currently embedders can include deno_core and deno_runtime, which gives a lot of great functionality, but doesn't include some really killer features like the TypeScript compiler, repl, etc.  \r\n\r\nIf this idea were to be executed, my recommendation/suggestion is not to add cli/ as a crate to cargo - I think that would be too much overhead for the core maintainers.  Instead, this would target users that just want to run `cargo build --lib`, or include a local source copy of Deno and then follow up with linking that product into their applications. ", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "DavidDeSimone", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-02-12T21:08:24Z", "updated_at": "2026-08-20T16:30:16Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9487", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9491, "title": "How to prevent Deno's fetch implementation from sending default headers?", "body": "I noticed that Deno adds default headers to every fetch request. They are even included as expected values in [this unit test](https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/cli/tests/unit/fetch_test.ts#L640-L643).\r\n\r\nHere is an example:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"accept\": \"*/*\",\r\n  \"user-agent\": \"Deno/1.7.2\",\r\n  \"accept-encoding\": \"gzip, br\",\r\n  \"host\": \"localhost:8000\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nHow can I prevent this so that a fetch request can be sent with no header values?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jsejcksn", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-02-13T09:05:57Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T19:49:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9491", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9521, "title": "How to free port with deno", "body": "Hello guys i am testing the deno http library for a tutorial and i noticed that after quiting the a deno server process the port being used by the process does not get freed. is there a way to free ports in deno", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "C-NERD", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-16T19:23:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-06T05:57:11Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9521", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9534, "title": "Requiring modules like Node.js results in an access denied error message", "body": "Trying trivial snippet, which runs fine in node, but fails with deno:\r\n\r\ntypescript:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nimport axios from './node_modules/axios';\r\nconst url = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1'\r\n\r\naxios.get(url).then(response => {\r\n    console.log(response.data);\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\njavascript generated with tsc version 4.1.5:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n\"use strict\";\r\nexports.__esModule = true;\r\nvar axios_1 = require(\"axios\");\r\nvar url = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1';\r\naxios_1[\"default\"].get(url).then(function (response) {\r\n    console.log(response.data);\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nFrom Windows 10 Professional Power Shell launched as Adminsitrator:\r\n\r\nWith node:\r\n\r\n```\r\nPS D:\\dev\\ts> node .\\getjson.js\r\n{ userId: 1, id: 1, title: 'delectus aut autem', completed: false }\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith deno:\r\n\r\n```\r\nPS D:\\dev\\ts> deno run .\\getjson.ts -A \r\nCompile file:///D:/dev/ts/getjson.ts\r\nerror: Uncaught PermissionDenied: Access is denied. (os error 5)\r\n    at unwrapResponse ($deno$/ops/dispatch_json.ts:43:11)\r\n    at Object.sendAsync ($deno$/ops/dispatch_json.ts:98:10)\r\n    at async processImports ($deno$/compiler.ts:736:23)\r\n    at async processImports ($deno$/compiler.ts:753:7)\r\n    at async compile ($deno$/compiler.ts:1316:31)\r\n    at async tsCompilerOnMessage ($deno$/compiler.ts:1548:22)\r\n    at async workerMessageRecvCallback ($deno$/runtime_worker.ts:74:9)\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 10, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dejannenov", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-18T03:23:44Z", "updated_at": "2026-04-01T05:08:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9534", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9576, "title": "What does this `extract_standalone` do?", "body": "# Why do you need to call 'standalone::extract_standalone' before using 'run xb.ts'? What does this function do\r\n## question 1\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/40654631/108679595-ba050e80-7527-11eb-894c-63d7344b2603.png)\r\n## question2 \r\n> pub const MAGIC_TRAILER: &[u8; 8] = b\"d3n0l4nd\"; // what?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "runnerSnail", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-22T08:06:31Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-11T12:03:18Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9576", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9595, "title": "Questions about embedding deno", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nI have some questions about embedding deno that I couldn't find in any docs:\r\n\r\n1. Which crate should I use: `deno_core` or `deno_runtime`? \r\n2. Why the `execute` method in both runtime and core is not a future? I suppose the `run_event_loop` is for when I want to run something like a server written in javascript that will run indefinitely, right?\r\n3. Is it somehow possible to have imports like in the deno manual, pointing to urls instead of local files? When I tried with the runtime it raised an error saying it wasn't a file. \r\n4. I suppose the typescript support is not available neither in the runtime nor in the core right?\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "augustocdias", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-24T11:18:36Z", "updated_at": "2024-04-15T15:41:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9595", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9597, "title": "Question about design decision to have no package registry", "body": "NPM has a public registry you publish your package to. So obviously if that one moves or is replaced by a local one it won't require all applications to have all their files touched in order to reflect a changed url.\r\n\r\nHow is this handled in Deno?\r\n\r\nI apologize for the filing a ticket for this and I'm only asking out of curiosity, but I did some 15m of digging before I did.\r\n\r\nThe best I could find [was this](https://deno.land/manual@v1.7.5/linking_to_external_code#it-seems-unwieldy-to-import-urls-everywhere), but that's only a subset of the issue. Specificall later it states about a failing source:\r\n\r\n> This, like the above, is a problem faced by any remote dependency system.\r\n\r\nThat's technically true, but then I only need to replace _one_ source (the central registry), not _one per dependency_.\r\n\r\nWhile Deno could offer a feature to remap the urls for a project, I would then still struggle to see what the point was to begin with.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jtheisen", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-24T21:27:11Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-06T05:14:11Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9597", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9612, "title": "[Resolved]Implement a module proxy protocol, just like Go", "body": "## Why?\r\nSimply put, registries cannot be accessed anywhere, or be accessed in a fast speed.\r\nAssume that there is a `mod.ts` in GitHub. What if a user in China (blocking GitHub User Contents domain) wants to access it? Or in a company that frobids outgoing connection?\r\nDon't we already have a CDN / `deno.land/x`? Yes, but a deno module has dependencies. If we use a CDN to access this module, what to do with its dependencies that cannot be accessed?\r\nBut hey, in my opinion, Deno is like Go. Go has a [module proxy protocol](https://golang.org/ref/mod#goproxy-protocol). Deno could have one too!\r\nIt would be cool to have a proxy that caches dependencies. This can help restricted developers get rid of unnecessary configurations about http proxy, and moreover, speed up development in some teams that shares a local network.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 1, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ZeroAurora", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-02-26T16:18:00Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-03T06:11:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9612", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9637, "title": "Windows10 can't read HTTP_PROXY environment variable", "body": "I have asked the same question at Stackflow. Not sure if it's my code wrong, or it's a Windows related bug.\r\n\r\nI set the Proxy url like\r\nDeno.env.set('HTTP_PROXY', 'https://138.68.60.8:3128/');\r\nDeno.env.set('HTTPS_PROXY', 'https://138.68.60.8:3128/');\r\n\r\nIn face the manual doesn't say how shall I set the environment variables. These are googled from somewhere.\r\n\r\nyet the fetch() still fetches without the proxy. Instead, if I set up a proxy in the Windows system setting, the fetch() does use the system set proxy. Which doesn't solve the problem though as I am trying to read the proxy list from an API.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Checkpoints", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-03-01T15:23:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-02T03:54:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9637", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9642, "title": "Is LTO in effect in Release mode?", "body": "According from the [Cargo reference](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/profiles.html#lto), ***No LTO is performed if codegen units is 1 or opt-level is 0.***\r\n\r\nBut it seems like `lto: true` is used with `codegen-units: 1` in [Cargo.toml](https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/main/Cargo.toml#L20)", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Brooooooklyn", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-03-02T03:40:58Z", "updated_at": "2024-05-08T02:34:02Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9642", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9644, "title": "Module usage metrics?", "body": "So basically it is the weekly downloads in npm that I am thinking of.\r\n\r\nWhen there are multiple alternatives, the number of active dependents is an important indicator when choosing a library during the design phase of every project.\r\n\r\n1. https://deno.land/x by design should not have this info\r\n2. GitHub Traffics tab is only accessible to repo authors/maintainers, and it only shows repo level stats, instead of the single file import that deno wants.\r\n\r\nI am curious on how developers, especially deno developers, should be assessing third party modules?", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "vicary", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-03-02T05:57:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T05:42:47Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9644", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9652, "title": "What is the proper way to read a number (int) from a binary file?", "body": "I want to:\r\n1. seek to position p from the beginning of a binary file;\r\n2. read the next 4 bytes;\r\n3. and turn what's read into a number (32bit integer);\r\n\r\nI can do the first with Deno.seek, but Deno.read doesn't provide a parameter for how many bytes to read. So how can I do it? Thanks.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "elgs", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-03-02T11:56:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-14T07:32:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9652", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9703, "title": "Handling server requests on different 'threads'", "body": "I'm trying to write a simple tcp server. Is there a way to give each client their own thread?\r\n\r\nI doesn't actually have to be OS threads. Go's go routines or dart's isolates will work just as well. As long as multiple cores and cpus are being utilized. \r\n\r\nI see that I can use web workers. However, every example I have seen shows that I need to pass the name of a source file to the constructor, which this worker then executes. I want to pass in a socket or some other js/ts object, not a source file.\r\n\r\nAm I missing something? ", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "falconair", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-03-06T02:38:58Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-16T19:07:45Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9703", "classification": "random"}
