{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9753, "title": "Equivalent of `npm run <script>`?", "body": "Could not find any info on it, but I'd like to run aliased commands like `deno run-script start` or `deno run-script publish` without needed to specify `deno run` with correct permissions, flags, import map and entry point each time.\r\n\r\nIs there some kind of an equivalent of `npm run <script>` in deno cli ?\r\n\r\nIf not, is there any plans to have something like this in the future, or has it been ruled out/already discussed and we need to rely on third party modules?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "lowlighter", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-03-10T19:45:33Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-16T00:03:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9753", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9820, "title": "Cannot find name 'KeyboardEvent'", "body": "This is my first time with Deno, and I started writing a [small library](https://github.com/davegomez/is-keyboard-event) that I usually use in many projects I own.\r\n\r\nThe problem I have is Deno does not recognize some TypeScript types. This particular case is `KeyboardEvent`, and I can't find any hint of how I could solve this issue.\r\n\r\nThis is the error message I'm getting:\r\n```js\r\nerror: TS2304 [ERROR]: Cannot find name 'KeyboardEvent'.\r\nconst isEnter = ({ code, key, keyCode, type }: KeyboardEvent): boolean =>\r\n                                               ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n    at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/davegomez/is-keyboard-event/main/src/is-enter.ts:3:48\r\n```\r\n\r\nI know the type exists because TypeScript compiles the file correctly.\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "davegomez", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-03-17T09:54:24Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T13:14:55Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9820", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9819, "title": "Status/roadmap for Deno test coverage support?", "body": "I'm getting some odd coverage results (see #9865).\r\n\r\nI think it's because coverage is still immature and undergoing development, but I can't find any docs or a roadmap as to what it does now and what the future is expected to be... Are either (or both) published somewhere?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "rivy", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-03-17T19:27:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-04T05:55:00Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9819", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9855, "title": "Skypack, Preact, JSX Intrinsic Elements.", "body": "I have read through a couple of issues related to this, but not one that directly gives a solution.\r\n\r\nI have a project where I was wanting to use Preact, my `deps.ts` looks like this:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nimport * as preactRenderToString from \"https://cdn.skypack.dev/preact-render-to-string?dts\";\r\nconst { render } = preactRenderToString;\r\n\r\nimport * as preact from \"https://cdn.skypack.dev/preact?dts\";\r\nexport { preact, render };\r\n```\r\n\r\nAs I was using Preact, which has a different jsx factory, I had to use a custom `tsconfig.json`. It looks like this:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n    \"compilerOptions\": {\r\n        \"lib\": [\"esnext\", \"dom\", \"deno.ns\"],\r\n        \"jsx\": \"react\",\r\n        \"jsxFactory\": \"h\",\r\n        \"jsxFragmentFactory\": \"Fragment\"\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSkypack resolves modules with type definitions using their `?dts` query paramter. If I reload the cache I can see the `*.d.ts` files being fetched:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ deno cache --reload --config tsconfig.json deps.ts \r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/preact-render-to-string?dts\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/preact?dts\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-YBEbAK5M5p0s1c9aovEb/dist=es2020,mode=imports/optimized/preact.js\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-YBEbAK5M5p0s1c9aovEb/dist=es2020,mode=types/src/index.d.ts\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-YBEbAK5M5p0s1c9aovEb/dist=es2020,mode=types/src/jsx.d.ts\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact-render-to-string@v5.1.16-OpLkEM3eRx574UMXNSoo/dist=es2020,mode=imports/optimized/preact-render-to-string.js\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact-render-to-string@v5.1.16-OpLkEM3eRx574UMXNSoo/dist=es2020,mode=types/src/index.d.ts\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-V3GjhSMJg71SAoQzY34u/dist=es2020,mode=imports/optimized/preact.js\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-V3GjhSMJg71SAoQzY34u/dist=es2020,mode=types/index.d.ts\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-V3GjhSMJg71SAoQzY34u/dist=es2020,mode=types/src/index.d.ts\r\nDownload https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-V3GjhSMJg71SAoQzY34u/dist=es2020,mode=types/src/jsx.d.ts\r\nCheck file:///app/deps.ts\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotably, the last file downloaded `https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/preact@v10.5.13-V3GjhSMJg71SAoQzY34u/dist=es2020,mode=types/src/jsx.d.ts` contains the `JSX` namespace that I am having an issue with.\r\n\r\nWhenever I actually use jsx within the code:\r\n```typescript\r\nimport { render, preact } from \"./deps.ts\";\r\nconst { h } = preact;\r\n\r\nconsole.log(render(<h1>hello, world!</h1>));\r\n```\r\n\r\nI am returned an error about the JSX namespace:\r\n\r\n```\r\nTS7026 [ERROR]: JSX element implicitly has type 'any' because no interface 'JSX.IntrinsicElements' exists.\r\n  return <h1>Hello, world!</h1>;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe Deno docs (https://deno.land/manual@v1.8.1/typescript/types#type-declaration-semantics) aren't clear, outside of suggesting Skypack.\r\n\r\nIs there a solution outside of hosting the module directly like @lucacasonato did for dext.ts?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 11, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tbremer", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-03-22T06:09:32Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-12T01:16:45Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9855", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9858, "title": "*.spec.ts files for deno test", "body": "Is there any chance `deno test` glob could include `.spec.ts` files?\r\n\r\nThe rationale behind this is that tests can also be considered an specification for how a module, function, etc. behaves and in that case, should a project really embrace this concept, it would make sense to have tests (specs) written in `.spec.ts` files.\r\n\r\nI first learned of this \u201ctests as specs and examples\u201d idea in the [HtDP](https://htdp.org/) book, and have worked in some projects which actually use \u201cspec\u201d instead of \u201ctest\u201d in the filenames. Jest is an example of testing framework that accepts this `spec` thing in test file names.\r\n\r\n`deno test --help` says that\r\n\r\n```\r\n{*_,*.,}test.{js,mjs,ts,jsx,tsx}\r\n```\r\nis the current glob. Given the reasons explained above, I think it would be nice to have the glob be this instead:\r\n\r\n```\r\n{*_,*.,}{test,spec}.{js,mjs,ts,jsx,tsx}\r\n```", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 15, "reactions_plus": 15, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "FernandoBasso", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-03-22T15:35:55Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-19T09:36:02Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9858", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9891, "title": "How to send headers in deno?", "body": "I am working with an socket api where i need to send headers with the socket connections. However in nodejs it allows me to input headers in the second param. But as deno uses the browser like `WebSocket` api. I can't send headers with it. Any way so how can i send it with deno?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 19, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "scientific-dev", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-03-25T07:53:50Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-08T21:02:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9891", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9941, "title": "Server vs Client distinction", "body": "How can we tell if we are on the server or in the browser?  In the Node world, we mostly just use the lack of a ```window``` object.  But of course Deno has this object.  What's a great, fool-proof way to tell in Deno?\r\n\r\nI'm asking because I'm using Deno + SSR and would like to avoid the React useLayoutEffect warning like I'm getting when using Zustand: https://github.com/pmndrs/zustand/issues/347  in Aleph: https://github.com/alephjs/aleph.js/issues/221", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aadamsx", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-03-31T04:15:46Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-01T16:45:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9941", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 9973, "title": "Cant run compiled demo file (default server example): \"run again with --allow-net\", but I'm actually using the flag.", "body": "Hi. I was trying demo by the first time, and I already have some issues.\r\nI really like the fact that demo can be compiled, so, I tried compiling the default server example.\r\n\r\nHere's my code, the command and the error:\r\n\r\n`demo-test.ts`\r\n```ts\r\nimport { serve } from \"https://deno.land/std@0.92.0/http/server.ts\";\r\nconst server = serve({ port: 8000 });\r\nconsole.log(\"http://localhost:8000/\");\r\nfor await (const req of server) {\r\n  req.respond({ body: \"Hello World\\n\" });\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI compiled using `demo compile --unstable demo-test.ts --allow-net`,\r\nAnd runned the executable using `./deno.test --allow-net`\r\n\r\nIt gives me this error:\r\n\r\n```log\r\nerror: PermissionDenied: Requires net access to \"0.0.0.0:8000\", run again with the --allow-net flag\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ibnz36", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-04-02T15:36:00Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T12:57:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/9973", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11160, "title": "Feature Request: VM/Workers", "body": "A great feature to have would be a VM/Worker Thread which allows for finer control over threads (e.g. start/stop plus pause/resume, rate throttling, etc) along with an event engine attached to a VM/Worker state object.  This could be achieved with external libraries in some ways, but would be far more efficient inside the actual Deno threading engine, allowing for things like server-side AWS-styled Lambda processors without having to do something absurd like [extract an AST from a piece of code, modify it to wrap blocks in code allowing for hooks, and then recompile to JavaScript before injection into a worker](https://github.com/CoryGH/js-lambda).", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 22, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "CoryGH", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-04-04T20:16:21Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-18T20:03:11Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11160", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 10020, "title": "release more platform and arch executable file", "body": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/rustc/platform-support.html#tier-1\r\n\r\nAccording to rust's documentation, it can support `linux/arm64` build.\r\n\r\nIs there any problem to build `linux/arm64`? (V8 or something else)\r\n\r\nIn my opinion, there will be more and more processors with arm64 architecture.\r\n\r\n| Platform/Arch  | Real Machine |\r\n| ------------- | ------------- |\r\n| windows/arm64  | `Surface Pro X`  |\r\n| linux/arm64  | `Pinebook`  |\r\n| linux/mips64  | `-`  |\r\n| linux/mips64le  | `-`  |\r\n| linux/ppc64  | `-`  |\r\n| linux/ppc64le  | `-`  |\r\n\r\nAll of the above platforms and archs, rust all supports.\r\n\r\nI think it will be great to supports multiple platforms.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "axetroy", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-04-05T12:43:59Z", "updated_at": "2022-12-02T08:32:16Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/10020", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 10100, "title": "How to list sub commands in runtime", "body": "Does `deno` have a way to list the sub-commands supported in runtime?\r\n\r\nIn the following repo, I'm trying to use `deno compile` in the CLI tool running on `deno` in order to reduce the size of the deployment package.\r\nhttps://github.com/horihiro/azure-functions-deno-worker/blob/bundleAsExecutable/denofunc.ts#L97\r\n\r\nI want to check if the `deno` which a developer uses is supporting the sub-command `compile` at the beginning of the CLI tool.\r\n\r\nI would appreciate any insights.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "horihiro", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-04-10T00:10:20Z", "updated_at": "2024-01-25T22:52:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/10100", "classification": "need-related"}
