{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 10176, "title": "How to avoid required type narrowing for (Deno.PermissionStatus).state?", "body": "Using this example:\r\n\r\n`example.ts`:\r\n```ts\r\nexport async function ensurePermission<T extends Deno.PermissionDescriptor>(\r\n  descriptor: T,\r\n): Promise<Deno.PermissionStatus> {\r\n  const status = await Deno.permissions.query(descriptor);\r\n  if (status.state === \"granted\") return status;\r\n  await Deno.permissions.request(descriptor);\r\n  // It would be nice to not have to use this assertion:\r\n  // if ((status.state as Deno.PermissionState) === \"granted\") return status; // \u2705 Ok\r\n  if (status.state === \"granted\") return status; // \u274c Compile error: deno-ts(2367)\r\n  throw new Deno.errors.PermissionDenied(\"Permission denied\");\r\n}\r\n\r\nasync function greet(): Promise<void> {\r\n  const variable = \"USER\";\r\n  const desc: Deno.EnvPermissionDescriptor = { name: \"env\", variable };\r\n  let user: string;\r\n  try {\r\n    await ensurePermission(desc);\r\n    user = Deno.env.get(variable)?.trim() || \"anonymous user\";\r\n  } catch (ex) {\r\n    if (ex instanceof Deno.errors.PermissionDenied) user = \"skeptical user\";\r\n    else throw ex;\r\n  }\r\n  const message = `hello ${user}`;\r\n  console.log(message);\r\n}\r\n\r\nif (import.meta.main) greet();\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote the commented lines in the function `ensurePermission`. If I try to run the script, I get the following compilation error:\r\n\r\n```\r\n% deno run example.ts\r\nCheck file:///Users/jesse/example/example.ts\r\nerror: TS2367 [ERROR]: This condition will always return 'false' since the types '\"denied\" | \"prompt\"' and '\"granted\"' have no overlap.\r\n  if (status.state === \"granted\") return status; // \u274c Compile error: deno-ts(2367)\r\n      ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n    at file:///Users/jesse/example/example.ts:9:7\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut if I comment the line in the message and uncomment the line above it, everything works fine:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\n  if ((status.state as Deno.PermissionState) === \"granted\") return status; // \u2705 Ok\r\n  // if (status.state === \"granted\") return status; // \u274c Compile error: deno-ts(2367)\r\n```\r\n\r\n```\r\n% deno run example.ts\r\nCheck file:///Users/jesse/example/example.ts\r\n\u26a0\ufe0f  \ufe0fDeno requests env access to \"USER\". Grant? [g/d (g = grant, d = deny)] g\r\nhello jesse\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs there a way that the `state` property/getter of `PermissionStatus` can be refactored in order to avoid requiring this kind of assertion or is it simply an upstream limitation of [TypeScript's CFA](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/9998)?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>I am using Deno v<code>1.9.0</code></summary>\r\n\r\n```\r\n% deno --version\r\ndeno 1.9.0 (release, x86_64-apple-darwin)\r\nv8 9.1.269.5\r\ntypescript 4.2.2\r\n```\r\n</details>", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jsejcksn", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-04-14T05:58:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-27T11:37:59Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/10176", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 10185, "title": "How to make `deno fmt` to ignore Markdown files", "body": "Is there a way to make `deno fmt` to ignore Markdown files, in a similar way TypeScript files start with a comment `// deno-fmt-ignore-file`?  I tried to insert `<!-- deno-fmt-ignore-file -->` at the top of a Markdown file, it didn't work.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dahlia", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-04-14T12:52:22Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-21T14:02:24Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/10185", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 10195, "title": "Optional arguments with default values", "body": "In the style guide docs an options object to place all optional parameters is regarded as good practice https://deno.land/manual@v1.5.4/contributing/style_guide#exported-functions-max-2-args-put-the-rest-into-an-options-object \r\n\r\n The docs don't expand how one may go about using default values to these optional parameters. \r\n\r\nTake the use case here \r\n\r\n```\r\nconst foo = (\r\n  requiredArg1: string,\r\n  options: {\r\n    optionalArgument1?: string;\r\n    optionalArgument2?: string;\r\n  } = {\r\n    optionalArgument1: 'defaultValueForOptionalArgument1',\r\n    optionalArgument2: 'defaultValueForOptionalArgument2',\r\n  },\r\n): void => {\r\n  console.log('requiredArg1', requiredArg1);\r\n  console.log('options.optionalArgument1', options.optionalArgument1);\r\n  console.log('options.optionalArgument2', options.optionalArgument2);\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nCalling this like    \r\n```\r\nfoo('passedInRequiredValue', {\r\n      optionalArgument1: 'passedInOptionalArgument1',\r\n    });\r\n```\r\n\r\nGives this output\r\n```\r\nrequiredArg1 passedInRequiredValue\r\noptions.optionalArgument1 passedInOptionalArgument1\r\noptions.optionalArgument2 undefined\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEven though I understand why the output is such it seems a very easy thing to miss and if I were to look over the code quickly I would expect the below\r\n \r\n```\r\nrequiredArg1 passedInRequiredValue\r\noptions.optionalArgument1 passedInOptionalArgument1\r\noptions.optionalArgument2 defaultValueForOptionalArgument2\r\n```\r\n\r\nTaking this into account I was wondering if Deno developers still think the options pattern when used with default values is a good style. I know there are arguments for both for and against but I would like to hear your thoughts \ud83d\ude03 \r\n\r\nThanks! ", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "patelnets", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-04-15T13:21:14Z", "updated_at": "2024-10-31T16:50:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/10195", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 10894, "title": "How to read stdin and still prompt for permissions?", "body": "Here's an example script prompting for read permissions:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nconst desc: Deno.ReadPermissionDescriptor = { name: \"read\" };\r\n\r\nconst status = await Deno.permissions.query(desc);\r\nconsole.log(\"Before request:\", status);\r\n\r\nawait Deno.permissions.request(desc);\r\nconsole.log(\"After request:\", status);\r\n\r\nawait Deno.readTextFile(\"example.ts\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen I run the script, piping the output of another process to it, I am never shown the permission prompt (it is automatically denied):\r\n\r\n```\r\n% cat example.ts | deno run example.ts\r\nBefore request: PermissionStatus { state: \"prompt\", onchange: null }\r\nAfter request: PermissionStatus { state: \"denied\", onchange: null }\r\nerror: Uncaught (in promise) PermissionDenied: Requires read access to \"example.ts\", run again with the --allow-read flag\r\nawait Deno.readTextFile(\"example.ts\");\r\n^\r\n    at unwrapOpResult (deno:core/core.js:100:13)\r\n    at async open (deno:runtime/js/40_files.js:46:17)\r\n    at async Object.readTextFile (deno:runtime/js/40_read_file.js:40:18)\r\n    at async file:///Users/jesse/example/example.ts:9:1\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut if I simply run the script, I am presented the prompt:\r\n\r\n```\r\n% deno run example.ts \r\nBefore request: PermissionStatus { state: \"prompt\", onchange: null }\r\n\u26a0\ufe0f  \ufe0fDeno requests read access. Grant? [g/d (g = grant, d = deny)] g\r\nAfter request: PermissionStatus { state: \"granted\", onchange: null }\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis example script does not need data from `stdin`, but if another one did, how could it get it while still being able to prompt for permissions?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jsejcksn", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-04-15T19:55:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-07T14:47:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/10894", "classification": "random"}
