{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11235, "title": "RFC: Test Collections\u2014Declarative approach to subtests", "body": "#10771 proposed the idea of adding subtests, hooks, and concurrent test execution via a mixed declarative/imperative API similar to what is found in the Go programming language. This proposal is going to explore a similar solution, but keeping the test definitions declarative expanding on @caspervonb's [comment](https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/10771#discussioncomment-791983). Tests would continue to work in three separate phases of defining, filtering, and finally executing tests.\r\n\r\n## Interface\r\n\r\nThe `TestDefinition` interface is the same, but a new `TestCollection` interface is added that may be provided to `Deno.test`.\r\n\r\n```ts\r\ndeclare namespace Deno {\r\n  export interface TestDefinition {\r\n    name: string;\r\n    fn: () => void | Promise<void>;\r\n    /** Does not run this test when `true`. */\r\n    ignore?: boolean;\r\n    /** If at least one test has `only` set to true, only run tests that have\r\n     * `only` set to true and fail the test suite. */\r\n    only?: boolean;\r\n    /** Check that the number of async completed ops after the test is the same\r\n     * as number of dispatched ops. Defaults to true.*/\r\n    sanitizeOps?: boolean;\r\n    /** Ensure the test case does not \"leak\" resources - ie. the resource table\r\n     * after the test has exactly the same contents as before the test. Defaults\r\n     * to true. */\r\n    sanitizeResources?: boolean;\r\n    /** Ensure the test case does not prematurely cause the process to exit,\r\n     * for example via a call to `Deno.exit`. Defaults to true. */\r\n    sanitizeExit?: boolean;\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  export interface TestCollection {\r\n    name: string;\r\n    cases: Array<TestDefinition | TestCollection>;\r\n    /** Hook to run before the descendant test definitions are executed. */\r\n    before?: () => void | Promise<void>;\r\n    /** Hook to run after the descendant test definitions are executed.\r\n     * This is executed regardless of whether the descendant tests pass. */\r\n    after?: () => void | Promise<void>;\r\n    /** Does not run this test collection when `true`. */\r\n    ignore?: boolean;\r\n    /** If set to true, only runs tests in this test collection unless `only` is `true`\r\n     * on at least one test definition in the test suite. */\r\n    only?: boolean;\r\n    /** Executes asynchronous sub tests at the same time when able. This will automatically move\r\n     * sanitization checks to a higher level based on the descendant test definitions.\r\n     * For more accurate reporting of which sub test is leaky, set this to `false`. Setting this to\r\n     * `true` may affect sub test execution order. Defaults to `false`. */\r\n    concurrent?: boolean;\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  export function test(test: TestDefinition | TestCollection);\r\n  export function test(name: string, fn: () => void | Promise<void>): void;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Behaviours\r\n\r\nThis document will show many of the same examples as #10771 and largely mirror (copy/paste) the text from there, but written with this proposed declarative API.\r\n\r\nSub tests can be defined by providing a test collection to `Deno.test`:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\n/** Test with subtests (sequential), but no initalizers or destructors. */\r\nconst cases = [\r\n  [1, 1, 2],\r\n  [1, 2, 3],\r\n  [-1, 2, 1],\r\n];\r\nDeno.test({\r\n  name: \"addition (sequential)\",\r\n  cases: cases.map(([a, b, expected]) => ({\r\n    name: `${a}+${b}=${expected}`,\r\n    fn: () => {\r\n      const actual = a + b;\r\n      assertEquals(actual, expected);\r\n    },\r\n  })),\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nSub tests can also run concurrently and the resource sanitizers **do not** need to be disabled as the test executer can move that responsibility to a higher level based on the descendant test definitions (more on that below):\r\n\r\n```ts\r\n/** Test with subtests (concurrent), but no initializers or destructors. */\r\nDeno.test({\r\n  name: \"addition (concurrent)\",\r\n  cases: cases.map(([a, b, expected]) => ({\r\n    name: `${a}+${b}=${expected}`,\r\n    fn: async () => {\r\n      const actual = a + b;\r\n      await delay(100);\r\n      assertEquals(actual, expected);\r\n    },\r\n  })),\r\n  concurrent: true, // note this\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nTest collections can have descendant test collections:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nDeno.test({\r\n  name: \"group with subgroup\",\r\n  cases: [{\r\n    name: \"subgroup\",\r\n    cases: [{\r\n      name: \"case 1\",\r\n      fn: () => {},\r\n    }, {\r\n      name: \"case 2\",\r\n      fn: () => {},\r\n    }]\r\n  }],\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nSetup and teardown steps are not as convenient as an imperative approach out of the box...\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nlet database: Database;\r\n\r\nDeno.test({\r\n  name: \"database tests\",\r\n  before: async () => database = await setupDatabase(),\r\n  after: () => database.destroy(),\r\n  cases: [\r\n    {\r\n      name: \"first case using database\",\r\n      fn: async () => {\r\n        assertEquals(await database.getData(), \"data\");\r\n      }\r\n    },\r\n    { name: \"second case using database\", fn: () => {} },\r\n  ],\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n...however, this inconvenience can be mitigated in many ways by userland testing libraries (who would be the consumers of this API).\r\n\r\nOnly running a specific sub test is possible by adding `only: true` to the test definition similar to today, but this could also be added to a test collection. Similarly, entire test collections can be ignored by adding `ignored: true`. See the interface JS docs above for more details.\r\n\r\n### Sanitizers\r\n\r\nSanitizers (`sanitizeOps`, `sanitizeResources`, `sanitizeExit`) would remain at the test definition level, but if a user specifies `concurrent: true` on a test collection, then the test execution phase would analyze the descendant test definitions to figure out which tests it could run concurrently based on the test definitions. It would then run all these tests in groups and do sanitization checks after each group ran. In most cases, the entire test collection would be run concurrently and the sanitization check would occur afterwards.\r\n\r\nThis has the obvious downside of reduced resolution on which test is leaking, however an instructive message can be given to the user on a failure to tell them to run the tests again with `concurrent: false` to drill down to the specific test or the tests could be run again serially when this occurs in order to tell them which test it is (doesn't always work if it's flaky). Either way, this may provide a good user experience because it does not require manually disabling a lot of test case sanitizers.\r\n\r\n### Permissions\r\n\r\nThese would work the same way as sanitizers and the test executer would be able to figure out which tests can run concurrently based on the defined test definitions that it knows about.\r\n\r\n## Reporter Output\r\n\r\nLargely the same as #10771, except accurate numbers on how many tests are left to execute and how many total tests there are before filtering are possible to report to the user.\r\n\r\n## Filtering\r\n\r\nFiltering would work well and fast because filtering happens after defining tests and before executing them or their setup code. \"Only\" is also possible because of this. Additionally filtering based on a substring of a subtest name is possible.\r\n\r\n## Shuffling\r\n\r\nShuffling sub tests would be possible with this approach.\r\n\r\n## A Building Block\r\n\r\nSimilarly to #10771, this API is directed towards testing framework authors (ex. using `describe` and `it` like mocha). It would not be very convenient for end users to use this API.\r\n\r\ncc @lucacasonato @caspervonb ", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 14, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dsherret", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-02T22:04:58Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-29T10:41:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11235", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11246, "title": "defining main deno file with no extension", "body": "I know this Topic is just my ego in writing app style using VSCode IDE.\r\n\r\nI just want to create deno main file without extension i.e **_www_** that contain listener method to the server. This is just work fine but the problem arises when using VSCode where Deno treat **_www_** file as TS file thus throwing error message.\r\n\r\nIs there anyway to treat no extension file as JS file i/o TS file in VSCode IDE.?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Srabutdotcom", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-07-03T14:04:56Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:46:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11246", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11269, "title": "Is there Deno VM like NodeJS.vm ?", "body": "Hi! In NodeJS there was module \"vm\" which allowed you to run untrusted code in new context \r\nThe one limit was that you couldn't run TYPESCRIPT code in NodeJS's vm\r\n\r\nBut I didn't find any of that in Deno. Is there a way to run untrusted TYPESCRIPT code in Deno with a context? \r\n\r\n ", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 9, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "gilvex", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-07-04T09:43:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-16T02:05:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11269", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11317, "title": "deno repl --eval flag", "body": "**Update 2021-08-06:** The `--eval` flag with a string landed in https://github.com/denoland/deno/pull/11590\r\n\r\nBased on #3173, #7425, and #7938, I think providing users a way to start a REPL and have some setup code execute would be incredibly useful.\r\n\r\nFor example, this would enable someone to create an alias on their system to a command that starts a Deno REPL with their favourite math library already imported and ready to use.\r\n\r\n## Proposed Approach\r\n\r\nThe `--eval` flag would be added that accepts a string containing code:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ndeno repl --eval 'import * as hash from \"https://deno.land/std@0.77.0/hash#stdhash\"'\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. On success, the result of running the script will not be outputted, but still assigned to `_`.\r\n2. On thrown error, the error will be displayed to the user and assigned to `_error`. The REPL will not fail to start in order to not block the user allowing them to ignore any setup errors and just use the REPL.\r\n\r\n### Drawback - Relative module specifiers in file in different directory\r\n\r\nProviding the file text of a source file in a different directory may cause confusion in certain cases. For example, take a file called *scripts/mod.ts* containing the following relative module specifier:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nimport * as log from \"./log.ts\";\r\n\r\nexport function doStuff() {\r\n    log(\"Hello.\");\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSomeone may run...\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nfile_text=$(cat scripts/mod.ts)\r\ndeno repl --eval $file_text\r\n```\r\n\r\n...and be initially confused about why it says it can't find `\"./log.ts\"` (it doesn't find it because it's searching from the parent directory of the file location). I think most people will realize what's going on eventually though.\r\n\r\n**Workaround:** The easy way around this would be to change the file to use absolute module specifiers.\r\n\r\n## Additional Features\r\n\r\nI do not believe it is worth the effort to implement these additional features as I believe users will be able to get by just fine with the `--eval` flag as described above.\r\n\r\n### Piping file?\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# pipe stdin\r\ncat script.ts | deno repl --eval\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'm not very familiar with how this works behind the scenes, but perhaps if this is possible then we should just make it `cat scripts.ts | deno repl`.\r\n\r\nFrom comments below:\r\n\r\n> This is very unlikely to work well, based on my past experience with terminal programs, because `deno repl --eval` would have to read stdin to end-of-file, and then attempt to detect and switch to reading from the tty, which might also muck with a readline library's initialization steps, and so on.\r\n\r\n### Provide URL?\r\n\r\nIt may be beneficial to support providing a URL instead of an evaluation string for the purpose of sharing a REPL setup. For example:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ndeno repl --eval https://dprint.dev/repl.ts\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis would strip any export declarations from the file and evaluate it so even non-exported declarations would be available in the REPL. The code evaluated would run with full permissions similarly to importing a module in the REPL today.\r\n\r\nAlthough this may be useful for people to easily share custom REPL setups, it is not so beneficial for using library exports. For that purpose someone may do:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ndeno repl --eval 'import * as myModule from \"https://deno.land/x/something.ts\"'\r\n# or\r\ndeno repl --eval 'import { myFunction } from \"https://deno.land/x/something.ts\"'\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Provide file path?\r\n\r\nSimilarly to URLs, it may be beneficial to support file paths. For example:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ndeno repl --eval folder/repl.ts\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis could strip any export declarations and rewrite any relative module specifiers to point to the correct files based on the current working directory. The advantage this has over providing file text as a string is that the module specifiers in the file could remain relative.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dsherret", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-07T14:19:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T14:30:53Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11317", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11363, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-06-21", "body": "- `Deno.emit()` does not rewrite module specifiers (denoland/deno#4538, denoland/deno#9866, denoland/deno#10770)\r\n  - @ry wondered how general purpose we should make `Deno.emit()`\r\n  - @kitsonk feels we should try to address these issues\r\n  - *ACTION* @kitsonk agreed to write up specific use cases and what changes needed to occur to accomodate them\r\n- Recommendation to add `Deno.info()` (denoland/deno#10758)\r\n  - @kitsonk discussed the API and how it aligned to `deno info`\r\n  - There was a general discussion of how much we should ensure comannd line features are available as programattic APIs. There was no general consensus.\r\n  - @ry stated that we need to be really careful about expanding any non-standard APIs, as they they always come back to haunt us.\r\n  - *ACTION* @ry stated he would review the PR in detail\r\n- The _new_ file system API (and the road to deprecating `Deno.Reader` and `Deno.Writer` (denoland/deno#11018)\r\n  - Discussion how the web platform has increasing standard APIs that accomodate file access (ref: https://wicg.github.io/file-system-access/)\r\n  - @piscisaureus wondered if WASI solved this problem with WASI \"streams\" (ref: https://github.com/WebAssembly/wasi-io)\r\n  - There was discussion that WASI/IO might be too low level\r\n  - *ACTION* continue to move forward with denoland/deno#11018 and continue to evaluate WHATWG and WASI standards that help solve this problem space.", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-12T01:16:03Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-25T01:18:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11363", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11364, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-07-02", "body": "- Default to `h` and `Fragment` for JSX under CLI (denoland/deno#11186)\r\n  - General discussion about the problem space.\r\n  - Discussion how almost every other JSX library `h()` versues `React.createElement()`.\r\n  - Discussion how every pre-configured situation is _ugly_ and how the pragma leaves it up to the user.\r\n  - *ACTION* Consider no JSX configuration by default and help the transistion with linting rules.\r\n- Plugins and FFI (denoland/deno#10908 and denoland/deno#11152)\r\n  - Existing plugins subvert the Deno secuirty module (therefore require `--allow-plugin`)\r\n  - The plugin API is _poor_ and not well supported in the community.\r\n  - There is demand for a plugin interface in the community.\r\n  - There are lots of issues with async code with the existing plugin interface and basically there are more problems then good solutions.\r\n  - @eliassjogreen has proposed a new plugin API that is basically FFI support, sync only. @ry discussed briefly with @eliassjogreen and if it is enough to migrate the webview stuff to this, and @eliassjogreen feels it is sufficient.\r\n  - @piscisaureus feels that this could be built on in the future, which is great.\r\n  - @ry wants to make sure we encourage people to use Web Assembly over _plugins_ and having a _plugin_ interface gives people an excuse to not prefer web assembly.\r\n  - @piscisaureus reminds everyone that there are just some problems you can't solve with Web Assembly, and so plugins are an eventuality.\r\n  - *ACTION* Plugins to be removed and FFI support to be landed.\r\n- Lock files by default? (denoland/deno#7237)\r\n  - General positive feelings about it, but implementation likely to be complex.\r\n  - Discussion to continue\r\n- Is there consensus on a new test API? (denoland/deno#10771)\r\n  - There are challenges in opening up a new API surface for testing\r\n  - There is not conensus, discussion to continue\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-12T01:16:57Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-28T14:44:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11364", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11365, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-07-08", "body": "- `--prompt` by default (denoland/deno#10183)\r\n  - General support for it if we address the security issues with promiting first (denoland/deno#9666 and denoland/deno#9750)\r\n  - *ACTION* proceed with `--prompt` by default after issues resolved or mitigated\r\n- `window.location` undefined without `--location` (denoland/deno#10828)\r\n  - Lots of discussion about how we can have a location without a location being explicitly set.\r\n  - @lucacasonato and @kitsonk highlight the challenges on `localStorage` and `fetch()` with some sort of implicit location that would need to be considered.\r\n  - Discussion around usability of types having `window.location` defined, but undefined at runtime. It was considered _acceptable_ as there are other APIs that are undefined at runtime, and it allows \"sniffing\" without throwing.\r\n  - *ACTION* proceed with `window.location` being undefined.\r\n  - *ACTION* discussion and research to continue on how we might be able to provide a default `--location` without breaking everything.\r\n- Add a `--worker` flag to type check workers (denoland/deno#10050)\r\n  - General dislike of adding flags.\r\n  - There are current documented ways of type checking with workers (https://deno.land/manual@v1.11.5/typescript/types#type-checking-web-workers)\r\n  - *ACTION* we will not proceed with denoland/deno#10050\r\n- Discussion around how many pragmas we have\r\n  - Most of the pragmas aren't created by Deno, just supported to support wider compatability\r\n  - *ACTION* Venting in denoland/deno#11334\r\n- Syntax highlighting for `deno lint`, `deno doc` and REPL (denoland/deno#10998)\r\n  - This highlighted that we probably shouldn't maintain context documentation in the CLI, `deno lint` should link to the `lint.deno.land` website instead of printing out rich documentation to the console. We should improve `lint.deno.land` versus maintaining documentation in to places.\r\n- Skip type checking by default (denoland/deno#8549)\r\n  - This has been a long running dicussion.\r\n  - There are usability issues and performance issues with type checking all the time.\r\n  - Most users use an editor which does the type checking, making type checking all the time redundant.\r\n  - Ackowledged that the community is divided on this.\r\n  - While behaviour changes, we think it is unlikely to break workflows, it just won't not-break when it should.\r\n  - Broad agreement of those on the call that we should do this.\r\n  - @kitsonk concerned that if emit is swc only that it will impact the code, as we are still finding bugs with swc emit.\r\n  - *ACTION* deprecate `--no-check` (do this by default) and `--check` will continue to use tsc for the emit (denoland/deno#11340)\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-12T01:17:54Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-25T01:18:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11365", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11394, "title": "Utilize a playground sandbox for issue reproduction", "body": "Presently, it's challenging to provide reproduction data that's accessible to issue viewers and useful to people wanting to reproduce the issue. Here's [an example](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11388) where I posted the file contents for viewers and uploaded an archive for anyone wanting to reproduce the issue.\r\n\r\nI think that providing issue creators with the ability to create and link to a playground sandbox for reproduction would be a great enhancement for issue management. (It would also be great for Deno adoption.)\r\n\r\nI just had this idea, so I haven't done much research yet on available options, but perhaps something like [Replit](https://replit.com/languages/deno) might be a good fit? I'm sure the concept of a browser-based deno sandbox has been discussed previously, but this issue is specific to reproduction.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jsejcksn", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-14T02:33:48Z", "updated_at": "2023-08-11T17:27:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11394", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11420, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-07-15", "body": "- Reviewed and updated the Deno CLI Q3 roadmap (#11168)\r\n  - The removal of the plugin API is \"blocked\" by the FFI PR which needs a bit more work, but should be ready for 1.13\r\n  - There are no known blockers to stabilising the native HTTP server API for 1.13\r\n- Breaking out the manual to a seperate repository (#11391)\r\n  - There was general consensus about the approach, there was some questions raised about the workflow. @bartlomieju indicated that we have proven all the workflow in one way or another.\r\n  - _ACTION_ proceed with breaking out the manual to a seperate repo.\r\n- Signing the `deno` executable (#575)\r\n  - @piscisaureus has done some research but @dsherret has recent experience.\r\n  - Everyone is strongly in favour, just need to work out the details.\r\n  - `deno compile` signing is going to be complex and not in scope for this.\r\n  - _ACTION_ proceed with doing what is needed to sign binaries.\r\n- Disable SSL verification (#1371)\r\n  - Robust conversation about if this a good thing or bad thing. Strong arguments that any \"bypass\" will lead people to weaken their security, often unintentionally. Strong arguments though that legitimate development often leads to situations where the CLI would need to connect to an \"invalid\" secure connection.\r\n  - Strong agreement that it should make it very clear that you are doing something \"bad\" like Chrome's `--unsafely-treat-insecure-origin-as-secure` flag and make it very explicit and narrow.\r\n  - _ACTION_ we need to improve documentation about current ways of dealing with self-signed or unrecognised trust authorities that come up when developing.\r\n  - _ACTION_ Proceed with introducing a flag to bypass \"invalid\" certificates. (Noted, that there was not unanimous consensus on this action)\r\n- Import assertions and JSON modules (#11168)\r\n  - Mostly the infrastructure is ready (core, cli, swc).\r\n  - TypeScript (`tsc`) still doesn't have support. The changes will not make TypeScript 4.4 so the earliest is TypeScript 4.5 (late-October).  Discussed that if it was just a \"diagnostic\" issue, but didn't mess up the emit, that we might be able to move forward, but identified that the TypeScript emit borks the code.\r\n  - No choice but to move it to Q4. :cry:\r\n- How do we deal with the design of the standard library?\r\n  - The public forums around `std` stopped earlier this year because they weren't as effective as needed.\r\n  - Lots of discussion about how much of the core teams discussions should be made public. General consensus was that we should continue issuing the minutes like this for now.\r\n  - General agreement that we don't want more meetings if we can avoid it.\r\n  - _ACTION_ the `std` library and it design discussion will be part of the CLI Roadmap and the CLI Design Meetings. We will re-evaluate if it doesn't work effectively.\r\n- JSX transforms (#8440)\r\n  - The PR is coming along well, though there is some \"magic\" in being able to support it that we consider ugly and hacky, but it is what it is.\r\n  - It does seem to be the way forward for transpiling JSX, so with #11186 it might make sense to standardise on the _new_ JSX transforms than other varieties.\r\n  - More to discuss as and when the PR is ready to be landed.\r\n \r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-16T04:53:17Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-29T18:49:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11420", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11425, "title": "What is the deno equivalent to \"npm link\" ?", "body": "Suppose I have two repos, one depends on the other `A -> B`. I'm working on a feature in `B` and testing it with code in `A` before publishing.\r\n\r\nWhat is the best way to create a local only link from `A` to the local folder containing the code of `B`? Ideally `A` would still have a dependency on `https://deno.land/x/B/mod.ts` in the deps.ts file but not necessarily.\r\n\r\nWith npm you would cd into B and run `npm link` and that essentially registers that module globally and then cd into A and run `npm link B` and it would use symlinks to do some `node_modules` trickery.\r\n\r\nOther projects like [lerna](https://www.npmjs.com/package/lerna) allow you to have multiple packages in a single repo and then you can configure relationships between them and it automatically links them all together with a single command.\r\n\r\nSo I'm just wondering what the equivalent in Deno is? Whats the recommended way to temporarily link modules in local folders together?\r\n\r\nI was thinking of using the [import map](https://deno.land/manual/linking_to_external_code/import_maps), but sort of in reverse? Though I'm not sure if it would work... but basically:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\n// link.json\r\n{\r\n  \"imports\": {\r\n    // \"fmt/\": \"https://deno.land/std@0.101.0/fmt/\"\r\n    \"https://deno.land/example@0.0.1/\": \"../example/\"\r\n   }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen when I run locally:\r\n```sh\r\ndeno --import-map=link.json main.ts\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'm not sure if that would actually work though.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 1, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 4, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "justinmchase", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-16T19:58:07Z", "updated_at": "2023-03-01T20:34:43Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11425", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11436, "title": "Listen TCP", "body": "Hi! \r\n\r\nI apologize in advance if my question seems silly. I didn't find the answer in the Runtime API documentation.\r\nI wrote a test TCP echo server in Golang:\r\n\r\n(Simplified code without error handling)\r\n```go\r\npackage main\r\n\r\nimport (\r\n  \"bufio\"\r\n  \"fmt\"\r\n  \"net\"\r\n  \"strings\"\r\n)\r\n\r\nfunc main() {\r\n  fmt.Println(\"TCP server...\")\r\n  line, _ := net.Listen(\"tcp\", \":2812\")\r\n  for {\r\n    connect, _ := line.Accept()\r\n    message, _ := bufio.NewReader(connect).ReadString('\\n')\r\n    echo := strings.ToUpper(message)\r\n    fmt.Println(echo)\r\n    connect.Write([]byte(echo))\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen i wrote a client in Deno:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst connect = await Deno.connect({\r\n    hostname: 'localhost',\r\n    port: 2812,\r\n    transport: 'tcp',\r\n})\r\n\r\nconst message = new TextEncoder().encode('test')\r\nconnect.write(message)\r\n```\r\n\r\nEverything is working fine, the server receives and processes messages. \r\nBut how to get a response on the TCP client side?\r\n\r\n\u0417\u0430\u0440\u0430\u043d\u0435\u0435 \u0441\u043f\u0430\u0441\u0438\u0431\u043e!", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-07-18T09:44:39Z", "updated_at": "2021-07-18T09:56:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11436", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11473, "title": "How to test browser code under Deno", "body": "`deno test --config ./tsconfig.json --watch --allow-all --no-check test/ ` (or without `--no-check`) returns:\r\n\r\n```\r\nerror: Uncaught ReferenceError: document is not defined\r\nconst elem = document.getElementById(\"test\") as HTMLElement;\r\n             ^\r\n```\r\n`tsconfig.json`\r\n```\r\n{\r\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\r\n    \"target\": \"esnext\",\r\n    \"lib\": [\"dom\", \"dom.iterable\", \"dom.asynciterable\", \"deno.ns\"]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`main.ts`\r\n```ts\r\nconst elem = document.getElementById(\"test\") as HTMLElement;\r\n```\r\nThe LSP picks up my `tsconfig.json` fine and accepts `document` when use `config = \"./tsconfig.json\"`, and `test` is reading the file as it says:\r\n```\r\nUnsupported compiler options in \"/home/david/Documents/apps-sites/typeScript/dependency-heaven-\r\ntypescript/tsconfig.json\".\r\n  The following options were ignored:\r\n    target\r\n```\r\n\r\nI have a test repo here to show the problem: https://github.com/David-Else/dependency-heaven-typescript", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "David-Else", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-07-20T16:44:21Z", "updated_at": "2024-12-06T18:19:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11473", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11475, "title": "Minimal Deno Runtime", "body": "Deno includes many tools that are only needed during development and not by users of scripts, e.g. fmt, lint, compile, bundle etc.\r\nSo, I suggest to publish an slimmed down Deno runtime for purely running scripts.\r\nThis can be useful if a large company with strict security requirements wants to distribute such a runtime to all server and desktops.\r\nThe slimmer runtime will have less bugs in it and thus more secure.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "yerlaser", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-07-20T23:45:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-21T20:35:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11475", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11493, "title": "How to get Deno running under OpenWrt?", "body": "```\r\nError relocating ./deno: __memcpy_chk: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: __vfprintf_chk: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: __vsnprintf_chk: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: backtrace: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: backtrace_symbols: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: __mbrlen: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: strtoll_l: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: strtoull_l: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: __isinf: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: __memmove_chk: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: __register_atfork: symbol not found\r\nError relocating ./deno: __res_init: symbol not found\r\n\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mokeyish", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-07-22T14:44:16Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T08:58:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11493", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11497, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-07-22", "body": "- Refactoring CLI so that reading and writing ops can be more easily shared with something like Deno Deploy\r\n  - Lots of general discussion, but key team members unavailable, discussion deferred.\r\n- Should [deno.land/x/sqlite](https://deno.land/x/sqlite) be moved to standard?\r\n  - @bartlomieju suggested that other \"standard libraries\" include SQLite and SQLite is ubiqitious.\r\n  - @kitsonk suggested that the web platform has a \"standard\" for persistance, IndexedDB and that we should focus on that, and there is no compelling reason to add it to `std`.\r\n  - @ry also reflected that if it is working, why add it to `std`.\r\n  - _ACTION_ reach out to the author and understand their feelings\r\n  - _ACTION_ evaluate addressing the [limitations and caveats](https://github.com/dyedgreen/deno-sqlite#disadvantages) in Deno.\r\n- Discussion around testing proposals (#10771 and #11235)\r\n  - Lots of discussion about both proposals, but also the general \"scope\" of testing.\r\n  - Agreement that built in testing is a key feature of Deno CLI that is part of the attraction.\r\n  - Acknowledgement that it is difficult to get simplicity and flexibility right.\r\n  - @dsherret is away, and unable to discuss his proposal, so conversation deferred, but a lot of the background for everyone else has been covered, so should be more actionable next time.\r\n- Refactor code so that the logic behind `deno doc` and `deno info` could be obtained by using web assembly.\r\n  - This would enable other runtimes, like Deploy (or even Node.js) to have a Deno \"view\" of the world.\r\n  - It may eliminate the need to add other APIs to the `Deno` namespace like `Deno.info()` and `Deno.doc()`.\r\n  - Tentatively named the new crate `deno_graph`.\r\n  - Identified several objectives for this:\r\n    - A Deno Deploy endpoint that you can give a URL to a TypeScript file and it responds with the `deno doc` JSON.\r\n    - The Deno CLI uses the same `deno_graph` and `deno_doc` crate.\r\n    - The duplication of analysis logic between the runtime and the language server is eliminated.\r\n  - _ACTION_ @kitsonk to pull together a design document, as there are several questions about implementation that need to be answered to ensure the APIs are usable.\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-07-23T03:46:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-16T10:38:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11497", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11534, "title": "Is FFI a suitable replacement for Plugins?", "body": "It was recently announced that in Q3 2021, the Plugin API will be removed entirely and replaced with a new FFI API. I'm wondering if that's really the best solution? The current plugin API is broken, no doubt, but I'm not sure FFI will be a suitable replacement. It works for calling functions from shared libraries, but for more complex cases like C++ header-only libraries, it will be very difficult to use.\r\n\r\nIt's also version-specific, which means if the user is accidentally using the wrong shared library, it could take a while to figure out the source of the problem.\r\n\r\nMy suggestion for a replacement API would be something like having Deno.openPlugin act as a dynamic import() behind the scenes. Basically, openPlugin would create a new V8 Module via InstantiateModule, and the plugin would act as the ResolveCallback. The plugin is handed a name and a Module and a Context and told \"resolve this name and populate this module.\"\r\n\r\nDoes this sound feasible? If not, does anyone else have any other proposals, or is FFI the only way to go now?", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Symbitic", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-07-27T11:40:49Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:43:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11534", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11606, "title": "Deno.run(...) in test", "body": "I have a deno test which executes version. Pretty simple. And deno even detects that this is leaking resources!!!\r\n\r\n> `deno test --allow-run`\r\n\r\ndeno.test.ts\r\n```\r\nDeno.test(\"deno runs\", async () => {\r\n    const p = Deno.run({\r\n        cmd: [\"deno\", \"--version\"],\r\n        stdout: \"piped\",\r\n        stderr: \"piped\",\r\n    });\r\n    console.log(JSON.stringify(await p) + '');\r\n    const output = await p.output();\r\n    const outStr = new TextDecoder().decode(output);\r\n    // const err = await p.error();\r\n    // const errStr = new TextDecoder().decode(err);\r\n    p.close();\r\n});\r\n```\r\n**Output:**\r\ndeno runs\r\nAssertionError: Test case is leaking resources.\r\n  \"2\": \"stderr\"\r\n}\r\nAfter: {\r\n  \"0\": \"stdin\",\r\n  \"1\": \"stdout\",\r\n  \"2\": \"stderr\",\r\n  \"4\": \"childStderr\"\r\n}\r\n\r\nvs\r\n```\r\nDeno.test(\"deno runs\", async () => {\r\n    const p = Deno.run({\r\n        cmd: [\"deno\", \"--version\"],\r\n        stdout: \"piped\",\r\n        stderr: \"piped\",\r\n    });\r\n    console.log(JSON.stringify(await p) + '');\r\n    // const output = await p.output();\r\n    // const outStr = new TextDecoder().decode(output);\r\n    // const err = await p.error();\r\n    // const errStr = new TextDecoder().decode(err);\r\n    p.close();\r\n});\r\n```\r\nBefore: {\r\n  \"0\": \"stdin\",\r\n  \"1\": \"stdout\",\r\n  \"2\": \"stderr\"\r\n}\r\nAfter: {\r\n  \"0\": \"stdin\",\r\n  \"1\": \"stdout\",\r\n  \"2\": \"stderr\",\r\n  \"4\": \"childStderr\"\r\n}\r\n\r\nShould awaiting on output() be required to clean up resources? How do I await on p.error()? error isn't a function", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tamusjroyce", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-08-06T22:31:32Z", "updated_at": "2023-09-25T04:50:45Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11606", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11636, "title": "Idea: Could Deno be self-hosting someday?", "body": "As we all know, Deno right now is written in Rust with several libraries like Tokio. But could the day ever come where Deno is self-hosting, as in the Deno source code is written in TypeScript and is turned into an executable using e.g. `deno compile`?\r\n\r\nDeno's goal is to be a complete toolchain. If it does become self-hosting, then it joins C/C++ and Rust as one of the few toolchains powerful enough to not need to depend on anything except itself.\r\n\r\nAdvantages:\r\n* **Completes the Circle of Life**: Becoming self-hosting or self-compiling is a big milestone for any language or project. It proves just how strong and mature your technology is. People started taking Rust and LLVM much more seriously once they were feature-complete enough to be used for building themselves.\r\n* **Security**: Rust is safer, but it's not invincible. Unsafe code is still possible. If Deno ever becomes self-hosting enough to compile itself and all dependencies are written in TypeScript, then all code will be written in a language that doesn't allow low-level access to unsafe things like memory in the first place.\r\n* **Package management at its finest**: Decentralized ES Modules for every dependency. No one single source or website to rely on for its building blocks.\r\n\r\nDisadvantages:\r\n* **Performance**: Deno right now has Rust and Tokio to provide as much performance as it needs. [NectarJS](https://github.com/NectarJS/nectarjs) proved that JavaScript can be compiled to native code without GC, but getting things like TCP working on at or near the same level as Rust would likely require quite a bit of time and effort. \r\n* **Cost of Refactoring**: Deno only became stable in May 2020. Adding new features is what the core team is focused on right now. Refactoring the codebase into TypeScript may not be something welcomed by the developers.\r\n\r\nDoes anyone have any thoughts? Are there any pros/cons I'm not thinking of? What does the Deno team think?", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 1, "reactions_heart": 2, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Symbitic", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-08-10T17:42:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T22:09:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11636", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11638, "title": "How to run Deno script in background?", "body": "Now if you exit the terminal, the oak server crashes. And the script is not available at the URL.\r\nHow to run Deno in the background so that it always runs on the server until it is explicitly stopped by a command in the terminal?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "goodwin74", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-08-10T20:55:12Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T18:40:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11638", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11695, "title": "What is `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` used for?", "body": "I'm trying to fetch a WebAssembly module from the server with the new `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming`, which works fine for Rust functions with number arguments but returns `undefined` when a function with string arguments is called.\r\n\r\nThe Rust code:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nuse wasm_bindgen::prelude::*;\r\n\r\n#[wasm_bindgen]\r\npub fn greet(name: &str) -> String {\r\n    return format!(\"Hello, {}!\", name);\r\n}\r\n\r\n#[wasm_bindgen]\r\npub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\r\n    a + b\r\n}\r\n```\r\nIt's compiled with `wasm-pack build --target web` and then served like this:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nconst server = Deno.listen({ port: 8080 });\r\n\r\nfor await (const conn of server) {\r\n  handle(conn);\r\n}\r\n\r\nasync function handle(conn: Deno.Conn) {\r\n  const httpConn = Deno.serveHttp(conn);\r\n  \r\n  for await (const requestEvent of httpConn) {\r\n    try {\r\n      await requestEvent.respondWith(new Response(\r\n        await Deno.readFile(\"./pkg/wasm_bg.wasm\"), {\r\n          status: 200,\r\n          headers: {\r\n            \"content-type\": \"application/wasm\",\r\n          },\r\n        },\r\n      ),\r\n    );\r\n    } catch (e) {\r\n      console.log(e);\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen I run the following code client-side with Deno after the server is up and running:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nconst wasm = await WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming(fetch(\"http://localhost:8080\"));\r\n\r\nconst greet = wasm.instance.exports.greet as (name: string) => string;\r\n\r\nconst add = wasm.instance.exports.add as (a: number, b: number) => number;\r\n\r\nconsole.log(greet(\"World\"));\r\n\r\nconsole.log(add(1, 2));\r\n```\r\n\r\n`add` works as expected but `greet` returns undefined instead of `Hello, World!`. I though it might have something to do with using `format!` So I've tried to return `String::from(name)` as well with the same `undefined` result. Is this not a recommended way to compile WebAssembly for Deno or is it perhaps not possible to convert the Rust mutable `String` type to a JavaScript string in this way? If you need more information I'd be happy to provide it.\r\n\r\nMy `deno --version`:\r\n```shell\r\ndeno 1.13.0 (release, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu) <-- Ubuntu WSL 2\r\nv8 9.3.345.11\r\ntypescript 4.3.5\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "GJZwiers", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-08-13T19:34:56Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-12T11:47:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11695", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11751, "title": "RFC: Inline tests and nested test modules via module blocks", "body": "Note: early very rough draft, most properties are missing from the interface.\r\nThis would be one or two years into the future and most likely an additive feature to declarative tests (#11235).\r\n\r\nOur unit of testing are modules, the test runner runs each module in isolation and already offers a shared scope via the top level module scope.\r\n\r\nWe have before and after hooks via the module's evaluation phase for top level await and we also have lifecycle events in the form of `load` and `unload`.\r\n\r\nPeople are asking for a way to group tests into smaller scopes with its own lifecycle hooks, the answer to that today is to for you to create another module but having to create a module for each logical local block isn't that great.\r\n\r\nThe [module blocks proposal](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-js-module-blocks) (which is currently in stage 2) makes this declarative and we can extend the test harness to embrace module blocks.\r\n\r\n# Interface\r\n\r\n```ts\r\ninterface TestSpecification {\r\n  name: string,\r\n  // TBD what the actual runtime type is (and name for that matter).\r\n  // See https://github.com/tc39/proposal-js-module-blocks\r\n  mod: string | Module;\r\n  ignore: boolean,\r\n  permissions?: TestPermissions;\r\n  // The rest of the options would probably be here and used as the defaults within this scope.\r\n}\r\n\r\ndeclare namespace Deno {\r\n  export function test(test: TestSpecification);\r\n  export function test(name: string, module: string | Module);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n# Example\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nexport function hello() {}\r\n\r\nDeno.test(\"mod.ts\", module {\r\n  import { hello } from \"./mod.ts\";\r\n  \r\n  console.log(\"inline test module evaluated\");\r\n  \r\n  addEventListener('load', () => {\r\n    console.log(\"load event dispatched\");\r\n  });\r\n \r\n  addEventListener('unload', () => {\r\n    console.log(\"unload event dispatched\");\r\n  });\r\n\r\n  Deno.test(\"hello\", function() {\r\n    hello();\r\n  });\r\n  \r\n  Deno.test(\"inner\", module {\r\n    addEventListener('load', () => {\r\n      console.log(\"inner load event dispatched\");\r\n    });\r\n    \r\n    addEventListener('unload', () => {\r\n      console.log(\"inner unload event dispatched\");\r\n    });\r\n\r\n    Deno.test(\"case\", function() {});\r\n  });\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nLife cycle visualised:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\n$ deno test mod.ts\r\nCheck file://example/mod.ts\r\ninline test module evaluated\r\nload event dispatched\r\ntest mod#hello ... ok (3ms)\r\ninner load event dispatched\r\ntest mod#inner#case ... ok (1ms)\r\ninner unload event dispatched\r\nunload event dispatched\r\n```", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "caspervonb", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-08-18T02:23:20Z", "updated_at": "2022-11-17T15:03:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11751", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11753, "title": "How to log `deno lsp` communications?", "body": "I'm having a ***very*** rough time trying to log the stdin and stdout/stderr communications of the `deno lsp` command, to debug [problems with my Nova editor Deno extension](https://github.com/jaydenseric/nova-deno/issues/4) (the command is invoked [here](https://github.com/jaydenseric/nova-deno/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/Deno.novaextension/Scripts/main.js#L114-L119)).\r\n\r\nHow do you do it?\r\n\r\nI've tried the `--log-level debug` option, but that doesn't log the stdio traffic, just Deno internal activity. I've tried crazy bash `tee` related hacks and it only manages to log a bit of traffic, then nothing over time. Googling how to log stdin, stdout, and stderr of a command without disrupting it in bash has been a hugely unproductive so far.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jaydenseric", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-08-18T05:04:51Z", "updated_at": "2023-04-26T04:48:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11753", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11767, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-07-29", "body": "## [Q3 2021 Roadmap](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11168) Review\r\n\r\n- Remove native plugins API, introduce FFI API\r\n  - Status update, that while FFI wasn't perfect, it was ready.\r\n  - _Action_ move forward, and improve things.\r\n- Introduce new sub-process API\r\n  - _Deferred_ due to interested parties being unavailable.\r\n- Stabilize native HTTP server API \r\n  - _Stabilized_ (except the web sockets)\r\n  - Need to move everything over to the new API and find a way to deprecate `std/http`\r\n- Add ability to ignore SSL certificate errors \r\n  - @lucacasonato objected strongly to this, as well as @bnoordhuis is on record as also objecting in other forums. The feeling is it erodes security and it often used by people who don't understand the implications of what they are doing, and don't understand the right ways to address the issues they are encountering.\r\n  - @ry strongly argued that it is a usability issue, and that other platforms (note-ably Chrome and cURL) how escape hatches.\r\n  - _Action_ move forward with a flag that is non trivial and makes it clear that you are doing something _bad_.\r\n- Overhaul `deno doc`\r\n  - @kitsonk has start working on `deno_graph` which was the first step in this process.  Progress will continue when we have some of the fundamentals in place.\r\n- More options in configuration file\r\n  - @bartlomieju indicated that while the initial work was done, that a conversation with him and @piscisaureus led him to believe that the solution was nonviable, especially when it came to how it would align with Deploy.\r\n  - Lots of discussion about the feature and the need. @ry and @kitsonk argued that it was very describable, as it was a big usability issue.  There was general agreement about this point.\r\n  - @ry suggested that some mocks and prototypes be made to see what would work, as it is really challenging to do through a pure \"design document\"\r\n- Implement Class Workers\r\n  - This is only internal purposes and web platform testing, as all the wpt tests related to workers and worker API use classic workers.  Class workers _ARE NOT_ desirable for end users, as they promote legacy patterns which we really don't want to support in Deno.\r\n  - _Action_ get them done to support web platform feature development and testing.\r\n- Code Signing\r\n  - Needs to be done.\r\n  - @ry was going to source certificates.\r\n- Prompt by Default\r\n  - A lot of debate about whether this can be done in a minor release, and what sort of changes can be done\r\n  - @ry strongly argued for it as a usability issue.\r\n  - _Action_ proceed, but it isn't the top of anyone's work queue.\r\n- Don't type check be default\r\n  - @kitsonk will attempt for 1.13\r\n- Websocket support in native HTTP server API\r\n  - Landed, a few issues\r\n  - Wait until 1.14 to stabilise at the earliest\r\n- Move docs to separate repo\r\n  - Done\r\n - Introduce cross-process BroadcastChannel \r\n   - This is desirable, will still try for Q3\r\n \r\n### Language Server\r\n\r\nWe briefly covered the issues here, but ran out of time.  Issue #11168 reflects things accurately.", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-08-19T05:05:54Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-18T15:11:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11767", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11768, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-08-05", "body": "- Update on `deno_graph`\r\n  - was discussed the previous week as part of Q3 roadmap review, but @kitsonk gave a further update on progress, well on track.\r\n  - Discussion about whether import map should be pulled into `deno_graph` or not, as those consuming it would want to have support for import maps.  The functionality is abstracted out as the `Resolver` trait, but there is no implementation.\r\n  - Decision was to wait until the integration of `deno_graph` into CLI occurs.\r\n  - Next step was integration into `deno_doc`.\r\n- Service Workers, do we actually need them? (#5957)\r\n  - Discussion around what they are actually good for as part of Deno.  There is no compelling use case that they solve, and to implement compliant service workers is complicated and full of challenges that would need to be solved.\r\n  - Note, this does not mean that the `Cache` and `CacheStorage` APIs are un-needed or unwanted.  They were implemented as part of service workers and are critical to service workers, but they aren't isolated to service workers in the web platform.\r\n  - _Decision_ the core team is not opposed to service workers, but there is no compelling use case we can see. If someone had a use case and wanted to contribute it to Deno we wouldn't oppose it, though we caution any implementors that _there be dragons_.\r\n- Quota Management and StorageManager API (https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11525)\r\n  - Discussed that all browsers have a non-user facing quota manager.  How they implement it varies from browser to browser.\r\n  - Quota Management is an internal API that enables other user facing features.  The StorageManager API gives a user some view into storage and capacity management to better manage their workloads.\r\n  - Discussed that `DENO_DIR` is unbounded in size and we need to do something eventually there.\r\n  - There is nothing _currently broken_ that this would fix, but if we were to add `CacheStorage` or `IndexedDB` it starts to become a problem.\r\n  - @ry concerned about expending effort on something that doesn't actually fix anything that is broken or doesn't deliver a direct user feature.  Is this pre-mature optimisation?\r\n  - _Action_ to have a breakout meeting once people have better context and have done more research.\r\n- Plan for deprecating `std/http` and `std/ws` (https://github.com/denoland/deno_std/discussions/1034)\r\n  - @kitsonk is concerned that the proposal is just another abstraction and API we have to support.  It isn't compatible with `std/http` as it exists.\r\n  - It is trivial to implement.\r\n  - @ry wondered what would happen with oak since native HTTP was stabilised, @kitsonk indicated it would just start using native.\r\n  - _Action_ determine how someone would change a `std/http` application to work with the new `std` abstraction. If it is change a couple lines, great, if it is more complicated, what value does a new abstraction bring?\r\n- Enable Deno APIs by default in Workers (#8178)\r\n  - Ultimately it is a _mistake_ we made with 1.0.\r\n  - It was not until Deno 1.4 that workers could access the `Deno` namespace and only with an explicit flag.\r\n  - Changing it before 2.0 is likely to cause strange and exotic regressions in code, because of the way permissions interact with workers, and code that might be expecting to run in an environment without the `Deno` namespace actually seeing it, and then trying to do something, but not having the right permissions would break.\r\n  - _Decision_ it is not burdensome to pass `deno: true` in the worker options, because `type: \"module\"` already has to be passed in Deno, and the risk of breaking changes for little to no value for the user isn't worth the risk, but we would like to correct the _mistake_ for Deno 2.0.\r\n - REPL `--eval` flag (#11317)\r\n   - Python has this and it's nice\r\n   - Really solves the problem of having a specific, easily repeatable, REPL context to work with\r\n   - _Action_ progress forward, though specific semantics to debate.", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-08-19T06:21:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-09T04:20:04Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11768", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11771, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-08-12", "body": "- Testing Face-Off \ud83e\udd4a \r\n  - In one corner @dsherret with a declarative approach\r\n    - Solves the filtering use case better\r\n    - Main drawback, extra features mean more syntax\r\n    - Declarative approach more similar to existing popular JavaScript testing frameworks\r\n  - In the other corner @lucacasonato with an imperative approach\r\n    - Functionality added with an argument passed to the testing function.\r\n    - It is _just_ code.\r\n    - Can't filter subtests, but this is how it works in Go and that seems fine\r\n  - Both solutions are backwards compatible\r\n  - \ud83d\uddf3\ufe0f  Team attending the meeting divided\r\n  - _Action_ wider polling of the core team before a final decision is made, but we need to make one.\r\n- NodeJS compatibility, what is our high-level strategy, goals, next steps?\r\n  - Are we ok with the likes of Skypack and esm.sh, or are we actually trying to have full API compatibility with Node? \"Node compatibility\" is a nebulous concept.\r\n  - `std/node` lacks major things like `http`, a lot of tools and workloads can't run on Deno because of it.\r\n  - Do we see Node and Deno co-existing, or do we want to think of Node as \"legacy\"?  (Note, there was no clear decision on this question, it was just a conversation)\r\n  - @kitsonk full parity would take forever and be \"useless\".  We should focus on particular workflows/workloads, fill in the gaps, and figure out a well supported migration path.\r\n  - Should we put effort into documenting how to write code for both platforms? _Yes_\r\n  - What Node has well established and Deno is generally lacking are well supported and performant DB drivers.  This is a problem, and problem for bring workloads over from Node to Deno.\r\n  - Discussion again that we need to prioritise this space, if we try to do everything we will do nothing.\r\n  - If we could say \"you can run your Express server under Deno faster than Node\" would that encourage adoption? _No clear answer_\r\n  - @bartlomieju pointed out we had previously prioritised `std/node` here: https://github.com/denoland/deno_std/discussions/756\r\n  - Further debate on if we focus on front-end workflows or backend servers.  There were a lot of good points.\r\n  - _Action_ lack of `std/node/http` is a problem, no matter what we should solve that.  Skypack has said they would move to `std/node` if we had `http` which would also be a win.\r\n  - _Action_ identify workflows/workloads we want to focus on and prioritise them, then figure out next steps.\r\n- `Deno.ConnectTlsOptions#certFile` is a bad name (#11608)\r\n  - _Action_ Yes it is.\r\n- IP CIDR ranges in `--allow-net` (#11509)\r\n  - No specific opposition, but there are some challenges and edge cases that need to be worked through.\r\n- Permission Flags: Instead of granular permissions, have \"macro\" sandbox modes (e.g. `red`/`green`/`orange`)\r\n  - If security is impractical then it's insecure is the motivation for this suggestion.\r\n  - example: `deno run --security=red main.ts`\r\n  - There was a lot of debate and discussion about the value of this, and if it actually improves usability and security, or just sort of obfuscates it more.  Further discussion to continue.\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-08-19T06:48:52Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-20T05:48:21Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11771", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11773, "title": "Should prompt always use UTF-8 even when the connected console may be using a different encoding?", "body": "My question is prompted (pun intended) by [unicode - Deno: how to take care of accents when reading from windows terminal prompt? - Stack Overflow](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/68839705/deno-how-to-take-care-of-accents-when-reading-from-windows-terminal-prompt).\r\n\r\nShould [neves](https://stackoverflow.com/users/10335/neves) not have run into an issue here? If [`prompt`](https://doc.deno.land/builtin/stable#prompt) were to detect and use whatever encoding the TTY is using (e.g. `windows-1252` instead of `utf-8`) then it would have worked with no problems. Would doing such cause some web standard compatibility/divergence issue or as it is isolated to Deno's terminal `window` it might be appropriate.\r\n\r\nThank you.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 2, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mfulton26", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-08-19T13:39:40Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-01T09:56:44Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11773", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11783, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-08-19", "body": "- Status of testing API proposals\r\n  - The core team \"voted\" and the declarative approach had more support\r\n  - That being said, @lucacasonato and @dsherret have being discussing together and with others outside the core team and feel that we will have some solid actions soon.\r\n- Deno Configuration File\r\n  - Reviewed the goals and non-goals (https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/3179#issuecomment-897393866)\r\n  - Reviewed the \"MVP\" (https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11686)\r\n  - Discussed format, and how @ry @lucacasonato and @bartlomieju had explored other formats together and that only viable format is JSONC. We acknowledged that while we will continue to parse files with the `.json` extension as JSONC, we want to encourage people to use the `.jsonc` extension.\r\n  - There was discussion lots of discussion about automatic lookup resolution logic, from what files names in what order and the \"location\" of the configuration file.  After a lot of discussion and various viewpoints, we acknowledged that while it would be great to have automatic discovery as part of the MVP, we should get out an MVP without it to get feedback from the community and then quickly determine the discovery logic.\r\n- Can we share `op_read`/`op_write` between CLI and Deploy?\r\n  - There are effectively 12 different resources that all have the same interface.\r\n  - There was a debate on if this is over indexing on DRY versus just being the \"convergent evolution\".\r\n  - There was no clear consensus on if this would really help anything and the change would be non-trivial.\r\n- Plugin & FFI Discussion\r\n  - Talked about feedback to date from the community\r\n  - We acknowledged FFI is very limited.\r\n  - We discussed how while it might feel seductive, Plugins are untenable.  They require exposing the innards of Deno and it is hard enough trying to keep the user space non-breaking changes and plugins add a whole new layer of compatibility issues, and people couple to even more \"private\" things, even if you say \"please don't do this\".\r\n  - The preferred pattern we want to promote is making it easy to consume some of the \"innards\" of Deno via web assembly, versus enabling exposing internal APIs.  The biggest advantage is that people can still run \"old\" code while taking advantage of an updated runtime.  We have seen good proof points of this via things like SQLite running under Deno and crates like `deno_graph`.\r\n  - We will continue to take feedback on FFI and what challenges and limitations the community are facing.\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-08-20T05:32:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-03T17:47:07Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11783", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11788, "title": "error: TypeError: Self-contained binaries don't support module loading", "body": "I want to ask a question. I compiled the oak based web framework using compile API, and there was an error when I started it on liunx, and I didn't find a corresponding solution at all. I understand that compiling is packaged into binary, and all HTTP dependencies are added to the binary package. I'm a little confused about this. Hope to get a reply, thank you for taking the time to answer, thank you", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "iwindx", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-08-20T08:21:27Z", "updated_at": "2023-06-19T13:11:20Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11788", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11809, "title": "Dead Packages?", "body": "Hey was just wondering if there's a procedure for packages which have been published to deno.land but are now defunct. For example: https://deno.land/x/dex@1.0.2. The repository linked to for this package no longer exists. ", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "shellscape", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-08-22T18:43:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:41:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11809", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11855, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-08-26", "body": "- Discussion on micro task queue issues deferred, will be breakout meeting (#11731)\r\n- `deno_lint` using the language server (denoland/deno_lint#805)\r\n  - Using the language server to weed out \"false positives\" of linting diagnostics that would be otherwise returned, but type information would be required (e.g. fall-throughs that are fall-throughs because a function that has return type `never` is called)\r\n  - Discussion around how `deno_lint` is _fast_ and spinning up of the tsc isolate is _slow_.  Having really fast rules and then every once in a while hitting a 1s delay when linting might be jarring from a UX perspective.\r\n  - Discussion around how a swc type checker (or at least a partial one which could infer some types) would be more ideal way.\r\n  - Discussion around how it would take a lot of re-architecting of CLI to be able to make it available\r\n  - Not a small amount of work for limited upside, though valuable upside, so low priority.\r\n  - _Action_ a low priority investigation/design\r\n- Restructuring \"tooling\" code for re-usability\r\n  - @bartlomieju brought up that there is always code that needs to be shared amongst the tools and that we have done some refactoring in CLI to co-locate that code, but then we also have seperate repos for `deno_graph`, `deno_lint`, `deno_doc`, etc.\r\n  - @bartlomieju and @lucacasonato identified that some of the scope analysis logic in `deno_lint` might need to be shared with `deno_doc`\r\n  - Is there a better way to share code like this?  A single \"tooling\" repo? Do we proactively refactor?\r\n  - Updating swc is a pain point.  There is also a lot of \"shared\" code when it comes to managing the AST, do we need a `deno_ast` crate?\r\n  - Discussion around multi-crate repos versus single crate repos.\r\n  - Discussion around more release automation to take some of the pain points away.\r\n  - _Action_ to not take any proactive steps, but keep discussing the _right_ choices as code needs to be shared/refactored.\r\n- Supporting config for `deno fmt` (#3695 #11553)\r\n  - @bartlomieju suggests at a minimum we need deal with file includes and excludes and tabs v. spaces\r\n  - @lucacasonato strongly feels that no config is better, and that if spaces v tabs is an accessibility issue we should switch the code base to tabs.\r\n  - Discussion around how Go gets away without a configurable formatter, but counter points that rustfmt allows a configuration.\r\n  - Discussion around how Prettier has _minimal_ options and Prettier has become the _gold standard_ in JavaScript/TypeScript and how if we drew the line at supporting Prettier options, we could go tell people to have _arguments_ with Prettier about configurable options.\r\n  - Discussion how a lack of a configuration is impeding adoption. Lots of \"could possibly\" with little specific examples of where a lack of a configuration was a barrier to adopting Deno (versus adopting just `deno fmt`)\r\n  - Discussion around how the community likely wouldn't say \"no please don't add configuration\", so community support is a bit hard to base a decision off of.\r\n  - Discussion around how tabs v spaces is a real issue, versus just a subjective one, while single-quotes v double-quotes and ASI are more \"emotional\" arguments, but real in some cases.\r\n  - @lucacasonato actually gave a counter example to the argument, saying that contributing to a repo recently that was all single quote, he couldn't use save on format, trying to point out it would be great if everyone used the same style.  On the other hand, if you could configure it, you could use save on format and still contribute to a code base.\r\n  - _Action_ @bartlomieju to put forward a proposal and to continue the discussion with the core team and the wider community. (#11857)\r\n- Default Location/Default Origin/Local File Fetch proposal\r\n  - There are two big \"usability\" issues with CLI that related to `--location` and `window.location`.\r\n    - You cannot access `localStorage` without a `--location` flag, making it difficult to persist data, and with the desire to have `CacheStorage` and `IndexedDB` in the future, not having a solution means even more of a challenge.\r\n    - You cannot currently fetch local files, but if you could, and you passed a relative path, what would happen? The standard says that you should use `window.location` as the base.\r\n  - There is an internal design document about how to achieve have an internal origin that is derived from the environment to persistence without a `--location`.\r\n  - Discussion how local file fetches, an implied `window.location` and an implied origin for persistence are 3 seperate topics.\r\n  - _Action_ @kitsonk to break out the 3 problem spaces\r\n  - _Action_ @kitsonk to raise the proposal for implied origin for public discussion (#11882)\r\n  - _Action_ @kitsonk to pull together some thoughts on semantics for local file fetch and circulate among the core team for discussion at the next design meeting\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-08-27T00:11:18Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-22T07:49:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11855", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11907, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-09-02", "body": "- Review of open items on the Q3 Roadmap #11168 \r\n  - Subprocess API is making good progress, but blocked on `Deno.kill()` stabilisation (discussed later)\r\n  - Testing overhaul to be discussed later\r\n  - Good progress on docs, with a lot of the infrastructure delivered, but it will be Q4 before a refreshed doc.deno.land is done.\r\n  - Config file making progress.\r\n  - No progress on code signing.\r\n  - No progress on prompt by default.\r\n  - No type check by default has a PR, but needs to be discussed (discussed later)\r\n  - No progress on cross-process broadcast channel\r\n  - Crypto is making good progress. Likely more changes/features for 1.14\r\n  - LSP v2 registry, import map improvements not likely for 1.14\r\n- Testing proposal update\r\n  - Going to implement the imperative API (with step metadata) first\r\n  - An API for a declarative approach will be built on top of the imperative API.  It will be then determined if it gets baked into CLI, lives in `std` or lives as a community API.\r\n  - A POC polyfill for mocha has been done by @lucacasonato though it needs the implementation of the imperative API to really prove it, but the concept is sound.\r\n  - _Action_ open an issue for next steps (#11901)\r\n- Microtask queue issues (#11731)\r\n  - A breakout meeting indicated that it isn't easily addressable.\r\n  - Impact is several WPT tests are failing, which is a strong indication that there are edge cases in some of the APIs that people are encountering, though it is really hard to pin them down to repeatable re-producable issues because of the nature of the problem.\r\n  - _Action_ further investigations by the team to see if there is something that can be done.\r\n- Fetch `file://` URLs via `fetch()` #2150\r\n  - There is an internal proposal to resolve the issue, being an \"MVP\" which doesn't support things like `HEAD` requests, range requests, relative URLs or content-type.\r\n  - Not incompatible with the spec, but defines undefined behaviour, which is a risk.\r\n  - Broad team support.\r\n  - _Action_ @kitsonk to research behaviour in Firefox of local file handling and raise an issue for implementation\r\n- Let move `deno_webgpu` out of CLI's tree (gfx-rs/wgpu#1421)\r\n  - The Rust wpgu implementation which we use in Deno would like our JavaScript bindings to be moved over to their org, so they can run the spec conformance tests and iterate quicker.\r\n  - This is a win/win for everyone, as we haven't had the time to maintain the bindings and there are long cycles in updating, as well as it is good collaboration between projects.\r\n  - There are some implementation issues to work out so we can re-consume back into Deno, but it feels worth the effort.\r\n  - _Action_ let's do it!\r\n- Stabilize `Deno.kill()` API #11757\r\n  - Agreed it should be stabilised, but we need to get rid of the clunky `LinuxSignal` and `MacOSSignal` enums first.\r\n  - _Action_ open an issue (#11900) and implement\r\n- Config file discovery\r\n  - @ry presented the outcome of a breakout meeting regarding config file auto-discovery.\r\n  - Of the 4 logical potential options, what is termed as _target'_ was recommend, that the target of the subcommand will be used as a base for searching for a `Deno.json`, ascending parent directories to the root.\r\n  - There will be some challenges to work out with implementing for the LSP, but should be achievable.\r\n  - _Action_ @ry will open an issue for implementation, target 1.15\r\n- Markdown formatting - wrapping links like prettier (dprint/dprint-plugin-markdown#53)\r\n  - Everyone agrees that no-wrap is good and aligned with prettier\r\n  - _Action_ @dsherret to implement\r\n- No type checking by default (#11810)\r\n  - Open questions with the PR about `deno test` not type checking being surprising, `deno cache` behaviour changes significantly and the potential introduction of `deno check`.\r\n  - There is a warning, that can't be disabled, that is really annoying.\r\n  - @lucacasonato wonders if we should just wait for 2.0\r\n  - _Action_ punt to 2.0\r\n- What about 2.0?\r\n  - The no type checking prompted a discussion about 2.0.\r\n  - Consensus that we should explore what 2.0 would look like, but that we would want to do it \"quickly\", like after 1.15.\r\n  - _Action_ explore what 2.0 scope would look like at next design meeting.\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 8, "reactions_plus": 5, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 3, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-09-02T23:08:14Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:41:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11907", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11919, "title": "Use dependabot to automate rust dependencies update", "body": "Would deno interested in using dependabot for automating rust dependencies update?\r\n\r\nI already setup my fork `deno` repo for dependabot. If this idea is accepted, I am happy to make a pr for dependabot setup.\r\n\r\nCommit:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/F3n67u/deno/commit/<HASH_REDACTED>\r\n\r\nExample deps update pr:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/F3n67u/deno/pulls", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "F3n67u", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-09-05T04:14:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-17T09:44:21Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11919", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11952, "title": "React Testing With Deno", "body": "It is well-documented how to use React with Deno. However, it is not well-documented on how to go about unit testing components. At first impulse, it seems the simplest solution would be to use the `react-test-renderer`.\r\n\r\nHowever, the `act` method only works if the global variable `__DEV__` is truthy ([link](https://github.com/facebook/react/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/packages/react/src/ReactAct.js#L19))\r\n\r\nWhen `__DEV__` is falsy, `act` outputs the following error message:\r\n\r\n```txt\r\nact(...) is not supported in production builds of React, and might not behave as expected.\r\n```\r\n\r\n_Note: \u26a0\ufe0f I don't like global variables. This issue is not advocating to use them, but rather this issue is primarily concerned with healthy testing practices when developing React applications._\r\n\r\nHow one might go about getting the following test to work?\r\n\r\n```tsx\r\nimport React from \"https://esm.sh/react@17.0.2\";\r\nimport { act, create } from \"https://esm.sh/react-test-renderer@17.0.2\";\r\nimport { assertNotEquals } from \"https://deno.land/std@0.100.0/testing/asserts.ts\";\r\n\r\nDeno.test({\r\n  name: \"react component test\",\r\n  fn: async () => {\r\n    const GreeterComponent: React.FC = () => <h1>Hello World</h1>;\r\n\r\n    let root: ReturnType<typeof create> = null as unknown as ReturnType<\r\n      typeof create\r\n    >;\r\n\r\n    await act(() => {\r\n      root = create(<GreeterComponent />);\r\n    });\r\n\r\n    const result = root.toJSON();\r\n    assertNotEquals(result, null);\r\n  },\r\n});\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "tatemz", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-09-07T20:53:41Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T17:46:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11952", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11972, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-09-09", "body": "## Deno 2.0 Scope Discussion\r\n\r\nWe dedicated this meeting to discussing tentative Deno 2.0 discussion.  Based on the discussion we feel we are still looking at doing a 2.0 release in November.  Prior to the meeting we had collected candidate items, and the process we followed in the meeting:\r\n\r\n- \"Owner\" briefly introduced the candidate item so that everyone had a shared understanding.\r\n- We added any last minute entries.\r\n- We voted against the items to prioritise the order of discussion.\r\n- We mapped each item in order of priority against low and high value and desirability versus low and high effort, planning to focus on those things that had high value/desirability and low effort.\r\n\r\nBased on that, we have the following scope items we are considering for Deno 2.0 at this point.  Note this is a working list, it is subject to change as some of the features get refined, scoped and investigated:\r\n\r\n- Move JavaScript only \"stuff\" out of the Deno Namespace (#9795)\r\n- Enable `Deno` namespace by default in workers (#8174)\r\n- Don't type check be default (#11340)\r\n- New subprocess API  (#11016, #5501, #11618)\r\n- Turn TypeScript `useUnknownInCatchVariables` on (#11826)\r\n- Remove deprecated `Deno.customInspect` (#10929)\r\n- Lockfile generated by default (#11971)\r\n- Switch JSX/TSX default constructors (#11186)\r\n- Have a single configuration for TypeScript (#7732)\r\n- Internal Origin for Persistance (#11882)\r\n- Don't report check diagnostics for _external_ scripts by default (#11970)\r\n- Use `--allow-import` for dynamic imports and workers (#8266)\r\n\r\nThere were a handful of issues we didn't have time to triage and will discuss them quickly at the start of next week's meeting:\r\n\r\n- Make documentation test executable (#11782)\r\n- Allow commas in args on the CLI (#6553, #9535)\r\n- `NotCapable` error to distinguish between permissions and capabilities (#7394)\r\n- Flatten `Deno.errors` into `Deno` namespace (#9646)\r\n- Remove `Deno.read()` and `Deno.write()`\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 4, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 4, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-09-10T01:46:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-05T17:51:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11972", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 11973, "title": "How to enable TypeScript `dom` lib via triple slash directives in `.js` files", "body": "I have a significant amount of isomorphic `.js` files that I intend to publish in a library that have have type checking enabled via a `// @ts-check` comment and JSDoc types throughout the code. This code uses the DOM (in React component hooks and event handlers, so it's ok during Deno SSR). Unfortunately, there doesn't seem to be a way to enable the built-in TypeScript `dom` lib for types within `.js` files, using the triple slash directive `/// <reference lib=\"dom\" />`:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"961\" alt=\"Screen Shot 2021-09-10 at 3 13 59 pm\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1754873/132804324-76da51fb-28aa-47e9-b43f-e8104499dce2.png\">\r\n\r\nThe Deno lint error being:\r\n\r\n```\r\nThis triple-slash reference directive is not allowed in JavaScript\r\nIn JavaScript only the `types` directive is allowed, like `/// <reference types=\"...\" />`\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhy does this limitation exist? Can it be lifted?\r\n\r\nUsing [a `lib` property in a `tsconfig.json` file](https://deno.land/manual@v1.13.2/typescript/configuration#targeting-deno-and-the-browser) is not a good solution, as only some files in the project are isomorphic, and these files will be published and deep imported by users so all types information needs to be contained within the file.\r\n\r\nI considered `/// <reference types=\"\" />` and using a URL to a third-party Deno-compatible DOM `.d.ts` file, but this sucks because Deno ships with official DOM types so surely there is a way to reference them. It would be bad for my DOM types to conflict in user's projects that use the proper built-in DOM lib.\r\n\r\nSometimes it feels like I'm the only one trying to use Deno to write isomorphic, published JS (see also https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11362#issuecomment-909748559). I'm getting tired and desperate trying to solve this, so any help is greatly appreciated.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jaydenseric", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-09-10T05:49:29Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-04T02:50:01Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11973", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12077, "title": "Is there documentation for Deno's FFI API?", "body": "I see that a FFI API was released in  1.13, but I haven't been able to locate any documentation on how to use it. Are docs missing from the manual and API reference because it requires `--unstable`?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "zachauten", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-09-14T22:17:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-07T23:47:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12077", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12108, "title": "Design Meeting 2021-09-16", "body": "- Deno 2.0 scheduled for release November 22nd\r\n  - Agreement there is sufficient scope for a 2.0 release\r\n  - Noted that TypeScript 4.5 should be GA'ed on the 16th\r\n  - @ry raised a concern about how close that is to the release, but discussed that the beta and RC come well in advance to have a high confidence in the release.\r\n- Discussion of additional scope items for 2.0.\r\n  - We went through the list of un-triaged items as well as a few more \"late additions\".  The results are the following items were added to the scope:\r\n    - `NotCapable` error to distinguish between permissions and capabilities (#7394)\r\n    - Remove `Deno.read()` and `Deno.write()` (#12107)\r\n    - JSON Modules (#7623) (TypeScript 4.5 should be the last piece of the puzzle for import assertions, so...)\r\n    - op metrics refactoring/cleanup (#12109)\r\n  - _Action_ @kitsonk to help ensure that all items have issues and are assigned to an \"owner\"\r\n  - _Action_ all \"owners\" will be ready next week to discuss if their items are viable for Nov 22nd and if there are any risks or issues that might block it.\r\n- Expanding the ecosystem\r\n  - There was a long discussion on how we can help expand the ecosystem of Deno.  The perceived challenges:\r\n    - Growth of users is relatively flat\r\n    - It is speculated a lot of users shy away because adopting Deno seems hard or is hard\r\n    - Perception that consuming Node.js code is hard (we don't believe it is, but the perception exists)\r\n    - Using Deno as your development platform and sharing code with Node.js is hard (we feel this is a real problem)\r\n  - @ry proposed a few actions to drive adoption of Deno and expand the ecosystem:\r\n    - Allow extensionless imports under a _legacy_ flag.\r\n    - Support bare specifiers for Node.js built-in modules.\r\n  - @kitsonk proposed a counter proposal of:\r\n    - Provide supported tooling to migrate a `package.json` to an import map and potentially other features to \"convert\" to a Deno-first project.\r\n    - Add `deno emit` subcommand to emit a program as a set of ESM and/or CommonJS modules and a package.json for publishing to npm or other downstream consumption.\r\n    - Complete `std` `node/*` modules\r\n    - Consider a ruggadised polyfill for the `Deno` namespace to use with `deno emit`\r\n  - There was a lot of discussion and debate around the extensionless imports which sort of came down to the following:\r\n    - For: a belief that extensionless imports would allow a lot of existing TypeScript code to run under Deno and once there is a big enough headwind, they can be removed.\r\n    - Against: a belief that extensionless imports doesn't actually solve compatibility problems, confuses users, likely will lead to huge support challenges and introduces _legacy_ features that will exist for ever.\r\n    - Further actions were that we needed to do analysis and discussion on this topic.\r\n  - The biggest concern around @kitsonk proposal was the concern that people would be resistant to rewriting all their code to use extensions, even if that were automated.\r\n", "category": "Design Meetings", "category_slug": "design-meetings", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 4, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 2, "author": "kitsonk", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-09-16T22:54:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T12:50:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12108", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12113, "title": "How to check types for the entire project via CLI?", "body": "There is [an issue about introducing a `deno check` CLI command](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11786), which presumably would allow TypeScript types to be checked for the entire project via CLI (for CI), but that could be weeks/months away from a resolution so how do we do it today?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 20, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jaydenseric", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-09-17T03:32:39Z", "updated_at": "2024-07-13T15:36:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12113", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12132, "title": "Dependency analysis of deno", "body": "Is there a command like `npm ls` in `Deno` to view dependencies?\r\nRecently, when I used `Deno` to build a `React` application, I encountered the problem that external dependent packages refer to different versions of react many times, but I didn't find the relevant command to view the dependent package reference analysis in `Deno`.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "0x1af2aec8f957", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-09-18T05:39:38Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T16:06:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12132", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12149, "title": "Shedding some light on the ecosystem", "body": "There was some talk elsewhere on the less than desired rate of adoption Deno is getting. Just some thought on how to improve or work around what I think is the biggest barrier users have to actually start making stuff with deno - the small, relatively immature the ecosystem around it.\r\n\r\nMy personal experience with the core deno system has been wonderful. Simple, unopinionated, ergonomic, easy, highly browser compatible, modern, fast, stable-ish, consistently asynchronous, \"fun\", \"clean\", what's not to love? I feel like deno is on a point where the developer experience by itself is a great incentive for adoption. Judging from that, developers should be flocking around deno.\r\n\r\nThe main hurdle is the small amount and immaturity of third party modules. Obviously that situation can only be improved by deno gaining momentum/adoption. What we can do, though, is _helping users to find what good there already exist on the ecosystem._ \r\n\r\nThe deno.land/x is a frustrating experience as there so much noise from unmaintained or just plain placeholder modules (early name-grabs and such). The star system does not really tell much whether the module is actually usable, mature or maintained. It's incredibly frustrating, hard detective work to find anything useful from there.\r\n\r\n_Users need help finding modules they can actually use to accomplish stuff._  Rather than finding stuff from random articles around the net, I think it would be super helpful if there was some curated information on the official website. I propose two things:\r\n\r\n1) \r\n\r\nA manually curated list of the \"good stuff\" somewhere clearly visibile on deno.land. To be on this list, for ease of maintenance and minimizing noise, the modules should:\r\n\r\n- be somewhat stable/mature\r\n- have a big enough scope (no one-liners or other tiny utility classes, but actual libraries)\r\n- have a somewhat wide appeal for developers\r\n\r\nUnfortunately at the current state of the ecosystem, this would not be a huge list, and it would be feasible to maintain manually. \r\n\r\nI understand if deno does not want to officially enforce some third party packages over others, but at this point I think users gravely need help navigating the ecosystem, and this would be a justified. \r\n\r\nJust a few examples of the modules that should be covered: oak, sqlite, canvas \r\n\r\n2) \r\n\r\nAnother page again on deno.land, dedicated for users coming from node/NPM. This would involve identifying and listing the most popular, essential Node modules from NPM. For these, we would have notes on how to accomplish the same task in Deno. If any of the following things apply, it would be explained:\r\n\r\n- is there a deno port\r\n- can any of the newer web standard API's supported by deno be used to accomplish the same task (sqlite->webstorage)\r\n- is there a deno package that answers the same problem (express->oak)\r\n- can the NPM module in question be used \"as is\" in deno through esm.sh or such service, if so, how.\r\n\r\n\r\n---------------\r\nJust some thoughts for your consideration, I truly hope deno soon gains the momentum it deserves!", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 14, "reactions_plus": 10, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 4, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jtoppine", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-09-20T08:11:56Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-19T19:42:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12149", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12225, "title": "Major releases and breaking changes", "body": "I was always under the impression that Deno would be backwards-compatible eternally, based on\r\n\r\n> As of Deno 1.0.0, the Deno namespace APIs are stable. That means we will strive to make code working under 1.0.0 continue to work in future versions. - [Deno Manual, Stability](https://deno.land/manual@v1.14.1/runtime/stability) / [Release Schedule](https://deno.land/manual@v1.14.1/contributing/release_schedule)\r\n\r\nBut this is obviously not the case, as there are several breaking changes listed above. Could you release some information on how often major breaking version changes are expected in Deno? This seems like a fairly important question to consider, especially in enterprise context. For example, its strong backwards compatibility is arguably one of the core reasons Java has succeeded in staying relevant over the years. Personally I'm a big fan of Deno but would not really consider it in any large-scale project things keep changing so much.\r\n\r\nIn other words, don't you think it would be enough (at least regarding Deno namespace changes) to deprecate things, but not break them?\r\n\r\nIt's already happening, unfortunately, as a *lot* of Deno code found on the internet does in fact not work anymore (pre 1.0, especially). Often, imports in code samples are dead (removed from std, which itself is also still not stable). A phenomenon quite familiar from web browsing, but in programming languages, a rather unique aspect of Deno.\r\n\r\nAnyway, what's the philosophy like for future breaking changes, especially long-term? I was unable to find any information on this\r\n\r\n(Not taking into account TS version bumps)\r\n\r\n(migrated from #12110, where you can also find answers on this by [@vwkd](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/12110#issuecomment-927134246) and [@GJZwiers](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/12110#issuecomment-927179184)", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "phil294", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-09-25T21:20:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-26T06:30:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12225", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12261, "title": "Using custom JSX transforms in Deno?", "body": "How does one use a custom JSX transform in Deno?\r\n\r\nFor example, I need this:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst div = <div>hello</div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nto be essentially this at runtime:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst div = document.createElement('div')\r\ndiv.textContent = 'hello'\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn non-Deno projects, this can be achieved with https://www.npmjs.com/package/babel-preset-solid for Babel, for example.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "trusktr", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-09-28T20:09:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:41:06Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12261", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12326, "title": "Re-exporting a type when the '--isolatedModules' flag is provided requires using 'export", "body": "I have an old Deno code like below and want to update it, but don't know how:\r\n\r\n\r\n    export {\r\n      Application,\r\n      Router,\r\n      RouterContext, //FIRST ERRRROR HAPPENS HERE\r\n      Context,\r\n      send,\r\n    } from \"https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/mod.ts\";\r\n    export { MongoClient } from \"https://deno.land/x/mongo@v0.27.0/mod.ts\";\r\n    export {\r\n      hashSync,\r\n      compareSync,\r\n    } from \"https://deno.land/x/bcrypt@v0.2.4/mod.ts\";\r\n    import \"https://deno.land/x/dotenv@v3.0.0/load.ts\";\r\n    export  {\r\n      makeJwt,\r\n      setExpiration,\r\n      Jose,\r\n      Payload,\r\n    } from \"https://deno.land/x/djwt@v2.4/create.ts\"; //SECOND ERRROR HAPPENS HERE\r\n    \r\n    export  {\r\n      validateJwt,\r\n      JwtObject,\r\n    } from \"https://deno.land/x/djwt@v2.4/validate.ts\"; //THIRD ERRRROR HAPPENS HERE\r\n\r\nIt gives me these errors:\r\n\r\nFirst error:\r\n\r\n    Re-exporting a type when the '--isolatedModules' flag is provided requires using 'export type'.\r\n\r\nSecond error:\r\n\r\n    Uncached or missing remote URL: \"https://deno.land/x/djwt@v2.4/create.ts\".\r\n\r\nThird error:\r\n\r\n    Uncached or missing remote URL: \"https://deno.land/x/djwt@v2.4/validate.ts\".\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-05T03:17:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-28T14:49:49Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12326", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12340, "title": "Property '' does not exist on type 'Promise<Uint8Array> | Promise<any>| Promise<URLSearchParams> | FormDataReader | Promise<string>|undefined'.deno-ts", "body": "I am a new Deno learner following a tutorial video. The following code is a part of the tutorial's Authentication file code. As it's written by previous version of Deno has some errors that I could resolve most of them except the following errors:\r\n\r\n    import {\r\n      create, verify, decode, getNumericDate, \r\n    } from \"../deps.ts\";\r\n    import {RouterContext} from \"https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/mod.ts\";\r\n    import { userCollection } from \"../mongo.ts\";\r\n    import { hashSync, compareSync } from \"../deps.ts\";\r\n    import User from \"../models/User.ts\";\r\n    \r\n    export class AuthController {\r\n      async register(ctx: RouterContext) {\r\n        const { value: { name, email, password } } = await ctx.request.body();\r\n    \r\n        let user = await User.findOne({ email });\r\n        if (user) {\r\n          ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n          ctx.response.body = { message: \"Email is already used\" };\r\n          return;\r\n        }\r\n        const hashedPassword = hashSync(password);\r\n    //////////////// FIRST ERROR HAPPENS HERE///////////////////\r\n        user = new User({ name, email, password: hashedPassword }); \r\n    \r\n        await user.save();\r\n        ctx.response.status = 201;\r\n        ctx.response.body = {\r\n          id: user.id,\r\n          name: user.name,\r\n          email: user.email\r\n        };\r\n      }\r\n      async login(ctx: RouterContext) {\r\n    /////////////////SECOND ERROR HAPPENS HERE /////////////////////\r\n        const { value: { email, password } } = await ctx.request.body(); \r\n        if (!email || !password) {\r\n          ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n          ctx.response.body = { message: \"Please provide email and password\" };\r\n          return;\r\n        }\r\n        let user = await User.findOne({ email });\r\n        if (!user) {\r\n          ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n          ctx.response.body = { message: \"Incorrect email\" };\r\n          return;\r\n        }\r\n        if (!compareSync(password, user.password)) {\r\n          ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n          ctx.response.body = { message: \"Incorrect password\" };\r\n          return;\r\n        }\r\n    \r\n        const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey(\r\n          { name: \"HMAC\", hash: \"SHA-512\" },\r\n          true,\r\n          [\"sign\", \"verify\"],\r\n        );\r\n    \r\n        const jwt = create( { \r\n          alg: \"HS256\",\r\n          typ: \"JWT\",\r\n        }, {\r\n          iss: user.email,\r\n          exp: getNumericDate(\r\n            Date.now() + parseInt(Deno.env.get(\"JWT_EXP_DURATION\") || \"0\"))\r\n        },\r\n        key\r\n        );\r\n    \r\n        ctx.response.body = {\r\n          id: user.id,\r\n          name: user.name,\r\n          email: user.email,\r\n          jwt,\r\n        };\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n    \r\n    export default new AuthController();\r\n\r\nFirst error:\r\n\r\n    Property 'name' does not exist on type 'Promise<Uint8Array> | Promise<any> | Promise<URLSearchParams> | FormDataReader | Promise<string> | undefined'.deno-ts(2339) \r\n    \r\n    Property 'email' does not exist on type 'Promise<Uint8Array> | Promise<any> | Promise<URLSearchParams> | FormDataReader | Promise<string> | undefined'.deno-ts(2339) \r\n    \r\n    Property 'password' does not exist on type 'Promise<Uint8Array> | Promise<any> | Promise<URLSearchParams> | FormDataReader | Promise<string> | undefined'.deno-ts(2339)\r\n\r\nSecond error:\r\n\r\n    Property 'email' does not exist on type 'Promise<Uint8Array> | Promise<any> | Promise<URLSearchParams> | FormDataReader | Promise<string> | undefined'.deno-ts(2339) \r\n    \r\n    Property 'password' does not exist on type 'Promise<Uint8Array> | Promise<any> | Promise<URLSearchParams> | FormDataReader | Promise<string> | undefined'.deno-ts(2339)\r\n\r\nI can't understand what does it mean and how can I fix them?\r\n\r\nPS: The whole project code can be find here: https://github.com/thecodeholic/deno-survey", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-05T20:04:30Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-16T08:36:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12340", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12365, "title": "error: Found argument '--allow-plugin' which wasn't expected, or isn't valid in this context Did you mean --allow-run?", "body": "I am a new Deno learner and am trying to following a web tutorial about it. But when I try to run my application with the following command, it gives me the followed error:\r\n\r\n    deno run --allow-net --allow-write --allow-read --allow-plugin --allow-env --unstable server.ts server.ts\r\n\r\n\r\n> error: Found argument '--allow-plugin' which wasn't expected, or isn't\r\n> valid in this context. Did you mean --allow-run?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "goodmanworld", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-07T21:13:22Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T09:30:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12365", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12377, "title": "error: Uncaught (in promise) AssertionError", "body": "I am a new Deno learner, I tried a tutorial code that uses MongoDB but I got the following error:\r\n\r\n```\r\nerror: Uncaught (in promise) AssertionError\r\n    throw new AssertionError(msg);\r\n          ^\r\n    at assert (https://deno.land/std@0.107.0/testing/asserts.ts:224:11)\r\n    at MongoClient.database (https://deno.land/x/mongo@v0.27.0/src/client.ts:67:5)\r\n    at file:///C:/Users/m/Desktop/Uproject/GuidApp/deno-survey/mongo.ts:6:19\r\n```\r\n\r\nAs the project has many files, Let me know if is it needed to add some parts of project codes here?\r\nBut the whole project code can be find here: https://github.com/thecodeholic/deno-survey", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "goodmanworld", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-08T19:18:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-30T09:00:26Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12377", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12402, "title": "at async dispatch (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/middleware.ts:41:7)", "body": "I get this error message when I try to test the `/api/register` end point with Postman:\r\n\r\n```\r\nrequest: { url: \"http://0.0.0.0:8000/api/register\", method: \"POST\", hasBody: true }\r\nresponse: { status: 404, type: undefined, hasBody: false, writable: true }\r\n\r\n    at register (file:///C:/Users/m/app_back/controllers/auth_controller.ts:9:22)\r\n    at async dispatch (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/middleware.ts:41:7)\r\n    at async dispatch (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/middleware.ts:41:7)\r\n    at async dispatch (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/middleware.ts:41:7)\r\n    at async EventTarget.#handleRequest (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/application.ts:379:9)\r\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'name')\r\n    at register (file:///C:/Users/m/app_back/controllers/auth_controller.ts:9:22)\r\n    at async dispatch (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/middleware.ts:41:7)\r\n    at async dispatch (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/middleware.ts:41:7)\r\n    at async dispatch (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/middleware.ts:41:7)\r\n    at async EventTarget.#handleRequest (https://deno.land/x/oak@v9.0.1/application.ts:379:9)\r\n\r\n\r\n```This is my `auth_controller.ts` file:\r\n```\r\n```\r\n\r\nimport {\r\n  create, verify, decode, getNumericDate, RouterContext, hashSync, compareSync\r\n} from \"../deps.ts\";\r\nimport { userCollection } from \"../mongo.ts\";\r\nimport User from \"../models/user.ts\";\r\n\r\nexport class AuthController {\r\n  async register(ctx: RouterContext) {\r\n    const { value: { name, email, password } } = await ctx.request.body().value;\r\n\r\n    let user = await User.findOne({ email });\r\n    if (user) {\r\n      ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n      ctx.response.body = { message: \"Email is already used\" };\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n    const hashedPassword = hashSync(password);\r\n    user = new User({ name, email, password: hashedPassword });\r\n    await user.save();\r\n    ctx.response.status = 201;\r\n    ctx.response.body = {\r\n      id: user.id,\r\n      name: user.name,\r\n      email: user.email\r\n    };\r\n  }\r\n  async login(ctx: RouterContext) {\r\n    const { value: { email, password } } = await ctx.request.body().value;\r\n    if (!email || !password) {\r\n      ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n      ctx.response.body = { message: \"Please provide email and password\" };\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n    let user = await User.findOne({ email });\r\n    if (!user) {\r\n      ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n      ctx.response.body = { message: \"Incorrect email\" };\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n    if (!compareSync(password, user.password)) {\r\n      ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n      ctx.response.body = { message: \"Incorrect password\" };\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey(\r\n      { name: \"HMAC\", hash: \"SHA-512\" },\r\n      true,\r\n      [\"sign\", \"verify\"],\r\n    );\r\n\r\n    const jwt = create( { \r\n      alg: \"HS256\",\r\n      typ: \"JWT\",\r\n    }, {\r\n      iss: user.email,\r\n      exp: getNumericDate(\r\n        Date.now() + parseInt(Deno.env.get(\"JWT_EXP_DURATION\") || \"0\"))\r\n    },\r\n    key\r\n    );\r\n\r\n    ctx.response.body = {\r\n      id: user.id,\r\n      name: user.name,\r\n      email: user.email,\r\n      jwt,\r\n    };\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default new AuthController();\r\n\r\n```\r\nWhat is the problem and how can I resolve it?\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "goodmanworld", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-12T00:47:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-11T03:12:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12402", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12404, "title": "How to declare types when importing within a JSDoc type?", "body": "There is a way to declare types when importing:\r\n\r\n> ```js\r\n> // @deno-types=\"./coolLib.d.ts\"\r\n> import * as coolLib from \"./coolLib.js\";\r\n> ```\r\n> \u2014 https://deno.land/manual@v1.14.3/typescript/types#providing-types-when-importing\r\n\r\nBut how do you do the same when importing within a JSDoc type?\r\n\r\nFor example in `x.js`:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n/** @param {import(\"foo\").Foo} foo A foo. */\r\nfunction x(foo) {\r\n  // \u2026\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn the above situation, if `Foo` is a type that comes from a `.d.ts` file and isn't actually a named export in the ESM module, trying to import the `x.js` module in a TypeScript module with type checking enabled will cause a TS error: `TS2694 [ERROR]: Namespace '__' has no exported member 'Foo'.`.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jaydenseric", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-12T05:44:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T02:18:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12404", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12415, "title": "Why Deno prefers underscores to dash?", "body": "I used Node.js for several years. and used 'dashes (hyphens)' for the filenames.\r\n\r\nI started learning Deno yesterday. Then I found the style guide.\r\n\r\n> Use underscores, not dashes in filenames.\r\n> \r\n> - [Style guide | Manual | Deno](https://deno.land/manual@v1.14.3/contributing/style_guide#use-underscores-not-dashes-in-filenames)\r\n\r\nWhy Deno prefers underscores to dash? I want to know the reason, background story.\r\nIs there the problem with using dashes?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Neos21", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-13T00:03:26Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-27T17:44:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12415", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12442, "title": "error: Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: listener.accept is not a function", "body": "Hi, \r\n\r\nI'm trying to get started with a Deno Hello World and can't find any info online about the error I'm getting. (Note, I'm using proper permission flags).\r\n\r\nSteps:\r\n\r\n1) Installed Deno 1.15.1 on Mac OS\r\n2) Tried running this script which I've checked and rechecked:\r\n\r\nsimpleServer.ts\r\n```\r\nimport { serve } from \"https://deno.land/std/http/server.ts\";\r\nconst s = serve({ port: 8080 });\r\nconsole.log(\"http://localhost:8080/\");\r\nfor await (const req of s) {\r\n   req.respond({ body: \"Hello World\" });\r\n}\r\n```\r\nbut when I run\r\n```\r\ndeno run --allow-net simpleServer.ts\r\n```\r\nI get:\r\n```\r\nerror: Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: listener.accept is not a function\r\n        conn = await listener.accept();\r\n                              ^\r\n    at Server.#accept (https://deno.land/std@0.111.0/http/server.ts:417:31)\r\n    at Server.serve (https://deno.land/std@0.111.0/http/server.ts:181:32)\r\n    at serve (https://deno.land/std@0.111.0/http/server.ts:570:23)\r\n    at file:///Users/(omitted)/test/simpleServer.ts:2:11\r\n```\r\n\r\nAny help would be much appreciated.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "oaklandgit", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-14T04:20:21Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-10T14:44:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12442", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12460, "title": "WebAssembly and zero-copy buffers", "body": "I'm trying to find a way to pass zero-copy buffers from Rust to JS to WASM.\r\n\r\nFor now, according to [this issue in WebAssembly design](https://github.com/WebAssembly/design/issues/1162), we have to copy buffers from JS to WASM.\r\nWe can pass WASM pointers to JS, but they are valid until the WASM memory grows, so we can't rely on this.\r\n\r\nAny idea? Could Deno go beyond this design choice? Is this impossible?", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "hazae41", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-16T16:44:57Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-08T07:48:59Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12460", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12493, "title": "how to run commands through the user shell with deno?", "body": "I'm trying to build a  simple CLI tool to run dev scripts with Deno. The goal is to create cross-platform scripts with type safety.\r\n\r\nI'm not succeeding with Deno.run(). I think this is due to part of my scripts are based on global packages installed with NPM like, for example, the firebase CLI tool.\r\n\r\nIf i run something like this:\r\n```js\r\n  const { code } = await Deno.run({\r\n    cmd: [\"firebase\", \"emulators:start\"],\r\n  }).status();\r\n```\r\n\r\nI get this error\r\n```\r\nerror: Uncaught (in promise) NotFound: O sistema n\u00e3o pode encontrar o arquivo especificado. (os error 2)\r\n  const { code } = await Deno.run({\r\n```\r\n\r\nI found some clarification in this [issue](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/5921) about echo. But, I'm afraid if I prefix everything with `\"cmd\", \"/c\",` the program will tie itself to the OS. Is this a valid assumption?\r\n\r\nIs there another API that enables us to run commands through the current shell instead of attempt to find binaries in the system directly?\r\n\r\nI think in node this is done with something like this:\r\n``` js\r\n    const p = spawn(\"firebase emulators:start\", {\r\n      shell: true,\r\n    });\r\n```\r\n\r\nThanks for the awesome tool. \r\nJM", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "joaomelo", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-18T23:55:58Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T16:42:02Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12493", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12511, "title": "how to run commands through the user shell with deno?", "body": "### Discussed in https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12493\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI get you folks closed the original [issue](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/12487#event-5483127970) and moved it to discussions. But I think I have a legitimate situation here and the response to the original issue didn't address what was in question or even clarify a mistake from my part. \r\n\r\nI don't want to be rude or bully the project team, far from it. Just really trying to get myself out of a block. \r\n\r\nThis time I added a gif in attempt to better illustrate the case.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI'm trying to build a  simple CLI tool to run dev scripts with Deno. The goal is to create cross-platform scripts with type safety.\r\n\r\nI'm not succeeding with Deno.run(). I think this is due to part of my scripts are based on global packages installed with NPM like, for example, the [firebase CLI tool](https://www.npmjs.com/package/firebase-tools).\r\n\r\nIf i run something like this:\r\n```js\r\n  const { code } = await Deno.run({\r\n    cmd: [\"firebase\", \"emulators:start\"],\r\n  }).status();\r\n```\r\n\r\nI get this error\r\n```\r\nerror: Uncaught (in promise) NotFound: O sistema n\u00e3o pode encontrar o arquivo especificado. (os error 2)\r\n  const { code } = await Deno.run({\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere in live:\r\n![reproduce](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5727050/137894758-35e0f9fd-f688-4c93-8846-e655f63c9157.gif)\r\n\r\nI found some clarification in this [issue](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/5921) about echo. But, I'm afraid if I prefix everything with `\"cmd\", \"/c\",` the program will tie itself to the OS. **Is this a valid assumption?**\r\n\r\n**Is there another API that enables us to run commands through the current shell** instead of attempt to find binaries in the system directly?\r\n\r\nI think in node this is done with something like this:\r\n``` js\r\n    const p = spawn(\"firebase emulators:start\", {\r\n      shell: true,\r\n    });\r\n```\r\n\r\nThanks for the awesome tool. \r\nJM", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "joaomelo", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-21T01:00:21Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T16:41:35Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12511", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12638, "title": "Deno.run hangs when a child process returns more than 8200 characters and the stdout == piped.", "body": "`Deno.run` **hangs** when a child process returns **more than 8200 characters** and the **stdout == piped**.\r\n\r\nRepro source code is in repository: https://github.com/CarlosOnline/deno-bugs.git\r\n\r\n# Repro:\r\n- git clone `https://github.com/CarlosOnline/deno-bugs.git`\r\n- cd deno-bugs\r\n- `deno.exe run -A repro-run-hang.ts`\r\n\r\n**Deno will hang between 8100 and 8200 characters.**\r\n\r\n# Test code:\r\n```\r\n\"a\".repeat(parseInt(Deno.args[0]))\r\n```\r\n\r\n# Repro code:\r\n```\r\n/**\r\n * Execute Deno.run with file that generates specified length of characters.\r\n * @param length Length of output to generate.\r\n */\r\nasync function Run(length: number) {\r\n  console.log(`Running with length ${length}`);\r\n\r\n  const p = Deno.run({\r\n    cmd: [\r\n      \"deno\",\r\n      \"run\",\r\n      \"--allow-read\",\r\n      \".\\\\write-output.ts\",\r\n      length.toString(),\r\n    ],\r\n    stdout: \"piped\",\r\n    stderr: \"piped\",\r\n  });\r\n\r\n  const { code } = await p.status();\r\n  const rawOutput = await p.output();\r\n  console.log(`Exit code: ${code} length: ${rawOutput.length}`);\r\n}\r\n\r\n/**\r\n * Loop through different lengths until hangs or succeeds.\r\n */\r\nfor (let idx = 100; idx < 10000; idx += 100) {\r\n  await Run(idx);\r\n}\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"Success\");\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "CarlosOnline", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-25T00:24:15Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-12T16:38:22Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12638", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12637, "title": "How can Worker memory usage be managed ?", "body": "In this issue #6916 the ability was added to register a callback when an isolate is under memory pressure, so it can be terminated without bringing down the whole v8 instance.  How can we access this capability ?\r\n\r\nSpecifically we would like the means of running untrusted code within a Worker instance, taking advantage of the sandboxing provided by Deno, and the efficiencies in resource usage and latency afforded by v8 isolates.  Thank you for this great project.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "inverted-capital", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-26T01:20:45Z", "updated_at": "2024-10-13T05:40:26Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12637", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12575, "title": "Expose current import map in Deno as readonly", "body": "Related issue #12213 explains that we can't have a dynamic import map because of WICG standards\r\n\r\nBut can't we expose the current import map in Deno as readonly?\r\n\r\nThis would be a game changer for libraries that manage import maps:\r\n- https://deno.land/x/importmap@0.2.0\r\n- https://github.com/alephjs/aleph.js/issues/155\r\n\r\ne.g. `Deno.imports`\r\n```js\r\n{ \r\n  path: \"import_map.json\",\r\n  value: { \r\n    imports: { ... },\r\n    scopes: { ... }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "hazae41", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-28T13:23:58Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-12T23:56:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12575", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12580, "title": "What should be the endpoint address when I use Flutter + Deno + MongoDB?", "body": "I am using Flutter, VSCode and Android emulator in Windows 10. Also Deno as the backend server. I am trying to connect Flutter app to Deno app locally in my PC. But I don't know what address exactly I must use?\r\n\r\nThis is the address I am trying to send `get, post, etc.` requests from the Flutter app to:\r\n\r\n    http://10.0.2.2:8000/api/register\r\n\r\n\r\nAnd this is the address I am trying to receive those requests inside my deno app:\r\n\r\n    Listening on: http://0.0.0.0:8000\r\n\r\nAnd the endpoints such as `/api/register` work when I use postman to test them with the follwong address for example:\r\n\r\n    0.0.0.0:8000/api/register\r\n\r\nI don't know what is the problem and how can I fix it?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "goodmanworld", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-28T18:30:18Z", "updated_at": "2022-12-13T09:04:19Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12580", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12601, "title": "How to read request body from inside a Deno application?", "body": "I am trying to write a simple signup using Deno, and the following is my code:\r\n\r\n    async register(ctx: RouterContext) {\r\n        const { email, password } = await ctx.request.body().value;\r\n        let user = await User.findOne({ email });\r\n        if (user) {\r\n            ctx.response.status = 422;\r\n            ctx.response.body = { message: \"Email is already exist\" };\r\n            return;\r\n        }\r\n        const hashedPassword = hashSync(password);\r\n        user = new User({ email, password: hashedPassword });\r\n        await user.save();\r\n        ctx.response.status = 201;\r\n        ctx.response.body = {\r\n            id: user.id,\r\n            name: user.name,\r\n            email: user.email\r\n        };\r\n    }\r\n\r\nThe problem is that, what I get from the frontend side is like below:\r\n\r\n    {\r\n        email: \"<email>\",\r\n        password: \"<password>\"\r\n    }\r\n\r\nBut it seems my backend expects something like this:\r\n\r\n    {\r\n        value: {\r\n            email: \"<email>\",\r\n            password: \"<password>\"\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\nAnd when I try to remove that `.value` part from the end of the following line of the code and change it like below:\r\n\r\n    const { email, password } = await ctx.request.body();\r\n\r\nIt gives me error messages like:\r\n\r\n    Property 'email' does not exist on type 'Body'.\r\n    Property 'password' does not exist on type 'Body'.\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 10, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "goodmanworld", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-10-30T06:18:12Z", "updated_at": "2024-11-02T17:49:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12601", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12603, "title": "How to spy on declared functions with `sinon` without passing extra arguments", "body": "I have a function A that calls another function B:\r\n```typescript\r\nfunction A() {\r\n  B();\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunction B() {\r\n\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd I want to write a test that wraps function `B` using `sinon` and makes some assertions, such as if `B` has been called after invoking `A`:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nconst spy = sinon.spy(B);\r\n\r\n// do test, call A somewhere\r\n\r\nassertEquals(spy.called, true);\r\n```\r\nThe only way this works is if I add a parameter to `A` so I can replace `B` with the spy function for the test:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nfunction A(fn) {\r\n  fn();\r\n}\r\n\r\nA(B); // and A(spy) in the test\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut in reality `A` is more complex and I don't really want to add parameters purely for testing. In other JavaScript environments `B` can be accessed from a global such as `window`, like in the example from [this post](https://stackoverflow.com/a/29801191/12769288):\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nfunction bar(x,y) {\r\n  console.debug(x, y);\r\n}\r\nfunction foo(z) {\r\n  bar(z, z+1);\r\n}\r\n// Spy on the function \"bar\" of the global object.\r\nvar spy = sinon.spy(window, \"bar\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs there an equivalent of this in Deno? I've tried `window` and `self`, but those error saying `B` is undefined on those objects. Also in my project `B` is first imported from another file, and I've tried `import * as foo from \"my_file.ts\"` and then `sinon.spy(foo, 'B')`, but in that case `sinon` errors saying `ES modules cannot be spied`. Can I spy on `B` without having to rewrite `A` to take an extra parameter?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "GJZwiers", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-10-30T09:50:38Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T14:07:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12603", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12626, "title": "cpu limit per isolate worker", "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nI'm executing untrusted javascript inside deno, and i have trouble\r\nwith some scripts that take cpu time.\r\n\r\nIs there any way I can set an execution cpu time limit of some deno executed\r\nscript?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "amirhosseinvz94", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-11-02T12:27:10Z", "updated_at": "2023-12-23T13:13:44Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12626", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12635, "title": "Get JsRuntime execution output", "body": "Hello, I'd like to reopen the issue #11105 of getting something useful from the script evaluation of deno_code.\r\n\r\nSo, I'd like to use deno to interpret js at runtime and get the result, for instance:\r\n```rust\r\nlet res = runtime.execute_script(\"<main>\", \"123\");\r\nassert!(res.unwrap() == serde_json::Value::from(123))\r\n```\r\nBut I cannot understand how to transform a Global<Value> into something useful.\r\n\r\nIs there any way to achieve this?\r\nI know there has been discussion in #11129 about the return value not always being deserializable.\r\nCouldn't a enum be returned?\r\nSomething like this:\r\n```rust\r\nenum EvalResult {\r\n  Deserializable(?)\r\n  Other(Global<Value>)\r\n}\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "gufoe", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-11-02T23:24:44Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T13:42:49Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12635", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12647, "title": "Wait until all connections have closed", "body": "Hi!\r\n\r\nI have this Server class that I had to add these 3 properties to:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nexport class Server {\r\n  protected listener?: Deno.Listener;\r\n\r\n  protected numberOfConnections = 0;\r\n  protected closingPromise: Promise<void>;\r\n  protected closingPromiseResolver?: (value: void | PromiseLike<void>) => void;\r\n\r\n  // ...\r\n\r\n  public constructor(port: number = 4000) {\r\n    // ...\r\n    this.closingPromise = new Promise((resolve) => {\r\n      this.closingPromiseResolver = resolve;\r\n    });\r\n    // ...\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  private handleSocketOpen(_socket: WebSocket) {\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#handleSocketOpen`);\r\n\r\n    this.numberOfConnections++;\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  private handleSocketClose(_socket: WebSocket) {\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#handleSocketClose`);\r\n\r\n    this.numberOfConnections--;\r\n\r\n    if (this.closingPromiseResolver && this.numberOfConnections === 0) {\r\n      this.closingPromiseResolver();\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  private async handleConnections() {\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#handleConnections`);\r\n\r\n    if (!this.isRunning || !this.listener) {\r\n      throw new Error(\"not running\");\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    let event: Maybe<Deno.RequestEvent> = null;\r\n\r\n    while (this.isRunning) {\r\n      try {\r\n        const connection = Deno.serveHttp(await this.listener.accept());\r\n        event = await connection.nextRequest();\r\n      } catch {\r\n        break;\r\n      }\r\n\r\n      if (event === null) {\r\n        break;\r\n      }\r\n\r\n      const { socket, response } = Deno.upgradeWebSocket(event.request);\r\n\r\n      socket.onopen = () => this.handleSocketOpen(socket);\r\n      socket.onmessage = (e) => this.handleSocketMessage(socket, e.data);\r\n      socket.onclose = () => this.handleSocketClose(socket);\r\n      socket.onerror = (e) => this.handleSocketError(socket, e);\r\n\r\n      event.respondWith(response);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#handleConnections:done`);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  public async untilAllSocketsClosed() {\r\n    if (this.numberOfConnections === 0) {\r\n      return;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    await this.closingPromise;\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  public async start() {\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#start`);\r\n\r\n    this.listener = await Deno.listen({\r\n      port: this.port,\r\n    });\r\n\r\n    this.isRunning = true;\r\n    this.startedAt = new Date();\r\n\r\n    this.handleConnections();\r\n\r\n    this.closingPromise = new Promise((resolve) => {\r\n      this.closingPromiseResolver = resolve;\r\n    });\r\n\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#start:started`);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  public async stop() {\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#stop`);\r\n\r\n    if (!this.isRunning || !this.listener) {\r\n      throw new Error(\"not running\");\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    this.listener.close();\r\n    this.listener = undefined;\r\n\r\n    await this.untilAllSocketsClosed();\r\n    this.closingPromiseResolver = undefined;\r\n\r\n    this.isRunning = false;\r\n    this.startedAt = null;\r\n\r\n    logger.debug(`Server#stop:stop`);\r\n  }\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs there a better way to wait until all sockets are closed?\r\n\r\nI am running tests and each test creates a new server. If I close the server in one test with a sync op, the next test will try to spawn a new `Deno.listen()` on the same port and I will get an error.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Industrial", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-11-04T08:06:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T15:17:16Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12647", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12657, "title": "About the future", "body": "An idea has been echoed in my mind for many years, that is, to release a language with the same syntax, but with script (dynamic) version and compiled (static) version. Until I met JavaScript and typescript, I thought such a language was finally born. But I found that typescript still needs to be compiled into JavaScript, so that there is no real typescript runtime\r\n\r\nMaybe a new language will be born at some point in the future. It has two aspects: script as a glue language to bond the advantages of various languages (the speed of C / C + + and a wide variety of libraries of JavaScript), and static compilation as a non interpretative language with excellent performance (compiled into machine code or relying on a visual machine like JVM).\r\n\r\nI hope it will be deno.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jihaohaaaa", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-11-05T07:08:33Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:40:24Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12657", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12665, "title": "\"deno repl\" has permissions by default?", "body": "I just tried and it seems like `deno repl` can write to the local file system by default. I was actually expecting the REPL to be constrained by the same \"no permissions\" default as running a script.\r\n\r\nAm I the only one who expected the REPL to *not* have permissions by default?\r\n\r\nIn my case, I was testing out a random third party module (that seemed legit... but you never know) and thought that I could use the REPL to safely play with the module, prior to vetting the source code. I thought this would be one of the main uses of the REPL.\r\n\r\nI'm curious to hear what others think, thanks!", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "closed", "state_reason": "resolved", "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jaredkrinke", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-11-07T04:27:07Z", "updated_at": "2023-04-22T20:30:22Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12665", "classification": "random"}
