{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12689, "title": "Show friendly error messages and react into it", "body": "I and community members talked about this a little bit on the [Discord group](https://discord.gg/deno).\r\n[Link to those messages on Discord](https://discord.com/channels/684898665143206084/684898665151594506/906905408654950452)\r\n\r\n![1-min](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44144724/140660584-a01ba328-b23b-40c9-aa8a-19e079a0676c.png)\r\n![3-min](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44144724/140660589-8db20696-cf68-40e9-90c3-735b5b00fc9a.png)\r\n![4-min](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44144724/140660591-91cee721-eb9b-40cc-99fd-4712a6195e64.png)\r\n![5-min](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44144724/140660592-3cfd708e-1f28-44a8-bc13-fdd6507942ef.png)\r\n![6-min](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44144724/140660593-8eed8f03-0d2f-4f34-8009-14400281a609.png)\r\n![15-min](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44144724/140700523-16c69a23-26f4-4d59-81b4-d35a7be7de47.png)\r\n![17-min](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44144724/140660606-cc7a482f-0e0d-4df6-81f4-b54f22654554.png)", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 6, "reactions_plus": 2, "reactions_minus": 1, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 1, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 2, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "babakfp", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-11-08T07:27:11Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-03T07:26:19Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12689", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12791, "title": "question about typewscript compiler", "body": "I hope it is ok for me to ask this question here as I think stack overflow would just write it off. \r\n\r\nI am using rollup to build my project, and I have written my project in typescript. Now everything does work as intended, but is horridly slow.\r\n\r\nI hope to be able to speed up my 30-45 second typescript compile times by utilizing the rust port of said compiler.\r\n\r\nnow the actual question: is this possible?\r\nAm I even in the right place to begin with?\r\nAm I perhaps better of migrating the whole toolchain from node to deno, and by extension, is deno production ready?", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "chris-kruining", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-11-17T08:56:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-21T14:34:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12791", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12859, "title": "Best practice for including non-Deno TypeScript libraries", "body": "What's the smoothest workflow for including/vendoring non-Deno TypeScript libraries *that aren't tightly coupled to Node* (e.g. [jsonc-parser](https://www.npmjs.com/package/jsonc-parser))?\r\n\r\nGiven these libraries aren't tightly coupled to Node, I thought this would be easy, but after reading and re-reading several sections of the Deno manual and searching the web, I'm still not seeing a great solution. Hopefully I just missed something obvious :)\r\n\r\nThe biggest issues I'm encountering are:\r\n\r\n* Non-Deno TypeScript wants imports to not have \".ts\" extension (whereas Deno requires an extension)\r\n  * Obviously, this prevents everything from \"just working\"\r\n* Finding/creating declaration (\".d.ts\") files is surprisingly difficult\r\n\r\n# Approaches I've considered\r\n## 1. See if someone already ported the library to Deno and published it to deno.land/x\r\nI don't like this solution because it inserts a random, untrusted contributor into the workflow. Note: for the Microsoft library example above, I'm fine with assuming Microsoft isn't doing anything nefarious, so I may not even look at all the code before using the module in my project.\r\n\r\n## 2. Fork the library and add \".ts\" to all imports\r\nThis works, and the only new contributor is myself, but it is tedious and feels like something I shouldn't have to do. It also means I have to potentially redo some or all of this tedious work when I want to upgrade the module (especially if the module has been refactored).\r\n\r\nThis could be automated, but a robust solution would probably have to use the TypeScript compiler API, which I hoped I'd never have to deal with.\r\n\r\nEdit: one more annoyance with this approach: if the library I'm importing has 100 source files, now I have 100 imports. If someone's aware of a TypeScript bundler that works, that would be a huge help here.\r\n\r\n## 3. Use a Node-to-Deno CDN\r\nIn practice, this hasn't worked well for me. The biggest issue is that the type declarations are often missing or incorrect. I'm also a bit paranoid about trusting these CDNs, especially when they send me unreadable, minified JavaScript.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure if the \"missing declarations\" problem is because NPM packages usually don't include declaration files in their \"main\" package (and instead toss the types into a separate `@types/` package, for good reason). Unhelpfully, I can't get \"deno info\" to tell me if types were provided at all (and I can't find any \".d.ts\" files in my Deno cache).\r\n\r\n## 4. Use WebPack/Rollup/esbuild\r\nI don't have a lot of experience with these tools, but in every case, it looked like TypeScript *declaration file* support was bolted on with some random plugin that hasn't been updated in years.\r\n\r\nAs a particularly egregious example, WebPack's ts-loader plugin says to use [declaration-bundler-webpack-plugin](https://www.npmjs.com/package/declaration-bundler-webpack-plugin) for delcarations *which doesn't even provide a source code link on NPM*.\r\n\r\nEdit: maybe there's a Deno-focused/compatible bundler I missed?\r\n\r\n## 5. Create an import map to add \".ts\" extensions\r\nThis works, but import maps are an application-level concept, so I can't really use them for a library I plan to publish. At least this seems trivial to automate.\r\n\r\n## 6. Just run `tsc` and copy all the \".js\" and \".d.ts\" files it produces\r\nThis probably works... as long as the tsconfig.json produces output compatible with Deno. I'm hoping there's a better solution lurking somewhere.\r\n\r\nEdit: this \"works\", but the declaration files still don't use \".ts\" extensions in their imports...\r\n\r\n## 7. Use `deno bundle` with an import map\r\nUnfortunately, `deno bundle` [can't produce declaration files](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/3385)! Otherwise, this would probably be the easiest option to automate.\r\n\r\n## 8. Copy the published lib and its corresponding types package from NPM\r\nI'm hoping to remove NPM from my workflow, but this has worked for me in the recent past.\r\n\r\nEdit: if the declarations are split across multiple files, I believe this will suffer from the same \"no extensions\" problem as option 6.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jaredkrinke", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-11-22T17:14:34Z", "updated_at": "2023-10-05T23:50:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12859", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12903, "title": "Create file if it doesn't exist, fail otherwise", "body": "In my application, I need to create a file if it doesn't exist but if the file already exist this should trigger some special handling. This can be done synchronously: \r\n```\r\nwriteNewFile(filename, data) {\r\n  try {\r\n    Deno.statSync(filename);\r\n    // successful, file or directory must exist\r\n    throw Error(\"There's alredy such a file\");\r\n  } catch (error) {\r\n    if (error instanceof Deno.errors.NotFound) {\r\n      // file or directory does not exist\r\n      Deno.writeFileSync(filename, data);\r\n    } else {\r\n      // unexpected error, maybe permissions, pass it along\r\n      throw error;\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n```\r\nHowever, as my applications handles many requests asynchronously and writing such files is a frequent, writing the files synchronously isn't satisfactory.  Using the async `Deno.writeFile`-method would entail the risk that before the file has been written, a second invocation of the function doesn't yet find an existing file and also starts writing.\r\n\r\nIs there a way to asynchronously write a file only if it doesn't yet exist?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "retog", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-11-26T16:07:51Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-07T14:44:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12903", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12941, "title": "Impl of a subsystem flag in \"deno compile\" for background process in Windows", "body": "Hello fellow denodevs\r\n\r\nI was wondering if it was possible to run a compiled deno binary in background on windows, after some research and discussion on the official Deno Discord, I found that yes indeed it was.\r\n\r\nSo I made an implementation that works, only change the subsystem to \"Windows\" (because I don't think it would be useful to add more but we never know)\r\n\r\n\r\n(I am new to Rust, you can probably do a better implementation !) \r\n``` rust\r\nlet mut standalone_bin = final_bin;\r\n  #[cfg(windows)]\r\n  {\r\n    use byteorder::{ReadBytesExt, LittleEndian, WriteBytesExt};\r\n    // This is just for adding support for Windows background process into compiled binaries\r\n    // By default the Deno.exe binary is set to Console,\r\n    // while this implementation may be junky it works\r\n    if subsystem == \"windows\" {\r\n        let mut reader = std::io::Cursor::new(&mut standalone_bin);\r\n        // We Read until the PE Pointer\r\n        reader.seek(SeekFrom::Start(0x3C))?;\r\n        let pe_header_start = reader.read_u32::<LittleEndian>()?;\r\n        //We go 92 bytes further to reach the Subsysem field\r\n        let subsys_offset = pe_header_start + 92;\r\n        reader.seek(SeekFrom::Start(subsys_offset as u64))?;\r\n        let subsys = reader.read_u16::<LittleEndian>()?;\r\n        //can be tweaked to add more subsytems\r\n        if subsys != 0x2 {\r\n            reader.seek(SeekFrom::Start(subsys_offset as u64))?;\r\n            reader.write_u16::<LittleEndian>(0x2)?;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is added at the end of the `write_standalone_binary` function, which also now has the `subsystem` param, that contains the value set in the flag `deno compile -A -s windows app.ts`\r\n\r\nI made this so it can works with multiple options \ud83e\udd37\u200d\u2642\ufe0f but it is not really useful.\r\n\r\nThis implementation can be useful for app that uses Webview (best example i have), I have been trying to work with Wry from the Tauri project, in order to implement Deno bindings for a personal project, with this it can run on windows in background and can display a Browser (or any really) window without showing up a new CMD.\r\n\r\nI found the solution in this thread  https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/11638\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "hironichu", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-11-30T09:22:06Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T09:42:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12941", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12955, "title": "lock.json work failed when the project has one file on CDN", "body": "Recently, I promoted deno within the team, but I encountered such a problem when creating a template project. \r\n\r\nI hope `lock.json` can be used in my project to ensure the version of the referenced code, but there is a file from CDN. Even if we lock the version number, its hash value will change from time to time. \r\n\r\nFirst time it maybe:\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/bson@v4.5.3-mUXcu8gnafOqHTcdyS0d/dist=es2019,mode=imports/optimized/bson.js\": \"a3382aaffe1a6a7930a06602192a47c5c83fa3de3090b5d7ff9b6af85b36a50d\",\r\n  \"https://cdn.skypack.dev/bson@v4.5.3\": \"a1d77618b8912305def6956cf85f166e66e33de7ab65caeee8001a173d807809\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\nBut it second time maybe:\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"https://cdn.skypack.dev/-/bson@v4.5.3-qyhgJPJ3fRFU6pNRUV8E/dist=es2019,mode=imports/optimized/bson.js\": \"a3382aaffe1a6a7930a06602192a47c5c83fa3de3090b5d7ff9b6af85b36a50d\",\r\n  \"https://cdn.skypack.dev/bson@v4.5.3\": \"4c2fd52b1a47f88905aa92bdf4c7d82a2c55c1f6f652d29a17c849c3debed0ab\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\nI have to give it up now. How can I solve this problem? \r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jiawei397", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-12-01T09:15:41Z", "updated_at": "2022-08-21T11:13:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12955", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 12970, "title": "Deno as wasm component within the browser", "body": "The Stackblitz editor introduced this year in beta its \"[Web Container](https://blog.stackblitz.com/posts/introducing-webcontainers/)\" component, which is a closed source wasm component emulating node.js directly within the browser sandbox.\r\n\r\nIt is quite impressive and allows to run node.js applications such as servers directly within the browser, with numerous benefits in terms of speed and safety compared to a traditional local node.js application. It relies on `service worker` for all the node.js server parts.\r\n\r\nAs Deno is built in Rust which is a language that can be compiled into wasm and as it closely follows browser implementations, how feasible would it be to run Deno as a wasm component into the browser ? \r\n\r\n[Native sockets](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=909927&can=2&q=proj-fugu&sort=pri&colspec=ID%20Pri%20M%20Stars%20ReleaseBlock%20Component%20Status%20Owner%20Summary%20OS%20Modified&x=m&y=releaseblock&cells=ids) are on the roadmap in chromium too, so it seems that browsers begin to have all the building blocks of a secure and immediatly available \"WebOS\" for many types of applications.\r\n\r\nA Deno Web WASM Component would allow to have offline-first Deno applications directly into the browser with a nodejs compatibility layer too.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 11, "reactions_total": 55, "reactions_plus": 50, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 3, "reactions_rocket": 2, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "abflow", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-03T06:39:46Z", "updated_at": "2025-04-06T10:21:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/12970", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13004, "title": "How to elegantly store a list of scripts?", "body": "Using npm we can run `npm run <script>` to run a specific command from `package.json`, I'd like to do something similar with Deno.\r\nI know you can get a long way with `deno install`, but I'm looking for a way to include a list of commands with my repository. That way new team members can easily see what the available commands are and run them without having to guess the required permissions and flags.\r\nIs there a good way to do this? For now I have added a bunch of tasks to `.vscode/tasks.json`, but I was hoping for something more IDE agnostic.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jespertheend", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-12-06T20:06:17Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T22:18:10Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13004", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13015, "title": "Is it possible to complie deno code to rust code?", "body": "Is it possible to compile deno to pure rust code as it is built on top of rust?\r\n\r\nThe benefit is we could deploy the \"rust\" code on AWS Lambda without long cold start time.\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-07T12:17:51Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T22:16:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13015", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13070, "title": "Fetch: connection closed before message completed", "body": "My application executes many fetch requests. Even if all `await fetch` calls are in try/catch blocks execution terminates with the message:\r\n```\r\nerror: Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: error sending request for url (http://fuseki:3330/ds/update): connection closed before message completed\r\n    at async mainFetch (deno:ext/fetch/26_fetch.js:266:14)\r\nProcess exited with code 1\r\n```\r\nThe same message seems to appear also when serving HTTP: https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/cli/tests/unit/http_test.ts#L153, https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11595\r\n\r\nI've tried adding delays between fetches as well as setting keepalive to false. Any idea on how to avoid this error? \r\n\r\n**Update**\r\n\r\nTurns out I did miss catching the rejections of a promise. Catching the exception prevents the program from terminating.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "retog", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-13T17:04:42Z", "updated_at": "2022-09-01T13:33:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13070", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13073, "title": "Best practice for publishing libraries that consume the Deno standard library", "body": "I want to publish a \"library\" module (i.e. one that is intended to be consumed by other modules), and my module depends on functionality in Deno's standard library (deno.land/std).\r\n\r\n**What's the best way to consume deno.land/std *in a library*?**\r\n\r\n(I didn't see any information about this scenario in [Linking to external code](https://deno.land/manual@v1.16.4/linking_to_external_code#linking-to-third-party-code))\r\n\r\nConsiderations:\r\n\r\n* I'd like the library to work on as many Deno versions as possible\r\n* I don't want to have to update the module for each new Deno/std release (because there are *a lot* of releases)\r\n* Let's *ignore unstable APIs* for now (because they generally require explicit versioning)\r\n\r\nEdit to add: this situation for Deno's standard library is unique in that the std version is updated often, all the modules are lumped into a single library (with one version), and the std versions are mapped to specific Deno versions (I assume mainly for unstable APIs... hopefully not much else!).\r\n\r\nThe two options I see are:\r\n\r\n1. Import without a version (`https://deno.land/std/testing/asserts.ts`)\r\n2. Import with a version (`https://deno.land/std@0.117.0/testing/asserts.ts`)\r\n\r\nThe first option is attractive, but it seems fragile (especially when Deno 2.0 arrives). The second option is probably best, but I don't like that it duplicates code unless consumers use an import map to align std versions.\r\n\r\nSome notes follow:\r\n\r\n# Import without a version\r\nNote: I haven't found a module that does this.\r\n\r\nI see a number of benefits to this:\r\n\r\n* My library doesn't need to be updated as bugs are fixed in the standard library\r\n* Multiple libraries will end up using the same standard library code (instead of whatever version the library author happened to use)\r\n\r\nBut there are significant drawbacks:\r\n\r\n* **What happens when Deno hits 2.0?** Does my module break? Or is `std` renamed to `std2`? :)\r\n* My library could break if the standard library subtly changes some behavior I depended upon (intentionally or unintentionally)\r\n\r\n# Import with a version\r\nBenefits:\r\n\r\n* I know exactly what code is running (... until someone uses an import map)\r\n* No worries about Deno 2.0 :)\r\n\r\nDownsides:\r\n\r\n* If there's a bug in the version of the standard library I use, either I'll have to update the module or all consumers will have to use an import map (and of course an import map can introduce bugs if there are subtle behavior changes between std versions)\r\n* When consuming N different libraries, I'll be pulling in potentially N different copies of standard library modules (unless I use an import map)\r\n\r\nStill, this seems like the most reliable option. Consumers that want to align std versions need to use an import map (and hope nothing breaks!), e.g. the following maps `@0.117.0` and `@0.116.0` to `@0.113.0` (I just made up the version numbers):\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n    \"imports\": {\r\n        \"https://deno.land/std@0.117.0/\": \"https://deno.land/std@0.113.0/\",\r\n        \"https://deno.land/std@0.116.0/\": \"https://deno.land/std@0.113.0/\"\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jaredkrinke", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-13T17:33:04Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T22:23:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13073", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13091, "title": "Can I directly download the JSON schema for `deno.jsonc`?", "body": "It is not available on https://www.schemastore.org/json/ , is it available somewhere in the same format as https://json.schemastore.org/tsconfig.json ? \r\n\r\nThis would mean it could be used outside of VS Code, for example using the same JSON language server from VS Code via its own LSP in Neovim:\r\n```\r\n        schemas = {\r\n          {\r\n            fileMatch = { 'package.json' },\r\n            url = 'https://json.schemastore.org/package.json',\r\n          },\r\n        },\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "David-Else", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-15T11:15:08Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-13T06:16:13Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13091", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13133, "title": "Are worker threads green?", "body": "From what I understood after reading some of really [helpful articles by Mayank](https://choubey.gitbook.io/internals-of-deno/threading-model/tokio-threads), Deno internally uses green threads to carry out async tasks (which I assume are I/O tasks). \r\n\r\nBut it's not immediately clear if worker threads are internally green threads or internally backed by native threads. From my very limited understanding after reading the [spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/workers.html#workers) - it isn't explicitly specified _how_ worker threads should be implemented internally. Having ability to spawn green threads which communicate via message passing to me personally sounds like a [great idea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actor_model). ", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "shivam-tripathi", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-18T05:55:38Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T22:21:32Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13133", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13167, "title": "Adding conda package recipe for deno", "body": "Greetings, and thank you for your work on this very useful software. I've been working on adding a recipe for deno to conda-forge, which is a community effort to create conda packages.\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/conda-forge/staged-recipes/pull/17315\r\n\r\nIt is customary there to invite the maintainers of the upstream code to be \"maintainers\" of the recipe on conda-forge. There is no obligation to do any work as a maintainer, but being a maintainer gives you the power to merge PRs, including the automatic updates submitted by the conda-forge bot. If any of the deno maintainers would like to be added as maintainers to the recipe, please comment here or on https://github.com/conda-forge/staged-recipes/pull/17315.\r\n\r\nAfter that PR is merged, you can add yourself as a maintainer in a PR to the generated \"feedstock\" repository. When that is available, I'll post a link on this issue.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "msarahan", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-21T18:34:28Z", "updated_at": "2025-01-06T17:53:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13167", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13168, "title": "Add support of ping/pong frames to WebSocket class", "body": "Since the version 0.118.0 of `deno_std`, `ws` module was removed. The documentation says to use [`Deno.upgradeWebSocket()`](https://doc.deno.land/deno/stable/~/Deno.upgradeWebSocket) API instead. This function returns `{ response: Response, socket: WebSocket }` where [`WebSocket`](https://doc.deno.land/deno/stable/~/WebSocket) is a type that, as I understand, the authors of Deno aim to use both for client-side and server-side WebSockets. \r\n\r\nThe `WebSocket` class in Deno has been implemented in accordance with W3C WebSocket API, which is the standard meant primarily for browsers. It is unfortunate that the control over ping/pong frames [is not exposed](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-sockets.html#ping-and-pong-frames) through this API. Browsers do not provide to the scripts they run any means to deliberately send ping frames to the server, or to detect incoming pings. Thankfully, browsers, as well as all conforming implementations at least [automatically respond](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455#section-5.5.2) to pings when the other party sends them.\r\n\r\nIt is then crucial that the other party\u2014server\u2014has control over ping/pong frames. This control is necessary for latency instrumentation, maintaining of NAT mappings and timely detection of broken connections ([example from ws, a node.js library](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ws#how-to-detect-and-close-broken-connections)). Contrary to what some may believe, TCP just by itself does not provide these features. Broken TCP connections [can be present for a very long time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TCP_half-open#:~:text=According%20to%20RFC%20793%2C%20a,for%20unbounded%20periods%20of%20time.) without any notification to the other party!\r\n\r\nMy question is whether the authors of Deno consider it feasible to add methods to send ping/pong frames and events to act upon their receipt to the `WebSocket` class.\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 6, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "milaz", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2021-12-21T21:17:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T22:17:23Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13168", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13197, "title": "How to run Windows cmd ' start \"\" \"c:\\\\Users\" '", "body": "### one\r\n```typescript\r\nDeno.run({cmd: ['start \"\" \"C:\\\\Users\"']});\r\n```\r\n> error: Uncaught (in promise) NotFound\r\n\r\n### two\r\n```typescript\r\nDeno.run({cmd: [\"c:\\\\windows\\\\system32\\\\cmd.exe\", \"/c\", \"echo\", \"hi\"]});\r\nsetTimeout(()=>{}, 2000)\r\n```\r\n> log: hi\r\n\r\n### two\r\n```typescript\r\nDeno.run({cmd: [\"c:\\\\windows\\\\system32\\\\cmd.exe\", \"/c\", `start \"\" \"C:\\\\Users\"`]});\r\nsetTimeout(()=>{}, 2000)\r\n```\r\n> error\r\n\r\nnodejs can execute\r\n```\r\nrequire('child_process').exec('start \"\" \"c:\\\\Users\"');\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "yefu24324", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-24T12:47:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T22:16:14Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13197", "classification": "need-related"}
