{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13243, "title": "Why is deno eval allowed all permissions by default?", "body": "Why is `deno eval` allowed all permissions by default? How can I restrain it?\r\nI would like to eval ts code without any permissions.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "firstdorsal", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-30T16:12:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-28T17:26:41Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13243", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13249, "title": "How safe is the Deno sandbox? Can i execute arbitrary code in a subprocess?", "body": "I am creating a subprocess like this \r\n```ts\r\n// create subprocess\r\n    const evalPolicy = Deno.run({\r\n        cmd: [\"deno\", \"run\", \"./run.ts\", JSON.stringify(input)],\r\n        stdout: \"piped\",\r\n        stderr: \"piped\"\r\n    });\r\n```\r\nand let it execute the arbitrary code in the file run.ts\r\n\r\nI see that it has access to the global Deno object what sure could extract some parameters like\r\n```js\r\n...\r\nversion: { deno: \"1.17.0\", v8: \"9.7.106.15\", typescript: \"4.5.2\" },\r\n  build: {\r\n    target: \"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu\",\r\n    arch: \"x86_64\",\r\n    os: \"linux\",\r\n    vendor: \"unknown\",\r\n    env: \"gnu\"\r\n  }\r\n...\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs this safe? \r\nIs it possible to mitigate the use of the Deno object?\r\nIs there a better alternative to this?\r\nThe code will be sent via an HTTP endpoint. My concern is not RCE as it runs in docker anyway but more about the manipulation of consecutive requests.\r\n\r\nThank you and all the best \r\nPaul\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "firstdorsal", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2021-12-31T10:49:13Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-09T01:15:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13249", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13258, "title": "How can Deno.readDir be promisified?", "body": "In all my code, I don't use async functions. Now, to perform a directory search, I need to use async and Promise doesn't work.\r\nIn my view, returning a Promise would be more effective, and you can await the result if necessary. Is there any design reason to do this? Is there any alternative?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "papiando", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-01T22:02:27Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-21T17:53:12Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13258", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13298, "title": "`deno fmt --check` is too strict on md files", "body": "Markdown files are very handy so we can edit docs instantly via Github editor in seconds, without need to clone the repo and do editing stuff on local machine.\r\n\r\nbut current `deno fmt --check` behavior is too strict on markdown files. often I should do editing steps on my local machine to make sure there's no format mistakes. It's very time consuming.\r\n\r\n`dprint-ignore-file` is not a solution, because we need have this behavior as default behavior to affect the community.\r\n\r\nWhat do you think? my preferred solution is to just ignore .md files in `deno fmt --check`", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "erfanium", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-01-06T21:30:57Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-25T08:50:29Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13298", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13333, "title": "Support `power-assert` as official", "body": "In Node.js, [power-assert](https://github.com/power-assert-js/power-assert) is often used to display test results in an easy-to-read format.\r\n\r\n```\r\n  1) Array #indexOf() should return index when the value is present:\r\n     AssertionError: # path/to/test/mocha_node.js:10\r\n\r\n  assert(ary.indexOf(zero) === two)\r\n         |   |       |     |   |\r\n         |   |       |     |   2\r\n         |   -1      0     false\r\n         [1,2,3]\r\n\r\n  [number] two\r\n  => 2\r\n  [number] ary.indexOf(zero)\r\n  => -1\r\n```\r\n\r\nDeno also has [a third party implementation in userland](https://github.com/numb86/power-assert-deno), but it requires AST parsing and is not easy to use. I think it would be useful if Deno provides an official command options to convert the test results.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "petamoriken", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-01-11T01:06:02Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T12:51:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13333", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13373, "title": "remove window variable #13367", "body": "Why would the `window` variable be a candidate for removal? I seem to be missing some kind of feedback or discussion. @ry opened #13367 and I'm wondering what the driving thought is for the issue, and I didn't want to clutter the issue with pointless discussion.\r\n\r\nThanks!", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "aaronhuggins", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-14T16:02:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-22T22:11:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13373", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13386, "title": "best practice for private local common modules?", "body": "## Use Case\r\n\r\na **local private** common utils module that used by multiple independent other projects.\r\nCurrently, I think of two ways: 1. import localhost URL. 2. import files\r\n\r\n## import localhost URL\r\n1. start a local file-server at common-module's parent directory\r\n2. `import {...} from \"http://localhost:port/a-common-module/mod.ts\"`\r\n3. `deno run -r main.ts`\r\n\r\nNote:\r\n1. In this way, different projects use the **same** import URL.\r\n2. Because deno run will cache remote(including localhost) modules, to keep the common module update, you needs to use the --reload(-r) option to run in other projects.\r\n\r\n## import file directly\r\nFor example, `import {...} from \"../../some-path/all-local-modules/a-module\"`.\r\n\r\nBut local projects in different locations need to use **different** relative import path.\r\n\r\n## Question\r\nIs this the best practice for private local common modules?", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "hanguokai", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-16T05:44:58Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:38:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13386", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13407, "title": "Conn.write and concurent buffer mutation", "body": "Is it ok to mutate buffer after it was passed to conn.write without awaiting conn.write ?\r\nI made simple test and got no broken bytes in case of concurent buffer mutation.\r\n\r\n```js\r\n// expect dots (.) in stdout if concurent buffer mutation breaks conn.write behavior\r\n// otherwise expect all Os\r\n\r\n// public tcp echo server\r\nconst conn = await Deno.connect({ hostname: 'tcpbin.com', port: 4242 });\r\n\r\nawait Promise.all([\r\n  Deno.copy(conn, Deno.stdout),\r\n  write(),\r\n]);\r\n\r\n\r\nasync function write() {\r\n  const buf = new Uint8Array(81);\r\n  buf[80] = 10; // \\n\r\n  for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\r\n    buf.fill(79/*O*/, 0, 80);\r\n    await Promise.all([\r\n      // if buffer is not fully written than\r\n      // line will be different length than other,\r\n      // so we can see that and repeat test\r\n      conn.write(buf),\r\n\r\n      // mutate buffer concurently\r\n      buf.fill(46/*.*/, 0, 80),\r\n    ]);\r\n  }\r\n  await conn.closeWrite();\r\n}\r\n\r\n```", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "exedealer", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-18T06:05:25Z", "updated_at": "2023-01-10T01:59:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13407", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13470, "title": "How to make a node/deno compatible ts module - import statements", "body": "first of all: deno is great!\r\n\r\nI want to create a typescript codebase that works with deno but also compiles to js to be executed with nodejs\r\n\r\nin ts the import has to end in js else node wont know where to load the files from\r\n```ts\r\nimport { vaultLoginUserpass, getVaultValue } from \"./vault/vault.js\";\r\n```\r\nbut in deno the files wont load from js because the path is (of course) plain wrong.\r\nI know this is typescripts incompetence but is there a nice way in deno to fix this?\r\ni tried: https://deno.land/manual@v1.1.0/getting_started/typescript#compiler-hint\r\nbut this is neither very comfortable nor seems to work\r\n\r\nthank you\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "firstdorsal", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-22T14:50:37Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-17T21:07:54Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13470", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13487, "title": "How does multipart processing work on the server when using the Fetch API?", "body": "I'm writing a web server in Deno, and I'm at the stage where I need to do multipart processing.\r\n\r\nPreviously when I did this in Node, I needed to specify and handle configuration settings like where file uploads go and how much memory can be used during multipart processing. However, based on the information I can gather from the docs, there's no need to specify these settings for multipart thanks to `await req.formData()`, which takes care of the low-level stuff for you.\r\n\r\nBut now I'm curious about the specifics of multipart processing when using `await req.formData()` on the server. (i.e. How much memory does this use? Where do files go? Do they stay there or should I delete/move them?) My guess is that I don't need to worry about those things because Deno usually uses best practices by default, but I'm having a hard time checking that hunch.\r\n\r\nI'd love some pointers on this if possible. I've searched for a bit but came up empty handed, and I felt a discussion question might be helpful for others as well.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ghost", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-25T05:21:18Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T15:18:07Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13487", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13505, "title": "fetch throttling", "body": "Is there a way to throttle fetch in deno?\r\nwonder how is it solved in [deno deploy](https://deno.com/deploy)", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "alexanderGalushka", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-27T00:39:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-07T12:09:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13505", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13523, "title": "Let's discuss React in Deno", "body": "I decided to start this discussion because I didn't find any official recommendations.\r\n\r\n1) For example, how to switch dev and production builds?\r\n\r\n2) How to write a React component library in Deno?\r\n\r\n3) Without peerDependencies, how to ensure only one instance of React in an app? \r\n\r\nAs for 3), one idea I have is to pass the whole React object to component factories. \r\nSuch a component library would import only React namespace type. What do you think?\r\n\r\nAs I see it, the only alternative to peerDependendies is \"types as a contract\". ", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "steida", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-01-28T17:15:32Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-12T01:19:50Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13523", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13559, "title": "Feature request: Support global files require", "body": "**Example use case:**\r\nI have to write tests for a deno app. I don't want to use the Deno API directly. Instead I want to use a custom Mocha pollyfill, which itself uses the Deno API. The file declares `globalThis.it = ...`, `globalThis.describe = ....` etc.\r\n\r\nI want to be able to tell Deno to require this file and to provide the global variables/functions to every test file I have, so that I don't manually write import statements in all of my files.\r\n\r\n**How to support it?**\r\nWhat I envision as a support is to use the `files` key of the `tsconfig` which is supplied to Deno or some special Deno only key.\r\n\r\n**Motivation**\r\nThis could save a lot of manual work in certain use cases.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 7, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "nbabanov", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-02-01T08:56:50Z", "updated_at": "2023-02-06T03:26:03Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13559", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13604, "title": "`baseUrl` and `paths` in tsconfig.json compatibility", "body": "Hi, this issue comes for the reason of a limitation which I found when creating my projects in deno. I usually like to create a folder where I incorporate all the routes to third party modules and from there I export everything to include it from those files\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nproject\r\n\u251c\u2500deno_deps\r\n\u2502  \u251c\u2500djwt.ts\r\n\u2502  \u251c\u2500mongo.ts\r\n\u2502  \u2514\u2500oak.ts\r\n\u251c\u2500src\r\n\u2502  \u251c\u2500app.ts\r\n...  ...\r\n```\r\nej: oak.ts\r\n```typescript\r\nexport { oakCors } from \"https://deno.land/x/cors@v1.2.1/mod.ts\";\r\nexport { Application, Router } from \"https://deno.land/x/oak@v6.5.0/mod.ts\";\r\nexport { getQuery } from \"https://deno.land/x/oak@v6.5.0/helpers.ts\";\r\nexport type { RouterContext, Context, RouteParams } from \"https://deno.land/x/oak@v6.5.0/mod.ts\";\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd I think that I am not the only one who does this, and that in a certain way it is a good practice to be able to visualize in the same point all the modules on which the project depends\r\n\r\nHowever, this causes that, if I want to import what I have in the folder, there are relative paths in the project which will mess up readability a bit\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\nimport { RouterContext } from \"../../../_dependencies/oak.ts\";\r\nimport { Query } from \"../../../database/queries/mongo.queries.ts\";\r\nimport { UserCollection } from \"../models/user.model.ts\"\r\nimport { JwtHelper } from \"../../helpers/jwt.helper.ts\";\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor this reason, I consider that if it does include the possibility of establishing the `baseUrl`, and even the paths, in the tsconfig, it would greatly improve this aspect. Something that the typescript cli already brings with it.\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\n\"baseUrl\": \"src\", \r\n...\r\n\"paths\": {\r\n    \"@deps/*\": [\"deno_deps/*\"],\r\n    ...\r\n},\r\n```\r\n", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dkaerit", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-02-05T18:52:41Z", "updated_at": "2025-02-25T09:53:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13604", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13607, "title": "How to run a script on deno deploy with --unstable and --no-check flags?", "body": "Hi Deno team,\r\n\r\nthe title sums it up. I would like to run a script on deno deploy but the build/deployments fails. Locally it only runs with --unstable and --no-check flags. Is there a possibility to run script on deno deploy with these flags.\r\n\r\nKind regards,\r\n\r\nLeo", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "l3onh4rd", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-02-06T14:01:28Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T13:11:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13607", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13652, "title": "proper way to handle dynamic properties from JS code", "body": "```js\r\n// test.js\r\nconst x = {x: 2};\r\nx.y = 3;\r\nexport { x };\r\n\r\n// app.ts\r\nimport * as x from './test.js';\r\nconsole.log(x.x.x);\r\nconsole.log(x.x.y);\r\n```\r\n\r\nDeno fails with\r\n\r\n```\r\nerror: TS2339 [ERROR]: Property 'y' does not exist on type '{ x: number; }'.\r\nconsole.log(x.x.y);\r\n                ^\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn cases like this, where the object is modified, is there a way to hint from the JS code (no TS definition file) that the variable should be typed as `any` or `object`?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "SheetJSDev", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-02-12T07:37:13Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-07T16:40:34Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13652", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13707, "title": "Suggestion: Show file system intellisense for custom functions if tagged", "body": "I have this function named `importAll` which can recursively import files from directories and their subdirectories.\r\n\r\nI have this proposal where my custom function will show the relative file system using intellisense such as import statements and `import()`.\r\n\r\nI think this will also be useful when importing it from another file. For example, we have two files named `main.ts` and `import_all.ts` where `import_all.ts` is located at `/sub1/sub2/import_all.ts`. When `main.ts` imports `import_all.ts`, the function it exports `importAll` would still show relative location of where `import_all.ts`, not `main.ts`'s relative location.\r\n\r\nAnother idea adding to the one above: functions could identify their relative location within the file system. For example, if the file reads from CWD instead, we would know that that is the specified location.\r\n\r\nMy idea of how it could be implemented is maybe some kind of JSDOc comment that code editors could read easily, but maybe there could be a more intuitive way.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "apacheli", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-02-18T00:42:36Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-30T22:15:19Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13707", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13717, "title": "Unable to set global \"document\" available to imported ts", "body": "I am setting `globalThis.document` in my `main.ts` and expected this to be available to imported modules, but get an error when I execute:\r\n\r\n```\r\ndeno run -c tsconfig.json main.ts\r\n\r\nerror: Uncaught ReferenceError: document is not defined\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs this behaviour correct? Is there another way to inject globals, to implement SSR for example via a simulated DOM?\r\n\r\ntsconfig.json:\r\n```\r\n{\r\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\r\n    \"target\": \"ES2015\",\r\n    \"lib\": [\r\n      \"dom\",\r\n      \"esnext\"\r\n    ]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nmain.ts:\r\n```\r\nglobalThis.document = {} as any;\r\nwindow.document = {} as any;\r\n\r\nimport {\r\n  attr,\r\n  customElement,\r\n  FASTElement,\r\n  html,\r\n  Observable,\r\n} from \"https://cdn.skypack.dev/@microsoft/fast-element?dts\";\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "cryptographix", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-02-19T10:41:53Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T02:15:05Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13717", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13722, "title": "disable Deno object in javascript", "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nI'm executing untrusted javascript (users script) inside deno, and i need to disable users to use Deno functionality like Deno.core or Deno.core.opAsync or anything related to Deno, how to do it?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "amirhosseinvz94", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-02-19T16:08:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-06T00:50:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13722", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13802, "title": "Ignoring template strings when formatting on save", "body": "I use deno to format my javascript on save. Sadly my template strings are put on multiple lines or in one line according to the formatting rules but it sometimes goes against my desired output (since the format of template strings is translated in the output string).\r\n\r\nFormatted by deno:\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/64891486/156148068-c1cd9ea2-7d46-4d53-9033-735b6e290489.png)\r\n\r\nIs there a way for me to exclude template strings from being processed by deno ? Maybe everything between backticks ?\r\n\r\nDesired:\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/64891486/156147820-085e27cc-50f5-4bc3-a2e2-1ab292550953.png)\r\n\r\nI'm using deno with null-ls in neovim. I can provide you with any additional info you might need.\r\n\r\nThanks", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "zakissimo", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-01T10:03:49Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-10T16:23:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13802", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13806, "title": "deno vendor should check compressed archive into git by default", "body": "so I have been mulling over the new `deno vendor` command. It seems like a very useful tool for safety, and robustness. However, I suspect I will find two things about it annoying:\r\n1. editor fuzzy file searches will include the `./vendor` folder by default\r\n2. updating dependencies will explode git history\r\n\r\n1 is solveable locally, but becomes annoying for contributors. The best you can do is find something common editors read from like an `.editorconfig` and add notes to the readme.\r\n2 is another issue, which will make PRs harder to sift through and increase the size of repo significantly over time.\r\n\r\nSo, I had a thought, what if `deno vendor` did two things. The first is download all files into a `./vendor` folder like it already does, but the second is to create an archive `./vendor.tar.gz` (or `./vendor.zip`) which is simply the vendor file compressed. The idea is, that the uncompressed vendor folder will be gitignored and `vendor.tar.gz` will be checked into git. Updating deps should change both the uncompressed vendor folder and the compressed vendor.tar.gz file. Git changes to those dependencies will still be reflected in version numbers where deps are used, so its not like we need the code diffs on deps.\r\n\r\n\r\nThis is something that can be built outside of deno now pretty simply, just with a script like so:\r\n```bash\r\n#! /bin/bash\r\n\r\ntar xzvf ./vendor.tar.gz\r\n```\r\n\r\nthe value in including this workflow in deno though, would be that deno could include some magic in `deno run` that detects if the uncompressed vendor folder and the compressed vendor.tar.gz file have become out of sync (like when a `git pull` is done), and then uncompress vendor.tar.gz before executing the program.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 1, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "andykais", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-01T19:08:42Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T16:08:42Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13806", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13858, "title": "Deno.error", "body": "Hi,\r\n\r\nI'm executing users script inside deno, And I do not want the user to know that I am using Deno to do this(jvascript runtime), for example why I need handle errors that come back to user. The user Should not understand that this is a Deno error. Do you have a solution?", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "amirhosseinvz94", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-07T08:58:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-10-06T11:52:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13858", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14053, "title": "Is there any plan to add support on mongoose library for Deno?", "body": "I like Deno and I am working on a project using it. But it seems it's MongoDB driver doesn't support Mongoose library and I don't know how can I use schemas in my Deno application?\r\n\r\nMay I ask either if there is any other alternatives for Mongoose in Deno or a plan to support Mongoose or schemas in Deno soon?", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 5, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "learner00000", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-15T00:32:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-12T06:18:11Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14053", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 13983, "title": "sessionStorage in worker module", "body": "Hi\r\n\r\nI was trying to use `sessionStorage` in worker module, and vscode didn't give me any error\r\n\r\nbut when I try to run main module (`deno run`), it typecheks the worker module and exists complaining: \r\n```typescript\r\nerror: Uncaught (in worker \"\") TS2339 [ERROR]: Property 'sessionStorage' does not exist on type 'WorkerGlobalScope & typeof globalThis'.\r\n  self.sessionStorage.setItem(\"threadId\", e.data.threadId.toString());\r\n       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n    at file:///Users/mohammad/....some-path...../worker.ts:46:8\r\n```\r\n\r\nworker module options are:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\n  const worker = new Worker(new URL(\"./lib/worker.ts\", import.meta.url).href, {\r\n    type: \"module\",\r\n    deno: {\r\n      namespace: true,\r\n      permissions: {\r\n        net: \"inherit\",\r\n        read: true,\r\n        write: true,\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  });\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\nI tried adding `read: true` and `write: true` to worker permissions, and changing `sessionStorage` to `localStorage` to see if anything changes, but none of these helped\r\ndeno version is:\r\n```bash\r\n:; deno --version\r\ndeno 1.18.2 (release, aarch64-apple-darwin)\r\nv8 9.8.177.6\r\ntypescript 4.5.2\r\n```\r\nany thoughts on this?\r\nthanks", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "mmohammadi9812", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-16T11:57:34Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-30T00:27:40Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/13983", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14008, "title": "How to Deno.listen on existing file descriptor?", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nI apologize if this has already been answered, I looked everywhere I could - I promise!\r\n\r\nHow does one use Deno.listen() to listen on an existing file descriptor / resource?\r\n**OR**\r\nPlease allow Deno.listen() to listen on existing file descriptors / resources.\r\n\r\nSimilarly Deno.read(), Deno.write(), etc. allows one to read/write/etc. from a given resource id (which is obviously an essential function as files are a widely adopted system concept). Using the Deno documentation I was able to successfully utilize Deno.listen() to simultaneously create a socket() resource and to bind() to it, which is neat, but in some particular instances one would prefer not to (or can not) do that first step as there already exists a socket (that can still be bound to). \r\n\r\nFor example, the popular software [spawn-fcgi](https://linux.die.net/man/1/spawn-fcgi) creates a socket, executes a specified process, and assigns the socket to fd 0. Systemd is also a now popular software that creates a socket for your process (but I think it assigns it to fd 3?).\r\n\r\nIf this feature isn't yet available, may I suggest this syntax: `Deno.listen(0)` where 0 represents Deno's first resource. This makes it similar to Deno's read(), write(), etc functions and also virtually every other popular definition of listen() (see both [posix](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/functions/listen.html) and win32). I'd greatly prefer that syntax, but if that isn't acceptable then the next obvious form would be `Deno.listen({rid: 0})`. Additionally, in my opinion `Deno.listen({transport: \"rid\", rid: 0})` or similar is **not** preferable, but I also realize that it may be required if Deno needs to know whether the socket is a datagram socket or not.\r\n\r\nAlso if this feature isn't yet available, could someone direct me towards some literature that illustrates how to utilize either op calls or Deno's newer FFI API to just use the system's listen() functionality in an async manner? With the instance I have in mind FFI and \"unstable\" is fine and listen() is available, but Deno.run() is not (nor is any foreign subprocessing functions). \r\n\r\nThank you!\r\n\r\nUpdate: I just discovered [op_net_accept](https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/main/ext/net/ops.rs#L162) which I'll attempt to experiment with, but some usage hints would still be appreciated. ", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 1, "author": "Higgs1", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-17T04:24:38Z", "updated_at": "2025-02-23T10:05:17Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14008", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14010, "title": "Hosting and maintaining a deno web server in production", "body": "This question is about the surrounding environment and tools that is needed to host and maintain a stable deno web server in production.\r\n\r\nI have a web server written in JS that I run with `deno run --allow-net webserver.js` inside a `screen` session on Ubuntu. Web server is exposed at `localhost:8000`. I then have `apache2` acting as a proxy, mapping incoming connections from the outside via a domain name to the web server. This is basically what every guide I found says (perhaps minus `screen` and more in favour of `nginx`). It works.\r\n\r\nProblem is deno crashes every now and then because of bugs in my code and with the current setup I receive no alert and I would like deno to restart the web server after a crash. And it isn't reboot friendly either.\r\n\r\nI can think of multiple ways of solving the problems, like `systemd` and `syslog`, but I feel like I'm missing important pieces of the puzzle that already have been solved many times over.\r\n\r\nHow does one host, manage and monitor these type of applications?\r\n\r\nUseful tips and pointers in the right direction is greatly appreciated.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "domo84", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-17T10:36:25Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-08T03:45:37Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14010", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14025, "title": "Speeding up subprocess/deno.run", "body": "I'm running a webserver with Deno which executes third party code. To isolate that code, I'm using Deno.run to create a new Deno instance to sandbox it, but also to set a memory limit on that code via V8 flags. \r\n\r\nAt the moment, Deno is taking about 40-50ms to start a new Deno instance via Deno.run, for it to `console.log('Hello World')` and `self.close()`. Is there a way of speeding this up at all? Perhaps passing in a V8 flag or an alternative to Deno.run?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "joelkennedy", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-18T13:47:33Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T16:40:55Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14025", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14041, "title": "How can I use Deno.run() and get output in \"tty\" mode ? (like progress bar)", "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nThanks for your help ! \r\n\r\nI would like to use Deno.run() and execute a command on the system.\r\nThis command generally return a progress bar when it's executed directly in the terminal (by using tty ?).\r\nBut when I read the output generated by Deno.run() line by line, I don't receive update for progress bar !\r\n\r\nDo you know if it's possible to have the \"dynamic\" output or if it's a feature not implemented ?\r\n\r\nThanks again !", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "FabienZa", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-19T13:45:42Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T04:17:25Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14041", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14036, "title": "`deno task` feedback", "body": "I've been using `deno task` for a bit now and [the blog post](https://deno.com/blog/v1.20#:~:text=We%20would%20appreciate%20your%20feedback%20to%20help%20shape%20this%20feature.) was asking for feedback. I'm running into some minor usability issues so I figured I'd post them here.\r\n\r\n### Introduction\r\n\r\nFirst let me describe how I was running scripts before `deno task` was added. Basically all scripts have a shebang at the top of the file similar to what many of the scripts in the Deno project use:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/<HASH_REDACTED>/tools/format.js#L1-L2\r\n\r\nI like this method because it keeps the required permissions close to the actual file, and running the script is extremely simple because all you have to do is run `./tools/format.js`.\r\n\r\nBut the downside is... it doesn't work very well on Windows. Maybe I'm missing a simple solution for this, but as far as I can tell `/usr/bin/env` is not really a thing on Windows, and shebang support isn't great either. So Windows users are stuck with having to run scripts using `deno run` while trying to figure out what the required permissions are (or running with `-A`).\r\nThis wasn't a huge problem for me though. I'm mostly developing on macOS, so I was using shebangs for now, though I did want to have better cross platform support eventually. \r\n\r\n### `deno task` to the rescue\r\n\r\n`deno task` makes it possible to easily run scripts cross platform, but the benefits from the shebang method are now gone:\r\n\r\n- Permissions are no longer in the same file as the main entry point.\r\n- `deno task build -- --dev` is more cumbersome to write than `./build.js --dev`\r\n- I reckon this issue is a bit more niche, but I feel the need to mention it anyway: Autocomplete for files no longer works when using `deno task`. I guess this depends on your shell as well, but before I used to do `./test.js path/to/file.test.js`, where the last part of the command would get autocompleted by my shell. This is not the case with `deno task test -- path/to/file.test.js`. I suppose this is also caused by `deno completions` being installed.\r\n\r\n### Potential solutions\r\n\r\nI'm currently using both methods, so every script has both a task configured as well as a shebang. That way Windows users can use `deno task build` and unix users can keep using `./build.js`. But now there's two places where arguments need to be updated. If a developer on a unix system makes a change that requires different permissions they might only update the shebang, causing the `deno task` to break. And vice versa for developers using Windows.\r\n\r\nPerhaps something like https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/12763 would make this easier. Though this won't take care of other arguments such as `--unstable`. And the location of the permissions would still be separate from the main entry point.\r\n\r\nThough I think what I would prefer the most is if `deno task` were able to parse `/usr/bin/env` shebangs somehow. This way the file itself could use a `#!/usr/bin/env` shebang, and the configured task would only need to point to the file. Maybe a task could even look like\r\n\r\n```\r\n\"tasks\": {\r\n    \"build\": \"./build.js\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis already works on unix, on Windows it gives an error. This way, unix users could keep running files like `./build.js`, and systems that don't support running files like this could use the tasks.\r\nI'm not sure how far you'd want to go with making the tasks run cross platform. Since not only would this have to be able to handle running files via `./` syntax, it would also have to parse shebangs and implement `/usr/bin/env` somehow. Sounds like this is very much out of scope for Deno, but it would solve all issues mentioned above.\r\n\r\n### Conclusion\r\n\r\nAnyway, those are my ramblings. Not sure how big of an issue this really is. I could certainly live with the current situation, I just feel like DX could be better here. Maybe someone knows a neat trick that allows you to run files on Windows in a similar manner. That would eliminate the need for tasks like these altogether.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 9, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jespertheend", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-03-19T14:43:23Z", "updated_at": "2024-09-21T20:31:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14036", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14055, "title": "How to run a Linux Command in the Background with Deno.run", "body": "I want to try run `deno run -A deno.ts &` using `Deno.run`\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nconst cmd = [\"deno\", \"run\", \"-A\", \"deno.ts\", \"&\"];\r\n\r\nconst p = Deno.run({ cmd });\r\n\r\nawait p.status();\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut it doesn't work.\r\n\r\nIs there anything missing?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "ynwd", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-19T23:30:41Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-27T07:50:30Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14055", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14048, "title": "Compiled code shows secrets in the clear", "body": "Is there a way to encode all the JavaScript files prior to or during deno compile?  I did not see an option.\r\n\r\nBackground:\r\nIn the Javascript code, I include some secrets (like API KEYs) in the clear, with hope that the codes would be hidden in the exe file.  However, after compiling for Windows, when looking at the executable with a text editor, towards the end, the javascript is in the clear and is visible, as well as my secrets.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 9, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "dinogregorich", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-20T16:18:56Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-12T22:28:36Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14048", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14058, "title": "Automatic Body Compression Clarification", "body": "It should be made clear in the manual how Deno internally handles automatic body compression:\r\n\r\n1. Does Deno internally maintain a cache of compressed bodies?\r\n2. If so, how long do those cache entries last?\r\n\r\nIf Deno does not maintain a cache, it could be inefficient for servers with high traffic to compress bodies upon every request, especially during a denial-of-service attack.\r\n\r\nIf Deno does maintain a cache, it is not specified how long cache entries last, and long-lived servers may end up keeping cache entries that are no longer needed.\r\n\r\nA potential solution would be to specify that Deno does indeed utilize a cache, and to provide a Deno API to inspect/remove entries from such a cache.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 4, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "cinolt", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-21T05:51:11Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-31T16:08:39Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14058", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14084, "title": "`deno task` descriptions", "body": "I think it could be nice to have a way to describe what tasks do. I realise it is possible to add comments above the tasks when, but I'm specifically looking for a way to show the descriptions when running `deno task` (without specifying a task).\r\n\r\nThe commands from tasks are often not very readable, so if a user is new to a repository it can be hard to find the task you're looking for. And having to open `deno.json` just to see the comments is not ideal.\r\n\r\nI'm thinking maybe the comments themselves can be used for printing descriptions. But I reckon this might make things a lot more difficult to parse. So perhaps something like this is more appropriate:\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n    \"tasks\": {\r\n        \"test\": \"deno test --no-check -A\",\r\n        \"check\": {\r\n            \"description\": \"Performs several checks that are needed before committing.\",\r\n            \"command\": \"deno test & deno fmt & deno lint\"\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nBy the way, I would personally be fine with completely hiding the command for tasks that have a description. It would remove a lot of visual clutter. It might not be the best security wise, but generally speaking if I'm running tasks from a project, I already trust the project enough to not worry about any malicious commands.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 7, "reactions_plus": 7, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jespertheend", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-03-22T22:15:24Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T15:56:33Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14084", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14143, "title": "How to set console log level?", "body": "I assumed that setting the log-level to info would prevent messages passed to `console.debug` from appearing, however this doesn't seem to be the case:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ echo 'console.debug(\"hi\") ' | deno -L info run -\r\nhi\r\n```\r\nIs there away to have only messages passed to `console.info` but not those passed to `console.debug`?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 8, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "retog", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-28T20:05:18Z", "updated_at": "2023-11-15T03:01:22Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14143", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14147, "title": "Default Deno.bench warmups may not be high enough for some CI runners", "body": "Recently I have been benchmarking with `Deno.bench` and having fun, although it seems that on some CI runners the default 1000 warmups is not sufficient. On the Ubuntu runner in Github Actions in particular there is a major difference in the measured time of whatever benchmark is run first, compared to other runners and my local machine.\r\n\r\nFor example, I am benching a JS and WASM implementation of a string distance algorithm. The results are very consistent on my local setup and the MacOS/Windows runners: JS is faster on relatively short strings while WASM is faster on long ones:\r\n\r\n```\r\nbench Myers 32bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 1,035 ns/iter (700..59,100 ns/iter) ok (17ms)\r\nbench Myers 32bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 2,726 ns/iter (2,400..8,900 ns/iter) ok (12ms)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 425,071 ns/iter (401,300..565,900 ns/iter) ok (884ms)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 80,577 ns/iter (78,500..122,900 ns/iter) ok (166ms)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn the Ubuntu runner though, there is a time anomaly in the first bench, showing a much higher average `ns/iter`:\r\n\r\n```\r\nbench Myers 32bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 10,783 ns/iter (9,300..88,499 ns/iter) ok (35ms)\r\nbench Myers 32bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 6,350 ns/iter (3,400..60,500 ns/iter) ok (24ms)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 673,224 ns/iter (556,596..3,331,975 ns/iter) ok (1s)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 152,748 ns/iter (118,299..4,364,268 ns/iter) ok (294ms)\r\n```\r\n\r\nI can actually shift the extra time in the CI to a different benchmark by having it run first:\r\n\r\n```\r\nbench test bench JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 9,218 ns/iter (1,400..96,701 ns/iter) ok (59ms)\r\nbench Myers 32bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 1,322 ns/iter (1,000..5,600 ns/iter) ok (16ms)\r\nbench Myers 32bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 4,417 ns/iter (4,300..22,500 ns/iter) ok (33ms)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 750,559 ns/iter (736,909..3,943,152 ns/iter) ok (1s)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 141,181 ns/iter (140,301..225,303 ns/iter) ok (290ms)\r\n```\r\n\r\nUsing more warmups solves this problem, somewhere around 3000-5000 giving results that are more in line with my expectation:\r\n\r\n```\r\nbench Myers 32bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 1,470 ns/iter (1,100..64,300 ns/iter) ok (47ms)\r\nbench Myers 32bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 4,473 ns/iter (4,100..129,601 ns/iter) ok (17ms)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string JavaScript ... 1000 iterations 739,221 ns/iter (731,106..1,865,416 ns/iter) ok (1s)\r\nbench Myers 1024bp string WebAssembly ... 1000 iterations 144,435 ns/iter (143,501..224,102 ns/iter) ok (298ms)\r\n```\r\n\r\nDon't know if anyone else has encountered something similar, but this made me wonder if the default warmups for `Deno.bench` should be higher, assuming more people will be using this in CI environments in the future.\r\n", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "GJZwiers", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-03-29T18:49:41Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T08:05:57Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14147", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14156, "title": "`deno test` printer feedback", "body": "I've been using deno as a test runner for some time now, and after having written about 900 tests, I've found a few issues with the way test results are printed. I'm not sure about any future plans for this, and I didn't want to create a separate ticket for every issue I had. So I figured I'd post something here first.\r\n\r\n## Finding the source of a failing test\r\nWhen a test fails, the first thing you want to do is go to source code of the failing test in order to find out what exactly the test is about. Right now this is not very easy, you can do one of the following:\r\n - Look for a path in the stack trace of the failing test, but sometimes stack traces are long and you won't be able to find the file you need right away.\r\n - Scroll up to your test results and find the one that says 'FAILED' then look up a few lines until you see the path of the file.\r\n - Use your ide to search for the name of the test in all your files, and hope that another file with a similar test name doesn't exist.\r\n\r\n### Possible solutions:\r\n\r\n - https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/13586 would certainlly help, but not eliminate the issue entirely\r\n - In the list of test results, print the `running n tests from file:///path/to/file.test.js` with a different color. That way when you scroll up you can immediately distinguish tests that come from one specific file.\r\n - Print the path of a failing test above the error. Currently the name of the test is already printed, but often this is not enough. Say you have a file `calculator.test.js` with test name `\"it adds two numbers together\"` and only the name is printed, it is not immediately clear that this test is from `calculator.test.js` rather than another file that might have a similar test.\r\n - Print the path above the final list of failures. This could look similar to how the first list of test results look, where it prints the path only once if multiple tests in a single file fail.\r\n\r\n## It's hard to distinguish `console.log()` calls from test results\r\nRight now if code calls `console.log` the result gets appended in between `[test name] ...` and `ok` without any new lines, and multiple calls print double new lines in between every call.\r\nThe same happens with `Check https://example.com/file.js` being printed when dynamically importing files.\r\n\r\nFor instance, this test, which dynamically imports blob urls:\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/2737650/160927670-d88a76d2-c298-4094-a301-80a5ac80a09c.png)\r\n\r\nThough I reckon this would be pretty nontrivial, it seems logging code is already pretty scattered accross the codebase (see https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/13001).\r\n\r\n## Hiding test results if they don't fail\r\nIf I remember correctly, the test runner used by [the Chrome DevTools team](https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/devtools-frontend) simply shows a progress bar of all the tests and only failing tests are reported. I believe they use Mocha.\r\n\r\nIn a way I like it because it's a lot easier to find failing tests, since you don't have to scroll through all the test results. And if you have a huge amount of tests, where one of the first ones fails, you won't have to wait for the entire suite to finish because the error would get printed right away.\r\n\r\nBut on the other hand I kind of like the verbosity that Deno has right now. Seeing a whole bunch of green ok's is pretty satisfying.\r\n\r\n## A quick mockup\r\nHere's a quick mockup of what some of these changes could look like.\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/2737650/160925198-9b11111b-465b-4574-b4b4-ecf17d31bbd2.png)\r\n\r\n<!--https://github.com/denoland/deno/files/8384024/testmockup.js.zip-->\r\n\r\nI think it's a lot easier to visually parse like this. Not sure how feasible it would be to print error messages in red though. Since error messages might have custom color formatting. Maybe they should only be printed in red if the error message does not contain any ansi codes.\r\n\r\n### Recap\r\nQuick recap of all the changes:\r\n- print file paths in the results with a different color\r\n- add file paths to the errors section\r\n- add file paths to the failures section\r\n- print error messages with a red color\r\n- divide the tests in three sections: \"results\", \"errors\" and \"failures\" and print each section in yellow\r\n- Better printing of `console.log()` calls in between the results (not shown in the mockup)\r\n\r\nFeel free to let me know if these changes are desirable (or if only a subset of them are), I'd be glad to create a new ticket for each of these.", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 3, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jespertheend", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-03-30T20:49:58Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-08T05:22:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14156", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14162, "title": "Porting Deno to NetBSD", "body": "I have been using Deno on Debian based servers with Chrome for front-ends for a couple of months. I have previously dallied with Duktape and quickJS but I find Deno far better suited to my use cases.\r\n\r\nI can't seem to find any BSD ports (Mac OS/X being an exception). Has anybody ported Deno to NetBSD?  I believe there is a Rust compiler for it.  Would it be a difficult undertaking?", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Cyberfonic", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-03-31T07:59:57Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-18T04:01:56Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14162", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14197, "title": "Design challenges with Stdin and Signals", "body": "\"on SIGINT -> do something\" can be accomplished with `Deno.addSignalListener(\"SIGINT\", ()=>/*do something*/)`\r\n\r\n\"on Stdin -> do something\" however, has been a bit more challenging. \r\n\r\nThe behavior I'm looking for is a callback that isn't put on the event loop. E.g. if the rest of the program finished => Deno.exit (e.g. don't sit and wait for Stdin). \r\n\r\nIs this possible? I've tried a few round-about methods with streams, but haven't found a non-promise based API.", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jeff-hykin", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-04-03T23:54:49Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-16T19:06:09Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14197", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14248, "title": "Converting UnsafePointers to string", "body": "Hello, playing round the unstable Deno FFI and so far loving it. One question tho\r\n\r\nSuppose I have this \r\n\r\n```ts\r\nconst sys = Deno.dlopen(lib, {\r\n  func: {\r\n    name: 'some_func',\r\n    parameters: ['pointer'], \r\n    result: 'pointer' \r\n  },\r\n});\r\n\r\nsys.symbols.func() // returns UnsafePointer { value: 105553155269408n }\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs there a method we can use to convert the pointer to a string for example? Thanks!", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "targetlucked69", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-04-10T20:49:01Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-14T09:04:44Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14248", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14273, "title": "Design for platform dependent operations", "body": "Perspective:\r\n\r\nI use Deno as a replacement for bash/powershell (which has been fantastic BTW). I've run into a handful of limitations like checking if a command is valid (if it's in the path), or getting the owner of a file (as a username not UID), etc.\r\n\r\nCases being considered :\r\n\r\n- macOS file attributes\r\n- selinux permission system\r\n- redox OS\r\n- all the non-POSIX garbage in Windows\r\n\r\n\r\nDiscussion:\r\n\r\nThere are currently a few parts of the Deno API that behave differently depending on the OS. And I think there's quite a few more in unstable that are also not universal.\r\n\r\nI'd like to propose having a separate part of the API for OS-specific functions, while keeping the core Deno API a bit more pure in terms of cross-platform-ness. For example, instead of having chmod-permissions married to Deno, it could be under `Deno.os.unix.chmod`. And instead of highly specific vanilla-linux result for `lstat` the returned value could contain abstracted valuest that are likely to work cross platform.\r\n\r\n1. I think this would help core devs, since cross platform API design is much much harder than OS specific API design. Platform specific can be done first giving users the functionality they need, then later a cross-platform API can be designed, tested, and implemented.\r\n2. It prevents bloat to the core Deno API\r\n3. It reduces the number of edgecases, we don't have to think \"does Deno.symlink work for just Unix or also windows?\" \r\n4. It would make it easy when reading a codebase to see OS specific methods at a glance.\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jeff-hykin", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-04-13T19:11:45Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:30:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14273", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14348, "title": "Delay between GitHub release and Blog Post", "body": "Hi all, thanks for the awesome work!\r\n\r\nI'm a subscriber of Deno Releases on GitHub. Every time a new version is published, I get notified by email. Then I go to check the changelog but it is too low-level, so I prefer reading the blog post at deno.com/blog\r\n\r\nI'm noticing this post is consistently delayed by a few hours or days.\r\n\r\nBy having this delay, we are loosing the very small window of opportunity between users receiving a notification and engaging it. If it is too difficult to read the release notes right after being notified, people will end up disabling them, reducing further engagement.\r\n\r\nI suggest one of the requirements for major/minor releases is to have the blog post ready and link to it when releasing on GitHub. As a developer, this wouldn't be something I like waiting for when releasing, but as a business standpoint it seems to make sense to me.", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 3, "reactions_plus": 3, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "danilofuchs", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-04-21T08:42:49Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-20T13:30:31Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14348", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14442, "title": "Opened file cannot be closed after the response returned", "body": "For example, I want to serve a static file with stream.\r\n\r\nI tried `Deno.open()` like this:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nserve(async () => { \r\n  const file = await Deno.open(\"./file.txt\");\r\n  return new Response(file.readable, {\r\n    headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\", },\r\n  });\r\n  // But the file cannot be closed \r\n  // due to the async arrow function has been returned\r\n  file.close();\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nI've found an example from [Deno Blog](https://deno.com/blog/deploy-static-files):\r\n```ts\r\nimport { serve } from \"https://deno.land/std@0.120.0/http/server.ts\";\r\n\r\nconst FILE_URL = new URL(\"/movie.webm\", import.meta.url).href;\r\n\r\nconsole.log(\"Listening on http://localhost:8000\");\r\nserve(async () => {\r\n  const resp = await fetch(FILE_URL);\r\n  return new Response(resp.body, {\r\n    headers: {\r\n      \"content-type\": \"video/webm\",\r\n    },\r\n  });\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nBesides using fetch fileURL, using [Low Level HTTP Server API](https://deno.land/manual@v1.21.1/runtime/http_server_apis_low_level) also works:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nasync function handle(conn: Deno.Conn) {\r\n  const httpConn = Deno.serveHttp(conn);\r\n  for await (const requestEvent of httpConn) {\r\n    const file = await Deno.open(\"./file.txt\");\r\n    await requestEvent.respondWith(\r\n      new Response(file.readable, {\r\n        status: 200,\r\n      }),\r\n    );\r\n    file.close();\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nconst server = Deno.listen({ port: 8080 });\r\n\r\nfor await (const conn of server) {\r\n  handle(conn);\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI have two questions:\r\n1. I know the API `serve()` provides is imitating `fetch()`, so the response is **returned**. But can we have another High Level API to cover this case?\r\n\r\nIt may look like this:\r\n```ts\r\nunnamed_serve(async (req, res) => { \r\n  const file = await Deno.open(\"./file.txt\");\r\n  res.respondWith(new Response(file.readable, {\r\n    headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\", },\r\n  }));\r\n  file.close();\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. What's the magic that fetching a fileURL needs no closing manually?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 1, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "Master-Hash", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-04-30T14:52:18Z", "updated_at": "2022-07-17T13:25:47Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14442", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14519, "title": "Monthly hiring discussions like Next.js", "body": "Next.js has two monthly GitHub hiring discussions, like:\r\n\r\n- [Who wants to be hired? (May 2022)](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/36737)\r\n- [Who's hiring? (May 2022)](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/36738)\r\n\r\nThat's very helpful for the community and I think we should have it too\r\n\r\n", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 1, "reactions_plus": 1, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "erfanium", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-05-07T08:22:36Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T12:10:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14519", "classification": "random"}
