{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14755, "title": "Programmatically compile: how to substitute Deno.emit?", "body": "With deno 1.22 [deno.emit](https://doc.deno.land/deno/unstable@v1.21.0/~/Deno.emit) is no longer available. What's the current way to programmatically execute the functionality provided by the command-line tool, such as compiling and bundling?", "category": "Q&A", "category_slug": "q-a", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "retog", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-05-29T12:04:01Z", "updated_at": "2022-05-29T13:29:46Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14755", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14803, "title": "Is it safe to store secrets in op_state?", "body": "Hi, I want to put some data into the op_state in my Rust functions to persist it between JS function calls.  But it's vitally important that the JS can't see the data.  Is this safe?  Is there a way for the JS to see into op_state?\r\n\r\nThanks", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "glitch003", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-05-31T19:14:09Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-06T01:46:38Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14803", "classification": "need-related"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14767, "title": "I can't seem to upgrade deno on macOS 10.14.6 via brew", "body": "```\r\nbrew upgrade deno\r\nWarning: You are using macOS 10.14.\r\nWe (and Apple) do not provide support for this old version.\r\n...\r\n==> Upgrading 1 outdated package:\r\ndeno 1.0.5 -> 1.22.0\r\n...\r\n==> perl ./Configure --prefix=/usr/local/Cellar/openssl@1.1/1.1.1o --openssldir=/usr/local/etc/openssl@1.1 no-ssl3 no-ssl3-method no-zlib darwin64-x86_64-cc enable-ec_nistp_64_gcc_128\r\n==> make\r\n==> make install MANDIR=/usr/local/Cellar/openssl@1.1/1.1.1o/share/man MANSUFFIX=ssl\r\n==> make test\r\nLast 15 lines from /Users/anjanesh-mac/Library/Logs/Homebrew/openssl@1.1/04.make:\r\n../test/recipes/95-test_external_boringssl.t ....... skipped: No external tests in this configuration\r\n../test/recipes/95-test_external_krb5.t ............ skipped: No external tests in this configuration\r\n../test/recipes/95-test_external_pyca.t ............ skipped: No external tests in this configuration\r\n../test/recipes/99-test_ecstress.t ................. ok\r\n../test/recipes/99-test_fuzz.t ..................... ok\r\n\r\nTest Summary Report\r\n-------------------\r\n../test/recipes/80-test_ssl_new.t                (Wstat: 256 Tests: 29 Failed: 1)\r\n  Failed test:  12\r\n  Non-zero exit status: 1\r\nFiles=158, Tests=2635, 106 wallclock secs ( 1.40 usr  0.18 sys + 70.58 cusr 25.94 csys = 98.10 CPU)\r\nResult: FAIL\r\nmake[1]: *** [_tests] Error 1\r\nmake: *** [tests] Error 2\r\n\r\nDo not report this issue to Homebrew/brew or Homebrew/core!\r\n```\r\n\r\nI can't seem to upgrade deno on macOS 10.14.6 and now `deno --verison` shows `-bash: deno: command not found` - now ev en 1.0.5 is gone.\r\n\r\nWhat is the alternative way to install deno 1.22.1 ?", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 2, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "anjanesh", "author_association": "NONE", "created_at": "2022-06-01T12:05:38Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-15T11:39:24Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14767", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14770, "title": "denoland organization .github health files and profile", "body": "I have created a repository containing the necessary health files for `denoland` organization, code of conduct, and of course a profile file.\r\n\r\nMore on this here: \r\nhttps://docs.github.com/en/organizations/collaborating-with-groups-in-organizations/customizing-your-organizations-profile\r\nand here:\r\nhttps://github.com/lucasmichot/denoland.github/issues/1\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI am willing to transfer the ownership of this repository to `denoland`: is someone willing to help me with this ?\r\n@bartlomieju or @kitsonk maybe ?", "category": "Ideas", "category_slug": "ideas", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "lucasmichot", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-06-01T14:34:43Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-01T14:34:48Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14770", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14790, "title": "How can I develop a proxy server with Deno or nodejs?", "body": "Sometimes I visit deno land gets stuck, and I can't even download the corresponding resources. I'm not sure whether this is related to the policy. \r\n\r\nBecause I need to install dependencies in the CICD pipeline, sometimes the installation failure will affect the service update, so I want to use proxy to solve my problem. \r\n\r\n> Deno supports proxies for module downloads and the Web standard fetch API.\r\nProxy configuration is read from environmental variables: HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY and NO_PROXY.\r\nIn case of Windows, if environment variables are not found Deno falls back to reading proxies from registry.\r\n\r\nBut I don't know how to develop this service? In my imagination, I want to cache the corresponding resources on my server and return directly from the server next time.This step is easy, but I don't know what rules can meet the proxy requirements.\r\n", "category": "General", "category_slug": "general", "state": "open", "state_reason": null, "comments": 0, "reactions_total": 0, "reactions_plus": 0, "reactions_minus": 0, "reactions_laugh": 0, "reactions_hooray": 0, "reactions_confused": 0, "reactions_heart": 0, "reactions_rocket": 0, "reactions_eyes": 0, "author": "jiawei397", "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR", "created_at": "2022-06-03T04:13:00Z", "updated_at": "2022-06-03T04:19:52Z", "url": "https://github.com/denoland/deno/discussions/14790", "classification": "random"}
{"repo": "denoland/deno", "number": 14833, "title": "deno for nodejs dev", "body": "\r\n\r\ndeno for nodejs developer \r\n\r\nAs a reminder :\r\nnodejs does not support esm by default but deno does\r\nnodejs does not support ts by default it needs a tool like tsc but deno supports ts of the box.\r\nin the js ecosystem we have several options:\r\nwrite the code in js or ts\r\nwrite our code to be executed by deno or node (we are not talking about the browser here)\r\nObviously there is another option. \r\n\r\nfor this I define js-node, js-node and js-node-after-deno-build-tools as follows: \r\n\r\na javascript code can be written to be executed by node let's call the js-node.\r\nin this case we only need node runtime install. \r\n\r\nJavaScript code can be written to be executed by deno let's call it js-deno.\r\nin this case you only need deno runtime install. \r\n\r\na typescrypt code can be written to be compiled by deno tools and executed by node with the possibility of accessing all the node api clet's all it js-node-after-deno-build-tools. \r\n\r\nin this case we need both deno and node runtime \r\n\r\nadvantage:\r\n- trick to have nodejs support esm\r\n- trick to create nodejs project with deps without node_modules\r\n- esm (for node) can be shared via deno registry or elsewhere\r\n- adopt esm for the different JavaScript runtimes (node, deno, browser)\r\n- no need to wait for deno compat to enjoy deno for node developer \r\n\r\nremark:\r\nI know there is dnt a tool